{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.497209+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_000105b5329d7d83db19e15c4bda44c617ccce1d7abb68baba81c6b0e13cd94e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2605.09706, 'A dyadic construction of a three-dimensional attractive point interaction Markov family'] Fix $T,\beta,\varepsilon>0$ and $0\le s<t\le T$. Let $\Pi_{[s,t]}$ denote the collection of all finite partitions of $[s,t]$, partially ordered by
refinement (so $\pi'\succeq\pi$ means $\pi'$ refines $\pi$). The map $\widehat{\mu}^{T,\beta,\varepsilon}_{[s,t]}:\;
E_\varepsilon \times \mathcal B(E_\varepsilon)\longrightarrow [0,1]$
defined, for $x\in E_\varepsilon$ and $A\in\mathcal B(E_\varepsilon)$, by
\begin{align}
\widehat{\mu}^{T,\beta,\varepsilon}_{[s,t]}(x,A)
\,:=\,
\inf_{\pi\in\Pi_{[s,t]}}
\widehat{\mathsf K}^{T,\beta,\varepsilon,\pi}_{[s,t]}(x,A)\,
\end{align}
is well-defined and satisfies the following.
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item Fix $x\in E_\varepsilon$ and $A\in\mathcal B(E_\varepsilon)$.
There exists a refining sequence $(\sigma_m)_{m\ge1}\subset\Pi_{[s,t]}$ with
$|\sigma_m|\downarrow0$ as $m\to\infty$ such that
\begin{align}
\widehat{\mu}^{T,\beta,\varepsilon}_{[s,t]}(x,A)
\,=\,
\lim_{m\to\infty}
\widehat{\mathsf K}^{T,\beta,\varepsilon,\sigma_m}_{[s,t]}(x,A).
\end{align}

\item For every $x\in E_\varepsilon$, the set function
$A\mapsto \widehat{\mu}^{T,\beta,\varepsilon}_{[s,t]}(x,A)$ is a sub-probability measure on
$(E_\varepsilon,\mathcal B(E_\varepsilon))$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:33.540105+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0003315bfb386f32e43e945f55dd36d89aaacccdbc06db2a0dc818dbe62c8741","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2603.28734, 'Free energy analyticity of the disordered XY model and Debye screening in the 2D Coulomb g'] Let $(\Psi(n))_{n\geq 0}$ be a positive sequence that we assume monotone decreasing to zero.
Assume that there exists $\kappa > 0$, $C,c > 0$, and a polynomial $P$ such that for any $1 \leq m \leq n$, 
\begin{equation}
\Psi(2n) \leq P(m)\Psi((1-\kappa)n)^2 + C\e^{-cm}. 
\end{equation}
Then, $\Psi(n)$ tends superpolynomially fast to 0, that is, for any $A>0$, 
\[n^A \Psi(n) \underset{n\rightarrow \infty}{\longrightarrow} 0. \]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:44:39.750170+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00041f7ab4e770e182f5fc574d656e1583e5742085090bceeafe1e24b809497a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 23 of arXiv:2608.29872, 'Local Laws and Outlier Eigenvalues for Spiked Separable Covariance Matrices'] We have the following estimates.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item For any fixed integer $l\ge 0$, we have $\partial_{i \mu}^{l}Q \prec (1+\phi)^{l+1}\Theta^{2}$.
    \item $Q\prec(1+\phi)^{4}(q+\Theta)^{3}.$
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:19:06.742479+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00047b9f12ba7c87e8e3e3574e7d572fc8c72adf78d35d44bb742f3fd3b83671","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theorem_size_bias_bound of arXiv:2606.03442, 'Stein's method in network analysis'] Let~$X$ be a non-negative random variable with finite variance~$\sigma^2$, and let~$X^{(s)}$ be constructed on the
 same probability space as~$X$, having the size bias distribution of~$X$;
 write $\nu^2 := \mathbb{E}[(X^{(s)}-X)^2]$. Let~$\mathscr{F}$ be any sub-sigma field with respect to the probability
 space on which $X$ and $X^{(s)}$ are defined.
 Then,
    \begin{align*}
        d_{{\mathrm{BW}}}\bigl(&\mathcal{L}(\sigma^{-1}  (X- \mathbb{E}[X])),N(0,1)\bigr) \\
        &\ \le\  \sigma^{-2} \mathbb{E}[X] \bigg( 4 (\mathbb{V}[\mathbb{E}[X^{(s)}-X \, \vert \, \mathscr{F} ] ]  )^{1/2} +
        \frac{7\nu}{\sigma} \bigg( \frac{\nu}{2} + (\mathbb{E}[\mathbb{V}[X \, \vert \, \mathscr{F}]]^{1/2} \bigg) \bigg).
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:24:34.416084+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00069377ea6e11f3f97b40af4c58fe85a4c963969141dae524ed561da089c777","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2608.31145, 'Gauge-compatible tensors on statistical manifolds: splitting and submanifold geometry'] A nonzero $(1,1)$-tensor field $\Theta$ on $M$ is called a statistical gauge tensor if
\begin{equation}
\nabla_X(\Theta Y)=\Theta(\nabla_X^{*}Y),
\qquad
X,Y\in\Gamma(TM).
\end{equation}
A statistical manifold endowed with such a tensor will be called a
$\Theta$-statistical manifold.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:55.645042+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0009ccf78a8316b308243c3258d883b28cd387e8e7389055a34b551f2e7aaec3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 (KL local-error bound for kTULA) of arXiv:2605.24937, '2605.24937'] Assume \hyperref[ass:PJG]{\textup{(A1)}}--\hyperref[ass:JLC]{\textup{(A5)}}.
Let $\lambda\in(0,\lambda_{\max}^{\mathrm{kTULA}}]$.
Then there exist constants $C_1,C>0$, depending at most polynomially on the dimension and independent of $N$ and $\lambda$, such that for all $\mu,\nu\in\mathcal P_2(\R^d)$ and all $N\in\mathbb N$ with $N\ge3$,
\begin{equation}
\KL\!\bigl(\mu\,\widehat P^{N}\,\big\|\,\nu\,P^{N}\bigr)
\;\le\;
C_1\,\frac{1}{\lambda}\,\W^2(\mu,\nu)
\;+\;
C\,\bigl((N\lambda)\vee \log N\bigr)\lambda^2.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.972683+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_000dcfcfe2b773b132a238e3b19d1560d4a6d4cf5e1cf0e539fcb1a2c71aa1f0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:defBmu of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] For every $\tau\in\mathcal{T}_*$, we define 
    \begin{equs}
        \text{$\mathrm{Ind}$}(\tau)\eqdef\Big\{(\tau_1,\tau_2)\in\mathcal{T}^2 :\exists\,\underline{\tau}_i\in\tau_i\in\mathcal{T}\,,\;\exists\,n\in N(\underline{\tau}_1)\,,\;\underline{\tau}_2\graft_n\underline{\tau}_1\in\tau\Big\}
    \end{equs}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.436961+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00123405b6363774d813fa54dc73e5a8c2955a65669943b6db5f3499a92f1a34","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2606.15178, 'The distribution of the de Moivre experiment'] Let $ X $ be a discrete random variable with the bi$^{s}$nomial distribution with parameters $ n $ and $ p $, $ X\sim \mathcal{B}^{(s)}(n,p) $. Then, since $X$ is a sum of $n$ i.i.d.\ $s$-Bernoulli variables, $G_X=(G_{X_1})^n$; explicitly,
	\begin{equation}
		G_X(t) =\left(\frac{q^{s+1}-(pt)^{s+1} }{q-p t}\right)^n=\sum_{k=0}^{sn}{n\choose k}_s (pt)^kq^{sn-k},\quad \text{ for } p\neq q,
	\end{equation}
	and
	\begin{equation}
		G_X(t) =\frac{1}{\left( s+1\right)^n}\left( \frac{1-t^{s+1}}{1-t}\right)^n=\frac{1}{\left( s+1\right)^n}\sum_{k=0}^{sn}{n\choose k}_s t^k, \quad \text{ for } p=q.
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:19:06.746507+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0012cfcaeba746404eb54ca74a2e99f61b548ed6243eff4742cbc2e695a3afe9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:vector-q-mass-identity-paper of arXiv:2606.08683, 'Exact Fourier dimensions of dyadic Mandelbrot cascades under minimal integrability'] For every \(q>0\) and every \(n\ge0\),
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\lvert u \rvert=n}L_u^q\right]=\rho(q)^n,
\end{equation}
where the identity is understood in the extended sense, and where
\(
\rho(q)^0=1
\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.749510+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00134c863a800ef3f1cb361cb8d1eb07233b3f1d5e7423ef729ed0c4c187e4e8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:fokker_superpo of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ and let ${\boldsymbol\mu}:[0,\infty)\to \mathcal{M}_{+}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ (with ${\boldsymbol\mu}_0 = \nu$) be such that
\begin{equation}
\big[(t,x)\mapsto {\bf b}(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t)\big],  \ \big[ (t,x)\mapsto {\boldsymbol\sigma} {\boldsymbol\sigma}^\top(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t)\big] \in L^{1}_{\text{loc}}([0,\infty) \times \mathbb{R}^d , \overline{\boldsymbol\mu}),
\end{equation}
with $\overline{\boldsymbol\mu}$ as in Notation (not:measure_dt_dx of the paper).

Then:

\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] If $X$ is a solution to MKV$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$, with $[X_t]={\boldsymbol\mu}_t $ for $t\geq0$, then ${\boldsymbol\mu}$ is a weak solution to NL-FP$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$.
\item[(ii)] [Superposition] Under the additional assumption that 
\begin{equation}
\Big[ t \mapsto \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \Big( |{\bf b}(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t)| + \| {\boldsymbol\sigma}{\boldsymbol\sigma}^\top(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t) \| \Big) {\boldsymbol\mu}_t (d x)\Big], \  \big[ t \mapsto {\boldsymbol\mu}_{t} (\mathbb{R}^d) \big]  \in L^{\infty}_{\text{loc}}([0,\infty) ,
\end{equation}

if ${\boldsymbol\mu}$ is a (weak) solution to NL-FP$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$, 
 then there exists a solution $X$ to MKV$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$ such that $ [X_t] = {\boldsymbol\mu}_t $.

\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.649058+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00152b42f7a290b779d3bd2c718792f3c4b04f8e34b6e3e40ed935b852c66f97","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem LastOne of arXiv:2411.13926, 'Random walks on random walks:\\ non-perturbative results in high dimensions'] Let $Y_k^{(1)} \in I^{(1)}$ be the random lift of $y_k \in I^{(k)}$. Then 
\begin{align}
\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}(Y_k^{(1)}=x) = P^{RW}(X \in x \;|\;  D_k), \quad x \in  I^{(1)}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.319332+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0016af85956542def1ce965efbae40a654d95e7b632c2b85068cd8deacb5a35a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:extra:2-ST of arXiv:2608.30973, 'Pseudo-Anosov flow and dynamics on guts'] Suppose that \((T,R_{+},R_{-},\gamma)\) is a non-longitudinal 2-ST. Then up to orbit equivalence, there is only one semiflow \(\varphi\) satisfying the following conditions.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \(\varphi\) is tight.
\item \(\varphi\) contains exactly one bi-infinite flowline \(\lambda\) and \(\lambda\) is a pseudo-hyperbolic closed orbit.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.953713+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0018e2f96282192913266094c67e494edafdb5a342b8e8d6e29e4771dda32a5f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 26 of arXiv:2604.05234, 'Quantitative propagation of chaos and universality for asymmetric Langevin spin glass dyna'] Fix $t,q\in (0,\infty)$. There exist constants $N_0(t, q) \in \bbN$ and $C(t, q) \in (0, \infty)$ depending on $(t,q)$ such that for all $N\geq N_0(t, q)$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \bbE\Big[\bbW_1^2(P_t^1(\bfJ),\gamma_q)\Big]\geq \frac{C(t,q)}{N}.
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:38:13.640000+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_001ce081dc4b4c3104d2b1d30094d7f00f7af1373371f97f40152667c87e3f24","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 15 of arXiv:2609.00690, 'Boundary behavior and optimal pointwise H\"older exponent of the total local time of $(1+\'] Let $V^\lambda$ be the bounded nonnegative distributional solution to \eqref{V lambda} which vanishes at infinity. Then we have
	\begin{equation}
		V^\lambda(x)=\bigg[\frac{\beta}{\sqrt{\beta+2}}|x|+\bigg(\frac{2}{\lambda\sqrt{\beta+2}}\bigg)^{\frac{\beta}{2+\beta}}\bigg]^{-\frac{2}{\beta}},\quad x\in\mathbb R,\ \lambda>0.
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.578871+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0022a926c4304dacb5149ae7b865212c443887973d2ae2cf8f9008f89b5ba249","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 54 of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] Let $d\geq 2$. For every $\beta\geq 0$, and every $x,y\in \mathbb Z^d$,
\begin{equation}
	\mathbf P_{\beta}^{\emptyset,\emptyset}[x,y\in \mathbf{C}_{\n_1+\n_2}(0)]\leq \langle \sigma_0\sigma_x\rangle_{\beta}\langle \sigma_x\sigma_y\rangle_{\beta}\langle \sigma_y\sigma_0\rangle_{\beta}+(x\Leftrightarrow y),
\end{equation}
where $(x\Leftrightarrow y)$ denotes the same term with $x$ and $y$ inverted. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.322076+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_002488fc7b24e4082e5a4d94030dba356bf73730007620f7c0e94b975d3bb01d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma keylemma of arXiv:2411.06245, 'On the longest/shortest negative excursion of a Lévy risk process and related quantities'] For $q,r>0$ and

for $x\in \mathbb{R}$,
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&\mathbb{E}_{x}\left[ \mathrm{e}^{-q\tau _{0}^{-}}\mathbb{P}_{X_{\tau _{0}^{-}}}\left(U_{\mathrm{e}_q}<r,\tau _{0}^{+}>\mathrm{e}_q\right) \right]  =\left(1-\mathrm{e}^{-qr}\right)\left(Z_q (x) -\dfrac{q}{\Phi _{q}} W_q (x) \right)  \\
&&\qquad-\left(q\int_{0}^{r}\mathrm{e}^{-qs}\Lambda \left( x,s\right) ds- W_q (x)\mathrm{e}^{-qr}\int_{0}^{r}\int_{0}^{\infty }\mathrm{e}^{\Phi _{q}z}\frac{z}{r}
\mathbb{P}\left( X_{r}\in \mathrm{d}z\right) \mathrm{d}s\right).
\end{eqnarray*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.754251+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0024a41e4cd1d2784b7c8ade47609a23af03f731fad5df1377483b88df436266","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:intro-lbm-spectrum of arXiv:2608.02459, 'Response Calculus for Spectral Simplicity\\ and Joint Eigenvalue Densities'] For every $0<\gamma<2$, almost surely, $A_h$ has compact resolvent, and its
positive eigenvalues, listed with multiplicity, satisfy
\begin{equation}
 0<\Lambda_1^h<\Lambda_2^h<\cdots\uparrow\infty.

\end{equation}
Moreover, for every $N\ge1$ and $1\le n_1<\cdots<n_N$,
\begin{equation}
 (\Lambda_{n_1}^h,\ldots,\Lambda_{n_N}^h)_\#\mathbb P
 \ll\mathcal L^N.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:08.777827+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0033789497d474c69b5681ff923f051beede6825324717335b52c4090e8f43d5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop unmin of arXiv:2411.18359, 'Large systems of symmetrized trapped Brownian Bridges and Schr\"odinger processes'] Under the assumptions of Proposition (cormain of the paper), the following hold
\begin{enumerate}

\item[(i)] We have that
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\inf_{\mu \in {\mathcal M }_1({\mathcal C }_\beta)}\Bigl\{I^{{\scriptscriptstyle{({\rm sym}})}}_{W,{\mathfrak m}}(\mu)-\langle \mu_{0,\beta},\log g\rangle\Bigr\}=\\
&\inf\limits_{q\in{\mathcal M }_1^{\ssup{{\rm s}}}(\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^d)}\Big\{H(q|\overline{q}\otimes{\mathfrak m})-\int_{\mathbb{R} ^d}\int_{\mathbb{R} ^d}\log \mathbb{E}^\beta_{x,y}\Big[e^{-\Tilde{W} }\Big]q({\rm d} x, {\rm d} y)-\langle g,\log g\rangle\Bigr\}.\\
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\item[(ii)] The unique minimizer of the (non-normalized) rate function defined in Proposition (cormain of the paper) (i) is given by 
\begin{equation}
    \mu^*_{g,{\mathfrak m},W}=\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}Q^{\beta}_{x,y}\circ B^{-1}q^*_{g,{\mathfrak m},W}({\rm d} x, {\rm d} y),
\end{equation}
where for $x,y \in \mathbb{R} ^d $, we define
\begin{equation}
    Q^\beta_{x,y}({\rm d} \omega):=\frac{1}{\mathbb{E}^\beta_{x,y}\big[e^{-\Tilde{W} }\big]}e^{-\Tilde{W}(\omega)}\mathbb{P}^\beta_{x,y}({\rm d} \omega),\qquad \omega \in {\mathcal C }_\beta,
\end{equation}
and $q_{g,{\mathfrak m},W}^*\in{\mathcal M }_1^{\ssup{{\rm s}}}(\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^d)$ is the unique minimizer of the formula on the right hand side of (equation infIg of the paper).
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.162672+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00338a3661f62d473270a2b3e03727db93be3f8107c82d4dd975922bfd05fde9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 14 (Theorem 2.3 of \cite{zhang2016central}) of arXiv:2604.17302, 'Elephant random walk with attributed steps and extractions of random sizes'] Suppose $\{Z_{n}\}$ converges to the constant $\theta^{*}$ almost surely, and that Assumptions~\eqref{gen_assump_dist_1} and \eqref{gen_assump_dist_3} are satisfied. Suppose $\rho>1/2$, and that
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\delta_{i}=o\left(\sqrt{n}\right) \text{ almost surely}.
\end{equation}
Then 
\begin{equation}
\sqrt{n}\left(Z_{n}-\theta^{*}\right)\convd N(0,\Sigma),
\end{equation}
where, with $\Gamma$ is the matrix mentioned in Assumption~\eqref{gen_assump_dist_3}, we set
\begin{equation}
\Sigma=\int_{0}^{\infty}\exp\left\{\left(J_{G}(\theta^{*})+I_{d}/2\right)u\right\}\Gamma\left[\exp\left\{\left(J_{G}(\theta^{*})+I_{d}/2\right)u\right\}\right]^{t}du.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.369521+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_003bbf8826fe291399fedbe5322f1bb69f67b77e27d43c1fb4a86d8621172e0d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Young_with_blow_up of arXiv:2412.01645, 'Regularisation by Gaussian rough path lifts of fractional Brownian motions'] Let $s \in [0,1]$,  $\alpha, \beta \in (0,1)$ such that $\alpha+ \beta >1$ and let $\gamma \in (0, \beta)$. Then, for all $f \in \mathcal{C}^\alpha([s,1])$, $g \in \mathcal{C}^\beta_\gamma([s,1])$ and  $(u,t) \in [0,1]^2_{\leq}$, we have 
\begin{equs}
\big| \int_u^t f_{u,r} \, dg_r \big| \lesssim [f]_{\mathcal{C}^\alpha} [g]_{\mathcal{C}^\beta_\gamma} |t-u|^{\beta+\alpha -\gamma}.
\end{equs} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:19.042401+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_004ce9813dec54b7a70a5e3d6c0b6db0fe5e471975ef30cef98a20fb237374dc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm-P1 of arXiv:2609.02552, 'Resolvent Estimates for the Stokes Operator \\ in a Three-Dimensional Lipschitz Domain'] Let $d=3$ and $\lambda\in \Sigma_\theta$.
Let $(u, \phi )$ be a weak solution of (equation eq-0 of the paper), where
$F\in C_{0, \sigma}^\infty(\Omega)$ and $\Omega$ is a bounded Lipschitz domain.
Let  $B(x, 2r)\subset \Omega$.
Then
\begin{equation}
\left(
\fint_{B(x, r)} |\phi -\fint_{B(x, r)}  \phi  |^q \right)^{1/q}
\le C  M_2(|\nabla u|)(\delta(x) )
\end{equation}
for any $2< q< \infty$, 
where $C$ depends only on  $\Omega$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.770915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0051a1adbe08c96111922dea142fa5738df798885969717d0b48a352e228a7b7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 43 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Assume the same parameters as in Definition \ref{Def.ParametersBulk}. Fix $m \in \mathbb{N}$, $t_1, \dots, t_m \in [0, \infty)$ with $t_1 < t_2 < \cdots < t_m$, and set $\mathcal{T} = \{t_1, \dots, t_m\}$. We also define for $t \in \mathcal{T}$ the quantity $T_{t} = T_t(N) =  \lfloor t N^{2/3} \rfloor$ and the lattice $\Lambda_t(N) = a_t(N) \cdot \mathbb{Z} + b_t(N)$, where 
\begin{equation}
a_t(N) = \sigma_1^{-1} N^{-1/3}, \mbox{ and } b_t(N) = \sigma_1^{-1} N^{-1/3} \cdot \left( - h_1 N - p_1 T_{t} \right).
\end{equation}

We let $\mathbb{P}_N$ be the Pfaffian Schur process from Definition \ref{Def.SchurProcess}. Here, we assume that $M = M(N)$ is sufficiently large so that $M \geq T_{t_m}$. If $(\lambda^0, \dots, \lambda^M)$ have law $\mathbb{P}_N$, we define the random variables
\begin{equation}
X_i^{j,N} = \sigma_1^{-1} N^{-1/3} \cdot \left( \lambda_i^{T_{t_j}} - h_1 N - p_1 T_{t_j}  - i\right) \mbox{ for } i \in \mathbb{N} \mbox{ and } j \in \llbracket 1, m \rrbracket.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:21:56.724863+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0054b28e407cb4a2095f0f08594508aa24d985f12442f2462ae63dbec4f2089d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma le:dist_across_chain of arXiv:2507.15589, 'Tightness of approximations to metrics on non-simple conformal loop ensemble gaskets'] Fix $a_1 > 0$. There exists $N\in\mathbf{N}$ such that the following is true. Let $\widetilde{z}\in D$ and $\delta>0$ such that $B(\widetilde{z},\delta) \subseteq D$, and let $\lambda \in (0,1]$. Let $\widetilde{G}_{\widetilde{z},\delta,\lambda}$ be the event that there is a set of at most $N$ points $z_1,z_2,\ldots \in A(\widetilde{z},\delta^{1+a_1},\delta)$ with $\abs{z_{i_1}-z_{i_2}} \ge \delta^{1+a_1}$ for each pair $i_1 \neq i_2$ and such that whenever $\eta \cap B(\widetilde{z},\delta^{1+a_1}) \neq \varnothing$, the following hold.
\begin{itemize}
\item The set $\{z_1,z_2,\ldots\}$ separates $\eta \cap B(\widetilde{z},\delta^{1+a_1})$ from $\partial B(\widetilde{z},\delta)$ in $\Upsilon_\Gamma$.
\item Let $K'$ be any connected component of $\Upsilon_\Gamma \setminus \{z_1,z_2,\ldots\}$ adjacent to $\eta \cap B(\widetilde{z},\delta^{1+a_1})$, and let $i_1,i_2$ so that $z_{i_1},z_{i_2} \in \partial K'$. Let $U'_1 \in \metregions$ be such that $\overline{U'_1}$ contains all \emph{simple} admissible paths within $K'$ from $z_{i_1}$ to $z_{i_2}$. Then
\[ \metapproxacres{\epsilon}{U'_1}{z_{i_1}}{z_{i_2}}{\Gamma} \le \lambda^{{d_{{\mathrm{dbl}}}}}\median{\epsilon}+\lambda^{a_0}\epsilon^{-a_0}\mathbf{a}_{\epsilon} . \]
\end{itemize}
Then $\mathbf{P}[\widetilde{G}_{\widetilde{z},\delta,\lambda}^c] = O((\delta/\lambda)^{{\alpha_{\mathrm{apr}}}})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:51:15.030740+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00584744a55aaa43f7ba0aff456f824b5012486af4d00fd5faea87c3c9a6812d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma proptfund1 of arXiv:2609.03909, 'Conic Pseudo-Finslerian Mechanical Systems'] Let  $\mathcal{U} \subset TM \setminus 0$ be an open subset invariant under positive scalar multiplication and let$ \mathcal L$ be a Lagrangian, of class $C^2$ on $\mathcal U$, which is positively $2$-homogeneous. Then its fundamental tensor satisfies the following properties:
