{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.224134+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_01238ee5ac0f7429ba463e8e37f96c84fd12b5d4a2cf500ce2bc44a3f9d2403a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary Cor:conformal of arXiv:2609.00324, 'Quantitative Stability for Fractional Yamabe minimizers'] Let $(M^n , [\hat{h}])$ be the conformal infinity of an asymptotically hyperbolic manifold $(X^{n+1},g^+ )$ such that $\Lambda_{\gamma} (M,[\hat{h}]) < \Lambda_{\gamma} (\mathbb{S}^n ,[h_{0}])$. Then, there exist constants $C>0$ and $\theta \geq 0$, depending on $M$ and $[\hat{h}]$, such that
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{I}_\gamma (h) - \Lambda_{\gamma} (M,[\hat{h}]) \geq C \left( \frac{\inf\{\|h - \tilde{h}\|: \tilde{h} \in \mathcal{M}  \} }{\operatorname{vol}_{h}(M)^{1/2^*} }   \right)^{2+\theta} \quad \forall h \in [\hat{h}].
\end{align}
Moreover, when $\Lambda_\gamma (M,[\hat{h}]) > 0$ and the deficit $\mathcal{I}_\gamma (h) - \Lambda_{\gamma} (M,[\hat{h}]) \leq 1$, there exist constants $C>0$ and $\theta \geq 0$, depending on $M$ and $[\hat{h}]$, such that
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{I}_\gamma (h) - \Lambda_{\gamma} (M,[\hat{h}]) \geq C \left( \frac{\inf\{\|h - \tilde{h}\|_*: \tilde{h} \in \mathcal{M}  \} }{\operatorname{vol}_{h}(M)^{1/2^*} }   \right)^{2+\theta} \quad \forall h \in [\hat{h}].
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.186473+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0123acca4ce0e7ac99b7677af256d7fa6bf644d3b7c4fc315dac4c5d3722f7a1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem logistic_limit_thm of arXiv:2412.07258, 'Permuton limit of a generalization of the Mallows and $k$-card-minimum models'] Let $g_{\alpha}, \alpha \geq 1$
satisfy Assumption (assumption_kk of the paper). Let
us define the random variables
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{U}_\alpha := \frac{X_\alpha + Y_\alpha}{2}, \qquad \mathcal{V}_\alpha := \gamma(\alpha)\cdot \frac{X_\alpha-Y_\alpha}{2}, \end{equation}
where the random vector $(X_\alpha, Y_\alpha)$ has  distribution $\mu_{g_{\alpha}}$ (cf.\ Definition (def_permuton_from_gt of the paper)). We have
\begin{align}
    (\mathcal{U}_\alpha, \mathcal{V}_\alpha) \Longrightarrow \big(U, \frac{1}{\psi(U)}\cdot L\big), \qquad \alpha \to \infty,
\end{align}
where $U \sim \mathrm{UNI}[0,1]$,  $L$ is independent of $U$ and $L$ has p.d.f. $\frac{2}{(e^t + e^{-t})^2}, \, t \in \mathbb{R}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:26:52.661283+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_01247b129dddd665d245aa53a7906db2063baba808b75b88be74c0e73c848bba","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 (bundle kernel and positive type) of arXiv:2609.03736, '2609.03736'] Every $A\in\mathcal H_E$ has a unique invariant bundle kernel
$K_A(x,y):E_y\to E_x$, normalized by
\begin{equation}
 (Af)(x)=\frac1{|X|}\sum_{y\in X}K_A(x,y)f(y).

\end{equation}
It satisfies
\begin{equation}
 K_A(gx,gy)=gK_A(x,y)g^{-1},\qquad
 K_{A^*}(x,y)=K_A(y,x)^*.

\end{equation}
A Hermitian bundle kernel $K$ is of \emph{positive type} if
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{a,b=1}^s\langle v_a,K(x_a,x_b)v_b\rangle\geq0

\end{equation}
for every finite collection $x_1,\ldots,x_s\in X$ and
$v_a\in E_{x_a}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.972597+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_01273239cb33b29d383116ce26c75b0ea069b63c9804228c9da4c9f6e2512ceb","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:proj of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] Given $\tt u\in\R$ and some force coefficients $(\mathfrak{z}^\tau)^{\tau\in\mathcal{T}_*}$, we define the projector $\Pi$ acting on functionals $L:C^\infty([0,1],\R^{\mcb{n}})\longrightarrow C^\infty([0,1],\R^{\mcb{n}})$ of the form 
\begin{equs}
    L[v](t)=\sum_{\tau\in\mathcal{T}_*}\int_{\R^{\mathfrak{s}(\tau)}}\langle \mathfrak{z}^{\tau}(t,s^{\tau}),\Upsilon^{\tau}[v+{\tt u}](s^{\tau})\rangle_{\mathcal{H}^{\tau}}\mathrm{d} s^{\tau}\,,
\end{equs}
by projection onto $\mathcal{T}$. More precisely, we have
\begin{equs}
    \Pi L[v](t)\eqdef\sum_{\tau\in \mathcal{T}} \int_{\R^{\mathfrak{s}(\tau)}}\langle \mathfrak{z}^{\tau}(t,s^{\tau}),\Upsilon^{\tau}[v+{\tt u}](s^{\tau})\rangle_{\mathcal{H}^{\tau}}\mathrm{d} s^{\tau}\,.
\end{equs}
Finally, we also define an operator $\mathrm{Pol}_\mu$ acting on families $(L_\mu)_{\mu\in(0,1]}$ of functionals of the form (equation eq:formL of the paper) -- where the $(\mathfrak{z}^\tau)^{\tau\in\mathcal{T}_*}$ is replaced with $(\mathfrak{z}^\tau_\mu)^{\tau\in\mathcal{T}_*}_{\mu \in (0,1]}$ -- by
\begin{equs}
    \mathrm{Pol}_{\mu}(L_\mu)[]\eqdef\Pi\big(\d_\mu L_\mu[]+ \mathrm{D} L_\mu\big[,\dot G_\mu L_{\mu}[]\big]\big)\,.
\end{equs}
Note that $\mathrm{Pol}_{\mu}$ is a truncated version of the Polchinski flow equation.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:04:39.767986+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0128be809dd5f08167684b30a5671c3f22d90f7f7541a201bdc3f3f1bb9409df","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:extension of arXiv:2609.01464, 'Removability of non-isolated singularities for Einstein metrics and RCD spaces'] Let $n\geqslant 2$ be an integer, and let $K\geqslant 0$. Let $(X,\mathrm{d},\mathcal{H}^n)$ be an $\mathrm{RCD}(-K,n)$ space. Let us assume the following:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item There is a closed set $\Sigma \subset X$ with Assouad codimension greater than $2$ (see (def:Codimension of the paper)) such that $X\setminus\Sigma$ is a smooth Riemannian manifold;
        \item $|\mathrm{Ric}| \leqslant K$ on $X\setminus \Sigma$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Let $X^{\mathrm{reg}}$ be the set of regular points of $X$. Then $X^{\mathrm{reg}}$ is open, and has the structure of a $C^{1,\alpha}$-Riemannian manifold for any $\alpha < 1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:40.982942+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_012d84e3afc2c6dde5ee72f108365fc27fe5362a1779cfae64d86f0c40b88d5b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop_17 of arXiv:2412.03774, '{ Refining Concentration for \\Gaussian Quadratic Chaos with\\ Applications in Sonar and C'] Let $r(A)\geq 3$, $\lambda_1,\cdots, \lambda_k$ be as in ((poloi_1 of the paper)) and $r(A)-k$ be even. If 
\begin{eqnarray}

t+b_k\ge0,
\end{eqnarray}
then 

\begin{eqnarray}

&&\Pr(\boldsymbol{\Delta}>t)\leq \Big(1+\frac{t+b_k}{(r(A)-k)a_k}\Big)^{\frac{r(A)-k}{2}}e^{-\frac{t+b_k}{2a_k}}\notag\\ 
&&\hskip2.5cm\times \sum_{\substack{i_0,i_1,\cdots, i_k\ge0\\ i_0+i_1+\cdots+i_k=\frac{r(A)-k}{2}}}c_{i_0,i_1,\cdots, i_k}\prod_{j=1}^k\frac{(\frac{-\lambda_j}{a_k})^{i_j}}{\big(n-k+\frac{t+b_k}{a_k}\big)^{i_j}(\frac{-\lambda_j}{a_k}+1)^{i_j+\frac{1}{2}}},
\end{eqnarray}

where $a_k, b_k$ are given in ((a_k_b_k of the paper)), the coefficients $c_{i_0,i_1,\cdots, i_k}$ are defined by 
\begin{eqnarray}
c_{i_0,i_1,\cdots, i_k}=\frac{(\frac{r(A)-k}{2})!}{i_0!i_1!\cdots i_{k}!}\prod_{j=1}^k(2i_j-1)!!
\end{eqnarray}
and the double factorial notation is given by
\begin{eqnarray}

(2i-1)!!=\left\{\begin{array}{cc}
    1  &  i=0  \\
