{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.668871+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0166e1d290e5d09382b982771d6943739975ccd352c879696e8cf2d67e28311e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2608.30875, 'A Decomposition Theorem for Topological Semi-small Maps'] A continuous proper surjection $ f: X \longrightarrow Y$ between path-connected, locally compact, Hausdorff spaces $X$ and $Y$ is called an \textbf{almost covering map}
     if 
     \begin{enumerate}
     	\item there exists a nowhere closed dense set $R \subset Y$ such that the restriction $f \vert_{f^{-1}(Y \setminus R)} : f^{-1}(Y \setminus R) \longrightarrow
     	Y \setminus R$ is a finite covering map, and
     	\item there is a finite decomposition $R=\bigsqcup_{\beta}T_{\beta}$ such that the restriction $f \vert_{f^{-1}(T_{\beta})}:f^{-1}(T_{\beta}) \longrightarrow T_{\beta}$ is a fiber bundle for each $\beta$.
     \end{enumerate}
     The set $Y \setminus R$ is called the regular set of the almost covering map $f$, and the set $R$ is referred to as a fibered set of the map $f$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:45.220264+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_01678cd90be67e0d48f2ae8ec8c1d75ae0fed86ebb3ad477ac0588ef46dde4b1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition Prop:11 of arXiv:2412.06086, 'Zeros of conditional Gaussian analytic functions, random sub-unitary matrices and q-series'] For every test function $h(q)$ satisfying the assumptions of Theorem (Thm:M of the paper),
