{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:47.661958+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_020f76231cc4d3da1b8aca58aa595886d8ae27a7c551e348333a5646dc89d13d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 of arXiv:2605.09372, 'Sharp weighted norm estimates for martingale square functions'] Given $1<p<\infty$ and a matrix weight $W$, we say that $W$ satisfies the $A_p$ condition (denoted by $W\in A_p$) if
		\begin{equation}
			[W]_{A_p}:=\sup_{n\geq 1}\|\widetilde W_n\widehat W_n\|^p<\infty.
		\end{equation}
Similarly, we say that $W$ satisfies the $A_1$ condition (denoted by $W\in A_1$) if
\begin{equation}
[W]_{A_1}:=\sup_{n\geq 1}\|\widetilde{W}_nW^{-1}\|<\infty.
\end{equation}
The number $[W]_{A_p}$ is called the $A_p$ characteristic of $W$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:17:17.060092+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0212060485f668c32424db6e870a4500586ce30a052edf40a9ccdec4ece7d4e3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 (Local limit theorem) of arXiv:2608.19744, 'Conditioned Brownian motion and local equivalence of path ensembles'] Fix $\beta >0$.
Let $Y_T=A_T-\theta_\beta T$ with $\theta_\beta = \lambda'(\beta)$ and $\sigma^2_\beta = - \lambda''(\beta)$.  For every directly Riemann-integrable
$h:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$, every compact $K\subset\mathbb R^d$, and every $M<\infty$,
\begin{equation}
    \nonumber
 \sup_{\substack{x\in K\\|a|\leq M}}
 \left|
  \sqrt T\,\bbE^{\bbPh^{\beta,T}_x}[h(Y_T-a)]
  -\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma_\beta^2}}
   \int_\R \de y \, h(y)
 \right|
 \longrightarrow0.
\end{equation}
In particular,
\begin{equation}
\nonumber
 \sqrt T\bbE^{\bbPh^{\beta,T}_x}[h(Y_T)]
 \longrightarrow
 \frac1{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma_\beta^2}}
 \int_\bbR \de y \, h(y),
\end{equation}
locally uniformly in $x$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.220904+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0213f6ae83781ff21ae6bf7c2e8859be72b9be4532f50beef43ec0e42c775352","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eqn:Ppos of arXiv:2603.01322, 'Gibbs conditioning, atypical consensus and splitting Gibbs measures on random regular grap'] Given any countable discrete set $\mathcal{Z}$ and $\mu\in \P(\mathcal{Z})$, we say that $\mu$ lies {\em in the interior} (of $\P(\mathcal{Z})$) if $\mu(z)>0$ for all $z\in \mathcal{Z}$. Otherwise, we say that $\mu$ lies {\em on the boundary} (of $\P(\mathcal{Z})$). The collection of all probability measures in the interior and the collection of all probability measures on the boundary are denoted, respectively,  by
\begin{align}
    \Ppos(\mathcal{Z})&\coloneqq \pr{\mu\in \P(\mathcal{Z}):\mu(z)>0 \, \forall z\in \mathcal{Z}};\\
    \Pzero(\mathcal{Z})&\coloneqq \pr{\mu\in \P(\mathcal{Z}):\exists z_*\in \mathcal{Z}\text{ s.t. }\mu(z_*)=0}.
\end{align}
We also define analogous subsets of symmetric measures as follows:
\begin{align}
        \Psympos{\X}&\coloneqq \pr{\pi\in \Psym{\X}: \pi(x,z)>0 \, \forall x,z\in \X}; \\
