{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.528757+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_02644293913b1355ebe774a99f125479f504a18568eff2449e7c5496ea3ee2c0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:well_connected of arXiv:2506.07158, 'The diffusivity of supercritical Bernoulli percolation is infinitely differentiable'] We say that a triadic cube ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}\in \mathcal{T}$ is \emph{well-connected} if there exists a crossing cluster $\mathscr{C}$ for the cube ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}$ such that:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item Each cube ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}'$ with $\size({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}') \in [\frac{1}{10}\size({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}),\frac{1}{2}\size({\scaleobj{1}{\square}})]$ and ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}' \cap \frac{3}{4}{\scaleobj{1}{\square}} \neq \emptyset$ is crossable.
			\item Every open path $\gamma \subseteq {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}'$ with $\diam(\gamma) \geqslant \frac{1}{10}\size({\scaleobj{1}{\square}})$ is connected to $\mathscr{C}$ within ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}'$.
		\end{enumerate}
		We denote this unique crossing cluster $\mathscr{C}$ by $\mathscr{C}_*({\scaleobj{1}{\square}})$, and call it  \emph{the maximal cluster} in the cube ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}$.

		A triadic cube ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}$ is said \emph{very-well-connected} if it is well-connected, all its $3^d$ successors,  $9^d$ second order successors, and $27^d$ third order successors are well-connected, and its size is larger than $10000$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.500902+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_026520671c963a2610114fc060b7cfd5ae012f504cb68e39baed975daf51bfb1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 32 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] The integral operator
\begin{align*}
\mathscr{I} : \big( C([0,T],W), \| \cdot \|_{\infty} \big) \to \big( \scrd_X^{2\alpha}([0,T],W), \interleave \cdot \interleave_{X,2\alpha} \big), \quad Y \mapsto \bigg( \int_0^{\cdot} Y_s \, ds, 0 \bigg)
\end{align*}
is a continuous linear operator between Banach spaces with operator norm bounded by $\| \mathscr{I} \| \leq T^{1-2\alpha}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.461095+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_02655fc06fceeb520e5f5f8a360a2244944b0233a3ad91bb216b1e45d494ce01","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem time-equivalence of arXiv:2601.19145, 'Stochastic Persistence in Infinite Dimensions'] For any $T > 0$, $x \in \mathcal{M}$, and $f: \mathcal{M} \to [0,\infty]$ satisfying $\int_0^Tf(X_t^x)dt < \infty$ a.s., we have 
\begin{align*}
    \limsup_{t \to \infty} \mu_t^x f &
    = \limsup_{n \to \infty} \frac{1}{T}\int_0^T \frac{1}{nT} \int_0^{r + nT} f(X_s^x)dsdr \\
    &= 
    \limsup_{t \to \infty} \frac{1}{t}\int_0^t \frac{1}{T} \int_0^T f(X_{r+s}^x)drds 
     \,.
\end{align*}
  In addition, if   $\mathbb{E}[\int_0^Tf(X_t^x)dt] < \infty$, then
\begin{align*}  
    \limsup_{t \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[\mu_t^x f] &= 
    \limsup_{t \to \infty} \frac{1}{t}
    \mathbb{E}\Big[\int_0^t \frac{1}{T} \int_0^T f(X_{r+s}^x)drds\Big] \\
    &= \limsup_{n \to \infty} \frac{1}{T}\mathbb{E}\Big[ \int_0^T \frac{1}{nT} \int_0^{r + nT} f(X_s^x)dsdr\Big] \,.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.388937+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0266eb2095870b2c35a46f99077ce414272d8d82fbdeab1f1673c7f6ab2d3c8e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:barbour of arXiv:2507.10531, 'Quantitative central limit theorems for exponential random graphs'] Suppose there is a set $\Lambda$ and a function $f$ such that the conditions (cond:variance of the paper) and (cond:smalljumps of the paper) in
the statement of Theorem (thm:barbour of the paper) hold for positive numbers $V$ and $\Delta$, as well as time $T$.
Suppose also that the following three additional conditions hold for some nonnegative numbers
$\epsilon, \delta$, and $M$, and some $z \in \Lambda$.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
    \setcounter{enumi}{2}
    \item

    The chain has a high probability of staying in $\Lambda$ for time $T$ when started from $z$:
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{P}[X_t^z \in \Lambda \text{ for } 0 \leq t < T] \geq 1 - \epsilon.
    \end{equation}

    \item

    The chain mixes rapidly with respect to $f$ when started from $z$:
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbf{d}_\mathrm{TV}(f(X_T^z), f(X)) \leq \delta.
    \end{equation}

    \item

    The function $f$ is bounded close to its mean: for all $x \in \Omega$,
    \begin{equation}
        |f(x) - \mathbb{E}[f(X)]| \leq M.
    \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
Then for all $\lambda \geq 2 \delta M$ we have
\begin{equation}

    \mathbb{P}[|f(X) - \mathbb{E}[f(X)]| > \lambda] \leq 2 \exp \mleft(
        - \frac{(\lambda-2\delta M)^2}{2V + \frac{4}{3} \Delta (\lambda - 2\delta M)}
    \mright) + \epsilon + \delta.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:59:24.050682+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0267d4f304f8f5de2ef11f8f3ae877633c7d96a345562b541e52baf5dc3cff2c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:orbit-matrix of arXiv:2608.30831, 'Sharp discrete Hardy constants in dimensions three and four\\ and strict upper bounds from'] For $1\le k\le N$, the compression of $K_N$ to $V_k(N)$ is the symmetric
