{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:30.064609+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_032f74ed91d0f5ee2a505de5bef1bdac69ce5e181faa1c20d4476ac20df18647","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 6 of arXiv:2606.00586, 'Stein's Method for Convergence Rates of Invariant Measures in the Nonlocal-to-Local Limit'] Let $s \in [7/4, 2]$. There exists a unique, real-valued, symmetric kernel $K_{A_s} \in C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d \setminus \{0\})$ such that $A_s$ can be represented as the principal value singular integral:
\begin{equation*}
    \begin{aligned}
        A_sf(x) = \mathrm{P.V.}\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} (f(x-y) - f(x))K_{A_s}(y) \, \d y, \quad f\in \sS(\mR^d). 
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Furthermore, $K_{A_s}(y)$ satisfies 
    \begin{equation}
        |K_{A_s}(y)| \leq C \left[ |y|^{-d-\beta} \wedge |y|^{-d-s} \right] (1+|\log|y||), 
