\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.516421+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_03d15365b5c76a8f860027d24e8bdc646855d67aa09b536e227fb951bf081737","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm commutation relation for chordal with marked point u infty of arXiv:2505.16093, 'Multiple chordal SLE($\kappa$) and quantum Calogero-Moser system'] In the upper half plane $\mathbb{H}$, $n$ chordal SLEs start at $x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n\in \partial{\mathbb{H}}$ with a marked boundary point $u$. 
\begin{itemize}

\item[(i)] Let the infinitesimal diffusion generators be
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{M}_i=\frac{\kappa}{2} \partial_{ii}+b_i(x_1, x_2,\ldots, x_n,u) \partial_i +\sum_{j \neq i} \frac{2}{x_j-x_i}\partial_j 
\end{equation}

where $\partial_i=\partial_{x_i}$. If n SLEs locally commute, then the associated infinitesimal generators satisfy:
\begin{equation}
[\mathcal{M}_i, \mathcal{M}_j]=\frac{4}{(x_i-x_j)^2}(\mathcal{M}_j-\mathcal{M}_i)
\end{equation}
There exists a partition function $\psi(\boldsymbol{x})$ such that the drift term $b_i(\boldsymbol{x})$ is given by:
\begin{equation}
b_i(\boldsymbol{x})= \kappa \partial_i \log \psi
\end{equation}
where $\psi$ satisfies the null vector equations with an undetermined function $h_i(x)$.
\begin{equation}
\frac{\kappa}{2} \partial_{ii} \psi+\sum_{j \neq i} \frac{2}{x_j-x_i}\partial_i \psi+ \left[\left(1-\frac{6}{\kappa}\right) \sum_{j \neq i}\frac{1}{(x_j-x_i)^2}+h_i(x_i)\right]\psi=0
\end{equation}

\item[(ii)]
 By analyzing the asymptotic behavior of two adjacent growth points $x_i$ and $x_{i+1}$ ( with no marked points between $x_i$ and $x_{i+1}$ on the real line $\mathbb{R}$), we can further show that the $h_i(x)=h_{i+1}(x)$ in null vector equations ((null vector equations in mathbb H of the paper)). As a corollary, if all growth points lie consecutively on the real line $\mathbb{R}$ with no marked points between them, then there exists a common function $h(x)$ such that $h(x)=h_1(x)=\ldots=h_n(x)$.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:04:59.428044+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_03d5ea64fffbafeca54a37467def92d0dc61aa834de9f81f2952029b2312f9f6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:burning of arXiv:2605.19419, 'Tail exponents of the three-dimensional uniform spanning tree and Abelian sandpile'] Let $K\Subset\mathbb{Z}^{d}$ contain $0$ and all nearest neighbours of $0$. Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an event of subsets of $K$ such that every $A\in\mathcal{A}$ contains at least one nearest neighbour of $0$. Then
\begin{equation}
\nu_{K}\bigl(W_{1,K}\in\mathcal{A}\bigr)
= g_{K}\,\nu_{K}^{(0)}\bigl(\mathfrak{T}_{K}\in\mathcal{A}\bigr),
\qquad
 g_{K}:=\frac{|R_{K^{\circ}}|}{|R_{K}|}=G_{K}(0,0),
\end{equation}
where $G_{K}$ is the inverse of the finite-volume toppling matrix.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:47:12.027967+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_03d8d6528c3f908c4ad57ff354cb04010b83974d306939d1ba1474d1b2a4c7ab","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:6 of arXiv:2512.09219, 'Strong confluence of geodesics in Liouville quantum gravity'] Almost surely, for any fixed $\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z}\in \mathbb{C}$ and simultaneously for all $0<t<s$, the following hold.
      \begin{enumerate}
      \item There is a finite set of points $\mathcal{X}_{t,s}(\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z})\subseteq \partial \mathcal{B}_t^\bullet(\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z})$ such that every leftmost $D_h$-geodesic from $\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z}$ to a point on $\partial \mathcal{B}_s^\bullet(\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z})$ passes via some $x\in \mathcal{X}_{t,s}(\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z})$.
      \item  There is a unique $D_h$-geodesic from $\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z}$ to $x$ for each $x\in \mathcal{X}_{t,s}(\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z})$.
      \item For $x\in \mathcal{X}_{t,s}(\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z})$, let $I_x$ denote the set of $y\in \partial \mathcal{B}_s^\bullet(\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z})$ for which the leftmost $D_h$-geodesic from $0$ to $y$ passes via $x$. Each $I_x$ for $x\in \mathcal{X}_{t,s}(\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z})$ is a connected arc of the Jordan curve $\partial \mathcal{B}_s^\bullet(\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z})$ and $\partial \mathcal{B}_s^\bullet(\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z})$ is a union of the arcs $I_x$ for $x\in \mathcal{X}_{t,s}(\text{\usefont{U}{bboldx}{m}{n}z})$, with any two such arcs being disjoint.
      \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.355861+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_03e057ab9f0fd3ea1b47de7f97f455c27b02a8391d223ab0d536c6a705a7b3eb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary FCLTApplicableUnderMixing of arXiv:2412.01635, 'On the Weak Convergence of the Function-Indexed Sequential Empirical Process and its Smoot'] Let the conditions of Theorem (SupremumInequalityUnderMixing of the paper) be fulfilled for  some $\nu > 2$ and $\lambda > 0$ and suppose there is a constant $1 \leq K < \infty $ with 
