{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.024496+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_04300ac6e455a1db307daaf6beada6f486430ba6a5facb46f767e3eaa6d835bc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 (\citeauthor{RP81}, \citeyear{RP81}) of arXiv:2606.06739, 'The relationship between the transition functions of the labeled and unlabeled versions of'] Let $E$ and $E_0$ be complete separable metric spaces, and let $\Phi:E\mapsto E_0$ be Borel measurable and surjective.  Let $\lambda$ be a one-step transition function from $E_0$ to $E$ $($i.e., $y\in E_0\mapsto\lambda(y,\cdot)\in\mathscr{P}(E)$ is Borel measurable$)$ satisfying $\lambda(y,\Phi^{-1}(y))=1$ for all $y\in E_0$.  Let $P(t,x,\cdot)$ be the transition function for a Markov process $X$ in $E$ with initial distribution $\mu:=\int_{E_0}\lambda(y,\cdot)\,\mu_0(dy)$ for some $\mu_0\in\mathscr{P}(E_0)$.  If the condition
\begin{equation}
\int_E P(t,x,\cdot)\,\lambda(y,dx)=\int_E\bigg[\int_{E_0}\lambda(z,\cdot)\,P(t,x,\Phi^{-1}(dz))\bigg]\lambda(y,dx),
\end{equation}
holds for every $t>0$ and $y\in E_0$, then $Y:=\Phi\circ X$ is a Markov process in $E_0$ with initial distribution $\mu_0$ and transition function
\begin{equation}
Q(t,y,\cdot):=\int_E P(t,x,\Phi^{-1}(\cdot))\,\lambda(y,dx).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

