{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.628106+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0555b6c9c645388af123983ed6ef98decda5c192edd7ce1c978fe080d72aaf27","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defin def:augmented-stable of arXiv:2411.03609, 'Asymptotically optimal Wasserstein couplings for the small-time stable domain of attractio'] A L\'evy process $\bm{X}=(\bm{X}_t)_{t \in [0,1]}$ is an augmented $\alpha$-stable process if it has no Gaussian component, and, for some $\alpha\in(0,2]$, its L\'evy measure $\nu_{\bm{X}}$ has the form
\begin{equation} 
\nu_{\bm{X}}(A)
= \int_0^\infty\int_{\mathbb{S}^{d-1}}  \1_{A}(x\bm{v}) \mathcal{Q}(x,\bm{v})  \sigma(\mathrm{d} \bm{v})x^{-\alpha-1}\mathrm{d} x, \quad \text{ for } A \in \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}^d_{\bm{0}}),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma$ is a probability measure on $\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{S}^{d-1})$ and $\mathcal{Q}:(0,\infty)\times \mathbb{S}^{d-1} \mapsto [0,\infty)$ is a measurable satisfying $\mathcal{Q}(\cdot,\bm{v})\in\mbox{SV}_0$ for $\bm{v}\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:28.041181+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_05582e8b08cd274cce664d426dfb9bc1f87d8ac81c37298e4c33299da04649ed","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: thm2 of arXiv:2608.12056, 'Deviation inequalities for U-processes of growing order, with applications to half-space d'] There exists an absolute constant $C>0$ such that for every integer $1\leq j \leq m$ and real number $p \geq 2$,
    \begin{align*}
    \mathbb E^{1/p} \sup_{h \in \mathcal{H}_m} \lvert V_{N,j}(h) \rvert^p  \leq C^j\max\left\{A_1(p,j), A_2(p,j), A_3(p,j)\right\},  
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
    A_1(p,j) &=  p^{j/2} \Sigma_{\mathcal H_m} \,, 
    \\
    \\
    A_2(p,j) &= p^{j/2}\left(\frac{m}{j}\right)^{j/2}\left(\frac{j}{N}\right)^{1/4} \left( p\sqrt{\frac{j}{N}}\bigvee \mathbb E \gamma_2 (\mathcal{H}_m, d^{(2)}_{N,j})\right)^{1/2}
    \\
    A_3(p,j) & =  \mathbb E^{1/p} \left(\gamma_{2/j, \lfloor \log_2 (pj)\rfloor}\left(\mathcal{H}_m, d^{(1)}_{N,j}\right) \right)^p  \,.\\
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:32:19.921214+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_055fb07b9aa0e5ccaa1ad928b1e213b090a3901045a4deb89a373b61f3ec7329","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2608.02927, 'Thinning Operation via the Poisson-F\"ollmer Process'] Fix $T>0.$ Let $X$ be a random variable whose density $f : \N \rightarrow (0, \infty) $ with respect to $\pi_{T}$ is ultra log-concave, then we have
