{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:09:59.300903+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_05988ecb590247f286e86b3f7472967e2e85da554d18b4be84f11326c91538e6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn rootedtest of arXiv:2608.06500, 'Free energy of Ising models under a spectral condition'] A \emph{rooted test graph} is a connected test graph with a
    distinguished root vertex. If $G=(\text{\boldmath $A$},\text{\boldmath $h$}_1,\ldots,\text{\boldmath $h$}_k)$ is a
    weighted graph on $[N]$ and $X=(\text{\boldmath $W$},\text{\boldmath $d$}_1,\ldots,\text{\boldmath $d$}_k)$ is a
    rooted test graph on $[n]$ with root vertex $1$, then we define
    the \emph{weighted homomorphism density vector} $\text{\boldmath $t$}(X,G)\in\mathbb{R}^N$
    as the vector whose $m$th component is given by

    \begin{align*}
    t_m(X,G) :=\sum_{\substack{f:[n]\rightarrow[N]\\f(1)=m}} \left( \prod_{i\in[n]}
\prod_{\ell=1}^k h_{\ell,f(i)}^{d_{\ell,i}} \right) \left( \prod_{i\le
j\in[n]} a_{f(i)f(j)}^{w_{ij}} \right).
\end{align*}

    A \emph{local vector} 
     is a vector-valued function on weighted
    graphs $G$ that can be expressed as a finite linear combination of
    $\text{\boldmath $t$}(X,G)$ for some rooted test graphs $X$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.333845+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_059f2dff04dc6a872028895e2324096d9ade9d21ba38db08e79c8314d6e12bdb","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2511.06196, 'Central limit theorem for high temperature spin models via martingale embedding'] Let $A$ be the event that $D_\infty=d\geq 0$ and $Y_\infty^{k,+}$ and $Y_\infty^{k,-}$ differ in the $d$ given locations $i_1,\dots, i_d$ (and take the same spin values in the other $n-1-d$ locations).
Let the event $B$ be 
\be{
B=\{Y^{k,+}_{\infty,i_1}=\dots=Y^{k,+}_{\infty,i_d}=1\},
}
that is, there is no restriction for the other spin values. 
Then, we have
\ben{
\E (\sum_{j\notin\{k,i_i,\dots, i_d\}}Y_{\infty,j}^{k,+}\vert A)\leq \E (\sum_{j\notin\{k,i_i,\dots, i_d\}}Y_{\infty,j}^{k,+}\vert B).
}
In fact, restricting the two Markov chains $Y_\infty^{k,+}$ and $Y_\infty^{k,-}$ (coupled to keep the partial order as above \cref{eq:EDt}) to take the same values for the $n-1-d$ locations decreases (increases, resp.) the expected spin values of the larger (smaller, resp.) Markov chain $Y_\infty^{k,+}$ ($Y_\infty^{k,-}$, resp.) in these locations.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:07:56.713338+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_059f61e7bf29ce598abd3f2df2bd423c42d050063790102766639e184ecdcaf7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def.CFH of arXiv:2411.04778, 'Coupling between Brownian motion and random walks on the infinite percolation cluster'] We denote by $\mathsf{CFH}$$(T,n,y)$ the coupling of $(S_{t},\bar{B}_{t})_{0\leqslant t \leqslant T}$ constructed in the following way. We define that 
		\begin{align}
			\Delta T = T/n, \quad t_k = k \Delta T, \quad I_k = (t_k, t_{k+1}),	\qquad k\in\{0,1,\dots, n-1\}.
		\end{align}
		\begin{enumerate}[label=(\Alph*)]
			\item The processes start from $S_0 = \bar{B}_0 = y$.
			\item Conditioned on $\big(S_{t_i},\bar{B}_{t_i}\big)_{0\leqslant i\leqslant k}$, we sample the increment $\big(\Delta S_{t_k}, \Delta\bar{B}_{t_k}\big)$ 
			\begin{align*} 
				\big(\Delta S_{t_k}, \Delta\bar{B}_{t_k}\big)  \in \varPi\big(p^\omega(\Delta T,S_{t_k} +\cdot,S_{t_k}), \bar{p}(\Delta T,\cdot) \big),
			\end{align*}
			 such that
			\begin{align*} 
				\mathbb{E}^\omega \left[\left\vert \Delta S_{t_k}-\Delta\bar{B}_{t_k}\right\vert \Big\vert \big(S_{t_i},\bar{B}_{t_i}\big)_{0\leqslant i\leqslant k} \right]=W_1\big(p^\omega(\Delta T,S_{t_k}+\cdot,S_{t_k}), \bar{p}(\Delta T,\cdot) \big).
			\end{align*}
			Then we  define 
			\begin{align*} 
				\big(S_{t_{k+1}},\bar{B}_{t_{k+1}}\big) := \big(S_{t_{k}},\bar{B}_{t_{k}}\big) + \big(\Delta S_{t_k}, \Delta\bar{B}_{t_k}\big).
			\end{align*}
			\item Given the sequence $\big(S_{t_k},\bar{B}_{t_k}\big)_{0\leqslant k\leqslant n}$, we sample the trajectories as follows:
			\begin{itemize}[label=---]
				\item $(S_t)_{t \in [0,T]}$ is sampled as the VSRW defined in (equation eq.VSRW of the paper) conditioned on the values $(S_{t_k})_{0\leqslant k\leqslant n}$ at the endpoints $(t_k)_{0\leqslant k\leqslant n}$.
				\item $(\bar{B}_t)_{t \in [0,T]}$ is sampled as Brownian motion with diffusion constant $\bar{\sigma}^2$ conditioned on the values $(\bar{B}_{t_k})_{0\leqslant k\leqslant n}$ at the endpoints $(t_k)_{0\leqslant k\leqslant n}$.
			\end{itemize}
		\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:30:07.995217+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_05a2a26838b6d1467fa2358907e8816d0755255895c13d85344fa0f3da82de55","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Half-Diamond Backtracking Intersection of arXiv:2608.12415, 'Sharp Phase Transition for Ellipsoid Fitting'] Let \(A\) and \(B\) be two \(M_\alpha\)-shapes. We say that the composition
