{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:40:26.984199+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0609740a323d0c8b5c9298ad1317cfb1cb3969fa8338f9d5afb4a3d662e581d8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:critical of arXiv:2604.13968, 'Divisible sandpiles via random walks in random scenery'] Let~$G = (V,E)$ be an infinite, locally finite, connected graph, let~$(X_n)_{n\geq 0}$ be simple random walk on~$G$, and let $(\xi(v))_{v\in V}$ be i.i.d.\ and independent of the walk with
		$\mathbb{E}[\xi]=0$, $\var(\xi)>0$, and $\mathbb{E}[\xi^2]<\infty$.
		Let $u_n(o)\coloneqq\sup_{\tau\leq n}\mathbb{E}_o[S_\tau\mid\xi]$.

		\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\alph*)}]
			\item \textup{(Variance bound)}\;
			For every~$o\in V$ and~$n\geq 1$,
			\begin{equation}
				\var(u_n(o))\leq\var(\xi)\,\Sigma_n(o)\,.
			\end{equation}
			\item \textup{(Explosion)}\;
			If~$\Sigma_n(o)\to\infty$, then there exist~$c_1,c_2>0$ depending only on~$\xi$ such that
			\begin{equation}
				\mathbb{P}\bigl(u_n(o)\geq c_1\sqrt{\Sigma_n(o)}\bigr)\geq c_2\,,
			\end{equation}
			for all~$n$ sufficiently large.
			In particular, $\sup_n\mathbb{E}_o[S_n\mid\xi]=\infty$ almost surely.
		\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.756508+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_060a0d2bdaf7b77f0b667ad4b99243eda7473bed17425199d27040e021122738","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma4 of arXiv:2411.02362, 'Limit theorems for random Dirichlet series: boundary case'] Fix any $\gamma\in (0,(\sqrt{5}-1)/2)$,
pick any $\rho=\rho(\gamma)$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
(1-\gamma)(1+\gamma)^2(2-\exp(2\sqrt{2}(1+\gamma)\rho))>1
\end{equation}
and put $s_n:=\exp(-\exp(n^{1-\gamma}))$ for $n\in\mathbb{N}$. Then
\begin{equation*}
\limsup_{n\to\infty} f(s_n)\sum_{k\ge M(s_n)+1}\frac{(\log k)^{-1/2}\widetilde{\eta}_{k,\rho}(s_n)}{k^{1/2+s_n}}\le 1+\gamma \quad\text{{\rm a.s.},}
\end{equation*}
where $\widetilde{\eta}_{k,\rho}(s):=\eta_k \1_{(A_{k,\rho}(s))^c}-\mathbb{E}\big(\eta_k\1_{(A_{k,\rho}(s))^c}\big)$ for $k\in\mathbb{N}$ and $s\in(0, {\rm e}^{-{\rm e}})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.130186+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_06105a76c01cf4ca64fbe08ecb23cb8ad8d211c687f2db3ac98668559437279c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn ofn of arXiv:2506.18207, 'Cartan's Path Development, the Logarithmic Signature and a Conjecture of Lyons-Sidorova'] Assume that $\gamma:[0,T]\to R^d$ is a bounded variation path and $(0,0)=\gamma_0\neq\gamma_T$. We define the associated (with $\gamma$, $T$) normalization operator $\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}_{\gamma,T}$ by
$$
\mathcal{A}(\alpha)_t:=k^{-1}A\alpha_t,~\forall~t\in[0,S],
$$
where $\alpha:[0,S]\to R^d$ is a bounded variation path, the rotation $A$ and the scaling $k$ are given by  
\begin{equation}
A
=\left(
\begin{array}{cc}
\cos(\theta) & \sin(\theta) \\
-\sin(\theta) &\cos(\theta)\\
\end{array} 
\right)
=\frac{1}{|\gamma_T|}\left(
\begin{array}{cc}
 x_T & y_T \\
-y_T & x_T\\
\end{array} 
\right),\quad\quad k=|\gamma_T|.
\end{equation}
For any one-form $f(\cdot):=\left(
\begin{array}{c}
f_1  \\
f_2 \\
\end{array} 
\right)(\cdot)$, we define its adjoint operator $\mathcal{A}^*$ by 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{A}^*(f)(\cdot)=k^{-1} A^{-1}\left(
\begin{array}{l}
f_1  \\
f_2 \\
\end{array} 
\right)\left(k^{-1}A\cdot\right)
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:45:07.264432+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0618a93479337f4545ddcdab19f4df7b8be57fe034456d588bd577a6943acf8b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2604.19924, 'Fluctuation theory for spectrally negative Lévy processes killed by additive functionals'] Let $q \geq 0$ and $x \in (c,b)$. Then 
			\begin{align}
				\E_x\left[e^{-q\tau_b^+-A_{\tau_b^+}};\tau_b^+<\tau_c^-\right]=\frac{W_{{\mathbf{T}}(\nu_A)}^{(q)}(x,c)}{W_{{\mathbf{T}}(\nu_A)}^{(q)}(b,c)},
