{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.462547+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0746dad409b5b1176ae393a66b535571fc55b6bc2cc87a4a070b9516964b4bfa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:ergopt of arXiv:2505.12187, 'Speeding up quantum Markov processes through lifting'] Let $\mathcal{P}_t = \exp(t \mathcal{L})$ be a QMS with invariant state $\sigma \in \mathcal{D}_+(\mathcal{M})$. Then, we have 
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{t \to \infty} \mathcal{P}_t (\mathrm{id} - E_{\mathcal{N}}) = 0\,,
    \end{align*}
    with $E_{\mathcal{N}}$ being the conditional expectation to $\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{L})$, and the limit 
    \begin{align} 
        \lim_{t \to \infty} \frac{1}{t} \int_0^t \mathcal{P}_s \,\mathrm{d} s =: E_{\mathcal{F}}
    \end{align}
    exists and defines the conditional expectation $E_{\mathcal{F}}$ onto $\mathcal{F}(\mathcal{L})$ with respect to $\sigma$. 
    Moreover, the following convergence holds:
    \begin{align} 
        \lim_{t \to \infty} \mathcal{P}_t  = E_{\mathcal{F}}\,,
    \end{align}
    if and only if $\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{L})$ coincides with the fixed-point algebra $\mathcal{F}(\mathcal{L})$, i.e., $\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{L}) = \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{L})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.262755+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_074a983d5042294117311c88ed98483cb4d179cbc4fb37973106fac1a0931c88","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma comparisonstep1 of arXiv:2507.10545, 'KPZ equation from a class of nonlinear SPDEs in infinite volume'] \fsp Fix any {\small$\mathrm{L}>0$}. There exists {\small$\gamma_{\mathrm{L},1}>0$} depending on {\small$\mathrm{L}$} such that with high probability, we have 

\begin{align}
\sup_{\t\in[0,1]}\sup_{|\x|\leq \mathrm{L}N}|\mathbf{Z}^{N}_{\t,\x}-\mathbf{Y}^{N,1}_{\t,\x}|\lesssim_{\mathrm{L}} N^{-\gamma_{\mathrm{L},1}},
\end{align}

where, upon recalling {\small$\mathbf{L}^{N,\zeta_{\mathrm{final}},\sim}$} from (equation eq:lzetasim-sde of the paper), we set

\begin{align}
\mathbf{Y}^{N,1}_{\t,\x}&:=\sum_{\y\in\Z}\mathbf{L}^{N,\zeta_{\mathrm{final}},\sim}_{0,\t,\x,\y}\mathbf{S}^{N}_{0,\y}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:08.848573+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_074b51728696d9852f718e389d08f0fbd1f08b2cc4d1df0dc321e9e5c1776f6b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defination 3 of arXiv:2511.10814, '\Large \bf \boldmath\ \\ Convergence of the extended Kalman filter with small and state-de'] Let $\varphi:[0,\infty)\times \R^n \to \R^n,$  be  a  family  of   functions and $x_1, x_2 \in \R^n $. 

			\begin{enumerate}
				\item 
				$\varphi$  is said to be  \textbf{almost  linear} if  there  exists  a family  of matrix-valued observable processes $F:[0,\infty)\to \R^{n\times n}$  such  that 				
				\begin{equation}
					\vert \varphi(t,x_1)- \varphi(t,x_2) - F(t) (x_1-x_2) \vert  \leq \mu^{\eps} \vert x_1-x_2 \vert,
				\end{equation}
				for some family of  numbers  $\mu=(\mu^{\eps})_{\eps>0}$ converging to zero as $\eps \to 0$. \\ The  process $F$  will be  called an  \textbf{almost  derivative} of $f$.

				\item The  function  $\varphi$  will be  said  to  be \textbf{strongly  injective} if 
				\begin{equation}
