{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.471787+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_076c8bc2ddb310dd174016c202e02967950b60deced96a038a9e3e461063c1a2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:hypocoercivesemi of arXiv:2505.12187, 'Speeding up quantum Markov processes through lifting'] We say that $\{P_t\}_{t \geq 0}$ \emph{converges to equilibrium} if for each $x \in \mathcal{H}$, there exists a decay function $r: \mathbb{R}_+ \to \mathbb{R}_+$ with $\lim_{t \to \infty} r(t) = 0$ such that
\begin{align} 
    \|P_t x - P_\infty x\|_{\mathcal{H}} \leq r(t) \|x - P_\infty x\|_{\mathcal{H}}.
\end{align}
When $r(t) = Ce^{- \nu t}$ for some $C >  1, \nu > 0$, we say the semigroup is \emph{hypocoercive}. In the case of $r(t) = e^{-\nu t}$ (i.e., $C = 1$), it is called \emph{coercive}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.166930+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0773b415c9f02df2060463fec896a6ef2b8affb3e2c4ebccca300a1d1985897c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 (Quasi-classical solution) of arXiv:2604.17973, 'Schauder estimates and classical solutions of the Dirichlet problem for stochastic parabol'] A predictable random field $u:\bar Q_T\times\Omega\to\R$ is called a quasi-classical solution of \eqref{eq:main}--\eqref{eq:boundary-initial} if
\begin{enumerate}[label=\rm(\roman*)]
\item for each $t\in(0,T]$, the map $x\mapsto u(x,t)$ is twice strongly differentiable from $G$ into $L_\omega^\gamma$, and $u(\cdot,t)$ together with its spatial derivatives up to order two extends continuously to $\bar G$ as an $L_\omega^\gamma$-valued function;
\item for each $x\in G$, the process $u(x,\cdot)$ is stochastically continuous and satisfies
\[
\begin{aligned}
u(x,t)-u(x,0) & =\int_0^t \bigl(a^{ij}D_{ij}u+b^iD_i u+cu+f\bigr)(x,s)\md s \\
& \quad +\int_0^t \bigl(\sigma^{ik}D_i u+\nu^k u+g^k\bigr)(x,s)\md w_s^k
\end{aligned}
\]
almost surely for all $t\in[0,T]$;
\item $u=0$ on $\Gamma_T$ and $u(0,\cdot)=0$ in $G$ almost surely.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.504610+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_077544cc471b6cae5a3768e1b1193fbb95f728874159c439571e28c3bcfb28f9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 82 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] The following statements are true:
\begin{enumerate}
\item For every $y \in D(A)$ the mapping
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{R}_+ \rightarrow W, \quad t \mapsto S_t y
\end{align*}
belongs to class $C^1(\mathbb{R}_+;W)$.

\item For all $t \geq 0$ and $y \in D(A)$ we have $S_t y \in D(A)$ and
\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dt} S_t y = A S_t y = S_t A y.
\end{align*}

\item For all $t \geq 0$ and $y \in W$ we have $\int_0^t S_s y \, ds \in D(A)$ and
\begin{align*}
A \bigg( \int_0^t S_s y \, ds \bigg) = S_t y - y.
\end{align*}

\item For all $t \geq 0$ and $y \in D(A)$ we have
\begin{align*}
\int_0^t S_s A y \, ds = S_t y - y.
\end{align*}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

