{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.368728+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08f2722690b2c48c9992786daa8c93d8803f2685af30599b178d2df8614e130e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition best strategy with feedback of arXiv:2507.10294, 'Guessing Strategies for Shelf-Shuffling Machines'] Let $\mathbf{G}=\{g_j\}_{j=1}^n$ be the following process.

    i.) $g_1=1$ a.s.

    ii.) $g_{j+1}=r_j +1$, $\tau>j$. If $r_j=n-2$, then $g_{j+1}=n-1$ or $n$.

    iii.) If $\tau=\tau^{n-1}$, then $g_{\tau+1}=n$ a.s. and $g_{\tau+m+1}=K_{\tau}^m$ a.s. Otherwise, if $\tau=\tau^{n}$, then $g_{\tau+m}=K_{\tau}^m$ a.s.
    Then, $\mathbf{G}$ maximizes $F^G$ for every $n$. 
    \begin{proof}
        For the first card, we resort to the no-feedback case. This proves i.)
        By Proposition $(properties of r of the paper)$ i.), we have 
        \begin{equation}
           P(r_{j+1}=r_j+1\,|\,r_j,\,\tau>j)=\frac{1}{2}
        \end{equation}
        for every $1\leq j\leq n-2$. Applying a pigeonhole argument as in Proposition $(first card of the paper)$, we deduce ii.). Part iii.) follows from Proposition $(properties of r of the paper)$ iii.), as the probabilities are all $1$ and thus must be the unique maxima.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.067926+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08f44f22dbed20252fce498255f9d13eaddef7bebdae65be6a13b1af7146037e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: main scaling of arXiv:2606.28575, 'Global smooth solutions by high mode Lie-Transport noise for Logarithmically Hyperdissipat'] Let $\nu>0$, $R> 0$, $M\ge 1$ and $\theta^n$ time independent satisfying (hyp_noise of the paper). Assume $p \in (4, \infty)$, $-1/2 < r<- 2/p$. Let $\omega^n$ be the solution of (equation main_ito_cutoff of the paper) associated to $\theta^n$ and some initial data $\omega_0\in \mathcal{B}_p(M)$. Let $\omega^{R,{\operatorname{det}}}$ be the solution of 
    \begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
    \partial_t\omega^{R,{\operatorname{det}}} +  \phi_{R}(\|\omega^{R,{\operatorname{det}}}\|_{H^r_g})\mathcal{L}_u \omega^{R,{\operatorname{det}}} + A\omega^{R,{\operatorname{det}}} - \nu\Delta \omega^{R,{\operatorname{det}}}   =0 \\
    u= K[ \omega^{R,{\operatorname{det}}}],
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
with the same initial datum $\omega_0$ (c.f. (rem:stoch2det of the paper)). Then for all $r_0 < -2/p$ and $\varepsilon >0$ we have 
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\sup_{\omega_0 \in \mathcal{B}_p(M)} \mathbb{P}\Big( \sup_{t\in [0, T]}\|\omega^n_t - \omega^{R,{\operatorname{det}}}_t\|_{H^{r_0}_g} > \varepsilon \Big) =0
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

