{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.158572+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09293a82c79cf8875a5ec174f34db988417d1dc78abfacacd56f6858ca335bc9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma centralizer-block-permutations of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $n_1,\dots,n_r\ge0$ and write $n\coloneqq n_1+\cdots+n_r$. Then the centralizer of $\smash{\widetilde{\vphantom{t}\smash{\Sigma}}}(n_1|\cdots|n_r)$ in ${\textup{O}}(n)$ consists precisely of the matrices of the form
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{pmatrix}
            \pm\textup{id}_{{\mathbb{R}}^{n_1}}\\
            &\ddots\\
            &&\pm\textup{id}_{{\mathbb{R}}^{n_r}}
        \end{pmatrix}.
    \end{equation}    
    \begin{proof}
        It is clear that the matrices of the above form are contained in the centralizer. To prove the converse, we view ${\mathbb{R}}^n$ as a representation of $G\coloneqq \smash{\widetilde{\vphantom{t}\smash{\Sigma}}}(n_1|\cdots|n_r)$, so that any element $A$ of the centralizer defines an isometric $G$-equivariant automorphism of ${\mathbb{R}}^n$. For every $1\le k\le r$ and every $n_1+\cdots+n_{k-1}<i\le n_1+\cdots+n_k$, the standard basis vector $e_i$ is fixed by the subgroup 
        \[
            G_{\lnot k}\coloneqq(\Sigma_{n_1}\wr C_2)\times\cdots(\Sigma_{n_{k-1}}\wr C_2)\times1\times(\Sigma_{n_{k+1}}\wr C_2)\times\cdots\times(\Sigma_{n_r}\wr C_2).
        \]
        Conversely, if $x\in{\mathbb{R}}$ is not contained in the span of these $e_i$'s, then it is not fixed by $\big((\textup{id};-1,\dots,-1),(\textup{id};-1,\dots,-1),\dots\big)\in G_{\lnot k}$. Thus, the $G_{\lnot k}$-fixed points are precisely given by the span $V_k$ of the $e_i$'s for $n_1+\cdots+n_{k-1}<i\le n_1+\cdots+n_k$; in particular, $A$ has to restrict to an automorphism of $V_k$. As ${\mathbb{R}}^n=\bigoplus_{k=1}^r V_k$, this expresses $A$ as an orthogonal direct sum of automorphisms of $V_k$ (centralizing $\Sigma_{n_k}\wr C_2$), and we may therefore restrict to the case $r=1$.

        For this, let us identify $A$ with an element of $\text{U}(n)$. Then $A$ has a complex eigenvalue $\lambda$ with corresponding eigenvector $v=(v_1,\dots,v_n)\not=0$. As $A$ centralizes $\Sigma_n\wr C_2$, we have $ABv=BAv=B\lambda v=\lambda Bv$ for any $B\in\Sigma_n\wr C_2$, i.e. $Bv$ is again an eigenvector with eigenvalue $\lambda$. If we fix $1\le i\le n$ such that $v_i\not=0$ and let $B\in\Sigma_n\wr C_2$ be the matrix sending the standard basis vector $e_j$ to $-e_j$ for $j\not=i$ and sending $e_i\mapsto e_i$, this implies that $e_i=(2v_i)^{-1}\cdot(v+Bv)$ is an eigenvector with eigenvalue $\lambda$. Similarly, for any $1\le j\le n$ we find a $B\in\Sigma_n\wr C_2$ with $e_i\mapsto e_j$, so that every standard basis vector is an eigenvector with eigenvalue $\lambda$, i.e.~$A=\textup{diag}(\lambda)$. As $A$ is an orthogonal matrix, we then have $\lambda=\pm1$, finishing the proof.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:46:00.377033+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_092faaf352ddca6e30a6403eeb157616d73622fe49940e366105c840fb387b24","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:green-gap-beta of arXiv:2607.21443, 'The critical KPZ scale for the averaging process'] Fix $\beta\in\mathbb{R}$ and recall $\mathfrak c_\beta\coloneqq \cosh(\beta)$ from (equation eq:c-beta of the paper).
	Let $\ensuremath{\mathcal L}_\beta^{\rm gap}$ be the generator of the tilted two-point gap
	process from Proposition~(pr:gapprocess-tilt of the paper). Define, for
	$x,y\in\mathbb{Z}$, with the convention $\operatorname{sgn}(0)\coloneqq0$,
	\begin{equation}
		G_\beta(x,y)
		\coloneqq
		\frac{|x-y|}{2\mathfrak c_\beta}
		+
		\frac{\mathfrak c_\beta-1}
		{2\mathfrak c_\beta(\mathfrak c_\beta+1)}
		\abs{\operatorname{sgn}(x)-\operatorname{sgn}(y)}
		+
		\frac{1-\mathbf{1}_{\{x=y=0\}}}
		{\mathfrak c_\beta(\mathfrak c_\beta+1)}\; \text{.}
	\end{equation}
	Then, $G_\beta$ is symmetric, nonnegative, and, for every $x, y\in\mathbb{Z}$,
	\begin{equation}
		\ensuremath{\mathcal L}_\beta^{\rm gap}G_\beta(x,{\,\cdot\,})(y) =\mathbf{1}_{\{x=y\}} = \ensuremath{\mathcal L}_\beta^{\rm gap}G_\beta({\,\cdot\,},y)(x)
		\; \text{.}
	\end{equation}
	In particular, if $f:\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{R}$ has finite support and satisfies $\sum_{x\in\mathbb{Z}} f(x)\,\pi(x)=0$,
	then
	\begin{equation}
		G_\beta f(x)
		\coloneqq
		\sum_{y\in\mathbb{Z}}G_\beta(x,y)f(y)\; \text{,}\;\;
		\qquad x\in\mathbb{Z}\; \text{,}\;\;
	\end{equation}
	is constant on $\mathbb{Z}_{>0}\setminus {\rm supp}(f)$ and $\mathbb{Z}_{<0}\setminus {\rm supp}(f)$, bounded, and satisfies $\ensuremath{\mathcal L}_\beta^{\rm gap}G_\beta f=f$.","status":"unverified"}

