{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.793038+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_093c3010fe6c075c03fa08de2ae6db1e66a7e9f7bf3e4ef2f3fc08d9534ed549","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.mu of arXiv:2605.11785, 'Regularization of a mean-field SDE by an additive common noise: the conditional expectatio'] There exists a Borel measurable map $\mu:[0,T]\times {\mathcal C}\to \mathcal P_2(\mathbb{R}^d)$ such that, for ${\mathcal W}^{\overline\mu_0}-$a.e. ${\bm{x}}\in {\mathcal C}$, $\mu^{{\bm{x}}} \in C([0,T], \mathcal P_1(\mathbb{R}^d))$ is the unique solution in the sense of distributions to 
\begin{equation}
\partial_t \mu^{{\bm{x}}}_t - \frac{1}{2} \Delta \mu^{{\bm{x}}}_t +{\rm div}\Bigl(\mu^{{\bm{x}}}_t \Bigl( b(y+{\bm{x}}_t, \bm{x}_t)-\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} b(z+{\bm{x}}_t,\bm{x}_t)\mu^{\bm{x}}_t(dz)\Bigr)\Bigr) =0
\end{equation}
with initial condition $\mu^{\bm{x}}_0(dy)= (Id- \overline\mu_0)\sharp \mu_0$.  In addition, the map $\bm{x} \to \mu^{\bm{x}}$ is nonanticipating: 
$$
\mbox{\rm for ${\mathcal W}^{\overline\mu_0}-$a.e. $\bm{x}, \bm{x}' \in \mathcal C$, $\bm{x}=\bm{x}'$ on $[0,t]$ implies $\mu^{\bm{x}}_t=\mu^{\bm{x}'}_t$ on $[0,t]$.}
$$
 Moreover this solution is stable with respect to $b$: namely, if $(b^n)$ is a sequence of Lipschitz maps from $\mathbb{R}^d\times \mathbb{R}^d$ into $\mathbb{R}^d$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\sup_n \|b^n\|_\infty < \infty,\qquad b^n(x,y)\to b(x,y) \;\mbox{\rm for $dx\otimes dy$ a.e. $(x,y)\in \mathbb{R}^d\times \mathbb{R}^d$},
\end{equation}
then for ${\mathcal W}^{\overline\mu_0}-$a.e. ${\bm{x}}\in {\mathcal C}$, the solution  $\mu^{n,\bm{x}}\in C([0,T], \mathcal P_1(\mathbb{R}^d))$ in the sense of distributions to 
\begin{equation}
\partial_t \mu^{n,{\bm{x}}}_t - \frac{1}{2} \Delta \mu^{n,{\bm{x}}}_t +{\rm div}\Bigl(\mu^{n,{\bm{x}}}_t \Bigl( b^n(y+{\bm{x}}_t, \bm{x}_t)-\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} b^n(z+{\bm{x}}_t, \bm{x}_t)\mu^{n,\bm{x}}_t(dz)\Bigr)\Bigr) =0
\end{equation}
with initial condition $\mu^{n,\bm{x}}_0( dy)= (Id- \overline\mu_0)\sharp \mu_0$, is unique,  has a density which is  locally bounded and continuous on $(0,T]\times \mathbb{R}^d$, and converges to $\mu^{\bm{x}}$ in $C([0,T], \mathcal P_1(\mathbb{R}^d))$, and weakly in $L^p([\epsilon,T]\times B_R)$ for any $\epsilon,R>0$ and any $p\in (1, \infty)$. Finally, for any $n$, the map $\bm{x} \to \mu^{n,\bm{x}}$ is continuous from $\mathcal C$ to $C([0,T], \mathcal P_1(\mathbb{R}^d))$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.764666+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_093c868d2389040455a4757e2e0d280d37132c60b46f0b1acd9bdb20b451942d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary c2 of arXiv:2411.19433, 'Singular mean-field backward stochastic Volterra integral equations in infinite dimensiona'] Let condition $(A_{2})$ hold. Then, for any \( \Psi(\cdot) \in L^2_{\mathcal{F}_b}(\delta, b; H) \), singular MF-BSVIE (equation 3.7 of the paper) admits a unique adapted M-solution

