{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.352435+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09d2a4ae5516d4dea2355a0331f3a9d63efe357488a439bc10a5fd5f3b25cb6a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] For each $\alpha>0$ and $d\in \mathbb{N}$, the \textbf{$d$-level Poisson-avoiding metric} with boundary parameter $\alpha$ is a random function
$$
H_d: \{(u, s; v, t) \in (\Lambda_d \times \mathbb{R})^2 : s < t\} \to \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}
$$
defined as follows. First, let $\Pi$ be a Poisson process on $\Lambda_d \times \mathbb{R}$ such that $\Pi|_{(x, a) \times \mathbb{R}}$ has intensity $\alpha$ if $x = 0$, and intensity $1$ if $x > 0$. From $\Pi$, we construct a random weighted directed graph where:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item $(u, t)$ is connected to $(v, t)$ by an edge of weight $1$ if $(u, v)$ is a directed edge in $\Lambda_d$.
    \item $(u, s)$ is connected to $(u, t)$ by an edge of weight $0$ if $(u, r) \notin \Pi$ for all $r \in (s, t]$.
\end{itemize}
Let $H_d:(\Lambda_d \times \mathbb{R})^2 \to \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0},$ be directed graph distance in this metric.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:09.856236+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09d93def40e91c40bcf7bed05d3ec389e09642a3217b95607812f589e413f92c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 13 of arXiv:2605.20115, 'Moment bounds on correctors for the degenerate random conductance model'] Suppose that $u:\Z^d\to \R$ and $f:\Z^d\to \R^d$ are related on $\Z^d$ via
\begin{equation}
-\nabla^*\cdot A\nabla u=\nabla^*\cdot f.
\end{equation}
There exists $\beta>1$ depending only on $\Lambda$ such that for any $p\in[1,\beta]$ and any ball $B$, we have almost surely
\begin{equation}
\fint_{x\in B}\Big(\fint_{B_{\di}(x)}A\nabla u\cdot\nabla u\Big)^{p}\,\lesssim \,\fint_{x\in 2B}\Big(\fint_{B_{\di}(x)}A^{-1} f\cdot f\Big)^{p}+\Big(\fint_{x\in 2B}\fint_{B_{\di}(x)}A\nabla u\cdot\nabla u \Big)^{p}.
\end{equation}
Furthermore, the following elliptic regularity estimates hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
(Global quenched Meyers' estimates) For $p\in[1,\beta]$,
\begin{equation}
\int_{x\in \ZZ^d}\Big(\fint_{B_{\di}(x)}A\nabla u\cdot\nabla u\Big)^{p}\,\lesssim  \,\int_{x\in \ZZ^d}\Big(\fint_{B_{\di}(x)}A^{-1} f\cdot f\Big)^{p}.
\end{equation}
\item
(Large-scale hole-filling estimates) For $\rd(x)\le r\le R$, if $f\equiv0$ in $B_R(x)$, then 
\begin{equation}
\int_{ B_r (x) }A\nabla u\cdot \nabla u\,\lesssim \, \Big(\frac rR\Big)^{\frac d{\beta'}}\int_{B_{R} (x) } A\nabla u\cdot \nabla u.
\end{equation}
Here we recall that $\beta':=\frac\beta{\beta-1}$ is the H\"older conjugate of $\beta$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

