{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.360153+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0bb2cb4b3834ffe79d96f393919106226e026fee1874f35e90ec65fc451a937a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop of arXiv:2507.09421, 'Stability of randomly switching stochastic reaction networks with asymptotically linear tr'] In Example (ex:IDK man of the paper), if Conjecture (conj: general conjecture of the paper) holds then for each fixed value of $\alpha>0$, for all small enough values of $\kappa$ every state is evanescent for $Z_\kappa$.
\begin{proof}
    The matrices associated to $\mathcal R_1$ and $\mathcal R_2$ are given by
    \begin{align*}
        M_1=\begin{pmatrix}3&1&0\\1&-1&0\\0&0&-\alpha\end{pmatrix}
        \qquad
        M_2=\begin{pmatrix}-\alpha&0&0\\0&-1&1\\0&1&3\end{pmatrix},
    \end{align*}
    respectively. If $v^1:=(2,1,0)$ and $v^2:=(0,1,2)$, then one has $v^1M_1=(7,1,0)$ and $v^2M_2=(0,1,7)$. Therefore, it would follow from Conjecture (conj: general conjecture of the paper) that for all small enough values of $\kappa$, all states with at least one molecule of $S_2$ were evanescent. But the existence of an inflow reaction for $S_1$ as well as a reaction taking $S_1$ as input and producing $S_2$ means that from every state, it is possible to reach another state with at least one $S_2$. Thus given Conjecture (conj: general conjecture of the paper) every state would be evanescent for small $\kappa$, as claimed.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

