{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.299241+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0bd5fef3ad09822377ff3907d37bece5ebe9e7a73148bd0f7c2f017ee861f35b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:eternal of arXiv:2605.26048, 'Classification of the eternal solutions and multiple coalescing shocks in the KPZ fixed po'] An \textbf{eternal solution} for the KPZ fixed point is any function $b:\mathbb{R}^2 \to [-\infty,\infty)$ such that, for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$ and $s < t$, we have 
\begin{equation} 
b(x,t) = \sup_{y \in \mathbb{R}}[b(y,s) + \mathcal{L}(y,s;x,t)]. 
\end{equation}
We say that $b$ is a \textbf{finite eternal solution} if $b(x,t) > -\infty$ for all $(x,t) \in \mathbb{R}^2$.","status":"unverified"}

