	where $\rho=\rho_1+\rho_2$ and $\rho_1=\mathbb{E}[\eta_+]+\sum_{k}(\delta_{s_k}\otimes\nu_{k})$ and $\rho_2=\mathbb{E}[\eta_-]$. The triplet $(\alpha,\rho_1,\rho_2)$ is unique and determines $\mathcal{L}(\xi)$, and any $\alpha$, $\rho_1$ and $\rho_2$ with the stated properties may occur. Further, $\eta_+$ and $\eta_-$ are the Jordan decomposition of the non-fixed atomic component of $\xi$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:47:32.881448+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0d127861a6038e6a2ed0a08bb3846f12af229ca40655daf9c7d25131d428e898","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 17 of arXiv:2609.02478, 'Improved Weyl bounds on short intervals'] Fix $d\ge3$, $s\ge1$, and $\eta>0$.  Under the hypotheses of \cref{cor:prime-short-intro}, suppose that
