{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:15:09.064602+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0d2e879621920ac87ef9e2b56d6470a7817da057785a1623d84330593944f320","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:A1 of arXiv:2411.10829, 'Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble and its Laplace transform'] For any $C_1,C_2>0$, there exists a constant $C_3=C_3(C_1,C_2)>0$, such that for each $N=1,2,\dots$, and  $x,h,g\in\mathbb R_{\ge 0}$, and  $X, H, G\in \mathbb Z_{\ge 0}$, satisfying: $C_1 N^{-2/3}<x<C_2$ and $C_1 N^{-1/3}< h,g < C_2 N^{1/100}$, and $|X-xN^{2/3}|, |H-hN^{1/3}|, |G-gN^{1/3}|<C_2$, and $X+H+G$ is even, we have
\begin{multline}  
\left|2^{-X-1}N^{1/3}|\mathscr F(X;H,G)| - \mathbf F(x;h,g) \right| \\ <  C_3\mathbf F(x;h,g) \left(1\wedge (N^{-3/5}x^{-3} + N^{-1/10} + N^{-1/3}/h+N^{-1/3}/g )\right).
\end{multline}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:38:36.827859+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0d36192b1293e7f3ca55078eac6a18b059397e7103cbffb3a93f1167a08c5ad5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:witt of arXiv:2606.28869, '\bfseries A General Theory of Paths:\\ \large Signatures, Jump Lifts, and Expected Signatu'] The degree-$k$ layer $\mathfrak{L}_k(\mathbb{R}^d)$ of the free Lie algebra has dimension
\begin{equation}
\ell_d(k)=\frac{1}{k}\sum_{q\mid k}\mu_{\rm Mob}(q)d^{k/q}.
\end{equation}
Consequently the step-$N$ free Lie algebra has dimension $\sum_{k=1}^{N}\ell_d(k)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.239663+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0d37b945f7f5b19c37e2a07be4f7a72a7e74d3e7c886851e8fdf41edcdd2440c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 53 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] Fix an $(N,k)$-periodic Bernoulli path $h^{\per}_0$ and define the \textbf{periodic ASEP height function} $h^{\pASEP}(h_0,0;y,t)$ for $t=0$ by $h^{\pASEP}(h^{\per}_0,0;y,0)=h^{\per}_0(y)$ for all $y\in \Z$. For times $t>0$, we define the height function $h^{\pASEP}(h^{\per}_0,0;y,t)$ in terms of the underlying periodic ASEP $(\eta^{\per}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ as follows. When a particle jumps from  $y$ to  $y+1$ at time $r$, set
\begin{equation}
h^{\pASEP}(h^{\per}_0,0;z,r) = \begin{cases}
h^{\pASEP}(h^{\per}_0,0;z,r^-) + 1 & \text{ if } z=y+jN \text{ for some } j \in \Z,  \\
h^{\pASEP}(h^{\per}_0,0;z,r^-)  & \text{ if } z \neq y+jN \text{ for some } j \in \Z . 
\end{cases}
\end{equation} Similarly, when a particle jumps in $(\eta^{\per}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ from site $y$ to site $y-1$ at time $r$, then we set
\begin{equation}
h^{\pASEP}(h^{\per}_0,0;z,r) = \begin{cases}
h^{\pASEP}(h^{\per}_0,0;z,r^-) - 1 & \text{ if } z=y-1+jN \text{ for some } j \in \Z , \\
h^{\pASEP}(h^{\per}_0,0;z,r^-)  & \text{ if } z \neq y-1+jN \text{ for some } j \in \Z .
\end{cases}
\end{equation} For starting times $s\geq 0$ for $h^{\pASEP}(h^{\per}_0,s;z,r)$, we consider the above construction, but with respect to $\eta^{\per}_s(x)=h^{\per}_0(x-1)-h^{\per}_0(x)$ for all $x \in \Z$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.022417+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0d3b7948a171c66129d249cf0fae4b86df8dceb895a1770a1379161a14bee761","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 12 of arXiv:2606.06193, 'Peng's Maximum Principle for McKean-Vlasov Stochastic Differential Equations with Common N'] On the probabilistic set-up $(\Omega,\cF,(\cF_t)_{t\in[0,T]}, \P)$ we call a strong solution to the conditional McKean--Vlasov BSDE \eqref{eq:Cond-MKVBSDE} on the interval $[0, T]$ a $4$-tuple $(P,Q,R,S)\in \mathbb{S}^{2,m}\times\Lambda^{2,m\times d}\times\Lambda^{2,m\times d}\times\Lambda^{2,m\times d}$ such that $\P$-a.s. for every $t\in[0,T]$,
            \begin{equation*}
                \begin{aligned}
                   P_t=&\xi-\int_t^T\tilde{\E}\left[F\left(t,
                    P_t,\tilde{P}_t,
                    Q_t,\tilde{Q}_t,
                    S_t,\tilde{S}_t,
                    R_t,\tilde{R}_t,
                    \chi_t,\tilde{\chi}_t\right)\right]\dt \\
                    &-\int_t^T R_t\dW^0_t-\int_t^TQ_t\dW^1_t-\int_t^TS_t\dW^2_t,
                \end{aligned}
            \end{equation*}
            such that all integrals are well-defined.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.320353+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0d3ce38a5ffd45f59ad8ba1f03d7b18879d7410e5b976bc71476c01a374cbf6a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:swfourier2 of arXiv:2602.11129, 'Information-Theoretic Thresholds for Bipartite Latent-Space Graphs Under Noisy Observation'] Whenever $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\beta$ is positive definite for all $\beta \subseteq \alpha$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \Expectedsub{\mathbf{x}}{ \SW{K_{1,\alpha}} \mid (\mathbf{x}_u)_{u \in \alpha} } &= \lim_{h \rightarrow \infty} \frac{-1}{(2\pi)^{|\alpha|}} \int_{[-\infty, \infty]^{|\alpha|}} e^{-\trp{i\mathbf{t}}{\mathbf{x}}_{\tau}} \left( \sum_{\beta \subseteq \alpha} (-1)^{|\alpha \setminus \beta|}  \hspace{.1cm}\varphi_{\beta}(\mathbf{t}) \right) \prod_{e \in \mathbf{t}} \frac{1 - e^{ih\mathbf{t}(e)}}{i\mathbf{t}(e)} \mathrm{d} \mathbf{t},
    \end{align*}
    where $\varphi_{\beta}(\mathbf{t}) = \Expected{\exp(i\trp{\mathbf{t}}{\mathbf{z}_\beta})}$ is the characteristic function of $\mathbf{z}_\beta$, and $\mathbf{x}_{\tau}$ is the vector that contains $\tau $ everywhere. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:06:18.055169+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0d3e2bd6d55ab03a642aa1a0e755defdfd4856d26cb26f73772e11500c17fb2f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 23 (Fixed-precision subcritical dimension cutoff) of arXiv:2608.15914, 'Absorption cutoff and stationary singularities for rounded Gaussian random dynamical syste'] Assume $A<A_{\mathrm{lat}}$, fix $\varrho\in(0,1)$, and let
  $x_N\in[-1,1]^N$ be deterministic. Suppose that $\bar t_N\to\infty$.
  Then
  \begin{equation}
    \Pp\left(
      \tau_{\varrho}^{(N)}
      >
      \bar t_N-1
    \right)
    \to1,
    \qquad
    \Pp\left(
      \tau_{\varrho}^{(N)}
      >
      \bar t_N+2
    \right)
    \to0.
  \end{equation}
  Equivalently, by the total-variation/absorption identity
  \cref{eq:tv-absorption},
  \begin{equation}
    \norm{
      P_{\varrho,A,N}^{\bar t_N-1}
      (Q_\varrho x_N,\cdot)-\delta_0
    }_{\TV}
    \to1,
    \qquad
    \norm{
      P_{\varrho,A,N}^{\bar t_N+2}
      (Q_\varrho x_N,\cdot)-\delta_0
    }_{\TV}
    \to0 .
  \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

