{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.318016+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0df0c016245318b6ba81ac91fa0585df6fa0719ef288ee75f60e4beafcd57ff5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 8 (Gaussian innovations -- deterministic thresholds) of arXiv:2506.20789, 'Central limit theory for Peaks-over-Threshold partial sums of long memory linear time seri'] Let the $\varepsilon_t$ be i.i.d. Gaussian centered, and suppose that Assumption~\ref{asu:coef} holds.
  If $u_n\to\infty$ is such that for some $\delta>0$, $
  u_n = (-2\log(n)(\kappa_0 + \delta - 1/2 -d)+o(1))^{1/2}
  $,
  where $\kappa_0$ is defined as in \eqref{eq:kappa_0} with $\alpha = 2$, then, as $n\to\infty$,
    \[
  \frac{n^{1/2-d}}{u_n}
