(recall Section~(sec:effpotes of the paper) for $\pi_0$ and $\pi_{{\sf ox}}$).

For $Q_j \in \cV$ defined as in [FSmap] (as a function of $(V_j, K_j)$),  let $\widehat{V}_j = V_j - Q_j$ and
\begin{align}
	\Phi_{j+1}^U (V_j, K_j) = \mathbb{V}^{(0)} \Phi_{j+1}^{\operatorname{pt}} (\widehat{V}_j ) .
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:45:02.090230+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0e60b049dceb45e83a52fb426e9ec53d84c518d6c9a1575c434624f17b423906","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem deter_lemma:no_sausages of arXiv:2601.00119, 'The largest common subtree of two random trees'] If $B_n^{(1)}$ occurs and if $n$ is large enough so that $14\max(c,n^{1/2-\alpha})<\tilde{\delta}\sqrt{n}$, then there exist $w \in \tau$,  $w'\in \tau'$, and $0\leq k\leq \mathrm{Ht}(\tau)-1$ such that at least one of the following two events happens:
