{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:08.278650+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0f4467351fc7ec177475c9fe5565b012ce12c64f127bcf2026a8f8a17f0b9b5a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] In the same setting of Theorem \ref{Theo_main1}, we have for $0<\alpha\le 1$ and $t\ge 0$
	\begin{align}
		\mathbb{P}\{\vert \xi^\top A\xi-\mathbb{E}\xi^\top A\xi\vert\ge L(\alpha)^{2}t    \}\le 2\exp(-c(\alpha)f(t)),\nonumber
	\end{align}
where  $L(\alpha)=\max_{i}\Vert \zeta_{i}\Vert_{\Psi_{\alpha}}$, $c(\alpha)$ is a positive constant depending only on $\alpha$, and
\begin{align}
	f(t)=\min\Big\{ \frac{t^{2}}{\sum_{k}a_{kk}^{2}p_{k}+\sum_{i\neq j}a_{ij}^{2}p_{i}p_{j}}, 
	& \frac{t}{\Vert (a_{ij}\sqrt{p_{i}p_{j}})_{n\times n}\Vert_{l_{2}\to l_{2}} }, \nonumber\\
	&\Big( \frac{t}{\max_{i}\big( \sum_{j}a_{ij}^{2}p_{j}\big)^{1/2}} \Big)^{\frac{2\alpha}{2+\alpha}}, \Big(\frac{t}{\Vert A\Vert_{\infty}}\Big)^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}\Big\}.\nonumber
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:08.851678+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0f46b752f2d21f137cdfc97c30a60ec9f479371ab9bd586ad2143343227a027e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 (Bulk case) of arXiv:2511.12623, 'The eigenvector bead process'] We recall Convention~\ref{conv:inverse-stieltjes-branches}. Fix \(E\in(-2,2)\) and define the anchor \\
\(
i_N(E)\;:=\;\min\bigl\{\,k\in\{1,\dots,N\}\ :\ \lambda^{(N)}_{k}\ \ge E\sqrt{N}\,\bigr\}.
\) Then the overlaps satisfy
\begin{equation}
\Omega^{(N)}_{\,i_N(E)+u,\;i_N(E)+v}
\;\mathrel{\underset{N\to\infty}{\overset{d}{\sim}}}\;
\frac{g_v}{\,S_{\mu,\,g^2}^{-1}(h)_u-\mu_v\,}\,
\sqrt{\bigl(S_{\mu,\,g^2}^{-1}\bigr)'(h)_u},
\qquad h=-\frac{E}{2\sqrt{4-E^2}}.
\end{equation}

\noindent
Here $(\mu_u)_{u\in\mathbb Z}$ follow the sine$_\beta$ point process, and $(g_u)_{u\in\mathbb Z}$ are i.i.d. standard Gaussians $\mathcal{N}_\beta(0,1)$ independent of $(\mu_u)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.637057+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0f46bb9da508c3861f6a3b3667dd09ccaff0647fff4cd4aae33c1d29d80cfc6d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:root deg of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] Let $\mu=\mu_n$ be such that $1\ll\mu\ll n^{1/4}$.
    Let $\mathbf{T}_n$ be a tree distributed according to~(equation eq:T dist of the paper).
