{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:52:17.596221+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0fd8b6efbb3332edb9ef3308657d612568cb63fb6d65084e2b313e86e2797e1f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm TH2 of arXiv:2411.15724, 'Wasserstein Convergence Rates for Empirical Measures of Random Subsequence of $\{nα\}$'] Let $X_1,X_2,\ldots$ be integer-valued i.i.d. non-constant random variables, and let $S_j = \sum_{i = 1}^{j} X_i$. Let $\alpha$ be an irrational number and set $\mu_n = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{j = 1}^{n} \delta_{\{ S_j \alpha \}}$, $n \geq 1$. Then there exists a constant $c > 0$ such that for any $n \geq 1$,
