{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:07.308445+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0fe0e49e915f2f9e44af6deddc937fde3ed2974eea25a36d13007fb2954f7da0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 (Coherence rejective sampling) of arXiv:2606.24323, 'Conditioning of incoherent sub-dictionaries sampled from a coherent dictionary'] \phantom{bla}\\
Assume we are given $K\in \N$, a sequence $p\in (0,1)^K$ and a symmetric matrix $M\in \{0,1\}^{K\times K}$ with $M_{ij}\in\{0,1\}$ and $M_{ii}=0$ for all $i,j\in[K]$, then we define the following discrete probability measures and random variables on $\Delta = \{0,1\}^K$.
For $A\subseteq \Delta$ we set
\begin{align}
\P(A) = \sum_{\delta \in A} p^\delta (\one -p)^{\one - \delta} = \sum_{\delta \in A} \: \prod_{i:\delta_i =1} p_i \prod_{i:\delta_i =0} (1-p_i).
\end{align}
We next define the set-valued random variable $I$ and the real-valued random variable $\mu$, as
\begin{align}
\begin{array}{llcl}
I:& \Delta &\to&  \mathcal{P}([K])\\
& \delta &\mapsto &\{i : \delta_i = 1\}
\end{array}\quad 
\begin{array}{llcl}
\mu: & \Delta &\to& \R \\
& \delta &\mapsto &\delta^t M \delta
\end{array}
\end{align}
and based on $\mu,I$ and a set $S\subseteq [K]$ the following discrete probability measures on $\Delta$, 
\begin{align}
    \P^S(\cdot) = \P(\cdot \mid I\subseteq S), \quad 
     \P_\mu(\cdot) = \P(\cdot \mid \mu = 0) \quad \mbox{and} \quad
     \P^S_\mu(\cdot) = \P(\cdot \mid \mu = 0, I \subseteq S).
\end{align}
The probability measure $\P$ is called Bernoulli or Poisson sampling. Further, we refer to $\P^S$ as $S$-trace (Bernoulli/Poisson) sampling and to $\P^S_\mu$ as coherence rejective ($S$-trace) sampling.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:58:40.818371+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0fe10aba2c02400b0160f27ded95721f5b31d53cd1bd8ff090a69e244a354296","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main2 of arXiv:2605.10361, 'Limit shape of single-source stochastic sandpiles with $p$-topplings on $\Z$'] For the boundaries $\mathtt{l}_{n}$ and $\mathtt{r}_{n}$ of the final stable configuration after placing $n\in\mathbb{N}$ particles at the origin as defined in $(equation eq:boundary of the paper)$, it holds that
