\end{align*}
where the infimum is taken over drifts $v$ such that $v(x)\in L^2([0,\infty),H)$ for almost every $x\in X$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.359570+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1142fead00624988840ecb77429beb247e3fac00fe9a5a5bb70aafe0e243389f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop of arXiv:2507.09421, 'Stability of randomly switching stochastic reaction networks with asymptotically linear tr'] In Example (ex:disjoint species of the paper), there exists vectors $v^1,v^2$ such that all hypotheses of Theorem (thm:slow-switching-transience of the paper) are satisfied, except that $\operatorname{supp}(v^1)\ne\operatorname{supp}(v^2)$. Nevertheless, the mixed process is positive recurrent for any choice of $\kappa$.
\begin{proof}
    If $M_i$ denotes the rate matrix for $\mathcal R_i$, then
    \begin{align*}
        M_1=\begin{pmatrix}-2&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}
        \qquad
        M_2=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-2\end{pmatrix}
    \end{align*}
    If $v^1=(0,1)$ and $v^2=(1,0)$, then $v^iM_i=v^i$ for $i=1,2$. We see that all hypotheses of Theorem (thm:slow-switching-transience of the paper) are satisfied, except that $\operatorname{supp}(v^1)\ne\operatorname{supp}(v^2)$.

    But fix $\kappa>0$, and define $h:\mathbb Z^2_{\ge0}\times\{1,2\}\to[0,\infty)$ via $h(a,b,1)=a+(1+2\kappa^{-1})b$ and $h(a,b,2)=(1+2\kappa^{-1})a+b$. Let $\mathcal L$ denote the generator of the mixed process; we see that
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal Lh(a,b,1)&=\kappa2\kappa^{-1}(a-b)+(b+1)(1)-2a(1+2\kappa^{-1})\\
        &=-4\kappa^{-1}a-b+1.
    \end{align*}
    Similarly, $\mathcal Lh(a,b,2)=-a-4\kappa^{-1}b+1$. We conclude that $\mathcal Lh$ is negative outside a finite set, and so the model is positive recurrent, as claimed.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.460183+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_114bb0ed7116f3a459b9173a276cbf0c698f6ea7fccc3b3f5ed527fad64a3f5b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 48 of arXiv:2604.03777, 'Equilibrium fluctuations for a multi-species particle system with long jumps'] Assume that  $a_H < b_H \in \mathbb{R}$ are such that $H(u)=0$ whenever $u \notin (a_H,b_H)$. Then,
