{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:05:52.423189+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1346144fe518779ba73205716218a615fc6fcf83165d4357b76c48f591d32f36","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma corollary4.88 of arXiv:2504.15992, 'Repeated singular values of a random symmetric matrix and decoupled singular value estimat'] Given any $B>0$, a random variable $\zeta\in \Gamma_B$ that has a finite, $n$-independent log-Sobolev constant, and a random matrix $A_n\sim\operatorname{Sym}_n(\zeta)$. Fix $\kappa>0$. Then we can find constants $C_1,C_2,C_3>0$ depending only on $\kappa$, $B$ and the log-Sobolev constant (and not on $c_0$) such that, for any $c_0>0$ and  $\lambda_1,\lambda_2\in[-(2-\kappa)\sqrt{n},(2-\kappa)\sqrt{n}]$,
    \begin{equation}
   \mathbb{P}\left( \frac{C_1\sqrt{n}}{k}\leq 
   \mu_k(\lambda_i)\leq \frac{C_2\sqrt{n}}{k}\text{ for both $i\in\{1,2\}$ and all } c_0n\leq k\leq C_3n
   \right)\geq 1-e^{-\Omega(n)}.     
