{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:19:46.228428+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_13b09cad90aa23f20757ec66298456df1891b56f5ffdd4dc9267aaa5678280a8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 12 of arXiv:2507.08909, 'Annealed almost periodic entropy'] Let $Q$ be as in~\eqref{eq:two-block}, and assume that $Q_{11}$ and $Q_{22}$ are positive semidefinite. Then the following are equivalent:
	\begin{itemize}
	\item $Q$ is positive semidefinite;
	\item $R$ is equal to $Q_{11}^{1/2}CQ_{22}^{1/2}$ for some contraction $C \in \Xi(\ell,k)$ (recall this notation from Section~\ref{sec:lin-alg});
	\item the generalized Schur complement $Q_{22} - R^\ast Q^\dag_{11} R$ is positive semidefinite.
	\end{itemize}

If $Q_{11}$ and $Q_{22}$ are both nonsingular, then the contraction $C$ promised above is unique, and the following additional conditions are equivalent:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item the whole of $Q$ is nonsingular;
		\item the contraction $C$ is strict;
		\item the Schur complement $Q_{22} - R^\ast Q^{-1}_{11} R$ is nonsingular. \qed
	\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:56:37.667527+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_13b4e3e73cccbe4de671636b83fc3e82eb24f25f7bc53384c51fa270bc27fa0a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem finite-n-cmj-martingale of arXiv:2609.00110, 'Phase Transition and Fluctuation Results for First-Passage Percolation on Spread-Out Cycle'] As $n\to\infty$, $\alpha_n=1+O(n^{-1})$ and $\theta_n=\theta+O(n^{-1})$. The densities of $\nu_n$ converge in $L^1(\mathrm d s)$ to the $\Gamma(\theta,1)$ density. Moreover, for some $\rho>0$ and $n_0<\infty$,
$\sup_{n\geqslant n_0}\int_0^\infty e^{\rho s}\nu_n(\mathrm d s)<\infty$.

The martingale in~(equation eq:prelimit-generation-martingale of the paper) converges in $L^2$ to a mean-one limit $\mathcal{W}_{n,\infty}$. There exist $C<\infty$, $q\in(0,1)$, and $n_0<\infty$ such that
\begin{align*}
 \sup_{n\geqslant n_0}
 \E\abs{\mathcal{W}_{n,\infty}-\mathcal{W}_{n,r}}^2
 \leqslant Cq^r,
 \quad r\geqslant0.
\end{align*}
Finally,
\begin{align*}
 \frac{\mathcal{W}_{n,\infty}}{\alpha_n\theta_n}
 \Rightarrow M_\infty,
\end{align*}
and the same convergence holds jointly for finitely many independent copies. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

