{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.729325+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_14ae83e6858ab63886306683b015ccd90bd87df1caa5ad850523938abcc03bfb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Einaiiagapi of arXiv:2412.09290, 'Asymptotics of Harish-Chandra transform and infinitesimal freeness'] Let $A$ be a random Hermitian matrix of size $N$. Assume that for every finite $r$ one has
  \begin{equation}
    \lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} N \left( \frac{1}{N} \log \mathbb{E}[H \text{}
    C (x_1, \ldots, x_r, 0^{N - r} ; \lambda_1 (A), \ldots, \lambda_N (A))] -
    \sum_{i = 1}^r \Psi (x_i) \right) = \sum_{i = 1}^r \Phi (x_i), 
  \end{equation}
  where $\Psi, \Phi$ are smooth functions in a (complex) neighborhood of $0$ and the
  above convergence is uniform in a (complex) neighborhood of $0^r$. Then one has the following     limit:
  \begin{equation}
