{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:51:11.511306+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_14e2942a36c66b1758662026df235e57e6edaa2d6688e6fea0bca7b605704158","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 (Cumulants and endpoint behavior) of arXiv:2609.02590, 'Directional Optimal Sub-Gamma Scales for Infinitely Divisible Laws'] For every integer $n\geq1$ such that
$\int |x|^{n+2}\nu(\dd x)<\infty$,
\begin{equation}
 \E R_X^n=\frac{2\kappa_{n+2}(X)}{(n+1)(n+2)V}.

\end{equation}
In particular, when the third cumulant is finite,
\begin{equation}
 \cplus(X)\geq\pos{\frac{\kappa_3}{3V}},
 \qquad
 \cminus(X)\geq\pos{-\frac{\kappa_3}{3V}}.

\end{equation}
The MGF boundaries give
\begin{equation}
 \cplus(X)\geq\frac{1}{\tplus(X)},
 \qquad
 \cminus(X)\geq\frac{1}{\tminus(X)}.

\end{equation}
If $\kappa_4$ is finite, then both directional objectives have the same
one-sided derivative at the origin:
\begin{equation}
 \Psi_+'(0+)=\Psi_-'(0+)
 =\frac{\kappa_4}{12V}-\frac{\kappa_3^2}{9V^2}.

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:00:30.491216+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_14e5ac506d40e2e8a57abe4b916c462b9c1c53bc171839329327420357666f89","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2607.04012, 'Hölder regularity for backward stochastic Volterra integral equations and applications to '] The BSDE system \eqref{def:BSDE-app-BSVIE} admits a unique solution $(Y^\pi, Z^\pi)$ such that, for each $k \in \{0, \dots, N-1\}$,
    $Y^\pi(t_k, \cdot)$ and $Z^\pi(t_k, \cdot)$ belong to $\mathbb{L}_a^{1,2}(t_k,T)$. Moreover, the Malliavin derivatives of the solution pair admit a version which satisfies the following linear BSDE system, of the same form as \eqref{def:BSDE-app-BSVIE}:
    \begin{flalign}
    D_\theta  Y^{\pi}(t_k, t) & =  D_\theta\Psi_k^\pi + \int_t^T \Big\{ \alpha_k^\pi(s)  D_\theta Y^{\pi}(\tau(s), s) + \beta_{k}^\pi(s) D_\theta Z^{\pi}(t_k, s)  \notag \\ & +  \big(D_\theta G_k^\pi \big) \Big(s, Y^{\pi}(\tau(s), s), Z^{\pi}(t_k, s)\Big)\Big\} ds - \int_t^T D_\theta Z^{\pi}(t_k, s) dB(s),  
    \end{flalign}
    for $\theta \in [0,T]$ and $t \in [t_k, T] \cap [\theta, T]$, where
    \begin{flalign*}
            \alpha_{k}^\pi(s) &= \partial_y G_k^\pi\Big( s, Y^{\pi}(\tau(s), s), Z^{\pi}(t_k, s)\Big), \quad
            \beta_{k}^\pi(s) = \partial_{z} G_k^\pi\Big( s, Y^{\pi}(\tau(s), s), Z^{\pi}(t_k, s) \Big) .
    \end{flalign*}
    For $t \in [t_k, T] \cap [0, \theta)$, we have
    $D_\theta Y^{\pi}(t_k, t)=0$ and $D_\theta Z^{\pi}(t_k, t)=0$, the latter identity holding $dt\otimes d\mathbb P$-a.e. Finally, for each $k$, the diagonal Malliavin derivative of $Y^\pi(t_k,\cdot)$ provides a version of $Z^\pi(t_k,\cdot)$. More precisely,
    \begin{gather}
        Z^\pi(t_k, s) = D_s Y^\pi(t_k, s) \quad ds \otimes d\mathbb{P}\text{-a.e.}
    \end{gather} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:05:37.773662+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_14ebff08f236c1a55de3a7b16877ce21c8d17ec844b344693c56a1ab18727a09","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem MainThm: Susceptibility of arXiv:2608.00399, 'The Heavy-tailed Frog Model'] Let $d \ge 2$, $\alpha > 0$, and $\lambda > 0$. Consider the corresponding frog model on $\mathbb{T}^d_L$. Then, for any $\varepsilon>0$, we have
