{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:27:14.855622+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16acf1a4cc156d46c1ae9beb58e39c64f82dba7b7d433b520bf15cde3764b1b1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2608.23462, '2608.23462'] Assume that Assumption~\ref{assu:frozenLipschitz} is satisfied. Then for any fixed time horizon $T>0$ and $p \geq 2$, the process $(\hat Y^{x,y}_t)_{t \in [0,T]}$ is locally $L^p$-bounded and continuous in $(x,y) \in \R^{d_1+d_2}$ with respect to $L^p$-convergence. More precisely, for any bounded set $U \subseteq \R^{d_2}$ there exists a constant $C_{p,T,U}>0$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{(x,y) \in \R^{d_1}\times U} \hat \E \Big[ \sup_{0 \leq t \leq T} |\hat Y^{x,y}_t|^p \Big] \leq C_{p,T,U},
