{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:42:47.765183+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16e27359252ce20abfa574316242ce7779e8d306cec2d75b4a173137e037e5a5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 9 (Martingale structure) of arXiv:2604.12797, 'A free boundary problem for the mean-field limit of diffusing particles with nonlinear bou'] For any $\phi \in C^1(\R)$, the limit points of $(Z^{n,\phi})_{n\geq 1}$ are continuous processes. Moreover, let $\psi_{\varepsilon}$ be the probability density function of a reflected
	Brownian motion on the positive half-line at time $\varepsilon>0$, and let $H$ be a bounded continuous process $H$ adapted to the filtration $(\mathcal{\bar{{F}}}_{t}^{n})_{t\geq0}$ from \eqref{eq:Filtration_F_bar}. Then the difference
    \begin{equation}
 \int_0^t H_s \di Z^{n,\phi}_s-\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}\int_{0}^{t}H_s\bigl(\tfrac{1}{2}\partial_x\phi(0)-\phi(0)\gamma(s,C_{s}^{n})\bigr)\langle\mu_{s}^{n},\sigma_{A^n}(s,\cdot)^{2}\psi_{\varepsilon}\rangle \ds 
    \end{equation}
is a martingale for $(\mathcal{\bar{{F}}}_{t}^{n})_{t\geq0}$ and it vanishes uniformly on compacts in $L^2(\Omega)$ as $n\rightarrow \infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:58.439506+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16e6d6ac6419808c209655563076d2081389c665262f2b2724804162cd8a8cb8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition generalXY of arXiv:2604.24743, 'The impact of disorder and non-convex interactions on delocalisation of height functions'] Let $n \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{N}}$ be an integer, $\Lambda \subseteq \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}_n$ be a finite set, and $(J_{xy})_{\{x , y \} \in E(\Lambda)} \subseteq [0, \infty)^{E(\Lambda)} $ be a collection of non-negative conductances. We define the XY and Villain models to be the probability distributions on the space $[0 , 2\pi)^{\Lambda}$ given by the formulae
    \begin{equation} 
        \mu_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{XY}} (d\theta) := \frac{1}{Z_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{XY}}}\exp \left( \sum_{x \sim y} J_{xy} \cos ( \theta_x - \theta_y ) \right) \prod_{x \in \Lambda} d \theta_x
    \end{equation}
    and
\begin{equation} 
    \mu_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{Vil}} (d\theta) := \frac{1}{Z_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{Vil}}} \prod_{x \sim y} v_{J_{xy}} \left( \theta_x - \theta_y \right) \prod_{x \in \Lambda} d \theta_x,
\end{equation}
where $Z_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{XY}}$ and $Z_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{Vil}}$ are the normalizing constants (N.B. Recall the definition of the heat kernel $v_J(\cdot)$ (or $v_\beta(\cdot)$) from~(equation def:introdefVillain of the paper)).

 We denote by $\left\langle \cdot \right\rangle_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{XY}}$ and $\left\langle \cdot \right\rangle_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{Vil}}$ the expectations with respect to the measures~$\mu_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{XY}}$ and~$\mu_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{Vil}}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:52:17.598838+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16eca883ca9087564ee69b2482c3ffdfc13cd4fba9c411d8673550d2510945ff","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lem6.1 of arXiv:2411.15724, 'Wasserstein Convergence Rates for Empirical Measures of Random Subsequence of $\{nα\}$'] For any $(m_1, \ldots, m_{2p}) \in \mathbb Z^{2p}$ and $n \geq 2p$,
\begin{align*}
\bigg| \mathbb E \bigg[   \prod_{s = 1}^{2p} \widehat{\mu_n}(m_s)   \bigg] \bigg| \leq \frac{1}{n^{2p}} \cdot \sum_{\sigma \in \mathrm{S}_{2p}} \prod_{s = 1}^{2p} \min \bigg\{ \frac{1}{1 - \big| \varphi \big(2 \pi \sum_{t = s}^{2p} m_{\sigma(t)} \alpha \big) \big|}, n \bigg\}.
\end{align*}
where $\mathrm{S}_{2p}$ denotes the set of all permutations of $\{1, \ldots, 2p\}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:29:27.956817+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16eebe566c352155c9296924b3337e13769120550cc096776af56cce49aae1e1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2608.24675, 'Towards Liouville Quantum Gravity on fractals'] Let $s>0$. The fractional Riesz kernel of order $s$ is given by
