{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:44.949618+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16f068e284f95cad87a63669fc194d302f1dee7ce0b58166926bba261f6b18a9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 (Properties of $v$) of arXiv:2606.24887, '2606.24887'] Let $v$ be the unique solution of \cref{eq:v}. Let $\delta \in (0,1)$ and $L>0$ be constants.
		Then, for all $w\in \CC$ and $\eta \in (0 , \infty)$, we have
		\begin{equation}
			v(w,\eta) \le \min \biggl\{1, \frac{1}{\eta}\biggr\}.  
		\end{equation}
		Uniformly for all $\eta \in (0,L]$ and $w \in \mathrm{D}_2$, we have 
		\begin{equation}  
			v(w,\eta) \asymp_L \begin{cases} (1 - \abs{w}^2)^{1/2} + \eta^{1/3} & \text{ if } \abs{w} \leq 1, \\ 
				\frac{\eta}{\abs{w}^2 - 1 + \eta^{2/3}} & \text{ if } \abs{w} \geq 1. \end{cases}
