		\end{equation}
		Finally, for all $\eta \in (0,L]$ and $w \in \mathrm{D}_{1-\delta}$, we have 
		\begin{equation} 
			v(w,\eta) \gtrsim_{\delta,L} 1. 
		\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:49:21.121236+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16f2f92b0e85fb9c2451263ab2ac970c5f0b11819141f223f04b9f42618b771e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 11 of arXiv:2608.26642, '2608.26642'] Assume that $1$ belongs to the spectrum of $T^\mu$. Then there exists a function $f^\mu\in C(K)$ such that 
