{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.067152+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1909ff363614364a77def35cff5571c81f8afc7d1ff8e3aa0659b86e532ac3e8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:local_well_posed of arXiv:2606.28575, 'Global smooth solutions by high mode Lie-Transport noise for Logarithmically Hyperdissipat'] Given $\omega_0 \in L^0_{\mathcal{F}_0}(\Omega, H^{-1}_{\operatorname{div}})$, $\theta$ satisfying (hyp_noise of the paper), $p\in [2, +\infty)$, a stopping time $\tau:\Omega\rightarrow [0, +\infty]$ and a progressively measurable process $\omega_t: \Omega\times [0, \tau) \rightarrow H^{0}_g(\mathbb{T}^3)$.  We say that :
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[$\bullet$] $(\omega,\tau)$ is a \emph{Local p-Solution} of equation (equation main_ito of the paper) if the following are satisfied:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[-] $\omega\in L^p((0, \tau); H^{0}_g) $ almost surely
        \item[-] $\mathcal{L}_u\omega \in L^p((0, \tau); H^{-2}_g) $ almost surely
        \item[-] almost surely, it holds for all $t\in [0,\tau)$ 
        \begin{equation*}
            \omega_t- \omega_0 + \int_0^t \left(\mathcal{L}_u  -A - \nu \Delta\right)\omega_s \mathrm{d}s + \sqrt{\nu}\sum_{k\in I} \int_0^t\mathcal{L}_{\sigma_k(s)}\omega_s \mathrm{d}W_s^k=0,
        \end{equation*}
        where the equality is intended as elements of $H^{-2}_g$.
    \end{itemize}
    \item[$\bullet$] A local p-solution $(\omega,\tau)$ to (equation main_ito of the paper) is a said to be a \emph{unique local p-solution} to (equation main_ito of the paper) if for any local solution $(\omega',\tau')$ we have $\omega=\omega'$ a.e. on $[0,\tau\wedge\tau')\times \Omega$.
    \item[$\bullet$] A unique local p-solution $(\omega,\tau)$ to (equation main_ito of the paper) is a said to be a \emph{unique maximal p-solution} to (equation main_ito of the paper) if for any local solution $(\omega',\tau')$ we have $\tau'\leq \tau$ almost surely and $\omega=\omega'$ a.e. on $[0,\tau\wedge\tau')\times \Omega$.
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:46:00.377201+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_190b66bd381aff976bcd550e0d3c8903e5b583d492978fac3a883d08a3e1f9d8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma vbound of arXiv:2607.21443, 'The critical KPZ scale for the averaging process'] Fix $k \geq 2$ and a finite-support function $h:\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{R}$. Then, there exist $C = C(k,h),\varepsilon = \varepsilon(k,h) > 0$ such that, for all $\beta  \in (-\varepsilon, \varepsilon)$, $0\le s\le t$, $1 \leq i, j \leq k$, and $p\ge 1$, 
    \begin{equation}\sup_{\mathbf x\in\mathbb{Z}^k} \mathbf E_\mathbf x^{(\beta,k)} \bigg[ \bigg( \int_s^t |h(R_r^i -R_r^j )|\,{\rm d}r \bigg)^p\bigg]^{1/p} \leq C\sqrt p \left\lvertt-s\right\rvert^{1/2}\; \text{.}
    \end{equation}
    As a consequence, Taylor expanding the exponential yields, for every $a\ge 0$,
    \begin{equation}\sup_{t>0} \sup_{\mathbf x\in\Bbb Z^k} \mathbf E_\mathbf x^{(\beta,k)}\bigg[ \exp\bigg\{a t^{-1/2} \int_0^t |h(R_r^i -R_r^j )|\,{\rm d}r \bigg\}\bigg]<\infty\; \text{.}
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

