{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.592529+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1ba4a74c49c070136ce377afc4220985814a1289e1de92009b304090cf695fa2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Satz: LR-Zerleg. superkrit. Reg. of arXiv:2509.15790, 'Covariance matrices of volume power functionals of random simplicial complexes -- an asymp'] For $n\geq2$ let $\Sigma_n$ be defined as in ((Eq: Kov.matrix Laengen Potenz Funktional of the paper)) in the supercritical regime. The following statements then hold.
\begin{enumerate}
\item 
An LU decomposition $\Sigma_n =LU$ of $\Sigma_n$ is given by the matrices $L= (l_{ij})\in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ and $U=(u_{ij})\in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ which have the entries
\begin{align*} 
l_{ij} = \left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
1&\text{ for }\,i=j,\\
\frac{a_1}{a_i} & \text{ for }\,i >1  \text{ and }  j=1,\\
0& \text{ else}.\\
\end{array}
\right.\, \text{ and }\,
u_{ij} = \left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\frac{1}{a_1a_j} & \text{ for }\,i=1,\\
0& \text{ else}.\\
\end{array}
\right.
\end{align*}
\item
A Cholesky decomposition $ \Sigma_n = G G^t$ of $\Sigma_n$ is given by the matrix $G= (g_{ij})\in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ which has entries 
\begin{align*} 
g_{ij} = \left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\frac{1}{a_i}& \text{ for } j=1,\\
0& \text{ else}.\\
\end{array} \right.
\end{align*}
\item
A matrix root $B=(b_{ij})\in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ of $\Sigma_n$ 
(i.e., $B^2=\Sigma_n$) has entries
\begin{align*} 
b_{ij} = \frac{\prod_{k=1}^na_k}{a_ia_j \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^n\prod_{k \in [n] \setminus \{i\}}a_k^2}}.
\end{align*}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:26:20.568042+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1ba7a0a2e9ae584895de21ee2782562a0214d45ddec848c2d003f85dfeea4b81","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l:2+eps of arXiv:2411.12003, 'Central Limit Theorem for non-stationary random products of $\SL(2, \R)$ matrices'] Let $X$, $Y$ be two independent random variables. Then
\begin{equation}
