{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:14:46.364051+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1cd04ea83879aa5f9a1a2ded9547ca32c13f123759369347c2ccde678c400ffc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] With the above notion of discrete probability measure in hand:
\begin{enumerate}
\item A discrete probability space is simply a space $X$, with a discrete probability measure on it $\nu$. In this picture, the probability function is $P(x)=d\nu(x)$.

\item Each random variable $f:X\to\mathbb R$ has a law, which is a discrete probability measure on $\mathbb R$. This law is given by $\mu=f_*\nu$, push-forward of $\nu$ by $f$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.287639+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1cd22719b02471ab337baf880a64954e210111cbd2ea09b3b7952df57becec37","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 26 of arXiv:2606.22964, 'Signature McKean--Vlasov stochastic differential equations'] Let $p\in[5/2,3)$. For every $t\in[0,T]$,
    \begin{align*}
