{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:37:20.370306+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e2566657370800c8c0b988e687923260be36c8e8c623e7a6b7da3a5e1243cd9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm1 of arXiv:2411.13430, 'Optimal subelliptic super-Poincar\'e and isoperimetric inequalities on stratified Lie grou'] Assume $N$ satisfies in the distributional sense 
    \begin{equation}
        \frac{\left\lvert x \right\rvert^\alpha}{N^\alpha} \lesssim \left\lvert \nabla N \right\rvert \lesssim \frac{\left\lvert x \right\rvert^\alpha}{N^\alpha}, \quad \Delta N \lesssim \frac{\left\lvert x \right\rvert^{2\alpha}}{N^{2\alpha+1}}, \quad \nabla N \cdot \nabla \left\lvert x \right\rvert \lesssim \frac{\left\lvert x \right\rvert^{2\alpha+1}}{N^{2\alpha+1}} 
    \end{equation}
    for some $\alpha > 0$ and where $\left\lvert x \right\rvert$ is the euclidean norm of a generic point $\xi \in \mathbb{R}^n$ written in the form $\xi = (x, x') \in \mathbb{R}^{n_1} \times \mathbb{R}^{n_2}$ with $n_1 \geq 2$. Assume moreover $\left\lvert x \right\rvert$ satisfies again in the distributional sense 
    \begin{equation}
        \nabla \left\lvert x \right\rvert \lesssim 1, \quad \Delta \left\lvert x \right\rvert = \frac{n_1 - 1}{\left\lvert x \right\rvert}, \quad \nabla \cdot x = n_1,
    \end{equation}
    and 
    \begin{equation}
        \left\lvert x \right\rvert \lesssim N, \quad d \lesssim N \lesssim d.
    \end{equation}
    If there exists a local $1$-super-Poincar\'e inequality 
    \begin{equation}
        \int \left\lvert f \right\rvertd\xi \leq \delta \int \left\lvert \nabla f \right\rvertd\xi + \widetilde{\beta}_1(\delta)\left(\int \left\lvert f \right\rvert^{1/2}d\xi\right)^2, \quad \widetilde{\beta}_1(\delta) \lesssim 1 + \delta^{-Q}
    \end{equation}
    for some $Q > 0$ and $f \in W^{1, 2}(\mu)$, and a local $1$-Poincar\'e inequality 
    \begin{equation}
        \int_{B_R} \abs{f - \frac{1}{\xi(B_R)} \int_{B_R} fd\xi} d\xi \lesssim \int_{B_R} \left\lvert \nabla f \right\rvertd\xi
    \end{equation}
    for $f \in W^{1,2}(B_R)$ where $B_R$ is the $d$-ball of radius $R > 0$ centred at the origin, then for each $p \geq \alpha + 1$, the measure $\mu$ defined according to (equation measure of the paper) satisfies the isoperimetric inequality
    \[ I_\mu \gtrsim \mathcal{U}_r, \quad r = \frac{(\alpha + 1)p}{(\alpha + 1) + \alpha p} \]
    with respect to the metric $d$ and where $\mathcal{U}_r(t)$ is the isoperimetric function of the measure $d\nu_r = Z^{-1}e^{-\left\lvert x \right\rvert^r}dx$ with respect to the euclidean metric $\left\lvert \cdot \right\rvert$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:02:08.143007+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e264387e59d6d7f796b4d40fd6621e92ff863a7db6d514ac53cdb6086a33939","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 9 (\cite{QuanTrig}) of arXiv:2608.27630, 'Oriented planar maps and \\ annular mating of trees'] Fix $W_1,W_2, W_3 \ge \gamma^2/2$, and let $\beta_i= \gamma+\frac{2-W_i}{\gamma}$ if $W_i\neq \frac{\gamma^2}{2}$ and $\beta_i=Q^-$ if $W_i=\frac{\gamma^2}{2}$.
    Consider a distribution $\phi$ sampled from 
    \begin{equation*}
        \prod_{\beta_i\neq Q^-} (Q-\beta_i)^{-1} \LF_{\bH}^{(\beta_1,0), (\beta_2, 1),(\beta_3, \infty)}.
    \end{equation*}
    The infinite measure $\QT(W_1,W_2,W_3)$ is defined to be the law of $(\bH, \phi, 0, 1, \infty)/\mathord\sim_\gamma$. A sample from $\QT(W_1,W_2,W_3)$ is called a quantum triangle with weights $W_1$, $W_2$ and $W_3$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:48:02.410022+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e27c094b62d56cffe0ab1861f99bcc7ab964ffab1a004fad78200f678763dd3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lma:trans of arXiv:2608.26564, 'Real ideal points, Conway spheres, and left-orderable Dehn fillings'] Let $\lambda=\ell_1\ell_2\ell_3\ell_4$ be a four-longitude decomposition in positive parabolic normal form along $B$. Then for every sufficiently small $t \neq 0$, 
