For every admissible representation $f=\mathcal T a$,
\begin{equation}
 f'(t)=\sum_{k\ge1}\frac1k a_k(t/k)
 \quad\text{for a.e. }t\in(0,1).
\end{equation}
The unique zero of $I_\theta$ is
\begin{equation}
 \bar f_\theta(t)=c_\theta t.
\end{equation}
Furthermore,
\begin{equation}
 I_\theta(0)=+\infty.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:32:20.720197+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e964ea8e538ae2c2f7534d167e1a305be94f604f17ebfc52d0927138330adb8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 20 of arXiv:2609.00285, 'Multicrossing complex of knot and secant classes'] Let $L$ be an oriented link. Then
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\sigma_2(L)$ is an invariant class in $H_2(L,\Z)$;
    \item $\sigma_3(L)$ is an invariant class in $H^-_3(L,\Z)$;
    \item $\sigma_4(L)$ is an invariant class in $H^+_4(L,\Z)$.
\end{enumerate}
The invariance of $\sigma_n(L)$ means that for any isotopy $f\colon L\to L'$,
\[
\sigma_n(L')=f_*(\sigma_n(L))\in H_n(L',\Z),\quad n=2,3,4.
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.157007+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e96e2d6e9b8782c80237f3006a9a8938e2271968c816a6e487030e4b94de35c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop equiv-thom-from-global of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] The square
