    for $q\in\N_0$ with $q<\max(\frac{d+p}{2}, 1)$, and where $\times$ denotes the component-wise product. If $\Phi$ is $p$-uniform and $p\leq 0$, then $K\Phi$ is also $p$-uniform. For $p<0$ the same holds for beyond $p$-uniformity. Further, if $K_0(\R^d)$ is deterministic, e.g., if $K$ is a probability kernel, $\Phi$ is $p$-uniform, and $p\leq 2$, then $K\Phi$ is also $p$-uniform. For $p<2$, the same holds for beyond $p$-uniformity. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

