{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:26:20.539100+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_24fa5a04da817d53bc5bd845adbe0d2af4f4364a8badcbed4db79ba23e960f1f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t.CLT of arXiv:2411.12003, 'Central Limit Theorem for non-stationary random products of $\SL(2, \R)$ matrices'] Let $\mathcal{K}$ be a compact subset in the set of probability measures on the group~${\mathrm{SL}}(2,{\mathbb R})$ that satisfies the measures condition, and there exists $\gamma \textcolor{black}{\, > 2}$ and $M>0$ such that for any $\mu \in \mathcal{K}$ one has
\begin{equation} 
    {\mathbb E}\,_{\mu} (\log \|A\| )^{\gamma} < M.
\end{equation}
Then the random variables
    $$
        \frac{\log \|T_n\|-L_n}{\sqrt{\mathop{\rm Var}\nolimits(\log\|T_n\|)}}
    $$
    converge in distribution to $\mathcal{N}(0,1)$, with the convergence that is uniform with respect to the choice of the sequence $\mu_1,\mu_2,\dots\in\mathcal{K}$.

     Also, there are constants $C_1, C_{2}>0$ and an index $n_0$ such that for all $n\ge n_0$ and all $\mu_1,\dots,\mu_n\in\mathcal{K}$ one has
    \begin{equation} 
        C_1^2{n}\le \mathop{\rm Var}\nolimits(\log\|T_n\|)\le C_{2}^2{n}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

