{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.064347+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_253813ebae08b696f94037baedf92b7b42860adbbb5753b70e77d9a747a13d07","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemmaNeu of arXiv:2606.28067, 'Ergodic approximation for the invariant distribution: An abstract framework for law-depend'] In addition to Assumption (assumptions_Neurons of the paper), we assume that there exists an integer $p\geq 1$ such that for every $q=1,\ldots,p$ and $x\in{\mathbb R}^+$,
		\begin{align}
			qx^{q-1}(b(x)+J\|f\|_\infty)-x^qf(x)\leq -\bar{b}_qx^q+C_q(1+x^{q-1}).
		\end{align}
		where $C_q$ denotes a positive constant. Then for $t-s\leq 1$, for every $q=1,\ldots,p$, $x\in{\mathbb R}_+$ and $\mu \in \mathcal{P}_0({\mathbb R}_+)$ one has
		\begin{equation}
			{\mathbb E}[\mathcal{X}_{s,t}^q(x,\mu)]
			\leq \Big(1-\big(\bar{b}_q-C_q(t-s)^{\frac 1q}\big)(t-s)\Big)x^ q+C_q(1+x^ {q-1})(t-s).
		\end{equation}
		Moreover, if $\mu\in\mathcal{P}_p({\mathbb R}_+)$ and $X$ is $\mathcal{F}_s$-measurable with $\mathcal{L}(X)=\mu$, then for every $q=1,\ldots,p$ one has
		\begin{equation}
			{\mathbb E}[\mathcal{X}_{s,t}^q(X,\mu)]
			\leq \Big(1-\big(\bar{b}_q-C_q(t-s)^{\frac 1q}\big)(t-s)\Big)\|\mu\|_q^ q+C_q(1+\|\mu\|_{q-1}^ {q})(t-s).
		\end{equation}
		In (equation neu6 of the paper) and (equation neu7 of the paper), $C_q$ denotes a constant depending on  $\|f\|_\infty$, $J$ and $b$.
		Furthermore, we have that the moments of order $2$ are uniformly bounded if $\mu \in \mathcal{P}_2({\mathbb R}_+)$. That is,
		$\sup_{k\geq r}\|\Theta^\pi_{t_r,t_k}(\mu)\|_2^2\leq e^{-b^*_1(t_k-t_r)}\|\mu\|_2^2+C.$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:58.363009+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_253e1291f1d9d2f72ca72df5f089f148620c804aa6ae394ea3a0f73f8a91cb8c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 of arXiv:2506.19272, 'Fully lifted \emph{blirp} interpolation -- a large deviation view'] Consider vector $\p=[\p_0,\p_1,\p_2]$ with $\p_0\geq \p_1\geq \p_2=0$,  vector $\q=[\q_0,\q_1,\q_2]$ with $\q_0\geq \q_1\geq \q_2=0$, and a scalar $\m_1$. For $k\in\{1,2\}$ let $G\in\mR^{m \times n},u^{(4,k)}\in\mR^1,\u^{(2,k)}\in\mR^{m\times 1}$, and $\h^{(k)}\in\mR^{n\times 1}$ be independent Gaussian objects with  i.i.d. zero-mean components.  Let the variances of the elements of $G$, $u^{(4,1)}$, $u^{(4,2)}$, $\u^{(2,1)}$, $\u^{(2,2)}$, $\h^{(1)}$, and $\h^{(2)}$ be $1$, $\p_0\q_0-\p_1\q_1$, $\p_1\q_1$, $\p_0-\p_1$, $\p_1$, $\q_0-\q_1$, and $\q_1$, respectively. Let ${\mathcal U}_k=[u^{(4,k)},\u^{(2,k)},\h^{(k)}]$ and  assume that sets ${\mathcal X}=\{\x^{(1)},\x^{(2)},\dots,\x^{(l)}\}$, $\bar{{\mathcal X}}=\{\bar{\x}^{(1)},\bar{\x}^{(2)},\dots,\bar{\x}^{(l)}\}$,  and  ${\mathcal Y}=\{\y^{(1)},\y^{(2)},\dots,\y^{(l)}\}$ where $\x^{(i)}, \bar{\x}^{(i)}\in \mR^{n}$ and $\y^{(i)}\in \mR^{m}$, $1\leq i\leq l$ are given. Let $p,\beta\geq 0$ and $s$ be real numbers and let $f_{\bar{\x}^{(i)}}(\cdot) : \mR^n  \rightarrow \mR$ be a given function. Consider 
\begin{equation}
\psi( t)  =  \mE_{G,{\mathcal U}_2} \frac{1}{p|s|\sqrt{n}\m_1}
\log \lp  \sum_{i_3=1}^{l}  \lp  \mE_{{\mathcal U}_1} \lp \sum_{i_1=1}^{l}\lp\sum_{i_2=1}^{l}e^{\beta D_0^{(i_1,i_2,i_3 )}} \rp^{s}\rp^{\m_1} \rp^p \rp,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{eqnarray}
 D_0^{(i_1,i_2,i_3)} & \triangleq & \sqrt{t}(\y^{(i_2)})^T
 G\x^{(i_1)}+\sqrt{1-t}\|\x^{(i_1)}\|_2 (\y^{(i_2)})^T(\u^{(2,1)}+\u^{(2,2)})\nonumber \\
 & & +\sqrt{t}\|\x^{(i_1)}\|_2\|\y^{(i_2)}\|_2(u^{(4,1)}+u^{(4,2)}) +\sqrt{1-t}\|\y^{(i_2)}\|_2(\h^{(1)}+\h^{(2)})^T\x^{(i_1)}
 + f_{\bar{\x}^{(i_3)} } ( \x^{(i_1)}  ). \nonumber \\
 \end{eqnarray}
Then
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{d\psi(t)}{dt}  & = &   \frac{\mbox{sign}(s)\beta^2}{2\sqrt{n}} \lp \phi_1^{(1)}+\phi_{21}^{(1)} +\phi_{22}^{(1)} +\phi_{01}^{(1)}+\phi_{02}^{(1)}\rp,
 \end{eqnarray}
where $\phi$'s are as in (\ref{eq:cpt8}).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:59:53.388661+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_253e2ed64270f3a5ca7c673071e1cfcea10e7ae1b59ddf4c75b272b2cc3226ef","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:lifts of arXiv:2608.29657, 'Hypocoercivity of Tempered Bouncy Particle Samplers for Heavy-Tailed Targets'] Recall ${\mathcal L}_\sigma g = - \nabla_x^\star \sigma^2 \nabla_x g $ for any $g\in C^2({\mathbb R}^d)$. The following equalities hold:
