{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.261504+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_273d6cc52f3e154922d29885f6337796a0aa40730049412220e149e478c0b182","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theorem_magnetic_SLE3 of arXiv:2411.16452, 'Interface scaling limit for the critical planar Ising model perturbed by a magnetic field'] Let $h: \Omega \to \mathbb{R}_{+}$ be a bounded and Lipschitz function. Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{C}$ be a bounded and simply connected domain with two marked boundary points $a,b \in \partial \Omega$. Let $(\Omega_{\delta};a_{\delta},b_{\delta})_{\delta}$ be discrete approximations of $(\Omega;a,b)$ where for each $\delta > 0$, $\Omega_{\delta}$ is a subgraph of $\delta\mathbb{Z}^2$. Consider the interface $\gamma_{\delta}$ of the critical Ising model in $\Omega_{\delta}$ with Dobrushin boundary conditions and external magnetic field $H(x,\delta)=C_{\sigma}^{-1}h(x)\delta^{15/8}$, where $C_{\sigma}>0$ is a constant that will be defined below and corresponds to the fact that we are working on the square lattice. Then, as $\delta \to 0$, $\gamma_{\delta}$ converges in law to a massive version of SLE$_3$ in $\Omega$ from $a$ to $b$, with law denoted by ${\mathbb{P}}_{h}^{(\Omega,a,b)}$. 

    The Radon-Nikodym derivative of ${\mathbb{P}}_{h}^{(\Omega,a,b)}$ with respect to the law ${\mathbb{P}}_{\operatorname{SLE}_3}^{(\Omega,a,b)}$ of SLE$_3$ in $\Omega$ from $a$ to $b$ is given by, for any $t \geq 0$,
    \begin{equation} 
        \left.\frac{{\operatorname{d}} {\mathbb{P}}_{h}^{(\Omega,a,b)}}{{\operatorname{d}} {\mathbb{P}}_{\operatorname{SLE}_3}^{(\Omega,a,b)}}(\gamma)\right|_{ \sigma(\gamma(s): 0\leq s \leq t)} = \frac{1}{\mathcal{Z}_h(\Omega)} \sum_{k \geq 0} \frac{1}{k!}\int_{\Omega_t^k} h(z_1)\dots h(z_k)f_{t}^{(k)}(z_1,\dots , z_k) \prod_{j=1}^{k} dz_j
    \end{equation}
    where $\mathcal{Z}_{h}(\Omega)$ is a normalization constant. Above, we have set $\Omega_t = \Omega \setminus \gamma([0,t])$ and the functions $f_t^{(k)}: \Omega_t^k \to \mathbb{R}$ are explicit. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:25:06.200760+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_273e078a3e3c0bb4766348655e2e6f861846fe3f18dceb87467137d575e22eed","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 20 of arXiv:2608.22636, 'Q-Learning with Stable Infinite-Dimensional \\Linear Function Approximation'] Let $\mfk N$ be a deterministic finite subset of $\mbb L$.  For every $\ell\in\mfk N$, let
