{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:37.368314+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_277376f27304eec5210c1ed4c96f3ae5e98916971c5b538a5c300e8dbac0e900","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 (Positive $\alpha$-stable law) of arXiv:2606.18458, 'Stable size-biasing and the positive scale-mixture order of generalized Gaussian laws'] Let $0<\alpha<1$. A positive $\alpha$-stable random variable $S_\alpha$ is the positive random variable whose Laplace transform is
\begin{equation}
\E e^{-uS_\alpha}=e^{-u^\alpha},\qquad u\ge0.

\end{equation}
Equivalently, $S_\alpha$ is the time-one marginal of the stable subordinator with Laplace exponent $u^\alpha$. This is the standard one-sided stable normalization used in classical treatments of stable laws \citep{Feller1971,Zolotarev1986,Nolan2020}.","status":"unverified"}

