{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.302593+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28e3a959e61cffe7f1a813dfb4fb8e8c9a1f9f2ca333ea3cd2f968bb3b9130e6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:general_restrict_max of arXiv:2605.26048, 'Classification of the eternal solutions and multiple coalescing shocks in the KPZ fixed po'] The following holds on a single event of probability one. Let $s \in \mathbb{R}$, and let $f:\mathbb{R} \to [-\infty,\infty)$ be any function which satisfies $f(y^\star) > -\infty$ for some $y^\star \in \mathbb{R}$ and $\sup_{y \in \mathbb{R}}[f(y) - ay^2] < \infty$ for all $a > 0$. Then, for any compact subset $K \subseteq \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}_{>s}$, there exists $A = A(s,K) > 0$ so that for all $(x,t) \in K$,
    \begin{equation} 
    \mathfrak{h}_t(x;f,s) = \sup_{y \in [-A,A]}[f(y) + \mathcal{L}(y,s;x,t)] > \sup_{|y| > A}[f(y) + \mathcal{L}(y,s;x,t)].
    \end{equation}
    Furthermore, the function 
    $
    (x,t) \mapsto \mathfrak{h}_t(x;f,s)$
    is continuous on $\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}_{>s}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:13:18.532388+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28e3ee29115f7ce539ecbb2e08cce1d0033712949cef6f1bf61c03bed05dee8a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 6 (no universal behaviour at the critical dimension) of arXiv:2608.30668, 'Recurrence and capacity of stable branching random walks'] Assume that \(\alpha > 2 \text{ and } \frac{2\alpha}{\alpha-1} \in \N\). Also assume that $\theta$, defined here on $\Z^{\frac{2\alpha}{\alpha-1}}$, has a finite moment of order $\delta > \frac{2\alpha}{\alpha-1}$. Consider \(\Tree_{n}\)-walk in \(\Z^{\frac{2\alpha}{\alpha-1}}\) with $\theta$-jumps.

\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
  \item 
If \Cref{Assum:SufficientConditionTransience} holds, then this \(\Tree_{n}\)-walk is transient:
\begin{equation*}{
\frac{R^{\theta}(\Tree_{n})}{n} \xrightarrow[n \to +\infty]{(\P)} \P(\esc_{+}) > 0.
}\end{equation*}
  \item If \(L(n) \to +\infty\) then this \(\Tree_{n}\)-walk is
recurrent:
\begin{equation*}{
\frac{R^{\theta}(\Tree_{n})}{n} \xrightarrow[n \to +\infty]{(\P)}  0.
}\end{equation*}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.611005+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_28e687d7ca3e3b272bc1450f56d2979b77c8a0b5fbe14aecdc7a8962b2d6029d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Problem thm:erik-goran of arXiv:2412.09087, 'General Markovian randomized equilibrium existence and construction in zero-sum Dynkin gam'] The associated game is a specification of the game so far presented corresponding to the functions 
