{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:07:17.255822+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2a01e22fc63fd93157c686406813d5495d5ad6538e960cc079c08a086ec1619e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma AJSJAsd of arXiv:2603.12774, 'Synchronization by noise for stochastic differential equations driven by fractional Browni'] Let \( H \in (0,1) \). For \( \omega \in C_0^\infty(\mathbb{R}^-, \mathbb{R}^d) \), define
	\begin{align}
		\mathcal{D}_H \omega(t)
		:= \frac{1}{\alpha_H} \int_{-\infty}^{0}
		(-r)^{H - \frac{1}{2}}
		\left( \dot{\omega}(t + r) - \dot{\omega}(r) \right)
		\, \mathrm{d}r, 
		\quad t \le 0.
	\end{align}
	This operator extends continuously from \(C_0^\infty(\mathbb{R}^-, \mathbb{R}^d) \) to \( \mathcal{B}_H \) and satisfies the following properties.
	\begin{itemize}
		\item The map
		\begin{align}
			\mathcal{D}_H : \mathcal{B}_H \longrightarrow \mathcal{B}_{1-H}
		\end{align}
		admits a bounded inverse.
		\item There exists a unique Gaussian measure \( \mathbf{P} \) on \( \mathcal{B}_H \) such that the associated canonical process is a time-reversed Brownian motion.
