{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:41:19.333937+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2ae014e8cda114045ed08e0befb5b2d9a121a23387eb98e200d0e927e500d618","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop subsequential limit of arXiv:2609.04138, 'The conformally invariant metric on $\CLE_4$ I:\\ subsequential limits of the non-simple C'] Let $\kappa_n \downarrow 4$. Then there exists a subsequence of $(\kappa_n)_{n\ge 0}$, again denoted by $(\kappa_n)_{n\ge 0}$, such that we have the joint convergence in law:
	\begin{equation}
		\begin{pmatrix}
			(\SCL^{\kappa_n}(x))_{x \in \BQ^2} \\
			(\ka_{\kappa_n}^{-1}D^{\kappa_n, \partial}(\SCL^{\kappa_n}(x), \SCL^{\kappa_n}(y)))_{x,y \in \BQ^2}			
			\\
			(K^{\kappa_n, \partial}_{\SCL^{\kappa_n}(x), \SCL^{\kappa_n}(y)})_{x, y \in \BQ^2}			
		\end{pmatrix}
		\mathop{\longrightarrow}\limits_{n\to \infty}^{(\mathrm{d})}
		\begin{pmatrix}
			(\SCL(x))_{x \in \BQ^2} \\
			(D^\partial(\SCL(x), \SCL(y)))_{x,y \in \BQ^2}
			\\
			(K^\partial_{\SCL(x), \SCL(y)})_{x, y \in \BQ^2}
		\end{pmatrix},
	\end{equation}
	where the loops $(\SCL(x))_{x \in \BQ^2} $ have the same law as the loops of a $\CLE_4$ $\Gamma$ in $\BD$ (and will be identified with the loops of $\Gamma$), where we write $\widehat{\Gamma} \defeq \Gamma \cup \{\partial \BD\}$, where $D^\partial$ is a random pseudometric on $\widehat{\Gamma}$, and where $K^\partial_{\SCL(x), \SCL(y)}$ 
	are random compact subsets of $\overline{\BD}$. The above convergence holds for the product topology, and the compact sets converge in the Hausdorff topology.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.569300+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2ae21f7497ed2eb8faa41f1b50d1b6a17c18a54c58ea7175b034c51d45695f21","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.speed-modulus of arXiv:2605.26082, 'Quantitative Einstein relation for reversible diffusions in a random environment'] Let $I_k=(2^{-k-1},2^{-k}]$ and $L_k=2^{-k}$. There exists $C=C(d,\Lambda,r_0)<\infty$ such that for every $k\ge 1$ and every $\lambda,\lambda'\in I_k$,
