{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:39:11.670072+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2bdd47ac376923e18361fe269bf69a6fdf1368394a35d70e5585f6a96b95a40d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 13 (Small components and surplus tails) of arXiv:2608.04799, '2608.04799'] Recall the coordinates \(X_{n,i}\) of \(\mathbf X_n\) from
\eqref{eq:Xn-def}.
There is \(C=C(\lambda)<\infty\) such that, for \(0<\varepsilon\le1\),
\begin{equation}
  \sup_{n\ge n_\lambda}
  \E\sum_{i:X_{n,i}\le\varepsilon}X_{n,i}^2
  \le C\sqrt\varepsilon.
\end{equation}
For every \(0<a<b<\infty\),
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{R\to\infty}\sup_{n\ge n_\lambda}
