{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.605648+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2d364f9a16c59e98653d61d7b1e3b40964572e298eb2b98ab1fbb26bd6a0d936","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 6 of arXiv:2607.08129, '2607.08129'] Almost surely, we have
\begin{equation}
 \limsup_{n\to\infty}
 \frac{\log L_n-\E\log L_n}
 {\sqrt{2n\log n\log\log n}}
 =\sigma_\theta,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 \liminf_{n\to\infty}
 \frac{\log L_n-\E\log L_n}
 {\sqrt{2n\log n\log\log n}}
 =-\sigma_\theta.
\end{equation}
More generally, if $\ell$ is a continuous linear functional on $C[0,1]$, then
\begin{equation}
 \limsup_{n\to\infty}\ell(\widehat\Xi_n)
 =\norm{\ell}_{\cH_\theta^\ast},
 \qquad
 \liminf_{n\to\infty}\ell(\widehat\Xi_n)
 =-\norm{\ell}_{\cH_\theta^\ast}
\end{equation}
almost surely, where $\norm{\ell}_{\cH_\theta^\ast}$ denotes the operator norm of the restriction of $\ell$ to the RKHS $\cH_\theta$, namely
\[
 \norm{\ell}_{\cH_\theta^\ast}^2
 :=\sup_{\norm{f}_{\cH_\theta}\le1}|\ell(f)|^2.
\]
This number is finite because the RKHS unit ball is compact in $C[0,1]$ and $\ell$ is continuous for the uniform norm. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.967866+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2d377ac18e8c8464cb680ee4ff11c7a9d5602cf4c68b59b922168c645eb69e91","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 14 of arXiv:2605.11279, 'Flocking with Multiple Types: Competition, Fluid Limits and Traveling Waves'] Suppose that a traveling-wave solution to \eqref{originalFluid} exists. Then $\rho_\pm(z) := F_\pm'(z)$, $z \in \real$, the associated traveling wave densities, satisfy 
\begin{align}

    v_\pm \int_{-\infty}^z \phi(z-y) \rho_\pm(y) \di y = \parens*{v_\pm + \beta^\pm(z)} \rho_\pm(z) - \beta^\mp(z) \rho_\mp(z) - \gamma \rho'_\pm(z),
\end{align}
where $\phi$ denotes the density of $J$, the jump step, and for $z\in\real$, we define
\begin{align*}
    \beta^+(z) &:= \alpha^+ - \int_z^\infty \rho_-(x)\di x = \alpha^+ - \rho^- + F_-(z), \\
    \intertext{and}
    \beta^-(z) &:= \alpha^- + \int_z^\infty \rho_+(x)\di x = \alpha^- + \rho^+ - F_+(z).
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:05:52.415186+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2d3e020b4fccae807bd9a86419817c6ba5708a6ab15474c628fe6e4413d26ddb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition finalfuckpropositionga of arXiv:2504.15992, 'Repeated singular values of a random symmetric matrix and decoupled singular value estimat'] Let $A_{n+1}\sim\operatorname{Sym}_{n+1}(\zeta)$, $X\sim\operatorname{Col}_n(\zeta)$  and $\lambda_1,\lambda_2\in\mathbb{R}$ . For any $p>1$ and $\delta_1,\delta_2>0$, we have the following estimate:
