{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:58.436521+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2d41dfd7453471d059f8a9e9d67784fc61d4877dbabe7def052d154e9da55514","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Partial order, increasing functions and stochastic domination of arXiv:2604.24743, 'The impact of disorder and non-convex interactions on delocalisation of height functions'] We introduce the three following definitions:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] \textit{Partial order:} Given a subset $\Lambda \subseteq \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}$, we define a partial order on the space $\{ 0 , 1 \}^{\Lambda}$ by writing, for any pair $r , r' \in \{ 0 , 1 \}^{\Lambda}$, $r \preceq r'$ if and only if, for all $x \in \Lambda, \, r_x \leq r_x'.$
\item[(ii)] \textit{Increasing function:} A function $f : \{ 0 , 1 \}^\Lambda \to \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ is called increasing if $f(r) \leq f(r')$ for all pairs of configurations $r , r' \in \{ 0 , 1 \}^{\Lambda}$ with $r \preceq r'$.
\item[(iii)] \textit{Stochastic domination:} Given two probability measures $\nu , \nu'$ on $\{ 0 , 1 \}^{\Lambda}$, we say that $\nu$ stochastically dominates $\nu'$, and denote it by $\nu \preceq \nu'$, if $\mathbb{E}_\nu \left[  f \right] \leq \mathbb{E}_{\nu'} \left[  f \right]$ for any increasing function $f : \{ 0 , 1\}^{\Lambda} \to [0, \infty)$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:49:21.127351+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2d44f1bd92df74c4204d815302cb272c643f6e4fbb8f4d535ba6bb05b6e14c3f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 26 of arXiv:2608.26662, 'Boundary–Moment Universality and Curvature Corrections in Random Geometric Graphs on Riema'] There exist constants $C<\infty$ and $r_0>0$ such that, for every
