	\phi_{G, \beta}^0[0 \connect{} B] \leq \sum_{\substack{u\in S\\ v\notin S\\ u\sim v}} \phi_{S, \beta}^0[0 \connect{} u]\beta\phi_{G, \beta}^0[v \connect{} B]. 
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.768761+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2f5a048b43a5d2ef413fc5277d2c4675fca3f7357228518b8eb1088cce6ca924","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 23 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Fix $T_1 \in \mathbb{N}$, $\vec{y} \in \mathfrak{W}_{2k}$, $q \in (0,1)$, $c \in [0, q^{-1})$, and any function $g:\llbracket 0, T_1 \rrbracket \to[-\infty,\infty)$. Let $\{\mathfrak{B}^i = (B^i_1, B^i_2)\}_{i = 1}^k$ be $k$ independent interacting pairs with laws $\mathbb{P}^{T_1, \vec{y}\,^i}_{\ice; q,c}$, where $\vec{y}\,^i = (y_{2i -1}, y_{2i})$. Let $\mathfrak{Q} = \{Q_i\}_{i = 1}^{2k}$ be the $\llbracket 1, 2k \rrbracket$-indexed geometric line ensemble whose distribution is the same as $(B^1_1, B^1_2, B^2_1, B^2_2, \dots, B^{k}_1, B^k_2)$, conditioned on the event
\begin{equation}
E_{\ice} = \left\{ B^{i}_{2} \succeq B^{i+1}_{1} \mbox{ for all } i\in\llbracket 1,k\rrbracket \right\},
\end{equation}
where $B_{1}^{k+1} = g$. We denote by $\Omega_{\ice}(T_1, \vec{y}, g)$ the set of $k$-tuples $\{\mathfrak{B}^i = (B^i_1, B^i_2)\}_{i = 1}^k$ satisfying the conditions in $E_{\ice}$, and note that in view of Lemma \ref{Lem.FinitePartitionFunction}, we have that the above definition is well-posed if for some $(\mathfrak{B}^1, \dots, \mathfrak{B}^k) \in  \Omega_{\ice}(T_1, \vec{y}, g)$, we have $\prod_{i = 1}^k W(\mathfrak{B}^i; q,c) > 0$.

We denote the law of $\mathfrak{Q}$ by $\mathbb{P}^{T_1, \vec{y}, g}_{\ice; q,c}$ and write $\mathbb{E}^{T_1, \vec{y}, g}_{\ice; q,c}$ for the expectation with respect to this measure. When $g=-\infty$, we omit the last superscript and simply write $\mathbb{P}^{T_1, \vec{y}}_{\ice; q,c}$, and $\mathbb{E}^{T_1, \vec{y}}_{\ice; q,c}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:18:19.199953+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2f5cd487d1ecb62fcf29d44d9c5a342ccef3fc24bf29c91cc5c6fe70aa436319","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 8 of arXiv:2608.28964, '2608.28964'] For any $g$ satisfying \eqref{eq g}, there exists a constant $\mathcal C_g>0$ such that for any $z_0\in \mathcal H$, there exists a control
	\begin{equation*}
	\zeta\in H_0^1(0,1;L^2(\omega;\R^2))\cap L^2(0,1;H^2(\omega;\R^2))\cap C([0,1];H^1(\omega;\R^2))
	\end{equation*}
    such that the solution $z$ of \eqref{eq z} satisfies that $z(1)=0$ and
	\begin{align}
	\|\zeta\|_{H^1(0,1;L^2(\omega))}+\|\zeta\|_{L^2(0,1;H^2(\omega))}+\|\zeta\|_{L^\infty(0,1;H^1(\omega))}&\leq\mathcal C_g\|z_0\|.
	\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:02:28.635992+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2f60721d786fde3fe1b70ca2ebe3d87ebae7449ce286bbf200793cf332ea7b44","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 23 of arXiv:2609.01362, '{Double cluster swapping for spin models: \\Pfaffian relations and sharpness}'] Let $G = (V,E)$ be a finite graph and $S \subset V$. If $P \in \Ec$, then
	\[
		\left< \vp_o \vp_x \right>_{G,\beta,h} \leq \beta \sum_{\substack{u \in S \\ v \in G \setminus S}} J_{uv} \left< \vp_o \vp_u \right>_{S,\beta,0} \left< \vp_v \vp_x \right>_{G,\beta,h} + h \sum_{u \in S} \left< \vp_o \vp_u \right>_{S,\beta,0} \left< \vp_x \right>_{G,\beta,h} 
	\] 
	for any $o \in S$ and $x \in V \setminus S$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.598572+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2f66de2bf1f3504c52254054eff7dc86e7491eca518fc5766674c744ccdcdfae","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 29 of arXiv:2607.07283, 'Multi-time Markov renewal chains and stratified renewal theorems'] Let $\Lambda$ be a compact subset of $\Delta_I$ and let $k_t:\Lambda\to\N^d$ satisfy $\sup_{\lambda\in\Lambda}|k_t(\lambda)-t\lambda|_1=o(t)$. If $\Ee_{\mathfrak a}[R_1^*(f)]<\infty$, then, under $\Pp_{\mathfrak a}$,

\begin{equation*}
\sup_{\lambda\in\Lambda}
\left|\frac{V_f(k_t(\lambda))}{t}-\ell\kappa(\lambda)\pi(f)\right|
\xrightarrow[t\to\infty]{}0
\end{equation*}

almost surely and in $L^1$. The same conclusion holds under every initial law for which the first entrance time into the atom, the entrance displacement and the reward accumulated before this entrance are integrable. In particular, for $A\in\calX$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\Psi_{k_t(\lambda)}(x,A)}{t}
\xrightarrow[t\to\infty]{} \ell\kappa(\lambda)\pi(A)
\end{equation*}

locally uniformly in $\lambda$ on every compact subset of a directional cell, whenever the preceding entrance condition holds for the starting point $x$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.442080+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2f6ab385cc138a2ec7b50feff250175dbcdb82a1d2902af711c8dcfaf44441d3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 35 of arXiv:2606.15425, 'Well-posedness of stochastic parabolic equations with gradient nonlinearities and applicat'] Assume that $g$ has bounded $k$-th order partial derivatives for $k \geq 2$. Let $1\geq \delta > \frac dq$ and ${\bm u}, {\bm v} \in H^{\delta,q}(\mathbb T^d)$. The Nemytskii operator defined by $g$ (cf. Assumption \ref{ass:nemytskiiLipschitz}) satisfies
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
    \item 
    $$\norm{g({\bm u})-g({\bm v})}_{H^{\delta,q}} 
        \leq \const (1+\norm{{\bm u}}^{k-1}_{H^{\delta,q}}+\norm{{\bm v}}^{k-1}_{H^{\delta,q}})\norm{{\bm u}-{\bm v}}_{H^{\delta,q}}$$
    \item  For $0 \leq \sigma < \sigma_0 \leq \frac{d}{q}$, $$\norm{g({\bm u})-g({\bm v})}_{\mathcal L(H^{\sigma_0,q}, H^{\sigma,q})} \leq \const (1+\norm{{\bm u}}^{k-1}_{H^{\delta,q}}+\norm{{\bm v}}^{k-1}_{H^{\delta,q}})\norm{{\bm u}-{\bm v}}_{H^{\delta,q}}.$$ 
    \item  For any $0 \leq \sigma < \sigma_0< \frac{d}{q}$ and $k \geq 1$,    $$\norm{g({\bm u})}_{\mathcal L(H^{\sigma_0,q},H^{\sigma,q})} \leq \const\left(1+\norm{{\bm u}}^{k-1}_{H^{\frac{d}{q},q}}\right)\left(1+\norm{{\bm u}}_{H^{\frac{d}{q},q}}\right).$$
\end{enumerate}
for some constant factors dependent on $\delta,q, \sigma, \sigma_0, g$ and $d$, but not on ${\bm u},{\bm v}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.728854+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2f6b4917cf5222f993fd31684023fa0bbfb6e8b65ce1ffd72c736f4b1c12f287","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:GCS of arXiv:2606.08325, 'Digit Mixing under Polynomial Maps'] Let $Q$ be a real multi-variate polynomial of degree at most $q$ in the variables \(Z_{j,k}\) -- written in multilinear form. For \(1\le k_1,\ldots,k_q\le L\), let \(A(k_1,\ldots,k_q)\) be the coefficient of the monomial $Z_{1,k_1}Z_{2,k_2}\cdots Z_{q,k_q}$.
			Let \(U_{j,k}\) be independent random variables with law
			\[\mathbb P(U_{j,k}=1)=\mathbb P(U_{j,k}=-1)=\frac14, \qquad
			\mathbb P(U_{j,k}=0)=\frac12.\]
			Then
			\begin{equation}
				\left|\mathbb E e(Q(Z))\right|^{2^q}
				\leq \left|\mathbb E e\!\left(\sum_{k_1,\ldots,k_q=1}^{L}
				A(k_1,\ldots,k_q) \prod_{j=1}^{q}U_{j,k_j}\right)\right|.
				\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.502116+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2f711a4aad3e8459e270047e846c082309ebabbcb5288ae3279e50b05a207fb2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 45 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] Let $E,F$ be two Banach spaces. Furthermore, let $h : W \to L(W,F)$ be of class $C^1$, and let $B \in L(E,W)$ be arbitrary. Then the mapping
\begin{align*}
h_B : W \to L(E,F), \quad h_B(y) := h(y) B
\end{align*}
is of class $C^1$, and we have
\begin{align*}
D h_B(y)v = (D h(y)v) B \quad \text{for all $y,v \in W$.}
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:43.413671+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2f7857419924345ffd505851fd8a26c4c5bdbdb88262826baf4a1c69247aa938","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem l.delta of arXiv:2507.16056, 'Conditional GMC within the stochastic heat flow'] Under the preceding setup, for every $t\in(0,\infty)$, 
