{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:05:52.431719+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3028482df68aca62e7082d65dd64b317dbaf5d30aea0838106f60904f7317deb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma7.27 of arXiv:2504.15992, 'Repeated singular values of a random symmetric matrix and decoupled singular value estimat'] Fix $B>0,\zeta\in\Gamma_B$ with a finite Log-Sobolev constant and $A_n\sim\operatorname{Sym}_n(\zeta)$.
Fix $\kappa>0,\Delta>0$ and consider two locations $\lambda_1,\lambda_2\in[-(2-\kappa)\sqrt{n},(2-\kappa)\sqrt{n}]$ with $|\lambda_1-\lambda_2|\geq\Delta\sqrt{n}$. Then we have the following initial estimate:
for any $\delta_1,\delta_2>0$,
