{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:04:30.580235+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_30583a82e7a16df5af95b9ff428b642af740b551cd0b869e6111f53b2a8e523a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:IndS of arXiv:2411.10333, 'Subcritical annulus crossing in spatial random graphs'] We call the following setup the \emph{independent setting}: 
		Let the underlying point process have independent increments and is thus either given by a Poisson point process, cf.~[LastPenrose2017], or by a Bernoulli site-percolated lattice. Further, for a given collection of mutually different vertex pairs \(\{\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{1_2}\},\dots,\{\boldsymbol{x}_{k_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}\}\), we have 
	\begin{equation*}\tag{Ind}
\mathbb{P}_\lambda^{\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{1_2},\dots,\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}}\Big(\bigcap_{j=1}^k \{\boldsymbol{x}_{i_1}\sim \boldsymbol{x}_{i_2}\} \, \Big| \, \mathscr{V} \Big) = \prod_{i=1}^k \mathbf{p}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{i_2}), 
	\end{equation*} 
	for a suitable, symmetric function \(\mathbf{p}\). Here, \(\mathbb{P}_\lambda^{\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{1_2},\dots,\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}}\) denotes the law where the given points \(\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\dots,\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}\) have been added to the graph in the Poisson point process case, or the law conditioned on the event that said vertices survived the site percolation, respectively.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.750052+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_30599e3673db3eb5efe5656bd6481e3cb73581694dd12ddcd15b8318cbb51d61","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:equivalence_nonlinear_mp_weak_sol_den of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, and let $\mathbb{P}_{\nu}$ be a probability measure on $(C_{\infty}, \mathcal{B} )$, such that
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb{P}_{\nu}(x_t \in d y) = v_t(y) dy, 
