{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.085523+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_325fba973e0b18275a3a4a4971fe93440e3eddd52e8357c968772f40ce52613f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition e-family of arXiv:2412.08400, '\vspace{-1.0cm} Characterization of Exponential Families of \\ Lumpable Stochastic Matrice'] We say that the parametric family of irreducible stochastic matrices $$\mathcal{V}_e = \left\{ P_\theta \colon \theta = (\theta^1, \dots, \theta^d) \in \mathbb{R}^d \right\} \subset \mathcal{W}( \mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E}),$$ 
forms an exponential family (e-family) of stochastic matrices with natural parameter $\theta$ and dimension $d$, when
there exist a function $K \in \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$ and $d$ linearly independent functions\footnote{Although $G_1, \dots, G_d$ are technically cosets, we treat them as coset representatives in $\mathcal{F}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$.} $G_1, \dots, G_d \in \mathcal{G}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$, 
such that
    \begin{equation*}
        P_\theta = {\mathfrak{s}} \circ \exp \left(K + \sum_{i = 1}^{d} \theta^i G_i\right),
    \end{equation*}
    where $\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$ is the quotient space
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{G}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E}) \triangleq \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})/\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E}),
\end{equation*}
with $\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{E})$ introduced in Definition~(definition:anti-shift-functions of the paper)
    and ${\mathfrak{s}}$-normalization follows from  Definition~(definition:s-normalization of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:04:39.218547+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3260d71d74a9a2ae321863f0e26d642991ef5050c9fa3d5762aa2e402cc1454b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.13732, '\textbf{On the First Hitting Time Problems \\ for Diffusion Processes: \\ Local Time-Space'] The cumulative distribution function $G(t,x)$ of the first-hitting time of the lower boundary $b(t)$ is given by
\begin{align}
   G(t,x)=\mathbb{P}_{T-t,x}\left(Y_T\le b(0)\right)-\frac{1}{2}\int_0^{t} f(u)\, q(T-u, b(u) ; T-t, x)\, du
\end{align}
for $t\in[0,T]$, where $f(t)=G_x(t, b(t)+) \,\sigma^2(t, b(t))$ and $q$ is the transition density function of the process $Y$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:00:30.491999+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3262fb657dbda77d1266cfdc385715210a043bb58173f60cf0c43199abf84932","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 14 of arXiv:2607.04012, 'Hölder regularity for backward stochastic Volterra integral equations and applications to '] Suppose that Assumption \ref{assumption-Holder-BSVIE} holds for some $p>2$,
