{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:53:54.887604+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_351c37a61e97bc6bebbe47c76ee163a7098f546954ecbbff0c7294f6400e9a4e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 32 of arXiv:2607.17030, 'Stationary measures for log-gamma polymer on a strip and in half-space'] {$ $}

\begin{enumerate}  [(i)]
\item If $\alpha,  x>0$, then   \cite[\href{http://dlmf.nist.gov/5.8.E3}{(5.8.3)}]{NIST:DLMF}
\begin{equation}
  |\Gamma(\alpha+\i x)|^2 = \tfrac{\Gamma(\alpha)^2}{\prod_{k=0}^\infty\left(1+\frac{x^2}{(\alpha+k)^2}\right)}.
\end{equation}
\item If $x\ne 0,-1,\dots$, then  \cite[\href{http://dlmf.nist.gov/5.2.E2}{(5.2.2)}]{NIST:DLMF}
\begin{equation}
   \tfrac{\partial \Gamma(x)}{\partial x}=\psi(x)\Gamma(x),
\end{equation}
where $\psi$ is the {\em  digamma function}.
\item Function $x\mapsto \Gamma(\alpha+x)\Gamma(\alpha-x)$  is convex on $(-\alpha,\alpha)$ and in particular it is
a strictly decreasing function on $(-\alpha,0]$ with the minimum $\Gamma(\alpha)^2$ at $x=0$.
\item If  $\alpha>0$ and $0<p<\alpha$, then
\begin{equation}
   \Gamma(\alpha)^2< \Gamma(\alpha+p)\Gamma(\alpha-p).
\end{equation}

\item For a Pochhammer  symbol
$(a)_j =\Gamma(j+a)/\Gamma(a)$ with $a\not\in\ZZ_{\leq -1}$, we have
\begin{equation}

    (-a)_j=\tfrac{(-1)^j \Gamma (1+a )}{\Gamma (1+a-j)}, \quad j=0,1,\dots.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:46:36.171863+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3520c7204c4549e18d004747583c27a8e50a64b0d122c0fb175cb61454c39af1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop Prop_TransversalityCriterion_EigenSpinors of arXiv:2609.02848, 'The universal moduli space of non-degenerate $\Z/2\Z$~harmonic spinors'] Let $B$ be a manifold and $\Gamma \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace B \to \mathbf{Dir}$ a smooth map.
  The following are equivalent:
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item
    $\Gamma$ is transverse to $\mathrm{pr}_\mathbf{Dir} \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace {\mathbf{P}}\mathbf{Eig}^* \to \mathbf{Dir}$.
  \item
    For every $b \in B$ and $({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}];[\Phi],\lambda) \in {\mathbf{P}}\mathbf{Eig}^*$ with $\Gamma(b) = {\mathbf{p}}$
    the map
    \begin{equation*}
      \delta_{\Phi,\lambda} \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace T_bB \times \mathbb{R} \to \paren[\big]{\ker \paren{D_{R_\Phi}-\lambda}}^*
    \end{equation*}
    defined by
    \begin{equation*}
      \delta_{\Phi,\lambda}(v,t)\kappa
      \coloneq
      \Inner*{T_{\Phi,\kappa},T_{{\mathbf{p}}}\mathrm{pr}_\mathbf{Met} \circ T_b\Gamma(v)}_{L^2}
      + \Inner*{\mu(\Phi,\kappa),\mathrm{pr}_{V_\nabla} \circ T_b\Gamma(v)}_{L^2}
      - t\Inner*{\Phi,\kappa}_{L^2}
    \end{equation*}    
    is surjective.
    Here $R_\Phi$ denotes the residue condition induced by $\Phi$.
    \qed
  \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.351563+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_35215782eee1b583f58a54c7665648028a119dd17b02f71da332e775aa95c6c6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:diff-tail of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] For any $0\le s<t \le 1$, under any coupling between $\{\mathcal{H}_i\}_{i=1}^k, \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{Q}, \{\mathcal{D}_i\}_{i=1}^k$, we have the following:
