{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:54:57.589285+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_35d8d05e56604725bad39a71576f976eb106e7e49d166e56f2bddb985b995948","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:caract of arXiv:2412.03366, 'Regularity of Weighted Tensorized Fractional Brownian Fields and associated function space'] Let $0 <p,q \leq \infty$, $s \in \mathbb{R}$ and $0 \le \alpha \le 1$ and let $ (c_{\bar{j},\bar{k}})$ denote the sequence of the Meyer wavelet coefficients of a function $f \in \mathcal{S}'(\mathbb{R}^2)$. The following assertions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f \in T^{s,\alpha}_{p,q}B(\mathbb{R}^2)$,
\item $ (c_{\bar{j},\bar{k}} )\in t^{s,\alpha}_{p,q}b $.

\end{enumerate}
Moreover, there exist two constants $C_1,C_2>0$ such that
$$
C_1 \Vert  (c_{\bar{j},\bar{k}}) \Vert_{t^{s,\alpha}_{p,q}b} \le \Vert f \Vert_{T^{s,\alpha}_{p,q}B(\mathbb{R}^2)} \le C_2 \Vert (c_{\bar{j},\bar{k}}) \Vert_{t^{s,\alpha}_{p,q}b} \, . 
$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.364384+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_35d8d2f4e16f0a946512df070f90673d9afa7464ea1a0e287b1c395616f15d6d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:uadelgd of arXiv:2606.02472, 'Correlated uniform attachment trees'] Let $T_n$ be a vertex-labeled $\mathrm{UA}$ tree and fix parameters $K\in \N$, $\delta\in (0,1)$, and $c_1\geq 1$. Then there exists a universal constant $C^\star < \infty$ such that for all large enough $n \geq n(K,\delta,c_1)$, we have
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{P} \left(  T_n  \text{ is $(K,\delta,c_1)$-good} \right) \geq 1- C^\star\left(\frac{   c_1 \log(c_1 K) }{\delta ^2 K}+(0.7)^{\delta K}\right).
    \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:50:30.566307+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_35db3ce28ff8a966013f2bbd42377f8ee657b8cc03404b0907ededb8aa1eaf1a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 24 of arXiv:2605.30616, 'Brownian motion: the hyperbolic number setting'] The stochastic process on the probability space
$(\Omega,\mathcal{C},P)$ where $P$ is $\mathbb{H}$-valued is called
a $\mathbb{H}$-valued Brownian motion if it is Gaussian and if 
\begin{equation}
E_{\mathbb H}(B_{p}B_{q})=p\wedge q,\quad p,q\in\mathbb{H}_{+}
\end{equation}
and more generally, using \eqref{p-q} 
\begin{equation}
E_{\mathbb H}(B_{p}B_{q})=\frac{1}{2}\left(|p|_{\mathbb{H}}+|q|_{\mathbb{H}}-|p-q|_{\mathbb{H}}\right),\quad p,q\in\mathbb{H}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:42:47.764938+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_35dd282645842e34b78fb34600393ae541454a2eef00707d9bdd1a24d2f9cab4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 6 ($L^2$ control) of arXiv:2604.12797, 'A free boundary problem for the mean-field limit of diffusing particles with nonlinear bou'] For any $T>0$, there exists $C>0$ such that
