{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:54:18.238330+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3626eef31386098069479e64796e522925e969e0cee4064e2085d4bdba7d66b4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:self_scaling_convergence of arXiv:2608.29448, '\method: Self-Scaled Adaptive Preconditioning for Physics-Informed Learning'] Let $\mathcal{H}_k \in \mathbb{S}_{++}^d$ be the true Hessian at step $k$, and let $\mathbf{K}_k = \mathbf{L}_k^{-1} \otimes \mathbf{R}_k^{-1}$ be the unscaled Kronecker-factored approximation of the inverse Hessian. Let $\mathbf{s}_k = \mathbf{W}_k - \mathbf{W}_{k-1}$ be the displacement vector and $\mathbf{y}_k = \nabla \mathcal{L}(\mathbf{W}_k) - \nabla \mathcal{L}(\mathbf{W}_{k-1})$ be the change in gradients.

The scaling factor $\tau_k$ defined in (equation eq:tau_k of the paper) minimizes the spectral disparity between the secant approximation and the preconditioner along the search direction. It is the unique solution to the trace-weighted spectral matching problem
\begin{equation}
    \tau_k = \arg\min_{\tau > 0} \left| \ln \left( \frac{\mathbf{s}_k^\top \mathbf{y}_k}{\mathbf{s}_k^\top (\tau \mathbf{K}_k)^{-1} \mathbf{s}_k} \right) \right|_{\mathbf{K}_k},
\end{equation}
assuming the approximation $\mathbf{y}_k \approx \mathcal{H}_k \mathbf{s}_k$. Furthermore, this choice centers the local Rayleigh quotient of the preconditioned system $\mathbf{P}_k \mathcal{H}_k$ around unity, where $\mathbf{P}_k = \tau_k^{-1} \mathbf{K}_k$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

