{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:47:40.720419+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_370f6cd802d2a77774b5d02d513749d300acbdf4181c5bb599398ef24393cf1e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma limit_rays of arXiv:2604.17170, 'CaTherine wheels from trees and Liouville quantum gravity'] Suppose $Z$ is a half-zipper with short hair. Fix $q\in Z$ and let $p_i \in Z$
be any sequence of points, and let $\eta_i\subset Z$ be the unique embedded path 
in $Z$ from $q$ to $p_i$.  Suppose $p_i \to p \in S^2$. 
Then after passing to a subsequence if necessary we may arrange that
\begin{enumerate}
\item{for any fixed $n$ the intersections $\eta_i \cap T_n$ converge as
subsets to a limit $\sigma_n \subset T_n$;}
\item{the $\sigma_n$ are an increasing nested sequence of (possibly degenerate) paths; and}
\item{the union $\sigma:=\bigcup_n \sigma_n$ is either a ray which lands at $p$ or
the unique path in $Z$ from $q$ to $p$ (which can only happen if $p\in Z$).}
\end{enumerate}
Consequently, any half-zipper with short hair has the strong landing property.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:47:07.705182+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3710ae00dd689efea52773133f0192b706eaed8313ad269e2d4537fa9d185077","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 19 of arXiv:2608.06038, 'Efron type identities\\ for stopping sets and Poisson hulls'] Let $B\in\cX$ satisfy $\lambda(B)<\infty$ and let $t\in\R$.  Then
