{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:15.493702+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_375462cf0d3553c252b538b8d2899a63bfffe0b4d6a72b1c09c615181c385ed3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 15 (Spectral decomposition of Liouville heat kernel) of arXiv:2512.02538, 'On the spectral geometry of Liouville quantum gravity'] For all $t\ge 0$, all $x \in \Sigma$, almost surely and for $\mu_\ph-$almost every $y \in \Sigma$ we have
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{p}_t (x,y) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty e^{-\bl_n t} \ef_n(x) \ef_n(y). 
\end{equation}
The series on the right hand side converges a.s. uniformly on $(\eps, \infty) \times \Sigma \times \Sigma$ for any $\eps>0$. As a result, there is a (necessarily unique) \emph{jointly continuous} version of the Liouville heat kernel $\mathbf{p}_t(x,y)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.331147+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3754a66286aa9af8fe8f7b2292168a4c5f2730f9f2d93dc35821e3f8458320f0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 15 of arXiv:2506.22020, 'Norm-dependent Lamperti-type MAP representations of stable processes and Brownian motions '] Let $(\rho,\Theta)$ be the underlying MAP of $\mathcal{R}$ from (\ref{ssMp_item_d_specific}) with respect to the norm  $\|\cdot\|_1$. Then, the operator $A:\mathcal{D}\to C^2_b(\mathbb{R}\times\mathcal{S}^{d,+}_{1})$, where $\mathcal{D}$ is the collection of functions from (\ref{def:8zillion}), is given by
	\begin{align*}
		Af(x,\boldsymbol{\theta})&= \sum_{i=0}^d b_i(\boldsymbol{\theta})\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_i}(x,\boldsymbol{\theta}) + \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,j=0}^d a_{ij}(\boldsymbol{\theta})\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y_i\partial y_j}(x,\boldsymbol{\theta}),
	\end{align*}
	where for $\boldsymbol{\theta}=(\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_d)\in\mathcal{S}^{d,+}_{1}$ and  $1\leq i\leq d$,
	\begin{equation*}
		b_0(\boldsymbol{\theta})=-\frac{d}{2},\quad b_i(\boldsymbol{\theta})=d\theta_i-1,\quad a_{00}(\boldsymbol{\theta})= d,\quad a_{ii}(\boldsymbol{\theta})=(1-\theta_i)^2+(d-1)\theta_i^2,\quad a_{0i}(\boldsymbol{\theta})=a_{i0}(\boldsymbol{\theta})= 1-d\theta_i,
	\end{equation*}

	\begin{equation*}
		a_{ij}(\boldsymbol{\theta})=a_{ji}(\boldsymbol{\theta})= d\theta_i\theta_j-\theta_i-\theta_j,\qquad i,j\in\{1,\ldots,d\},\enspace i\neq j,
	\end{equation*}
solves the martingale problem for $(\rho,\Theta)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:48:17.116391+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37550183fd71ec6a79d99d0668f18ca5d6b4e2edfb0f20830b45a8c0c2ba0d35","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 138 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] Adopt the notation and assumptions of \Cref{cor:cella2}, and denote
