Assume \((x_0,y_0)\in\Omega_+\). Then \(q_p'(z_0)\in(0,2)\). Consequently, if \(\Phi_p'(z_0)<0\), then \((x_0,y_0)\) is asymptotically stable and \(\lambda_+(x_0,y_0)<0\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.845011+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37e06deef9db1a22075a0a7178b87ba9550e68ea8134e08fc13953664a503f87","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 (Derivative of entropy dissipation) of arXiv:2609.03847, 'A counterexample to McKean's conjecture for the Landau-Coulomb equation'] Let $f$ be a classical solution of the Landau-Coulomb equation~\eqref{eq:Landau_Coulomb}. Then
	\begin{equation}
		\frac{\d}{\d t}D_H(f)
		=\int_{\R^3}Q(f,f)\,\delta_{f}\d v	-2\int_{\R^3}\frac{Q(f,f)^2}{f}\d v	,
	\end{equation}
where $\delta_f$ is given by
\begin{align}
	 \delta_f(v) \coloneqq 	\int_{\R^3} 	f_* \frac{	\left| 	\Pi_{v-v_*} ^\perp	\bigl(\nabla\log f-\nabla_*\log f_*\bigr)\right|^2}{|v-v_*|} 	\d v_*.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:08.852348+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37e10fc89c5d9098c8eeb1097528e7a206f604ebd1588d729b569ae826f8016a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 11 (Master equation for the eigenvectors) of arXiv:2511.12623, 'The eigenvector bead process'] Assume the spectrum $\{\lambda^{(N)}_j\}_{j=1}^N$ is simple.
