{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:28.041307+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3881b32eb4e6bbcad867ad6da8f6a51f3cc0d324fe6be8534ccdbb64f3e2a6ce","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 1 of arXiv:2608.12056, 'Deviation inequalities for U-processes of growing order, with applications to half-space d'] Let $\mathcal H_m$ be a Euclidean class with exponent $V$. Then for any $p \geq 2$ and $1 \leq j \leq m$,   
    \begin{align*}
    \left(\mathbb E \sup_{h \in \mathcal{H}_m} \lvert V_{N,j}(h) \rvert^p \right)^{1/p}  \leq C^j\max\left\{B_1(p,j), B_2(p,j)\right\} 
\end{align*}
where 
    \begin{align*}
    B_1(p,j) &= p^{j/2}\left(\frac{m}{j}\right)^{j/2} D(p,j)\,,
    \\
    B_2(p,j) &= 
    \left(C_1V \left[j\log\left(\frac{C_2m}{j}\right) \bigvee \log\left( \frac{e}{\Sigma_{\mathcal H_m}}\right)\right]\right)^{j/2} D(p,j).
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, if $p\geq 4V$, then $B_2(p,j)$ can be chosen as
    \[
    B_2(p,j) = ( pj)^{j/2} e^{-pj/V}.
    \] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

