{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:44:17.444329+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_39365c08e1b2ca0ddbc12fafa5595806c6a196d7f5cab9c40a16ff75fb9f4adf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition thm:observable-distance-eb-rate of arXiv:2608.25551, 'Beyond Optimal Rates in Stochastic Optimization: Trajectory-Adaptive Stopping Rules'] Fix $\alpha \in (0,1)$ and suppose the conditions of Theorem~(thm:observable-distance-eb of the paper) hold. Let $R_0 = G^2/\mu^2$, $\eta_t = 1/(\mu t)$, and $t_0 = 3$. Then there exist universal constants $C, C_{\mathrm r} < \infty$ such that, almost surely,
