{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:21:06.867401+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_397940af9e7cad781cd5c0d8ac075643987126d466fb2fd5f0566d0cdf26f951","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 of arXiv:2608.21255, 'Laws of the iterated logarithm for random Dirichlet series with general weights'] a) Assume that $\eta$ has a normal distribution with mean $0$ and variance $\sigma^2\in (0,\infty)$ and set $s_n=2^{-1}\eee^{-n^2}$ for $n\in\mn$. Then there exists a sequence $(a_k)$ satisfying \eqref{eq:standing} for which 
    \begin{equation}
        \limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac{X(s_n)}{(2v(s_n)\log\log v(s_n))^{1/2}}=\frac{1}{2^{1/2}}\quad\text{{\rm a.s.}}
    \end{equation}
    and
    \begin{equation}
        \liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{X(s_n)}{(2v(s_n)\log\log v(s_n))^{1/2}}=-\frac{1}{2^{1/2}}\quad\text{{\rm a.s.}}
    \end{equation}
b) Assume that $\mmp\{\eta=\pm \sigma\}=1/2$. Then, with the same sequence $(a_k)$ and the same $s_n$ as in part (a), 
    \begin{equation}
        \limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac{X(s_n)}{(v(s_n))^{1/2}}=1\quad\text{{\rm a.s.}}
    \end{equation}
    and
    \begin{equation}
        \liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{X(s_n)}{(v(s_n))^{1/2}}=-1\quad\text{{\rm a.s.}}
    \end{equation}
c) Assume that $\eta=\xi-\sigma$, where $\xi$ has an exponential distribution of mean $\sigma$. Then, with the same sequence $(a_k)$ and the same $s_n$ as in part (a), 
\begin{equation}
\limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac{X(s_n)}{(v(s_n))^{1/2}\log\log v(s_n)}=\frac{1}{2}\quad\text{{\rm a.s.}}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{X(s_n)}{(v(s_n))^{1/2}}=-1\quad\text{{\rm a.s.}}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.658737+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_397b48bf82cdc9711a039a39214026c6b19747fe963e61fa8fba494eb80c1eb2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2604.23461, 'Scaling limit of Sinkhorn-rescaled Random Matrices \\ via Stability of Static Schrödinger '] Fix $\phi \in L^{\infty}_{>0}(\mu_0 \otimes \mu_1)$. Also, let $(\xi, \eta) \in L^{\infty}(\mu_0) \times \overline{L}^{\infty}(\mu_1)$. Then $F$ is continuously (Frechet) differentiable at $(\phi, \xi, \eta)$. Furthermore, let $\partial_1$ be the partial derivative in the first coordinate and $\partial_{2}$ be the partial derivative with respect to $F$'s second two coordinates. The derivatives are the linear operators
	\begin{align}

		\partial_1F(\phi, \xi, \eta)\psi = \left( \int_{\Omega_{\c}} \psi(\cdot, y)e^{\xi(\cdot) + \eta(y)}d\mu_1(y), \int_{\Omega_{\r}}\psi(x, \cdot) e^{\xi(x) + \eta(\cdot)}d\mu_0(x) \right). 
	\end{align}
	and for $h = (h_0, h_1) \in L^{\infty}(\mu_0) \times \overline{L}^{\infty}(\mu_1)$, 
	\begin{multline}

		\partial_{2}F(\phi, \xi, \eta)h\\
		= \left(\int_{\Omega_c}\phi(\cdot, y)e^{\xi(\cdot) + \eta(y)}(h_{0}(\cdot) + h_{1}(y))d\mu_1(y), \int_{\Omega_{\r}}\phi(x, \cdot) e^{\xi(x) + \eta(\cdot)}(h_{0}(x) + h_{1}(\cdot))d\mu_0(x) \right).
	\end{multline}
	Furthermore, $\partial_2F(\phi, \xi, \eta)$ is an isomorphism from $L^{\infty}(\mu_0) \times \overline{L}^{\infty}(\mu_1)$ to $\mathcal{E}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:25:49.879865+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_397d0292001bb28199ee87d14c949139868b4de2f2d53a7f79a32058391fc724","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2609.02065, '\textbf{From Bowditch's question to Goldman's conjecture for type-preserving representatio'] \cite[Lemma 1.4]{goldman}
        Let $X, Y$ be smooth manifolds, and let $f: X\to Y$ be a smooth map. Suppose that 
        (a) $f$ is a submersion, and 
        (b) $f^{-1}(y)$ is path-connected for every $y\in Y$.
        Then $f$ satisfies the strong path-lifting property. Furthermore, 
        if $Y$ is path-connected, then $X$ is path-connected. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

