{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:19:06.753762+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_39a29ad52bef561955c980c75ad667df860c7700eb9b96b57e3bec6aebd03f87","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:raw-prefix-postbin-arrays-paper of arXiv:2606.08683, 'Exact Fourier dimensions of dyadic Mandelbrot cascades under minimal integrability'] Let \(d\in\mathfrak D_\xi\), and set \(m=m_{\xi,d}\).  Define 
\[F^{\mathrm{pre}}_{\xi,d}
=
\sum_{\ell=0}^{m-1}
\sum_{I\in\mathcal D_\ell^\circ}
\sum_{\substack{J\in\mathcal D_{\ell+1}^\circ\\ J\subset I}}
c_J(\xi,d,\ell)M_I(U_J-1).\]
For \(L>m\), define 
\[F^{\mathrm{post}}_{\xi,d,L}
=
\sum_{\ell=m}^{L-1}
\sum_{I\in\mathcal D_\ell^\circ}
\sum_{\substack{J\in\mathcal D_{\ell+1}^\circ\\ J\subset I}}
c_J(\xi,d,\ell)M_I(U_J-1).\]
Whenever the limit exists, set \(F^{\mathrm{post}}_{\xi,d}=\lim_{L\to\infty}F^{\mathrm{post}}_{\xi,d,L}\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.604497+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_39a701e45030945f449e84944fd0c751c80810baaef7a619aa4c205604a439e5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 (Sharp anisotropic Hermite root rate) of arXiv:2607.07712, 'Parameter-Space Heat Flow, Gaussian Density Ratios, and Sharp Hermite Truncation Rates'] Assume \(S=S^T\), \(I+S>0\), and
\[
0<\|S\|_{\mathrm{op}}<1.
\]
Then
\begin{equation}
\limsup_{m\to\infty}
a_m(u,S)^{1/m}
=
\|S\|_{\mathrm{op}}.

\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
\limsup_{m\to\infty}
\|\Pi_mK_{u,S}\|_{L^2(\gamma_d)}^{1/m}
=
\|S\|_{\mathrm{op}}^{1/2}.

\end{equation}

Let
\[
R_M^{\mathrm{sp}}(u,S)
:=
K_{u,S}-\Pi_{\le M}K_{u,S}
=
\sum_{m=M+1}^\infty \Pi_mK_{u,S}.
\]
Then
\begin{equation}
\limsup_{M\to\infty}
\|R_M^{\mathrm{sp}}(u,S)\|_{L^2(\gamma_d)}^{1/M}
=
\|S\|_{\mathrm{op}}^{1/2}.

\end{equation}
In particular, for every
\[
\|S\|_{\mathrm{op}}^{1/2}<\rho<1,
\]
there exists \(C=C(u,S,\rho)>0\) such that
\begin{equation}
\|R_M^{\mathrm{sp}}(u,S)\|_{L^2(\gamma_d)}
\le
C\rho^M,
\qquad M\ge0.

\end{equation}
The rate \(\|S\|_{\mathrm{op}}^{1/2}\) is optimal in the root-test sense.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.779445+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_39a9a08d0304c6075cb3ea4e97beadea92b665ef5f7100f5c37a555aeb3df16b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition of arXiv:2411.09257, 'Iterated Generalized Counting Process and its Extensions'] For $1\le i\le q$ and $1\le l\le q$, the covariance of $\{M_i(M_0(t))\}_{t\ge0}$ and $\{M_l(M_0(t))\}_{t\ge0}$ is given by
