uniformly over all $cF_{t^A_n}$-measurable random variables $\xi_n$ with ${\rm L}^\infty$-norm at most $1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:54:57.474622+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3a18ba8bf4c7092260ba6f761083fae803c0ffa28462504ee32886a055e2e628","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem : Topology/GMT/Rademacher of arXiv:2412.03320, 'Large deviation principle at speed $n$ for the random metric in first-passage percolation'] Let $\gamma : \intervalleff0T \rightarrow X$ be a Lipschitz path.
	\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
		\item For almost every $t\in\intervalleff0T$, $\gamma$ is differentiable at $t$, and for all $0\le t_1 \le t_2 \le T$,
		\begin{equation}

			\gamma(t_2) - \gamma(t_1) = \int_{t_1}^{t_2} \gamma'(t)\mathrm{d} t.
		\end{equation}
		\item For all $D\in \RegPD[\mu]$, for almost every $t\in \intervalleff0T$, the limit
		\begin{equation}

			\MD{\gamma}(t) \coloneqq \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{ D\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft( \gamma(t) , \gamma(t+h) \aftergroup\egroup\originalright) }{\module h}
		\end{equation}
		exists, and for all $0\le t_1 \le t_2 \le T$,
		\begin{equation}

			D\p{\restriction{\gamma}{\intervalleff{t_1}{t_2}}} = \int_{t_1}^{t_2} \MD{\gamma}(t)\mathrm{d} t.
		\end{equation}
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

