{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:30:36.756344+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3aaf592b55bf2aea73d3efe36755a668dca8c4c960d7b4ead22b61454f3b58ee","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 13 of arXiv:2607.09246, 'Scaling limit of 1+1 dimensional directed polymer with power-law tail and spatial correlat'] Assume that conditions \textbf{(A)} and \textbf{(B)} hold. Let the tail exponent satisfy $\alpha > \frac{6}{3-2r}$. If the inverse temperature sequence $\beta_N$ satisfies the thermodynamic scaling $\beta_N N^{H/2} \to \beta \in [0, \infty)$ as $N \to \infty$. Then the modified partition function $\mathfrak{Z}_N(\beta_N; \bar{\omega})$ converges in distribution to the continuous Wiener chaos $Z_{\sqrt{2}\beta}(1, \cdot)$ given by:
\begin{equation}
Z_{\sqrt{2}\beta}(1, \cdot) = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} (\sqrt{2}\beta)^{k} \int_{\Delta_{k}(1)} \int_{\mathbb{R}^k} \tilde{\rho}_{k}(\mathbf{t}, \mathbf{x}) W(\mathrm{d}\mathbf{t}, \mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}).
\end{equation}
Here, the notations
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\Delta_k(1) = \{0 < t_1 < \dots < t_k \le 1\}$ denotes the standard $k$-dimensional time simplex;
    \item $\tilde{\rho}_{k}(\mathbf{t}, \mathbf{x})$ is the symmetrized Gaussian heat kernel introduced in Lemma \ref{lem:3.2};
    \item $W(\mathrm{d}\mathbf{t}, \mathrm{d}\mathbf{x})$ is a centered Gaussian space-time white noise, whose spatial covariance structure is  the fractional kernel $K(z) = H(2H-1)|z|^{2H-2}$ defined in Section 1.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.324492+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ab06b73cf92cfe92ded28dc5064e62ab61de7dd28f22f92e41a313255f8ab24","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] A map $f\colon X\to Y$ is called a \emph{$G$-equivariant fibration} if it is a $G$-equivariant level fibration and for all closed subgroups $H\subset G$ and $H$-representations $V,W$, the naturality square
    \[
        \begin{tikzcd}
            X(V)\arrow[r,"\tilde\sigma"]\arrow[d,"f(V)"'] & \Omega^WX(V\oplus W)\arrow[d,"\Omega^Wf(V\oplus W)"]\\
            Y(V)\arrow[r,"\tilde\sigma"'] & \Omega^WY(V\oplus W)
        \end{tikzcd}
    \]
    for the adjunct structure maps is a homotopy pullback in $\textbf{\textup{$\bm H$-Top}}_*$.

    An orthogonal $G$-spectrum $X$ is called a \emph{$G$-$\Omega$-spectrum} if the unique map $X\to 0$ is a $G$-equivariant fibration, i.e.\ the adjunct structure map $X(V)\to \Omega^WX(V\oplus W)$ is an $H$-equivariant weak equivalence for each closed subgroup $H\subset G$ and all $H$-representations $V,W$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:17.371078+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ab3b842df9d9cdc546b51ab48295d017ff315b6bd6ca22a2f0b2c543cdaee55","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 11 of arXiv:2606.03661, '2606.03661'] For the Laplace transform $\mathscr{L}_t[t^\mu \Psi_\alpha^\nu(-\kappa t);z]$ 
