{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.801651+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3c78d0d702f8d1d8e3234b8b56bb0e73ac4afcd99ec6871dcbb439c35c9393a0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:black-noise of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\{\mathcal{F}_R\}_{R\in\mathcal{R}}, \mathbb{P},\{\theta_h\}_{h\in\mathbb{R}^d})$ be a $d$-dimensional noise.
    A random variable $X\in L^2(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$ is called \emph{linear} if 
    for all $R_1,R_2,R_3\in\mathcal{R}$ with $R_1\cap R_2=\varnothing$ and $\overline{R_1\cup R_2}=\overline{R_3}$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[X|\mathcal{F}_{R_3}] = \mathbb{E}[X|\mathcal{F}_{R_1}]+\mathbb{E}[X|\mathcal{F}_{R_2}].
    \end{align*}
    The noise is \emph{black} if the only linear random variable is zero.
    Note that a noise being black depends on the $\sigma$-algebras $\{\mathcal{F}_R\}_{R\in\mathcal{R}}$ only up to $\mathbb{P}$-null sets.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.487372+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3c813b5b3cd164e441f79edf8aa38dc0baa136a44cbd8722840039fa474e0088","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem characterization-of-interpolation-spaces of arXiv:2601.19145, 'Stochastic Persistence in Infinite Dimensions'] Let $a = 1$ if the boundary conditions in (sass-A of the paper) are Neumann, and $a = 0$ otherwise. Then for $1 < p < \infty$:
