{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:22:08.454329+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d30478a047e555ea506cfc75406a391b501ef75f16be163d207ab5dbbdd9416","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2607.29020, 'Entropic Symmetrization Resistance'] \label[lemma]{lem:support_size_one_or_two}
All $f \in \Sym$ satisfy $|\supp(f)| \ge 2$, 
and if $|\supp(f)| = 2$ then $f = f_{-X}$.  

\begin{proof}
Taking $Y \sim f$, the first assertion is clear: $Y$ a constant would force $Y = 0$ (as the support of $X+Y$ must be symmetric), 

contradicting asymmetry of $X$. 

Now consider $\supp(f) = \{y_1, y_2\}$ with $y_1 < y_2$.
By symmetry of $X+Y$, $(1-p)f(y_1) = pf(y_2)
= p(1-f(y_1))$; hence $f(y_1)=p$. 

\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

