{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.775726+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3e2483d80d2a2b55d3ffc29bdbfd561757068e53c9cdff51038adee75f2a3575","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 (Moments of Gaussian random variables) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] Let $X\sim\cN(0,1)$. 
For any $n\in\N$, one has 
\begin{equation}
 \expec{X^n} = 
 \begin{cases}
  (n-1)!! & \text{if $n$ is even\;,} \\
  0 & \text{if $n$ is odd\;,}
 \end{cases}

\end{equation} 
where
\begin{equation}
 (n-1)!! = \prod_{k=0}^{n/2-1} (2k+1)
 = 1\cdot3\cdot5 \dots (n-3)(n-1)
\end{equation} 
is the double factorial. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:51:11.500406+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3e24d332e3ae62fd761a9d8163b493e36de3707d8abbee4dad34429154a84b87","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lm 6 of arXiv:2609.02576, 'Sharp estimates for the Jacobi and trigonometric Dunkl heat kernels'] If $f\in \mathcal{C}^2((0,\infty))$, then
