    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.301380+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4313134e151945e67dd1e8d9eb727bf4e622d08446be427d707228c2ba1e1e9f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:resultunknownmask of arXiv:2602.11129, 'Information-Theoretic Thresholds for Bipartite Latent-Space Graphs Under Noisy Observation'] Consider any fixed $p \in (0,1)$. Then, the following holds whenever $d \gg \log(n)^3$.

    \vspace{0.5em}
    If $p \neq \frac{1}{2}$ then:\hspace{1em} $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \dtv{\mathbb{W}(n, m, q, p, d)}{ \mathbb{M}(n, m, p)} &= \begin{cases} 1 - o(1) & \text{if } d \ll nmq^4  \hspace{1.3cm} \text{ or } \hspace{.1cm} d \ll m\sqrt{n}q^2   \\
            o(1) & \text{if } d \gg nmq^4\log(n) \hspace{.1cm} \text{ and } \hspace{.1cm} d \gg m\sqrt{n}q^2 \log(n)
            \end{cases}
        \end{aligned}$

    \vspace{0.5em}
    If $p = \frac{1}{2}$ then:\hspace{1em} $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \dtv{\mathbb{W}(n, m, q, p, d)}{ \mathbb{M}(n, m, p)} &= \begin{cases} 1 - o(1) & \text{if } d \ll nmq^4 \\
            o(1) & \text{if } d \gg nmq^4\log(n)
            \end{cases}
        \end{aligned}$

    \vspace{0.5em}
    \noindent Here, $o(1)$ denotes a function that tends to zero as $n \rightarrow \infty$. Recall further that $m \ge n$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:32:23.663555+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4314d1ef72f62599054fd9583f08ace5d8415bb57aaf1208c9815d16734e9615","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lambda close then also w close of arXiv:2603.17858, 'Decay of correlations and zeros for the hard-core model'] Given $\lambda >0$, $C>0$ and $\alpha\in(0,1)$ there exists $C'>0$ such that the following holds. Given sequences $(\lambda_n)$ and $(\widehat\lambda_n)$ in $(0,\lambda]$ satisfying $|\lambda_n-\widehat\lambda_n|<C\alpha^n$ for all $n\in \mathbb N$, the associated orbits $(w_n)$  and $(\widehat w_n)$ satisfy
