{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:56:11.142932+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_431922dc09ad7fcb2d59a1eac7c47121516be62f6936c11cba116b91ba77bb47","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 (Scalar Cauchy information-estimation identity) of arXiv:2608.11031, 'A Bayesian Proof of the Bernoulli Theorem'] Let $U$ be a finitely supported real random variable, let $S$ be an arbitrarily distributed random variable, and let $Z$ be standard Cauchy and independent of
$(U,S)$.  Define
\begin{equation}
  F(t):=I(U;U+tZ\mid S).

\end{equation}
Then $F$ is decreasing and convex on $(0,\infty)$, with
$\lim_{t\to\infty}F(t)=0$.
Moreover, let $U_t^{(1)},U_t^{(2)}$ be i.i.d.\ draws from the
posterior of $U$ given $(S,U+tZ)$.  Then
\begin{equation}
  F''(t)
  \le
  \frac{2}{t^2}
  \E\left[
    1\wedge
    \frac{|U_t^{(1)}-U_t^{(2)}|^2}{t^2}
  \right].

\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation}
  -F'(t)
  \le
  2\int_t^\infty
  \frac{1}{s^2}
  \E\left[
    1\wedge
    \frac{|U_s^{(1)}-U_s^{(2)}|^2}{s^2}
  \right]ds.

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.812786+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_431c31794a23af5185bee2a34120a3668e6f160620d2a76c2e6d7aa40e5ec4a7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemm l:BM-maximum-drift of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] Let $W$ be a Brownian motion of variance $4$ started from $W(0)=0$.
    Then for all $T>0$, all $\kappa>0$, and all $\mu\in\mathbb{R}$,
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{P}\left(
            \max_{z\in[0,T]}\left(W(z) + \mu z\right) \leqslant \kappa
        \right)
        \leqslant C\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{T}} + |\mu|\right)\kappa,
    \end{align*}    
    where $C>0$ is a universal constant.","status":"unverified"}

