\mathcal{H}_k &:= \frac{H(k)}{2 L_\star k}, & &\text{(normalized spectral entropy)}
\end{align*}
where $H(k) = -\sum_{i\ge2} p_i(k)\log p_i(k)$ is the spectral entropy of (sec:thermodynamics of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:37:08.954597+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_452d18b8ee4cbe71c4b5bc2f486c843e1a3794bd0371ebf0b057aae4c645f9b8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:conc_current of arXiv:2609.01581, 'Concentration of additive functionals of Stratonovich-type'] Let $(X_t)_{t \geq 0}$ be an ergodic diffusion with invariant measure $\mu$, let
$\overline{J}_t(U)$ be the empirical current in the sense of Definition~(def:empirical_current of the paper) with 
ergodic mean $\E^\mu[\overline{J}_t] = \int j_{\textrm{s}} \cdot U \mathrm{d} x$, and let $\nu \ll \mu$
with $\mathrm{d}\nu/\mathrm{d}\mu \in L^2(\mu)$. Then for all $t > 0$ and $a > 0$,
\begin{align}
  \mathbb{P}^\nu\left(\overline{J}_t(U) - \E^\mu[\overline{J}_t] \geq a\right)
  \leq
  \left\|\frac{\mathrm{d}\nu}{\mathrm{d}\mu}\right\|_{L^2(\mu)}
  \exp\bigl[-tI^J\bigl(\E^\mu[\overline{J}_t] + a\bigr)\bigr],

\end{align}
where the Cram\'er transform
\begin{align}
  I^J(s) \equiv \sup_{k \in \mathbb{R}}\bigl[k s - \Lambda^J(k)\bigr],

\end{align}
is defined as the Legendre conjugate of the principal eigenvalue $\Lambda^J(k)$~(equation eq:Lambda_J_dirichlet of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:52:08.658564+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_452e41eff32a1b925e65ba2eaa9f897fdc15ed3bb6f7f553c338e6328acca4e5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2608.29984, 'On Cauchy Problems for Parabolic Equations with Rough Coefficients'] For any $ 0<s<\frac{1}{2}$, there exists a universal constant $\delta_{0}$ only depends on $c_{0}$ and $s$, such that: under the assumptions (H1), (H2), $(H3)_{\delta_{0},s}$, for any initial data $u_{0}(x)$ with $u_{0}(x)\in H^{s}(\R)$ and force term $f(t,x)\in L^{2}([0,T];H^{s-1}(\R))$, the divergence form equation \eqref{heateqdiv} exists a unique weak solution $u(t,x)$, which satisfies
