{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:57:16.076819+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4656c8f5aa4860e4e69d3dfcd21bf84410bf189b5cbf1427a358ff3fc95c5b18","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo full_dist-D of arXiv:2507.00737, 'Dispersion models on a circle: universal properties and asymptotic results'] Consider some masses $m_0,\cdots,m_{k-1}$, non-negative multiple of $1/n$, such that $W(m[k])<1$.
	For any $b \in\{1,\cdots,k\}$, any partition $(J_0,\cdots,J_{b-1})$ of the set $\{0,\cdots,k-1\}$ with non-empty parts, any sequence $(a_0,a_1,\cdots,a_{b-1})$ in ${\cal C}_n^b$ such that the $a_0,a_1,\cdots,a_{b-1}$ are cyclically ordered around ${\cal C}_n$ (i.e. such that turning around the circle we get $a_0\preceq 0\preceq a_1\preceq \cdots \preceq a_{b-1}\preceq a_0 \preceq 0 \cdots$) and such that
	\[ \Leb\left(\fleche{[a_i,a_{i+1 {~\sf mod~} b}]}\right)> W(m(J_i))~~\textrm{ for }i\in\{0,\cdots,b-1\}.\]
	For a valid DDM\xspace,  we have
	\begin{equation}
	\mathbb{P}\left( \bA{k}_j= a_j, \bI{k}_j =J_j,0\leq j \leq b-1\right)=  \prod_{j=0}^{b-1} \frac{Q^{(n)}(m(J_j))}n.
	\end{equation}
	Moreover, if we consider some non-negative masses $M_0,\cdots,M_{b-1}$,
	\begin{equation}
	\mathbb{P}\left( \bA{k}_j = a_j,  \left|\textbf{O}^{(k)}_j\right|=M_j, 0\leq j \leq b-1\right)= \sum_{(J_0,\cdots,J_{b-1}) \in P(k,b)} \prod_{j=0}^{b-1} \frac{Q^{(n)}(m(J_j))}n \mathds{1}_{W(m(J_j)) = M_j}
	\end{equation}
	where, as in Theorem (theo:dqgsrt of the paper), $P(k,b)$ is the set of partitions of $\{0,\ldots, k-1\}$ into $b$ parts. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:06:21.713187+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_465b64ec4c977ae648880fcb5b09df14bd760c7ba9d07e3734ac1cfa11171d31","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 9 of arXiv:2607.21374, 'Local structure at the maximum and sharp persistence asymptotics of rough fractional Brown'] For the grids of Proposition~\ref{prop:dyadic-grid-diagonal}, define
\begin{equation}
  \Lambda_{n,k}:=\{h_n(j-k):0\leq j\leq M_n\},
  \qquad 0\leq k\leq M_n,

\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
  Q_{n,k}:=\mathcal L\left(
    B\,\middle|\, B_t\leq0\text{ for every }t\in\Lambda_{n,k}
  \right),
  \qquad
  p_{n,k}:=\PP(B_t\leq0:t\in\Lambda_{n,k}).

\end{equation}
Then
\(p_{n,k}=\PP(K_n=k)>0\),
\(\sum_{k=0}^{M_n}p_{n,k}=1\), and
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal L(\Xi_n^{\mathrm{grid}})
  =\sum_{k=0}^{M_n}p_{n,k}Q_{n,k}.

\end{equation}
Write
\begin{equation}
  L_{n,k}^-=h_nk,
  \qquad L_{n,k}^+=h_n(M_n-k).

\end{equation}
For \(0<k<M_n\), the component \(Q_{n,k}\) is
\(Q_{h_n,L_{n,k}^-,L_{n,k}^+}\) from \eqref{eq:pure-wall-law}.  The endpoint
components \(k=0,M_n\) retain their direct conditional definitions in
\eqref{eq:mixture-components}; they are full-path laws constrained on only
one side of the pin and are not covered by the positive-two-horizon
definition \eqref{eq:pure-wall-law}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:56:37.669135+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_465dfe668d55d5b291312dbb1aa45833c618103dfc18013a966da73fc586b71e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem finite-n-uniform-renewal of arXiv:2609.00110, 'Phase Transition and Fluctuation Results for First-Passage Percolation on Spread-Out Cycle'] By enlarging $n_0$ if necessary,
