{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:06:42.721090+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_46742da3e147e1bba4bd94d1539e7327fabf19271b0fd1ec3b217af1a89f057d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi finite action of arXiv:2411.04157, 'Stochastic homogenization of dynamical discrete optimal transport'] Let $(\rho,j)\in \mathcal{M}((0,T)\times \mathbb{R}^n;[0,\infty)\times \mathbb{R}^n)$ be a finite Radon measure. We say that $(\rho,j)$ is a \emph{finite action curve} if
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item $(\rho,j)$ solve the continuity equation 
    \begin{align*}
\partial_t\rho+\dive j=0 \text{ in }\mathcal D'((0,T)\times \mathbb{R}^n).
\end{align*}
        \item $j\ll \rho$ and 
        \begin{align*}
            \int_{(0,T)\times \mathbb{R}^n}\left|\frac{dj}{d\rho}\right|^2\, d\rho<\infty;
        \end{align*}
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.844887+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_46779a12df6514950e371771fa6df68a697c531eff85250eab8fc78c400275d2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 2 (Failure of McKean's conjecture for grazing Boltzmann collisions) of arXiv:2609.03847, 'A counterexample to McKean's conjecture for the Landau-Coulomb equation'] Let $R>0$ be large enough and $f_R$ as in~\cref{thm:counter_example}, and $(Q_{B,\eps})$ be a grazing sequence of Boltzmann operators converging to the Landau--Coulomb operator. Then for $\eps>0$ small enough, the entropy dissipation increases at some positive time; there exists a sufficiently small $t^*_\eps$ such that 
