{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.800116+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_471858ffe5521162490831ce7637816a28104b920e204248b2e6022a80a501c4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2609.03070, 'Variants of order semicontinuity in Banach lattices'] Let $H\subset G\subset F$ be a chain of order dense sublattices and let $B\subset G$ be solid. The following sets are equal:
\item[(i)] The set of all $f\in F$ such that there is $Q\subset B_{+}$ with $Q\uparrow\left|f\right|$;
\item[(i')] The set of all $f\in F$ such that there is $Q\subset B_{+}\cap H$ with $Q\uparrow\left|f\right|$;
\item[(ii)] The order adherence of $B$ in $F$;
\item[(ii')] The order adherence of $B\cap H$ in $F$;
\item[(iii)] Order adherence of $\Sol B$ in $F$;
\item[(iii')] Order adherence of $\Sol\left(B\cap H\right)$ in $F$;
\item[(iv)] Sets similar to (ii)-(iii') with uo adherence replacing order adherence.\medskip

This set is solid and its intersection with $G$ and $H$ is the common order and uo adherence of $B$ in $G$ and of $B\cap H$ in $H$, respectively. The order and uo closures of $B$ and $B\cap H$ also coincide, and are a solid set. Both the adherence and the closure are convex, whenever $B$ is. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.313876+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4718a3a7082f30049f0355da6ea7fdbba6e9a83e6844e65430a26e547dc598f8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition pro:equivalence of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] Let $\widehat A $ be a closed set in $\mathbb R^3$ such that $\widehat A ^\circ=\varnothing$ and $U\setminus\widehat A $ is connected for any domain $U\subset\mathbb{R}^{3}$. Then, the following two statements are equivalent:

    \textnormal{(i)} For any rational point $x\in\mathbb R^3$ and any rational $r>0$, there exist positive constants $\delta_1$ and $\delta_2$ that only depend on $\widehat A , x$ and $r$ such that for all $A\subseteq\widehat A $ and $y\inB(x,r/2)\setminus A$,
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbf{P}_y\Big(W(\tau_{\partialB(x,r)})\notin\sausage{\widehat A}{\delta_1} \,\Big |\,  W[0,\tau_{\partialB(x,r)}]\cap A=\varnothing\Big)>\delta_2.
    \end{equation}

    \textnormal{(ii)} For each domain $U$ and compact set $K\subseteq U$, there exists a constant $C(U,K,\widehat A )$, such that for any closed subset $A$ of $\widehat A $, and any two bounded positive harmonic functions $u,v$ on $U\setminus A$ that vanish continuously on regular points of $A\cap U$, we have
    \[
    u(x_1)\,v(x_2)<C\,u(x_2)\,v(x_1),\quad \text{ for all } x_1,x_2\in K\setminus A.\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:59:53.321592+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4719a66921380940beeb94aab321d659660814cda002a405ac7488dd844c07e6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem-Poisson-dedicate of arXiv:2607.22324, 'The conclave process'] {}
    Suppose $\lambda=\omega(1)$, $X \sim \mathsf{Pois}(\lambda)$ and $|t|=o(\sqrt\lambda)$. 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(1)] If $\lambda + t\sqrt{\lambda} \in \mathbb Z$, then
        \begin{align*}
             \tfrac{1}{10\sqrt{\lambda}} \exp\Big(- \big( 1+O\big( \tfrac{|t|+1}{\sqrt \lambda} \big) \big) \tfrac{t^2}{2}\Big) \leq \mathbb P\left( X=\lambda+t\sqrt{\lambda} \right) \leq \tfrac{10}{\sqrt{\lambda}} \exp\Big(- \big( 1+O\big( \tfrac{|t|+1}{\sqrt \lambda} \big) \big) \tfrac{t^2}{2} \Big) \,.
        \end{align*} 
        \item[(2)] If $\omega(1)\leq s\leq t$ and $\lambda + s\sqrt{\lambda},\lambda + t\sqrt{\lambda} \in \mathbb Z$, then
        \begin{align*}
            \exp \left( \left(1-\tfrac{t}{\sqrt\lambda} \right) \tfrac{t^2-s^2}{2}\right) \leq \frac{\mathbb P(X=\lambda + s \sqrt{\lambda})}{\mathbb P(X=\lambda + t \sqrt{\lambda})} \leq \exp \left( \left(1+\tfrac{t}{\sqrt\lambda} \right) \tfrac{t^2-s^2}{2} \right) \,.
        \end{align*}
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.118410+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_471c163b0779788decbf57d9a7987297ba542ddd827b43dfa45a306dc35fd446","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:Intro:QuantShape of arXiv:2608.17031, 'A shape theorem for periodic branching diffusion'] Let $H_t$ be the convex hull of $\mathcal{X}_t$. Under the assumptions of Theorem (thm:Intro:ShapeHauss of the paper) on the $\mathbb{Z}^d$-periodic coefficients $b,\sigma$, the branching rate $g$, and the family of offspring distributions $\mathcal{M}$, there exists $\Tilde{\mathcal{W}}= \Tilde{\mathcal{W}}(b, \sigma, g, \mathcal{M})\subset \mathbb{R}^d$ with $0 \in \mathrm{int}(\Tilde{\mathcal{W}})$, and $\bar{\mathbf{v}} = \bar{\mathbf{v}}(b, \sigma, g, \mathcal{M})\in \mathbb{R}^d$ such that, for every $\epsilon \in (0,1)$, there exists $C=C(\epsilon)>0$, $\Gamma=\Gamma(\epsilon)>0$ and $T_0=T_0(\epsilon, d, g, \sigma, b, \mathcal{M})$ such that, for every $t>T_0$,
        \begin{equation}
            \inf_{x\in \mathbb{R}^d}\mathbf{P}_x[(1-\epsilon)\Tilde{\mathcal{W}}\subset t^{-1}(H_t-x-t\bar{\mathbf{v}}) \subset(1+\epsilon)\Tilde{\mathcal{W}}] \geq 1-Ce^{-t\Gamma(\epsilon)}.
        \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.226852+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_471ed5941f4dc43fd44729f3c594d4feb9566c2237949b2b5f074385b19a0d00","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def-fixed-point of arXiv:2608.23202, 'Locally finite Fixed points of Branching Brownian Motion'] A point process $\Pi$ is called a fixed point\footnote{Since this is a non-standard definition, we here and henceforth use the term \textit{fixed points} to avoid any confusion with invariant distributions.} of BBM with drift $\lambda$ if for all $f\in C_c^+(\mathbb{R})$ and for all $t\geq 0$,

    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{E}[e^{-\langle f, \theta_t^\lambda \rangle}] = \mathbb{E}[e^{-\langle f, \theta_0^\lambda \rangle}],
     \end{equation} 
    where $\theta_t^\lambda$ is given by (equation theta_t-def of the paper) with $\theta_0^\lambda = \Pi$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:43:16.154868+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4721ea2cfa54a7f5c07e1d8ac07c05315c29ea022ab9633471c7e3467e161653","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 18 of arXiv:2604.13819, 'Convolution, cumulants and infinitesimal generators in the formal power series ring'] Let $\mathfrak{q} = \{q_s\}_{s \ge 0} \subset \mathbb{R}[x]_d$ be a $\boxplus_d$-semigroup. 
Then the finite free infinitesimal generator of $\mathfrak{q}$ satisfies
\begin{align*}
