{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:48:27.282646+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_47bf9a82166ce43a3ed80a5a40f50845c89149b3cb6d9ed52b263d58f934e0b8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 of arXiv:2604.25336, 'Central limit theorem for slow-fast system under mixed fractional Brownian motion'] Let \(p\ge 2\) and \(\alpha\in(0,1/2)\) be such that \(1+\frac{1}{p}<\alpha+H\). There are exponents \(\eta>\frac{1}{2}\) and \(\bar \eta>1\) such that for \(h:\mathbb{R}^n\times \mathbb{R}^m\mapsto \mathbb{R}\) a bounded uniformly Lipschitz continuous function, one has the following:
\begin{itemize}
\item[1]. Suppose that \(\bar h(x)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^m}h(x,y)\mu(\dd y)=0\) for all \(x\in \mathbb{R}^n\). Then for any \(\beta<H\), there exists a constant \(\kappa>0\) such that 
\begin{equation}
\|\int_{s}^{t}h(x_r,y_r^\epsilon)\dd B_{r}^H\|_{p} \le C \epsilon^\kappa |h|_{BC^1}(|t-s|^\eta+\|x\|_{\alpha}^p(t-s)^{\bar \eta }).
\end{equation}
\item[2]. For general \(h\), one has the bound 
\begin{equation}
\|\int_{s}^{t}h(x_r,y_r^\epsilon)\dd B_{r}^H\|_{p} \le C |h|_{BC^1}(|t-s|^\eta+\|x\|_{\alpha}^p(t-s)^{\bar \eta }).
\end{equation}
where $C>0$ is a constant.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.127509+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_47c4222562911f129f646d925e365f2cec9d61d2b542fda88661840d565f3584","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def of arXiv:2411.15954, 'A gradient model for the Bernstein polynomial basis'] In what follows, fix $L\in\mathbb{N}_+$, let $0\leq j\leq L$ and $\eta\in\Omega_N$ be arbitrary:
