{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:54:57.452203+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_47e6d32e7303cd9aeb7daffeb99b20c5f9bafa1e581a163bf985c95ab9ec4603","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eqn : Intro/DefDLength of arXiv:2412.03320, 'Large deviation principle at speed $n$ for the random metric in first-passage percolation'] Given a subset $A$ of $\mathbb{R}^d$, a pseudometric $D$ on $A$ and a continuous path $\gamma : \intervalleff0T \rightarrow A$ for the usual topology, we define the $D$-length of $\gamma$ as
    \begin{equation}

        D(\gamma) \coloneqq \sup_{0=t_0 <\dots < t_r = T} \sum_{i=0}^{r-1} D\p{ \gamma(t_i), \gamma(t_{i+1})}.
    \end{equation}
    In the special case where $D$ is the metric induced by $\norme[1]\cdot$, we will denote by $\norme[1]\gamma$ the $D$-length of $\gamma$. We say that $\gamma$ is a $D$-\emph{geodesic} if it is Lipschitz for $\norme[1]\cdot$, and
    \begin{equation}
        D(\gamma) = D\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft( \gamma(0), \gamma(T) \aftergroup\egroup\originalright).     
    \end{equation}
    For all $t\in\intervalleoo0T$ such that the following limit exists, we define the \emph{metric derivative} of $\gamma$ at $t$, with respect to $D$ as
    \begin{equation}

        \MD{\gamma}(t) \coloneqq \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{1}{\module h}D\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(  \gamma(t) , \gamma(t+h) \aftergroup\egroup\originalright).
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.750257+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_47e7efc26abd76429551a1267576253b26d3f63984cb6ccfbc9580314c6e852f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma bound_u_n_2 of arXiv:2606.09386, 'High-degree vertices in uniform recursive directed acyclic graphs with freezing'] Fix $k\in\mathbb{N}$. Suppose that the choice sequence $\mathbf x$ satisfies Assumption~$\hyperref[ass:A]{\mathbf{x}}$(item:geq1 of the paper) and~(item:lb of the paper), where $I_{\hyperref[ass:A]{\mathbf{x}}}=\mathcal{O}((h_n^+)^{\gamma_0(1/2-\delta)})$ and $\gamma_0$ is as in~(equation eq:def_tngamma of the paper). For $v\in [k]$, let $d_v=d_v(n),\ell_v=\ell_v(n)\in\mathbb{N}_0$ such that $\ell_v> t_n$ with $t_n\coloneq\ceil{(h_n^+)^{\gamma_0}}$ and $d_v(n)\to\infty$ with $n$. Then, for any sequence $r_n=\omega((h_n^+)^{\gamma_0(1/2-\delta)})$, as $n\to\infty$,
