{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:45.222234+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_483377033be987176b354d394fc8c6d72f791aa4f68b02e67e47c25d584e627f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theorem1.56 of arXiv:2412.06246, 'The smallest singular value for rectangular random matrices with Lévy entries'] Fix $\alpha\in(0,2),$ $\delta>1$, and let $N=\lceil \delta n\rceil$. Let $\xi$ be a random variable with a symmetric distribution $\xi \overset{\operatorname{law}}{=}-\xi$ satisfying that, for some $C_u>C_\ell>0$ we have for any $t>0$,
\begin{equation}
C_\ell t^{-\alpha}\leq \mathbb{P}(|\xi|\geq t)\leq C_ut^{-\alpha}.
\end{equation}  Let $X=(x_{ij})_{1\leq i\leq N,1\leq j\leq n}$ be a rectangular random matrix with independent identically distributed (i.i.d.) entries having the distribution $\xi$. 
Then for any $D>0$ we can find $C_1,C_2>0$ and some $N_0\in\mathbb{N}_+$ such that, whenever $N\geq N_0$,
