{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.814690+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4888f0dca5ffb059023f174571948d9610fa3d1161ba5829fb667fa443e95dae","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:dist_iden_looperase of arXiv:2607.16911, 'Multipartite random graphs with given degrees: local limit, revisiting the giant, distance'] Consider any 1-dependent branching process $\mathrm{BP}(\boldsymbol{p},\boldsymbol{D},\Tilde{\boldsymbol{D}})$, and a vertex $v$ in it with $p(v)=u$ and such that $T_{p(v)}=s_0, T_v=s_1$. Furthermore, consider any infinite ray of the form $\pi=s_0 \rightarrow s_0s_1 \rightarrow \cdots$, and collections of positive integers $\{M_i:i\in I\}$, $\{N_i:i\in I\}$ such that conditions (equation eq:integer interval condition of the paper) hold, where $I$ is a countable index set. Then for any subset $\{i_1,\dots,i_N\}\subseteq I$, taking $\mathbb T=\mathrm{BP}(\boldsymbol{p},\boldsymbol{D},\Tilde{\boldsymbol{D}})$, viewed as trees labeled by vertex types, there is the distributional identity
