{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:31:57.826529+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4a00cfbb7e0d60e59303feb404194c9a1281cf5cdb929ec2d3ca8ae0fe0ade04","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thma* two and more dimensions of arXiv:2509.19809, 'Exact Limsup Growth of Rarely Visited Sites for One-Dimensional Simple Random Walk'] For $k,n\in{\mathbb N}$, let $Q_k^{(d)}(n)$ denote the number of sites visited exactly $k$ times up to step $n$ in a $d$-dimensional simple random walk $(S_n^{(d)})$ on $\mathbb{Z}^d$. Formally,
$$Q_k^{(d)}(n)=\#\{j:0\le j\le n,\,\xi(S_j^{(d)},n)=k\}.$$
Then for $k\in{\mathbb N}$, we have the following asymptotic results:
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&\lim_{n\to\infty}\dfrac{Q_k^{(2)}(n)\cdot(\log n)^2}{\pi^2n}=1~\quad\quad\quad ~~~ a.s.\\
&&\lim_{n\to\infty}\dfrac{Q_k^{(d)}(n)}{n}=\gamma_d^2(1-\gamma_d)^{k-1}~\quad\quad a.s.~~~~~\mbox{ if }~~d\ge3,
\end{eqnarray*}
where $\gamma_d:= {\mathbb P}\left(S_n^{(d)} \neq S_0^{(d)},\; \forall n\geq 1\right)$, which is the probability that a simple random walk never returns to the starting point. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.342250+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4a0397475dd2efe2cd6898161fd4c0fa7ec9c67a6ecfbc40bd2de67c198edf27","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:apriori of arXiv:2412.01381, 'Ergodicity and mixing for locally monotone stochastic evolution equations'] Assume that Hypotheses~(hyp:hemicont of the paper),~(hyp:coerc of the paper),~(hyp:coerc-g of the paper),~(hyp:monotonicity of the paper), (hyp:monotonicity-g of the paper), (hyp:growth of the paper), (hyp:regularity of the paper) are valid. Let $T>0$ be fixed.
