{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.579450+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4c44201d9c31fdadd21a023e040c1b9877ea04c190193230f62b558b0db44fc3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Deffr intro:def:MBsemimartingale of arXiv:2606.06187, 'How to Study Reflected Brownian Motion in a Quadrant via Kernel Functional Equations?\\ \m'] The process $Z_t=(Z_t^{(1)},Z_t^{(2)})^\top$ is called a semimartingale reflected Brownian motion in $\mathbb{R}_+^2$, driven by a Brownian motion $B_t$ with covariance matrix $\Sigma=(\sigma_{ij})_{1 \le i,j \le 2}\in\mathbb{R}^{2\times 2}$, drift $\mu=(\mu_i)_{i=1,2}\in\mathbb{R}^2$, and obliquely reflected on the axes according to a reflection matrix $R=(R_1,R_2)=(r_{ij})_{1 \le i,j \le 2}\in\mathbb{R}^{2\times 2}$ (where $R_j$ is its $j$-th column), if it admits the decomposition
\begin{equation}
Z_t = Z_0 + B_t + \mu t + R L_t, \qquad Z_t \in \mathbb{R}_+^2,
\end{equation}
where $L_t=(L_t^{(1)},L_t^{(2)})^\top$ is a continuous process with nondecreasing components, and each component $L_t^{(i)}$ increases only when the corresponding coordinate $Z_t^{(i)}$ is equal to $0$. The pair $(Z_t,L_t)$ is said to be a solution to the \textit{Skorokhod problem} in the quadrant for $B_t+\mu t$ with respect to the reflection matrix $R$.","status":"unverified"}

