{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.516568+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4c5d40e141e75343d280b67e6672f5d522171d6e9b210cac8099bf09c275f019","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm commutation relation for chordal with marked point u in H of arXiv:2505.16093, 'Multiple chordal SLE($\kappa$) and quantum Calogero-Moser system'] In the upper half plane $\mathbb{H}$, $n$ chordal SLEs start at $x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n\in \partial{\mathbb{H}}$ with a marked boundary point $u$. 
\begin{itemize}

\item[(i)] Let the infinitesimal diffusion generators be
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{M}_i=\frac{\kappa}{2} \partial_{ii}+b_i(x_1, x_2,\ldots, x_n,u) \partial_i +\sum_{j \neq i} \frac{2}{x_j-x_i}\partial_j +\frac{2}{u-x_i} \partial_u
\end{equation}

where $\partial_i=\partial_{x_i}$. If n SLEs locally commute, then the associated infinitesimal generators satisfy:
\begin{equation}
[\mathcal{M}_i, \mathcal{M}_j]=\frac{4}{(x_i-x_j)^2}(\mathcal{M}_j-\mathcal{M}_i)
\end{equation}
There exists a partition function $\psi(\boldsymbol{x},u)$ such that the drift term $b_i(\boldsymbol{x},u)$ is given by:
\begin{equation}
b_i(\boldsymbol{x},u)= \kappa \partial_i \log \psi
\end{equation}
where $\psi$ satisfies the null vector equations with an undetermined function $h_i(x,u)$.
\begin{equation}
\frac{\kappa}{2} \partial_{ii} \psi+\sum_{j \neq i} \frac{2}{x_j-x_i}\partial_i \psi+\frac{2}{u-x_i} \partial_u \psi + \left[\left(1-\frac{6}{\kappa}\right) \sum_{j \neq i}\frac{1}{(x_j-x_i)^2}+h_i(x_i,u)\right]\psi=0
\end{equation}

\item[(ii)]
 By analyzing the asymptotic behavior of two adjacent growth points $x_i$ and $x_{i+1}$ ( with no marked points between $x_i$ and $x_{i+1}$ on the real line $\mathbb{R}$), we can further show that the $h_i(x,u)=h_{i+1}(x,u)$ in null vector equations ((null vector equations in mathbb H of the paper)). As a corollary, if all growth points lie consecutively on the real line $\mathbb{R}$ with no marked points between them, then there exists a common function $h(x,u)$ such that $h(x,u)=h_1(x,u)=\ldots=h_n(x,u)$.

\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:29:35.076420+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4c5df68aa5a39490cfdc9e52b784a4a3e96487b64e0f08bbbadcb2958a85f54a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2609.03791, 'Canonical Bochner--K\"{a}hler potentials via the Sasaki--K\"{a}hler correspondence'] A real-valued function $F$ on a complex manifold $M$ is a K\"{a}hler potential of a Bochner--K\"{a}hler structure at a point $p\in M$ if and only if
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{bmatrix} 0& z^* & \frac{\I}{2} \end{bmatrix} \e^{2\I AF}\begin{bmatrix} 1\\ z\\ 0 \end{bmatrix}=0
    \end{equation}
    in some coordinates $z=(z_1,\ldots,z_n)$ centered at $p$, for some matrix $A\in \mathfrak{gl}_{n+2}(\mathbb{C})$ of the form 
    \begin{equation}   
    A=\begin{bmatrix}
        0& -2\I a^* & r\\0&\I X& a\\1&0&0
    \end{bmatrix},
    \end{equation}
    where 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $X\in \mathfrak{gl}_{n}(\mathbb{R})$ is diagonal with entries ordered such that $X_{j,j}\geq X_{j+1,j+1}$,
        \item $a$ is a vector with non-negative real entries satisfying $a_{j+1}=0$ whenever  $X_{j,j}=X_{j+1,j+1}$, and
        \item $r$ is a real number.
    \end{itemize}

    Moreover, such a matrix $A$ is uniquely determined by the germ at $p$ of the Bochner--K\"{a}hler structure being represented. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:43:55.660537+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4c5e62b7f843db9f28d5fa924563348e409560196e9d20f6c7050aaf5d2d2e1f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Thm:Burkholder-Davis-Gundy Inequality of arXiv:2604.07497, 'The Three-Dimensional Stochastic EMHD System: Local Well-Posedness and Maximal Pathwise So'] Let $p > 0$ and
\begin{equation}
