{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:40.048627+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d3c67fd63c5e33dee21b79d560963c45553cbb33c89cd4510fbdba410fc64f9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2510.25900, 'Dixie cup problem in an interlacing process'] Let the sequence $\alpha = \{a_{j}\}_{j=1}^{\infty }$ is formed as the \textit{interlacing} of two subsequences $\beta = \{b_{j}\}_{j=1}^{\infty }$ and $\delta = \{d_{j}\}_{j=1}^{\infty }$, as given in relation (\ref{sb1}).  Then,  as $N=2M \rightarrow \infty$ we have
