{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:46:12.277743+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4dcdea8613cb267926360b2543d947d1c4b0b6a7954a999384e170071ba91820","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2604.22461, 'Large deviation principles for stationary solutions and invariant measures of a class of S'] [Theorem \ref{thm: stationary solution}] 
 Assume that the conditions {\rm [A1-A5]} hold. If $  \tilde{\gamma}_0<\gamma_0$, then for any $0 \leq \epsilon< \tilde{\epsilon}$, there is a pathwise unique stationary solution $\cY^{\epsilon} \in  L^{\infty}(\R; L^{2}(\Omega; H))$ for cocycle $(U^{\epsilon},\theta)$.
For any $-\infty<t_0<t<+\infty$, the stationary solution $\cY^{\epsilon} \in L^{2}(\Omega;\sC_{-\infty})$ $\P$-a.s. satisfies
\begin{equation}
    \scalV{\cY^{\epsilon}(t),v} = \scalV{\cY^{\epsilon}(t_0),v} + \int_{t_0}^{t} \scalV{A^{\epsilon}(\cY^{\epsilon}(s)),v} \d s + \sqrt{\epsilon} \int_{t_0}^{t} \scalH{B (\cY^{\epsilon}(s)) \, \d W_s,v},
\end{equation}
for any $v \in V$. Conversely, if a $\cF_{t}$-adapted process $\cY^{\epsilon}$ satisfies Eq. \eqref{eq: stationary solution} and $\sup_{t \in \R} \E \|\cY^{\epsilon}(t) \|_{H}^2< +\infty$, then it is indeed the stationary solution for cocycle $(U^{\epsilon},\theta)$. There exists a Borel measurable map $\mathcal{G}^{\epsilon}_{-\infty}: \W \rightarrow \sC_{-\infty}$ such that $\mathcal{G}^{\epsilon}_{-\infty}(W)(\cdot)$ is an $\mathcal{F}_{t}$-adapted solution to Eq. \eqref{eq: stationary solution} and $\sup_{t \in \R} \E \|\mathcal{G}^{\epsilon}_{-\infty}(W)(t) \|_{H}^2< +\infty$, called the strong stationary solution for cocycle $(U^{\epsilon},\theta)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:58:09.903352+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4dcec8d19f9bc0495ea58b7f36a77d6d3d323fac8d1b776b37c32768275f72e6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def PCPS of arXiv:2607.19511, 'A Conditional Probability Hierarchy for Stochastic Choice\footnote{We thank Samson Abramsk'] Fix a CPS $p : {\cal G} \rightarrow \Delta(\Omega)$. Suppose there is a selection $s$ such that, for each $G \in \cal G$ and $F \in \cal F$:
\begin{equation}~
p_G(F) = \mathbf{1}_F(s(G)).
\end{equation}
That is, each $p_G(\cdot)$ is the Dirac measure concentrated on $s(G)$. We call such a CPS a \textbf{point CPS} (\textbf{PCPS}).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":["α real; for the infinite-expansion statements α irrational"],"created_at":"2026-09-13T01:59:57.491799+00:00","domain":"number theory, continued fractions","id":"claim_4dd0d9ece9166d1812156f3b42eca840a060258da83827d8e9c85f130a953389","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"Every real α has a regular continued fraction expansion α = a_0 + 1/(a_1 + 1/(a_2 + ...)) = [a_0; a_1, a_2, ...], produced by the Gauss algorithm: a_0 = floor(α), α_1 = 1/(α − a_0), a_k = floor(α_k), α_{k+1} = 1/(α_k − a_k). Here a_0 is an integer, every a_k (k ≥ 1) is an integer ≥ 1 (the partial quotients), and the α_k are the complete quotients, so a_k < α_k < a_k + 1 whenever the expansion continues. The expansion is finite iff α is rational (then it is unique once the last partial quotient is required to be ≥ 2); for irrational α it is infinite and unique.

The convergents p_n/q_n = [a_0; a_1, ..., a_n] are computed by p_{-1} = 1, q_{-1} = 0, p_0 = a_0, q_0 = 1 and p_{n+1} = a_{n+1} p_n + p_{n-1}, q_{n+1} = a_{n+1} q_n + q_{n-1} for n ≥ 0.

Basic identities: p_n q_{n-1} − p_{n-1} q_n = (−1)^{n-1} for n ≥ 0, so gcd(p_n, q_n) = 1; α = (p_n α_{n+1} + p_{n-1})/(q_n α_{n+1} + q_{n-1}); the even convergents increase strictly to α and the odd convergents decrease strictly to α (for irrational α); |α − p_n/q_n| < 1/(q_n q_{n+1}) < 1/q_n^2. The denominators satisfy 1 = q_0 ≤ q_1 < q_2 < q_3 < ..., with q_0 = q_1 exactly when a_1 = 1. The Gauss map x ↦ {1/x} on (0,1) preserves the Gauss measure dx/((1 + x) log 2), the ergodic-theoretic setting for the statistics of partial quotients.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:09.864295+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4dd1b954e617264a77cab49d7b55c2371fc24061c7c36be4efd8d7f25f341a36","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 3 of arXiv:2605.21638, 'Markov renewal theory for transfer operators and point processes on the line'] Assume that the pair potential $v$ has a hard core $r_\mathrm{hc} >0$, finite range $R<\infty$, and is finite and bounded from below on $[r_\mathrm{hc}, R]$.  Let $k\in \N$ with $(k+2)r_\mathrm{hc} \geq R$. Let $(Z_n)_{n\in \Z}$ be a stationary sequence of spacings associated with a Gibbs point process $\Phi \sim\mathsf P_{\beta,\zeta}$. Then: 
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item [(a)]
	$\bigl((Z_{kn-k+1},\ldots, Z_{kn})\bigr)_{n\in\Z}$, is a geometrically ergodic stationary Markov chain with state space $(r_\mathrm{hc},\infty)^{k}$. 
    \item [(b)] For all bounded intervals $A,B\subset \R$ and some $\eps>0$
	\begin{equation*}
		\E\bigl[ \Phi(A) \Phi(B+t)\bigr] - \E\bigl[ \Phi(A) \bigr]\E\bigl[ \Phi(B) \bigr] = \Or(\e^{-\epsilon t}) \quad \textrm{as } t\to\infty.
    \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.474217+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4dde14ce9d2dcafcd54ce7a44a898b6806b401e9269d0fe4a5431743ed6a7968","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Lambda-bounds of arXiv:2606.21006, 'Law of the Iterated Logarithm for Weighted Sums of Functionals of Long-Memory Gaussian Seq'] Assume $\alpha m<1$ and $2d+\alpha m<1$. Then
\[
    0
    \;<\;
    L_{m,\alpha,d}
    \;\le\;
    \Lambda_m
    \;\le\;
    (m!)^{-1/2},
\]
where the upper bound is the Hilbert--Schmidt norm of the limiting
kernel $Q_1$, and the lower bound is given in closed form by
\[
     L_{m,\alpha,d}
    :=
    \Bigl(\frac{2-\alpha}{2(1-\alpha)}\Bigr)^{m/2}
    \frac{\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{m}\binom{m}{k}
      B\bigl(1-d+(1-\alpha)k,\;1+(1-\alpha)(m-k)\bigr)}
    {\bigl(m!\,I_{m,\alpha,d}\bigr)^{1/2}},
\]
where $B(\cdot,\cdot)$ is the Beta function and
$I_{m,\alpha,d}=2B(1-d,1-\alpha m)/(2-2d-\alpha m)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:30:50.733766+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4ddfde11a598e61a24d3760a21337740eafa31112d557dc2996bf8a9c2f0f3a9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop: lower bound Phi4 of arXiv:2503.24372, 'A criterion on the free energy for log-Sobolev inequalities in mean-field particle systems'] Suppose Assumption~(ass_doublewell of the paper) on $V$. 
