{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:36:12.184482+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4e5ce18a07ecb717ab64b1161a1c9aaa7986fa320bc90da75735a72df0aa46ec","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (The factorial formula) of arXiv:2609.03881, 'Eulerian insertion operators and an Eulerian form of the Pieri rule'] In the coordinates \((\delta,u)\), the following identity holds as a
formal power series in \(z\):
\begin{equation*}
 \G(z)
 =\exp\bigl(-\log(1-\delta z)E\bigr).
\end{equation*}
Consequently, for $r\geq 1$, 
\begin{equation}
 \G_r
 =\frac{\delta^r}{r!}E(E+1)\cdots(E+r-1).
\end{equation}
Equivalently, on \(\mathbb Q[x,y]\),
\begin{equation}
 r!\G_r
 =\prod_{j=0}^{r-1}(xyD+j(y-x)),
\end{equation}
where \(y-x\) acts by multiplication.
