for some $C = C(\alpha, L)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.311974+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4ead01faa3b2d2446c7bb78e8bf9de3e4e12166c4725f7c70c0422b4d051e4a1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:sep of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] For $\mathbb{P}_{\vec x}$-almost  every $\mathcal{W}$, there exist $\delta_1(\mathcal{W})$, $\delta_2(\mathcal{W})>0$ such that for any closed subset $\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{W}$ and all $x\in B(0,1/2)\setminus \mathcal{A}$, we have 
    \begin{equation}
\mathbf{P}_x\left(\mathrm{dist}(W(\tau),\mathcal{A})>\delta_1 \,\Big |\,  W[0,\tau]\cap \mathcal{A}=\varnothing\right)>\delta_2,
    \end{equation}
    where $\tau$ is the first hitting time of $\partial B(0,1)$ by $W$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:38:58.706423+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4eb4a070663edc132dbf2ec0e2bade8e79eff72b7a4996fe67cf4c88cd6f5cc6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:lacoin of arXiv:2510.24424, 'On the Fourier Coefficients of Critical Gaussian Multiplicative Chaos'] Let $(X_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be defined as in Equation (equation eq:*scale of the paper) and 
\begin{align}
    U(t):= \begin{cases}
