{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:05:37.776566+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4faace4788c0fc420622eeaf5e78fa52cdbe318b8541adadd7984e9a55d44874","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: heat_kernel_typical of arXiv:2608.00399, 'The Heavy-tailed Frog Model'] Let $d \ge 2$ and $Q \in \mathcal{H}_{\alpha, K}$ for some $\alpha > 0$.
    Then, there exists $C = C(Q) \in (0, \infty)$ and, for any $\kappa > 0$, there exists $c = c(\kappa, Q) > 0$ such that, for all $n \ge 1$ and $|x| \le \kappa r(n)$,
    \begin{align} 
        c \frac{1}{r(n)^d} \le Q^{n}(o, x) \le C \frac{1}{r(n)^d}.
    \end{align}
    where
    \begin{align} 
        r(n) := \begin{cases}
            \sqrt{n} &\text{ if } \alpha > 2, \\
            \sqrt{n \log n} &\text{ if } \alpha =2, \\
            n^{1/\alpha} &\text{ if } \alpha < 2.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align}
    Moreover, for every $\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $K$ such that
    \begin{align} 
        \sum_{m = 0}^n \sum_{\substack{y \in \mathbb Z^d \\ |y| > K r(n)}} Q^m(o, y) \le n \varepsilon.
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.967915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4fab9cd13d7eae474be400d379451e0bbf1fd3661ea3ee9a42c36df1434cd67e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:equiv_discrete_path of arXiv:2411.07113, '\begin{LARGE}On differentiability and mass distributions of topologically typical multivar'] For $C \in \mathcal{C}_{ar}^d$ the following two assertions are equivalent: 
