{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:58.364274+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5211eb8983bd438dbc4dbb7d8f35f84c76959be96184f07f35d4183c3291529a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 5 of arXiv:2506.20146, 'Parabolic Anderson Model in the Hyperbolic Space. Part I: Annealed Asymptotics'] (i) {[}Global PAM{]} For a.e. realisation of $\xi$,
the solution $u(t,x)$ to the PAM defined by (\ref{eq:GlobalPAM})
admits the following Feynman-Kac representation:
\begin{equation}
u(t,x)=\mathbb{E}\big[e^{\int_{0}^{t}\xi(W_{s}^{x})ds}\big],
\end{equation}
where $\{W_{s}^{x}\}$ denotes a hyperbolic Brownian motion (generated
by $\Delta$) starting at $x$ that is independent of $\xi$, and
the expectation is taking with respect to $\{W_{s}^{x}\}.$

\vspace{2mm}\noindent (ii) {[}Localised PAM{]} Recall that $Q_{R}$
is the closed geodesic ball of radius $R$ centered at $o$. Consider
the following initial-boundary value problem:
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
\partial_{s}u_{R}(s,x)=\Delta u_{R}(s,x)+\xi(x)u_{R}(s,x), & (s,x)\in(0,\infty)\times Q_{R};\\
u_{R}(0,\cdot)\equiv1;\ u_{R}(s,\cdot)=0\ \text{on }\partial Q_{R}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Then solution $u_{R}(t,x)$ admits the following representation:
\[
u_{R}(s,x)=\mathbb{E}\big[e^{\int_{0}^{s}\xi(W_{r}^{x})dr};W^{x}([0,s])\subseteq Q_{R}\big].
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:07:35.014821+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_521454875039991f14232fd36f6db84498bb36bd26020a9a79002a9981118c6b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 15 of arXiv:2609.02878, '2609.02878'] Fix \(q\in V\), where \(V\Subset Y^{\rm rv}\) lies in a Zariski product
