{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:53.193593+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_537ac6ae8d16de434768b0d2557f6417ef4cca8a62288ed9ff58dbcc91f91a36","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 22 of arXiv:2606.31455, 'Discrete time-multidimensional renewal theory and applications'] Let $n:=N(m)$ and $u:=U_m$.
If $N_{A(u)}(m)>0$, the criterion \eqref{eq:loglik-np} is uniquely maximized by the pmf
$\widehat{f}(\cdot\,;m)$ given by

\begin{equation}
\widehat{f}(k;m)
=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{N_k(m)}{n+1},
& k\le_d u,\\[1.0em]
\dfrac{N_k(m)}{n+1}\left(1+\dfrac{1}{N_{A(u)}(m)}\right),
& k\not\le_d u.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

Equivalently, for $k\not\le_d u$,

\begin{equation*}
\widehat{f}(k;m)
=
\frac{N_k(m)}{n+1}
+\frac{1}{n+1}\cdot\frac{N_k(m)}{N_{A(u)}(m)}.
\end{equation*}

If $N_{A(u)}(m)=0$, any pmf satisfying $\widehat{f}(k;m)=N_k(m)/(n+1)$ for $k\le_d u$ and
$\sum_{k\not\le_d u}\widehat{f}(k;m)=1/(n+1)$ is a maximizer.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.328132+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_537c9bacace2f2c0e9f302dc40587a55a65e104299857d55f391dcb96d2b0bac","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2606.01937, 'Stationary fluctuations for an exclusion process with mass and energy conservation'] Assume that the parameters~$(\alpha_p,\alpha_e,\rho ,\mathcal{E})$ are chosen such that all the diagonal entries of the coupling matrices are non-zero (\textit{cf}.~(prop:noncancellation of the paper) in (appendix:couplingmatrices of the paper)). In the diffusive timescale $a=2$, and with a weak asymmetry given by $\gamma =\frac12$, the couple of processes $(\mathcal{Z}^{N,1},\mathcal{Z}^{N,2})_{N\ge 1}$ converges in distribution, as $N\to\infty$, to a pair of independent processes $(\mathcal{Z}^1,\mathcal{Z}^2)$, where each $\mathcal{Z}^i$ is an energy solution to the SBE
    \begin{equation}
        \partial_t\mathcal{Z}_t^i = (\kappa -1)\Delta\mathcal{Z}_t^i + \mathfrak{g}_i\nabla (\mathcal{Z}_t^i)^2 + \sqrt{\smash[b]{2(\kappa -1)\sigma_i^2}} \nabla\dot{\mathcal{W}}_t^i
    \end{equation}
    starting from independent spatial white noises with variances $\sigma_1,\sigma_2$ respectively. Here, $(\mathcal{W}^1,\mathcal{W}^2)$ are two independent $\mathscr{S}'(\mathbb{R} )$-valued Brownian motions, and the parameters are given by 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\mathfrak{g}_i=\mathbf{G}_{ii}^i$ ;
        \item $\sigma_i^2 = \begin{pmatrix}
            \mathfrak{c}_i & 1
        \end{pmatrix}\chi (\rho ,\mathcal{E}) \begin{pmatrix}
            \mathfrak{c}_i \\ 
            1
        \end{pmatrix}$ where $\chi (\rho ,\mathcal{E})$ is the compressibility matrix defined in (equation eq:compressibilitymatrix of the paper).
    \end{itemize}
    Above, the matrix $\mathbf{G}^i$ is the coupling matrix defined in (equation def:couplingmatrices of the paper), and discussed in (appendix:couplingmatrices of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.968362+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_538ff8f610fa4ff5c119a5b5125dab95a39d02eadbabb77de811a4ca9fde3943","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 29 of arXiv:2608.12291, 'When should one stop the most exciting game?\\ {\tiny Sequential Inference for win-marting'] Let $T\leq\infty$. There exists a lower semicontinuous function $b_T:[0,T)\to[1/2,1]$ such that the continuation region is given by
