where $\mu_1\ge\mu_2\ge\cdots\ge\mu_n$ denote the largest $n$ eigenvalues of a $DN\times DN$ GUE. The corresponding edge universality result also holds for $\gamma_-(DN)^{2/3}(\lambda_{DN}-E^-,\ldots,\lambda_{DN-n}-E^- )$ at the left edge $E^-$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:51:00.234770+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_53a7689099c939e88f3a398d25775547be454852d9a3bdf95190c651fe2c1abc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition d2.4 of arXiv:2604.22510, 'Asymptotics of Multi-Scale McKean--Vlasov Diffusions with Super-Linear Kernels: a Lifted S'] (Lifted viable pair)
	A pair $$(\varphi,{\boldsymbol{ {\Pi}}})\in C([0,T];\mathbb{R}^n)\times\mathscr{P}(\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathscr{P}_2({\mathbb R}^m)\times[0,T])$$
	is called viable w.r.t.~$(\Theta,\nu^{\mathscr{L}_{\bar{X}}})$ and write $( \varphi, {\boldsymbol{ {\Pi}}} ) \in \mathscr{V} _{(x,\bar{X},\Theta,\nu^{\mathscr{L}_{\bar{X}}})}$, if the following statements hold:

	(i) The measure ${\boldsymbol{ {\Pi}}}$ admits the following moment estimate

	\begin{equation}
		\int_{\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathscr{P}_2({\mathbb R}^m)\times[0,T]}\big[|h|^{2}+|y|^{4}+M_4(\rho)\big]{\boldsymbol{ {\Pi}}}(dhdyd\rho ds)<\infty.
	\end{equation}



	(ii) For all $t\in [ 0, T] , $

	\begin{equation}
		\varphi_{t}=x+\int_{\mathbb{R}^d\times{\mathbb R}^m\times\mathscr{P}_2({\mathbb R}^m)\times[0,t]}\Theta(\varphi_{s},\mathscr{L}_{\bar{X}_{s}},h,y,\rho){\boldsymbol{ {\Pi}}}(dhdyd\rho ds).
	\end{equation}

	(iii) For all $A_1\times A_2\times A_3\times A_4\in\mathscr{B}(\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathscr{P}_2({\mathbb R}^m)\times[0,T])$,

	\begin{equation}
		{\boldsymbol{ {\Pi}}}(A_{1}\times A_{2}\times A_{3}\times A_{4})=\int_{A_{4}}\int_{A_{2}\times A_{3}}\eta(A_{1}|y,\rho,t)(\nu^{\mathscr{L}_{\bar{X}_t}}\times \delta_{\nu^{\mathscr{L}_{\bar{X}_t}}})(dyd\rho)dt,	
	\end{equation}
	where $\eta$ denotes a stochastic kernel (see [MR3967100]) conditioned on $\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathscr{P}_2({\mathbb R}^m)\times[0,T]$. This ensures that the marginal of ${\boldsymbol{ {\Pi}}}$ on $[0,T]$ is Lebesgue measure  and in particular for all $t\in[0,T]$,
	\begin{equation}
		{\boldsymbol{ {\Pi}}}(\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathscr{P}_2({\mathbb R}^m)\times[0,t])=t.
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.286526+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_53a9addf1609b6bfc1cd126659577493ae52dc2c600eb1345fa8349e673a4c39","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop prop of arXiv:2411.01847, 'Well-Posedness of Stochastic Chemotaxis System'] For every $m\geq 1$, the process  $U_n(t)$ satisfies the following equation in $L^2(\mathcal{O})$ for $t< \tau_m$,
