{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:38:04.524700+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_53bd7fb2cb411309ba5635a8b1910216489bb31e856e0a621a6f05de4e00ea14","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2607.18011, 'From local giants to locality in long-range percolation'] \thlabel{lem:kernel_example}
    Let $G$ be a transitive graph of polynomial growth with $d \in \mathbf{N}$ and let $J :V \times V \to \mathbf{R}_+$ be the transitive kernel given by $J(x,y) = d_G(x,y)^{- d \alpha}$ for $\alpha > 0$.

    Let $(G_n)_{n \in \mathbf{N}}$ be a sequence of transitive graphs converging to $G$, let $(s_n)_{n \in \mathbf{N}}$ be a sequence converging to $d \alpha$, and let $(J_n)_{n \in \mathbf{N}}$ be the sequence of kernels $J : V_n \times V_n \to \mathbf{R}_+$ given by $J_n(x,y) = d_{G_n}(x,y)^{- s_n}$. 

    Then $(G_n,J_n)_{n \in \mathbf{N}}$ converges locally to $(G,J)$. Further, if $\alpha>1$, then the kernels $(J_n)_{n \in \mathbf{N}}$ are uniformly integrable.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.333541+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_53be7aa892f7f16325a6aaf71cf13da975058383e6a4aa0873171bc88d07f126","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 4 of arXiv:2511.06196, 'Central limit theorem for high temperature spin models via martingale embedding'] Let $H_n, n\geq 1$, be a sequence of $n\times n$ GOE matrices as above.
Let $\beta>0$ be a sufficiently small constant.
For each $n$, consider the SK model defined as in \cref{eq:Isingmodel} with the interaction matrix $A=A_n=\beta H_n$.
Let $\langle \cdot \rangle$ denote the conditional expectation with respect to the model given the random interaction matrix.
Let
\be{
W_n=\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\sum_{i=1}^n X_i,\quad \mu_n(H_n):=\langle W_n\rangle, \quad \sigma_n^2(H_n):=\langle W_n^2\rangle -\langle W_n\rangle^2.
}
Then, for any $\epsilon>0$, as $n\to \infty$,
\ben{
P\Big(\mathcal{W}_2 \big(\mathcal{L}(W_n \vert H_n), N(\mu_n(H_n),\sigma_n^2(H_n))\big)\geq \epsilon \Big)\to 0.
}
If, in addition, $\|h\|_\infty$ is bounded by a universal constant $c_h$ and $0<\beta<\beta_h$ for a sufficiently small $\beta_h$ depending on $c_h$, then $\E(\sigma_n^2(H_n))$ is bounded away from 0 for sufficiently large $n$, $\Var(\sigma_n^2(H_n))\to 0$ as $n\to \infty$, and hence \cref{eq:cor4} gives a meaningful CLT. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.302210+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_53bfb392383fd31f63aafc9652eafe4e6eb0a26b9e98cd78368aa9b290421166","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:inversion of arXiv:2602.11129, 'Information-Theoretic Thresholds for Bipartite Latent-Space Graphs Under Noisy Observation'] If $\varphi$ is the characteristic function of a random variable $X$ in $\mathbb{R}^k$ such that $|\varphi(\mathbf{t})|$ is integrable, then the density $f$ of $X$ is given by
    \begin{align*}
        f(\mathbf{x}) = \frac{1}{(2\pi)^k} \int_{[-\infty, \infty]^k} e^{i\trp{\mathbf{t}}{\mathbf{x}}} \varphi(\mathbf{t}) \mathrm{d} \mathbf{t}
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:04:39.221535+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_53c0454620657da913cb25315dfbd3541f883682e3168b23d24a734572d24dfe","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 19 of arXiv:2608.13976, 'Boundary Harnack principle for diffusion with jumps on metric measure spaces'] {\bf ($\bf {HK}(\phi_c, \phi_j)$ condition)} \rm
 We say that
${\rm HK}(\phi_c, \phi_j)$
holds if there exists a heat kernel $p(t, x,y)$
of the semigroup $\{P_t\}_{t\ge0}$ associated with $(\mathcal E,\mathcal F)$ in \eqref{e:E}
and the following estimates hold
for all $t>0$ and all $x,y\in \sX$,
\begin{equation}\begin{split}
&   c_1\Big(\frac 1{V(x,\phi_c^{-1}(t))}\wedge \frac 1{V(x,\phi_j^{-1}(t))} \wedge
 \big(p^{(c)}(c_2 t,x,y)+p^{(j)}(t,x,y)\big)\Big) \\
 & \le \   p(t, x,y)  \\
  &\le c_3\Big(\frac 1{V(x,\phi_c^{-1}(t))}\wedge \frac 1{V(x,\phi_j^{-1}(t))} \wedge
\big(p^{(c)}(c_4 t,x,y)+p^{(j)}(t,x,y)\big)\Big),
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $c_k>0$, $k=1, \cdots, 4$,  are constants independent of $x,y\in \sX$ and $t>0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:29:27.995749+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_53c0ae80f659ce4d78759d823703c825cbdaaf65a27c6aa00790c19605738bc1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 23 (Relative-coordinate conditional polynomial) of arXiv:2608.24863, 'Uniform Logarithmic Sobolev Inequalities for the 2D Coulomb Gas at the Diffusive Temperatu'] Let $\nu_{c,Z_{-ij}}$ denote the conditional law of $u$ given $(c,Z_{-ij})$, which is specified by
\begin{equation}
 \D\nu_{c,Z_{-ij}}(u)
 \propto e^{-\beta|u|^2/2}|p(u)|^{\beta/N}\,\D u,
 \qquad
 p(u):=u\prod_{k\notin\{i,j\}}\left(A_k^2-\frac{u^2}{2}\right).
\end{equation}
The polynomial $p$ is odd, has degree $2N-3$, and total exponent $\beta(2N-3)/N<2\beta$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.670164+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_53c20523fdb3e9cbcd2f0f07e7abe016f2bde0304f77a07a1af73925f971b7a9","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2604.24390, 'Weak solutions to distribution-dependent stochastic Volterra equations'] Let $f \colon [0,T] \times \bR^d \times \mathcal{P}_\eta(\bR^d) \to \bR^e$ be a function such that for all compact sets $\mathcal K \subset \bR^d$ and $\bar{\mathcal K} \subset \mathcal{P}_\eta(\bR^d)$ and every $\epsilon > 0$ there exists a $\delta >0$ such that
  \begin{equation*}
    |f(t,x,\mu)-f(t,y,\nu)|\leq \epsilon,
  \end{equation*}
  for all $t \in [0,T]$, $x,y \in \mathcal K$ satisfying $|x-y| \leq \delta$, and $\mu, \nu \in \bar{\mathcal K}$ with $W_\eta(\mu,\nu) \leq \delta$, and such that $f$ fulfills the growth condition
  \begin{align*}
    |f(t,x,\mu)| \leq C(1+|x| + W_\eta(\delta_0,\mu))^2, \quad t \in [0,T], ~x \in \bR^d, ~\mu \in \mathcal{P}_\eta(\bR^d).
  \end{align*}
  Let $K \colon \Delta_T \to \bR$ be measurable and bounded function in $L^1([0,T])$ uniformly in the second variable, i.e. $\sup_{t \in [0,T]} \int_0^t |K(s,t)| \dd s \leq M$ for some $M \geq 0$. Let $(\pi_k = \{t_0^k=0, \ldots, t_{N(k)}^k=T\})_{k \in \bN}$ be a sequence of partitions with $\lim_{k \to \infty} |\pi_k|=0$ and define, for every $k \in \bN$, the function $\kappa_k \colon [0,T] \to [0,T]$ by
  \begin{equation*}
    \kappa_k(T) := T \text{ and } \kappa_k(t) := t_i^k \text{ if } t_i^k \leq t < t_{i+1}^k,\quad \text{for all } i=0,1,\ldots,N(k)-1.
  \end{equation*}
  If $(X^k)_{k \in \bN}$ is a sequence of continuous stochastic processes with uniformly finite $\eta$-th moments such that $X^k \to X$ in $C([0,T];\bR^d)$ as $k \to \infty$ $\bP$-a.s., $W_\eta(\L(X^k),\L(X))\to 0$ as $k \to \infty$ and
  \begin{align}
    \bE[|X^k(s)-X^k(t)|^\eta] \to 0
  \end{align}
  uniformly in $k$ as $|s-t|\to 0$, $s,t \in [0,T]$, then one has that
  \begin{equation*}
    \int_0^{\cdot} K(s,\cdot) f\big(s, X^k(\kappa_k(s)),\L(X^k(\kappa_k(s)))\big)\dd s \xrightarrow{\bP} \int_0^{\cdot} K(s,\cdot) f\big(s, X_s,\L(X_s)\big)\dd s
  \end{equation*}
  with respect to $\|\cdot\|_\infty$ as $k \to \infty$, where $\xrightarrow{\bP}$ denotes convergence in probability.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:05:37.778018+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_53c583b0161b66f3eea123eaeb0f199638484afe18737c2677cdf791544350a7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 4.1 of arXiv:2608.00420, '{Weighted $L^p$ solutions of scalar BSDEs with general unbounded stochastic coefficients}\'] Assume that $\xi$ and $\xi'$ are two terminal values, $g$ and $g^{\prime}$ are two generators, and $(Y_\cdot, Z_\cdot)$ and $\left(Y_\cdot^{\prime}, Z_\cdot^{\prime}\right)$ are, respectively, solutions of BSDE$(\xi,\tau,g)$ and BSDE$\left(\xi^{\prime}, \tau, g^{\prime}\right)$. If $(Y_\cdot-Y_\cdot^{\prime})^+\in S_\tau^p(\rho_\cdot;{\mathbb R})$ and $\xi \leq \xi^{\prime}$, and either of the following conditions is satisfied:\vspace{0.2cm}

(i) $g$ satisfies (A:H2 of the paper)(iv)-(v), and $g\left(t, Y_{t}^{\prime}, Z_{t}^{\prime}\right) \leq g^{\prime}\left(t, Y_{t}^{\prime}, Z_{t}^{\prime}\right), \ \ t\in[0,\tau];$\vspace{0.1cm}

