{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:57:16.068753+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_53e513deacc99daa3ce8287b8d2d6a89ebc990aea83069f2aad807a4ee686d6b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:orthogonal of arXiv:2506.18673, 'Asymptotic Expansions of Gaussian and Laguerre Ensembles at the Soft Edge III: Generating '] There holds, for $\beta=1,4$ and any fixed non-negative integer $m \leq m_*$,\footnote{See footnote~(fn:mstar of the paper).}

\begin{gather*}
E_{\beta,n}(x;\xi) |_{x=\mu_{n'} + \sigma_{n'} s} = F_\beta(s;\xi) + \sum_{j=1}^m G_{\beta,j}(s,\tau_{n'};\xi)h_{n'}^j + h_{n'}^{m+1}O({\rm e}^{-s}),\qquad n\to\infty,
\end{gather*}
uniformly for $0\leq \xi \leq 1$ while $s$ stays bounded from below and, in the Laguerre case,~$\tau_{n'}$~stays bounded away from zero -- the Gaussian case being encoded with $\tau_{n'}=0$. Preserving uniformity, the expansion can be repeatedly differentiated with respect to the variables $s$ and $\xi$. The leading term $F_\beta(s,\xi)$ is given by (equation eq:Fbeta_Fpm of the paper)
and the correction terms are the multilinear forms
\begin{equation*}
G_{\beta,j}(s,\tau;\xi) = \sum_{k=1}^{2j} P_{\beta,j,k}(s,\tau)\cdot \partial_s^k F_\beta(s;\xi)
\end{equation*}

with certain polynomials $P_{\beta,j,k} \in {\mathbb Q}[s,\tau]$ that are independent of the dummy variable~$\xi$ and satisfy the duality relation $P_{1,j,k} = P_{4,j,k}$ -- the instances up to $j=4$ are displayed in Table~{\rm (tab:2 of the paper)}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:28.026303+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_53e8fb0563562a506927a9931ab2dce81c85ba6684e34a246a427f23954ff508","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:binom of arXiv:2608.09465, 'Balancing fractional Brownian motion'] For $|k|\leq n^{3/5}$,
