{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.802223+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55ceaf8c47cdc53c4db1ac560dd27171115ae19062a13e17356ce764f2beb0dc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem T:Main-Result of arXiv:2606.23865, 'Uniform-in-time Gaussian fluctuations for multiscale nonlinear stochastic systems via Mall'] Let $\theta_t^\varepsilon \triangleq \frac{X_t^\varepsilon-\bar{X}_t}{\sqrt{\varepsilon}}$ and $N \sim \mathscr{N}(0, \sigma_t^2).$ Assume that Assumptions (A:Boundedness of sigma-tau of the paper) through (A:eta-eps-interaction of the paper) hold. Then, for sufficiently small $\varepsilon$ and $\eta$, there exists a (time-independent) positive constant $K$ such that
\begin{multline*}
\begin{aligned}
\sup_{t \ge 0} d_W(\theta_t^\varepsilon, N) & \le  K \sqrt{\sqrt{\eta + \varepsilon} + \left( \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} - \frac{1}{\gamma^2} \right)}  + K \sqrt{ \varepsilon + \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} (\sqrt{\varepsilon}+ \sqrt{\eta})}\\
 & \qquad \qquad \quad + K \left[ \left( \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} \right)^{\frac{1}{2}} \eta^{\frac{1}{4}} + \varepsilon^{\frac{1}{4}}  +  \left( \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} \right)^{\frac{1}{8}} \eta^{\frac{1}{4}} + \left( \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} \right) \eta^{\frac{1}{4}} + \left( \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} \right)^{\frac{1}{4}} \eta^{\frac{1}{4}} +  \left( \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} \right)^{\frac{5}{8}} \eta^{\frac{1}{4}}  \right].
 \end{aligned}
\end{multline*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.745110+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55cfe9fbe383b753d85baa087558ae0d30e6d68724186653a2eb2ddc2ed5fba4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defin 22 of arXiv:2604.03548, 'Degree-preserving conservative processes and a unified approach for their hydrodynamics'] A probability generator is a linear operator $\mathcal L$ on $C(\Omega)$ satisfying the following properties: 
\begin{itemize}
\item[\textnormal{(a)}] The domain $\mathcal D(\mathcal L)$ is dense in $C_0(\Omega)$. 
\item[\textnormal{(b)}] If $f\in \mathcal D(\mathcal L)$, $\lambda\ge0$ and $g=f-\lambda \mathcal Lf$, then,
\begin{equation*}
\inf_{\eta\in \Omega} f(\eta) \ge \inf_{\eta\in \Omega} g(\eta).  
\end{equation*}
\item[\textnormal{(c)}] $\mathcal R(\lambda-\mathcal L)$ is dense in $C_0(\Omega)$ for all sufficiently small $\lambda>0$. 
\item[\textnormal{(d)}] If $\Omega$ is compact, $1\in \mathcal D(\mathcal L)$ and $\mathcal L1=0$. On the other hand, if $\Omega$ is not compact, for small $\lambda >0$ there exists a sequence $\{f_n\}_{n\in\mathbb N}\subset  \mathcal D(\mathcal L)$ such that $g_n=f_n-\lambda \mathcal Lf_n$ satisfies $\sup_{n}\|g_n\|<+\infty$, and both $f_n$ and $g_n$ converge to 1 pointwise.   \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

