    $\text{supp}(\mu_{\rm d}) \cup \text{supp}(\mu_{\rm a}) \cup \mathcal{R}(\mathcal{X})$. Therefore
    \begin{equation*}
        \text{dim}(\mathcal{R}(\mathcal{Y}^{(\rm d)}) \cup \mathcal{R}(\mathcal{Y}^{(\rm a)})) = \text{dim}(\text{supp}(\mu_{\rm d})) \vee \text{dim}(\text{supp}(\mu_{\rm a})) \vee (4 \wedge d) \text{   a.s.}
    \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:02:08.142430+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_561eb99d00f8f52f1f67778440ae19fbef8629df586febc06bef38c9868d1004","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2608.27630, 'Oriented planar maps and \\ annular mating of trees'] The measure $m(\d\tau)$ in \eqref{eq: MA 01 defn intro} is given by
