{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.248702+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_598ae3caed16ce04f0c7295fa1bf0367911d0f4c21b5996474915ce52da614be","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 34 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] [Level lines of the macroscopic shape]


		For any $a, b, c >0$, $\mq \in \mathbb{R}$ and $0<h<b$, define $\mathcal{U}_{\mq; a, b, c}^h : [0,a+c]\mapsto [0,b+c] $ by setting
		\begin{equation} 
                  \mathcal{U}_{\mq; a, b, c}^h (x) = y\in [0,b+c]: \mathcal{H}_{\mq; a, b, c}(x,y)=h.
		\end{equation}
 If instead $h=0$ or $h=b$, then given $x$ there may exist multiple $y$ satisfying $ \mathcal{H}_{\mq; a, b, c}(x,y)=h$. In that case,  we define
