{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.603985+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5a05e1aad49b1e490244bac61be858a030c46a15446b92415416bdf1a296d33a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 (Heat-kernel representation and closed form) of arXiv:2607.07712, 'Parameter-Space Heat Flow, Gaussian Density Ratios, and Sharp Hermite Truncation Rates'] For the temperature-extended generating function
\(U(\tau,u;v)=\Phi_\tau(u;v)\), the following heat-kernel representation holds.
For \(\tau>0\),
\begin{equation}
U(\tau,u;v)
=
\int_{\mathbb R^d}
\frac{1}{(2\pi\tau)^{d/2}}
\exp\!\left(-\frac{|u-z|^2}{2\tau}\right)
\Phi(z;v)\,dz.

\end{equation}
Moreover, this integral is explicitly equal to
\begin{equation}
U(\tau,u;v)
=
(1+\tau)^{-d/2}
\exp\!\left(
\frac{\tau |v|^2+2u\cdot v-|u|^2}{2(1+\tau)}
\right).

\end{equation}
In particular,
\[
U(0,u;v)=\Phi(u;v)
\]
by taking \(\tau\downarrow0\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:38.636453+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5a07ac73870da5b7ad18e3518558ee437a4332f88a7c26d891464a91e88a02e4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 4 of arXiv:2506.17470, 'The coalescent of a sample from a linear-fractional branching process'] Let $\tau$ be an ultrametric, oriented tree with $n$ tips and node depths $x_1, \dots, x_{n-1}\in \{1,\dots, T\}$ and $x_0=T$. The likelihood function of the parameters $(p,r)$ given the outcome $\{\mathbf{T}_y=\tau\}$ is
   \begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{L}(p, r \mid  \tau, y)=
      \mathbb{P}(\mathbf{T}_y=\tau)=\mathbb{P}(H_y>T)\prod_{i=1}^{n-1} \mathbb{P}(H_y = x_i).
   \end{equation*}
Moreover, the likelihood of $(p,r)$ conditioned on $\{K=n\}$ is given by
      \begin{equation*}
      \mathcal{L}(p, r \mid  \tau, y, n):= \mathbb{P}(\mathbf{T}_y=\tau \mid K = n)=\prod_{i=1}^{n-1} \frac{\mathbb{P}(H_y = x_i)}{\mathbb{P}(H_y\le T)}.
   \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:27:14.894799+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5a0c84d3239736647cc677f0491cce6da0fafaf4338a32398522c305ee5c9f67","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.23989, 'Collision Properties in Discrete and Continuous Time on Bounded-Degree Graphs'] Let $G$ be a bounded-degree connected graph. Then:
