{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.873911+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5b1d8c12de4cc76d19a7660861d0792422d91eaf433a8212632389801600f621","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:char. for ergodic of arXiv:2411.00663, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of invariant capacities and applications'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mu,T)$ be a capacity preserving system. If $\mu$  is subadditive and continuous then the following three statements are equivalent:
	\begin{longlist}
		\item $\mu$ is ergodic;
		\item if $f\in B(\Omega,\mathcal{F})$  is $T$-invariant then $f$ is  constant $\mu$-a.s.;
		\item if $f:\Omega\to \mathbb{R}$ is  $\mathcal{F}$-measurable and  $T$-invariant $\mu$-a.s. then $f$ is  constant $\mu$-a.s.
	\end{longlist}
	Note that (iii) $\Rightarrow$ (ii) $\Rightarrow$ (i) does not need the continuity of $\mu$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:25:39.117363+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5b1edd095d244432aa6b3dac0330ac56242963b4ff8f94c6f5b76607dada3ee7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2606.13147, 'Scaling limits of the single-curve interface and outermost loops in the planar random fiel'] For any constant $\iota>0$
and any integer $k\ge \lfloor-\log_2\iota\rfloor$, let $\mathcal{H}^{\mathrm{aty}}_k(\iota)$ denote the collection of external fields such that \begin{align}
    \sum_{\gamma:\frac{S(\gamma)}{N^{7/4}}\in (2^{k-1},2^k]}\mu^{\pm}_{\Lambda_N,{\epsilon} h}(\gamma)\le e^{-2^{k}}.\nonumber
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:34.578042+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5b22a046665d6c549b521d19a99cac098255837703e145164dceb6bb36cf3a27","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2606.14876, 'High-Order Talagrand and Eldan--Gross Inequalities via Besov-Type Variance Functionals'] For all $k\in \Nbb$, $f\in \Sob^k(\Rbb^n,\gamma_n)$ with $\left\|f\right\|_{\Leb^\infty}\leq 1$, and $p\in [1,2]$,
\begin{equation}
    \left\|\left|\grad^k f\right|\right\|_{\Leb^p}^{p} \geq C^{\text{\rm\ref{thm:high-order-Poincare-type-Gaussian}}}(p)^k\Wop^{\geq k}(f),
\end{equation}
with $C^{\text{\rm\ref{thm:high-order-Poincare-type-Gaussian}}}(p) = C^{\text{\rm\ref{thm:high-order-Poincare-type-Boolean}}}(p) \gtrsim 1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.368301+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5b236e9534ea581cacc278bc2cea5f86954ed7cf208711edfef7c5729f378bfc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2412.01645, 'Regularisation by Gaussian rough path lifts of fractional Brownian motions'] Let $(G^\bullet, G^\circ):  \Omega \times [0,1] \to V \times E$ and $(\mathbb{G}^{\bullet \bullet}, \mathbb{G}^{\circ \bullet}, \mathbb{G}^{\bullet \circ}) : \Omega \times [0, 1]^2 \to (V \otimes V ) \times (E \otimes V) \times (V \otimes E)$. Assume that for all $(s,u,t) \in [0,1]^3_\leq$, $(G^\bullet, \mathbb{G}^{\bullet \bullet})$ satisfies (equation eq:Chen_single_regularity of the paper) and $(G^\bullet, G^\circ, \mathbb{G}^{\bullet \circ}, \mathbb{G}^{\circ \bullet})$ satisfy (equation eq:chen_mixed of the paper). Let $p \geq 2$ and $\tilde{\gamma}_\bullet, \tilde{\gamma}_\circ \in (0,1)$ with $\tilde{\gamma}_\bullet, \tilde{\gamma}_\circ >1/p$. Assume further that  there exist constants $K_{ij}$, $i,j \in \{ \bullet, \circ\}$, such that for all $(s,t)$
    \begin{equs}
        \| G^i_{s,t}\|_{L_p}\leq  K_i |t-s|^{\tilde{\gamma}_i}, \qquad   
        \| \mathbb{G}^{i j }_{s,t}\|_{L_{p/2}}\leq  K_{ij} |t-s|^{\tilde{\gamma}_i+\tilde{\gamma}_j}
    \end{equs}
    for $i,j \in \{ \bullet, \circ\}$ with $(i,j) \neq (\circ, \circ)$. Then for any $\gamma_\bullet< \tilde{\gamma}_\bullet-1/p $, $\gamma_\circ< \tilde{\gamma}_\circ-1/p $, there exists a modification of ${\color{purp}G}:= (G^\bullet, G^\circ,\mathbb{G}^{\bullet \bullet}, \mathbb{G}^{\circ \bullet}, \mathbb{G}^{\bullet \circ} )$ such that with probability one, we have ${\color{purp}G} \in \mathcal{R}^{\gamma_\bullet, \gamma_\circ}$. Moreover, there is a constant $C=C(\gamma_\bullet, \tilde{\gamma}_\bullet, \gamma_\circ, \tilde{\gamma}_\circ, p)$ such that 
    \begin{equs}
        \| [G^i]_{\mathcal{C}^{\gamma_i}} \|_{L_p} \leq C K_i, \qquad \| [\mathbb{G}^{ij}]_{\mathcal{C}^{\gamma_i+\gamma_j}_2} \|_{L_p} \leq  C (K_{ij}+K_iK_j) . 
    \end{equs} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:22:48.887797+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5b239ccf73d17d2929bd21684c2bdd2b9ab1456cdec3c036068ed3466b404a00","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 32 of arXiv:2608.29157, 'Dilator-based analysis of $\KP$'] Fix a linear order $\alpha$.
    Let us define the deduction rules of $\sfu\RS_\alpha$ as follows, where every sequent is in the language $\calL^u_{\alpha+1,\alpha}$, so we identify $\ttOmega$ with $\alpha$ in $\alpha+1 = \alpha\cup\{\ttOmega\}$.
    \begin{center}
    \vspace{1em}
    \begin{mathprooftree}
        \AxiomC{}
        \RightLabel{$(\mathrm{Ax}) \quad (x\in y\in u)$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma,\ x\in y$}
    \end{mathprooftree}
    \hspace{3em}
    \begin{mathprooftree}
        \AxiomC{}
        \RightLabel{$(\mathrm{Ax}) \quad (x\notin y\in u,\ y\in u)$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma,\ x\notin y$}
    \end{mathprooftree}

    \vspace{1em}
    \begin{mathprooftree}
        \AxiomC{$\Gamma,\ A$}
        \AxiomC{$\Gamma,\ B$}
        \RightLabel{$(\land)$}
        \BinaryInfC{$\Gamma,\ A\land B$}
    \end{mathprooftree}
    \hspace{3em}
    \begin{mathprooftree}
        \AxiomC{$\Gamma,\ A$}
        \RightLabel{$(\lor)$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma,\ A\lor B$}
    \end{mathprooftree}
    \hspace{3em}
    \begin{mathprooftree}
        \AxiomC{$\Gamma,\ B$}
        \RightLabel{$(\lor)$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma,\ A\lor B$}
    \end{mathprooftree}

    \vspace{1em}
    \begin{mathprooftree}
        \AxiomC{$\Gamma,\ s\dotin t\land r=s$}
        \RightLabel{$(\in)$ \hspace{2em} ($|s|<|t|$, $r\in t$ not basic.)}
        \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma,\ r\in t$}
    \end{mathprooftree}

    \vspace{1em}
    \begin{mathprooftree}
        \def\fCenter{\vdash}
        \AxiomC{$\cdots \quad \Gamma,\ s\dotin t\to r\neq s \quad \cdots$ ($|s|<|t|$)}
        \RightLabel{$(\notin)$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma,\ r\notin t$}
    \end{mathprooftree}
    \hspace{3em}
    \begin{mathprooftree}
        \AxiomC{$\Gamma,\ s\dotin t \land B(s)$}
        \RightLabel{$(\rmb\exists)$ \hspace{2em} ($|s|<|t|$)}
        \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma,\ \exists x\in t B(x)$}
    \end{mathprooftree}

    \vspace{1em}
    \begin{mathprooftree}
        \AxiomC{$ \cdots \quad \Gamma,\ s\dotin t\to B(s) \quad \cdots$ ($|s|<|t|$)}
        \RightLabel{$(\rmb\forall)$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma,\ \forall x\in t B(x)$}
    \end{mathprooftree}

    \vspace{1em}
    \begin{mathprooftree}
        \AxiomC{$\Gamma,\ A^{\ttL(u)_\ttOmega}$}
        \RightLabel{(Refl) \hspace{2em} ($A$ is a $\Sigma$-formula)}
        \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma,\ \exists z\in \ttL(u)_\ttOmega A^z$}
    \end{mathprooftree}
    \hspace{3em}
    \begin{mathprooftree}
        \def\fCenter{\vdash}
        \AxiomC{$\Gamma,\ A$}
        \AxiomC{$\Gamma,\ \lnot A$}
        \RightLabel{(Cut)}
        \BinaryInfC{$\Gamma$}
    \end{mathprooftree}
    \vspace{1em}
    \end{center}
    Every tree formed by the given rule is an $\sfu\RS_\alpha$-preproof. We call a $\sfu\RS_\alpha$-preproof $\pi$ a \emph{proof} if $\pi$ has no infinite branch.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:30.015451+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5b25e2a3d0c773d593928f43b71593bd24f674446ade8cd8b27347705ad5c9e6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 4 of arXiv:2605.05430, 'Dirichlet problems and exit distributions for the telegraph process and its planar extensi'] For $x\in[a,b]$, it holds that

\begin{align}h_0(x)=\,&\frac{\lambda_0+\lambda_1}{\lambda_0c_1-\lambda_1c_0}\,(x-a)\nonumber\\
&\quad-\frac{(\lambda_0+\lambda_1)(b-a)}{\lambda_0c_1-\lambda_1c_0}\cdot\frac{\lambda_0c_1\,e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)x}-\lambda_1c_0\,e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)a}}{\lambda_0c_1\,e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)b}-\lambda_1c_0\,e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)a}}\nonumber\\
&\quad+\frac{\lambda_0c_1(c_0+c_1)}{\lambda_0c_1-\lambda_1c_0}\cdot\frac{e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)b}-e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)x}}{\lambda_0c_1e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)b}-\lambda_1c_0e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)a}}
\end{align}
and
\begin{align}h_1(x)=\,&\frac{\lambda_0+\lambda_1}{\lambda_0c_1-\lambda_1c_0}\,(x-a)\nonumber\\
&\quad-\lambda_1c_0\,\frac{c_0+c_1+(\lambda_0+\lambda_1)(b-a)}{\lambda_0c_1-\lambda_1c_0}\cdot\frac{e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)x}-e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)a}}{\lambda_0c_1e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)b}-\lambda_1c_0e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)a}}
\end{align}
Consequently, 
\begin{align}h(&x)=\frac{\lambda_0+\lambda_1}{\lambda_0c_1-\lambda_1c_0}\,(x-a)\nonumber\\
&-\frac{(\lambda_0+\lambda_1)(b-a)}{2(\lambda_0c_1-\lambda_1c_0)}\cdot\frac{(\lambda_0c_1+\lambda_1c_0)\,e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)x}-2\lambda_1c_0\,e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)a}}{\lambda_0c_1\,e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)b}-\lambda_1c_0\,e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)a}}\nonumber\\
&+\frac{c_0+c_1}{2(\lambda_0c_1-\lambda_1c_0)}\cdot\frac{\lambda_0c_1\,e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)b}-(\lambda_0c_1+\lambda_1c_0)e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)x}+\lambda_1c_0\,e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)a}}{\lambda_0c_1e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)b}-\lambda_1c_0e^{\left(\frac{\lambda_0}{c_0}-\frac{\lambda_1}{c_1}\right)a}}
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:39:11.666687+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5b29dc80317c348cfff26adacaaab26989a20a68e04e63ba498565c44b330940","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2608.04644, 'Khinchin's and Chung's Laws of the Iterated Logarithm at Time Zero for the Linear Stochast'] Recall that $H$ is defined in \eqref{def:H}. Suppose that  Condition \ref{hypo}  holds  and that $H<1$.  Then, for every fixed $x\in\mathbb{R}^d$, the following assertions hold.
\begin{itemize}
	\item[(1)] For every $t\ge0$,
	\begin{equation}
		\|u(t,x)\|_{L^2(\Omega)}^2
		=
		\textcolor{black}{\widetilde{\kappa}}^2 t^{2H},
	\end{equation}
	where $\textcolor{black}{\widetilde{\kappa}}$ is given by \eqref{def:tilde:kappa}.

	\item[(2)] The process $\{u(t,x)\}_{t\ge0}$ is a centered Gaussian self-similar process with Hurst index $H$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:04:59.431821+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5b2a5bd087b236580b5987e7fb4891179f441dc83baef30d9f582de4662a7c5c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem stopping_quantile_continuity of arXiv:2605.20471, 'Note on the Weak Convergence of Hyperplane $α$-Quantile Functionals and Their Continuity i'] Let $T>0,\alpha \in (0,1),\gamma \in \mathbb{R}^d$. Let $x^n\to x$ in the Skorokhod topology and $x$ such that $ \alpha \notin U(x,T)$. Define:
	\begin{equation}
	x\mapsto \tau_{M_{T,\alpha}}(x):=\begin{cases}
	\inf\bigg\{t>0:\sup_{s\leq t}(\gamma \cdot x)_s\geq  M_{T,\alpha}(x)\bigg\}&\textrm{ if }\,M_{T,\alpha}(x)>\gamma \cdot x_0\\
	0&\textrm{ if }\,M_{T,\alpha}(x)= \gamma \cdot x_0\\
	\inf\bigg\{t>0:\inf_{s\leq t}(\gamma \cdot x)_s\leq  M_{T,\alpha}(x)\bigg\}&\textrm{ if }\,M_{T,\alpha}(x)< \gamma \cdot x_0\\
	\end{cases}
	\end{equation}
	Suppose that $M_{T,\alpha}(x)\neq \gamma \cdot x_0$. Then:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $\liminf_{n}\tau_{M_{T,\alpha}}(x^n)\geq \tau_{M_{T,\alpha}}(x)$;
		\item If, equivalently, $y\mapsto \frac{1}{T}\int_0^T\mathbf{1}_{\{z:\gamma \cdot z\leq y\}}(x_s)ds$ is continuous or $\beta \mapsto M_{T,\beta}(x)$ is strictly increasing and additionally $\alpha\in  \{\alpha:\lim_{\beta \to \alpha}\tau_{M_{T,\beta}}(x)=\tau_{M_{T,\alpha}}(x)\}$, then we have $\tau_{M_{T,\alpha}}(x^n)\to \tau_{M_{T,\alpha}}(x)$.
	\end{enumerate} 
	In fact, if the conditions of (2) are satisfied, then we also have $$(M_{T,\alpha}(x^n),\tau_{M_{T,\alpha}}(x^n))\to (M_{T,\alpha}(x),\tau_{M_{T,\alpha}}(x))$$
	in the usual sense.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.224744+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5b30ba0763a85af07b8802b9a38fdbdf22fabc5ea3faef1801df7261c44e603c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor of arXiv:2411.16398, 'Large Deviations of Cover Time of Tori in Dimensions $d\geq 3$'] For all $\gamma \in (0,1)$,
