{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:15:34.405760+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c6cef8af000ba3c93106530acf0136308e76aaaefd1b3151f239594fca5e6bd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 (Beauville's conjecture for smooth toric varieties) of arXiv:2609.03282, 'Characteristic-free characterizations of projective space for smooth toric varieties'] Let $X=X_\Sigma$ be an $n$-dimensional smooth projective toric variety over $\Bbbk$, and let $\cL$ be an ample line bundle on $X$. Suppose that
\[
H^0\left(X,\bigwedge^pT_X\otimes\cL^{-p}\right)\neq0
\]
for some integer $1\leq p\leq n$. Then one of the following holds:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\roman*)}]
\item $(X,\cL)\simeq(\PP^n_\Bbbk,\cO_{\PP^n_\Bbbk}(1))$;
\item $p=n=1$ and $(X,\cL)\simeq(\PP^1_\Bbbk,\cO_{\PP^1_\Bbbk}(2))$;
\item $p=n=2$ and $(X,\cL)\simeq(\PP^1_\Bbbk\times\PP^1_\Bbbk,\cO(1,1))$.
\end{enumerate}
Conversely, each of these polarized varieties, with the indicated values of $p$, satisfies the nonvanishing condition. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.547790+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c6e9777fb21bd7b654f1fce050c4c7f3b9132555e8a38eba7cc15beaef85dd2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 31 (The Hausdorff dimension) of arXiv:2604.01852, 'The on/off Brownian snake'] Let $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ be a measurable set. For $\delta>0$ we call a $\delta$-cover any collection of open sets $U_j, j \in J$, such that 
    \begin{equation*}
        A \subseteq \bigcup_{j \in J}U_j; \hspace{0.5cm} \text{sup}_{j \in J}\text{Leb}(U_j) < \delta.
    \end{equation*}
    We define $\mathcal{H}^s_\delta(A) = \inf \sum_{j \in J}|U_j|^s$ where the infimum is taken over all $\delta$-covers, and $\mathcal{H}^s(A) = \lim_{\delta \rightarrow 0} \mathcal{H}^s_\delta(A)$. The \emph{Hausdorff dimension} of $A$, which we denote $\text{dim}(A)$, is defined to be the unique number such that
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{H}^s(A) = \infty \text{   for all   } 0 \leq s < \text{dim}(A); \hspace{0.25cm} \text{   and   } \hspace{0.25cm} \mathcal{H}^s(A) = 0 \text{   for all   } \text{dim}(A) < s < \infty.
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.600529+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c7958a152dd23d5fa3933b264c7575585a531023c17c14a513125cebeb1fe92","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:area of arXiv:2606.21049, '\textsc{Locality of rough path lifts}'] Let $\gamma\in (0,1]$ and $(X,Y) \in C^\gamma \times C^\gamma$.
An \emph{area process} on $(X,Y)$ is an element
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{A}(X,Y) = (\mathbb{A}_{s,t}(X,Y))_{(s,t) \in \triangle} \in B
\end{equation*}
such that, for all $0 \leq s \leq u \leq t \leq 1$, one has the \emph{Chen identity}
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{A}_{s,t}(X,Y) = \mathbb{A}_{s,u}(X,Y) + \mathbb{A}_{u,t}(X,Y) + \frac12(X_{s,u}Y_{u,t} - Y_{s,u}X_{u,t})\;.
\end{equation}
For a subset $P \subset C^\gamma \times C^\gamma$, an area process on $P$ is a map $\mathbb{A} \colon P \to B$ such that $\mathbb{A}(X,Y)$ is an area process on $(X,Y)$ for all $(X,Y) \in P$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:15:08.997987+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5c7aaf01e1aee63b5f26483611e6eb8e5406096ada0506616dd53db60996dea4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop conv of arXiv:2411.10829, 'Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble and its Laplace transform'] Take $m\in \mathbb N$, $\vec \mathbf k \in \mathbb R^m_+ $, and $\vec \boldsymbol{\tau}\in \mathbb R_{\ge 0}^m$ such that $\boldsymbol{\tau}_1\le \cdots \le \boldsymbol{\tau}_m$, and $C_1>0$.
For any large enough $N\in \mathbb N$, we take $\vec k$ and $N\ge N_1\ge \cdots \ge N_m$ satisfying (equation eq_x1 of the paper).
Then as $N\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}   
