	\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:00.765670+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5d1abc75f41a64ac72e5c1d7851dc7647bc4d1e10fe3d32fc2282abb72723618","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 17 of arXiv:2512.04807, 'Existence and uniqueness of the canonical Brownian motion in non-simple conformal loop ens'] Let $\vertexset_1,\vertexset_2$ be two finite sets with $\vertexset_1 \cap \vertexset_2 = \{z\}$. For $i=1,2$, let $w_i\colon \vertexset_i \times \vertexset_i \to [0,\infty)$ be edge conductances, each inducing a connected subgraph, and let $R_i\colon \vertexset_i \times \vertexset_i \to [0,\infty)$ be the associated effective resistance. Define $R\colon (\vertexset_1 \cup \vertexset_2) \times (\vertexset_1 \cup \vertexset_2) \to [0,\infty)$ by
 \[
 R(x,y) = \begin{cases}
 R_1(x,y) ,& x,y \in \vertexset_1,\\
 R_2(x,y) ,& x,y \in \vertexset_2,\\
 R_1(x,z)+R_2(z,y) ,& x \in \vertexset_1,\, y \in \vertexset_2 . 
 \end{cases}
 \]
 Then $R$ is the effective resistance associated with $w$ given by
 \begin{equation}
  w(x,y) =
  \begin{cases}
   w_1(x,y) ,& x,y \in \vertexset_1,\\
   w_2(x,y) ,& x,y \in \vertexset_2,\\
   0 & \text{otherwise.}
  \end{cases}
 \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.206656+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5d1f680d96e169982d0f76ad852f9ee8ca934aa6d09e30a38a663253750640a1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi Equ:Weight-cond of arXiv:2608.29013, 'Weighted Homology and Cohomology of Weighted Polyhedra'] A simplex $\sigma$ in a weighted simplicial complex $(K,\mu)$ is called \emph{divisibly weighted} if all the vertices of $\sigma$ can be ordered as
 $\{v_0,\cdots, v_k\}$ such that
   \begin{equation} 
     \mu(v_0)\, |\, \cdots\, | \, \mu(v_k).
   \end{equation}
  If all the simplices in $(K,\mu)$ are divisibly weighted, 
  the weight function $\mu$ is called \emph{divisible}
  and $(K,\mu)$ is called a \emph{divisibly weighted simplicial complex}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:47:40.734476+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5d296528d3b3d3cc13b4f8d2e1e8cca7ab31254f3cafb4308617113af65de53d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Nash of arXiv:2604.20008, 'Mixing times of Langevin dynamics for \\ spiked matrix models'] For $f\in W^1_2(\mathbf S_N)\cap L^1(\mathbf S_N)$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{N(q-2)}{N-1} \left\lVert\nabla f\right\rVert_2^2+\left\lVertf\right\rVert_2^2\geq \left\lVertf\right\rVert_q^2\geq \left\lVertf\right\rVert_2^{\frac{2N+2}{N-1}}\left\lVertf\right\rVert_1^{-\frac{4}{N-1}}\,.
    \end{align*}
    Equivalently
    \begin{align*}
        \left\lVert\nabla f\right\rVert_2^2\geq &\,\frac{N-1}{N(q-2)}\Big(-\left\lVertf\right\rVert_2^2+\left\lVertf\right\rVert_2^{\frac{2N+2}{N-1}}\left\lVertf\right\rVert_1^{-\frac{4}{N-1}}\Big)\nonumber\\
        >&\,c_2N\Big(-\left\lVertf\right\rVert_2^2+\left\lVertf\right\rVert_2^{\frac{2N+2}{N-1}}\left\lVertf\right\rVert_1^{-\frac{4}{N-1}}\Big)\,,
    \end{align*}
    for $c_2$ a constant independent of $N$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.048817+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5d2cc46c0926e738ae98b45a4adbd6876ed00b225427f9a65848ca3bbaedd33c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2606.16083, 'Eyring--Kramers asymptotics for infinite-dimensional stochastic gradient systems'] Assume that Assumptions~1 and~2 hold, that $\eta_x\in\mathcal K_A$ for every
$x\in \mathcal T_h$, and that $U\in C^3(B)$. Then the following asymptotics hold  as $\beta\to \infty$.

