in \eqref{primitive:eq:Dq}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.098577+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5d51fe32a1717587e27eb0f96947d952a5a7b14c264a1a7a00e066e831aec929","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2608.28198, 'Performative Privacy: When Differential Privacy Maximizes Utility'] Let $\mathcal{M}$ be a Gaussian mechanism for mean estimation over a dataset $Z_{t}$ of size $N_t$, which adds noise scaled to the population such that the variance is $\frac{\gamma^2}{N_t} I$. Let $P^{\mathcal{M}}_{\text{in}}(\cdot\mid Z_t)$ and $P^{\mathcal{M}}_{\text{out}}(\cdot\mid Z_t)$ denote the conditional distributions of the mechanism's output on adjacent datasets $Z_{t}$ and $Z_{t} \setminus \{z\}$, respectively. For any order $\alpha > 1$, the Rényi divergence between these distributions is given by:
\begin{align*}
D_\alpha(P^{\mathcal{M}}_{\text{in}}(\cdot\mid Z_t) || P^{\mathcal{M}}_{\text{out}}(\cdot \mid Z_t))
&=
\frac{\alpha N_t}{2\gamma^2 (N_t-1)^2}
\|z-\mu_z\|_2^2
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.567042+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5d52cce987a9c98267cc835f7d2dcdeaaa3732275f81e2f9f8db30c6b4581adc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem T:lpp-horizons of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] \begin{enumerate}
    \item Let $\theta > 0, \alpha \in (-\theta, \infty)$. Then there exists a random function $\mathcal{H}^{\mathsf{EL}}$ such that $-\mathcal{H}^{\mathsf{EL}}\in \mathcal{D}((-\theta, \alpha \wedge 0], \mathbb{Z}, \alpha \wedge 0)$, and for any finite set $I = (\gamma_1 < \cdots < \gamma_k) \subset (-\theta, \alpha \wedge 0]$, the restriction $\mathcal{H}^{\mathsf{EL}}|_I$ is equal in law to the exponential LPP joint stationary process defined above. In particular, $\mathcal{H}^{\mathsf{EL}}$ gives a coupling of all the (extended) half-space exponential LPP joint stationary processes identified above. We call $\mathcal{H}^{\mathsf{EL}}$ the \textbf{(extended) half-space exponential horizon}.
    \item Let $\theta \in (0, 1), \alpha \in (0, 1/\theta)$. Then there exists a random function $\mathcal{H}^{\mathsf{GL}}\in \mathcal{D}([\alpha \vee 1, 1/\theta), \mathbb{Z}, \alpha \vee 1)$ such that for any finite set $I = (\gamma_1 < \cdots < \gamma_k) \subset [\alpha \vee 1, 1/\theta)$, the restriction $\mathcal{H}^{\mathsf{GL}}|_I$ is equal in law to the geometric LPP joint stationary process defined above. In particular, $\mathcal{H}^{\mathsf{GL}}$ gives a coupling of all the (extended) half-space geometric LPP joint stationary processes identified above. We call $\mathcal{H}^{\mathsf{GL}}$ the \textbf{(extended) half-space geometric horizon}. 
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.581015+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5d540b0cfcf2dda21cd83d7c042ecf387943537ca47573e18c2b8ed104544f5c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition solution definition (rho u) of arXiv:2605.16963, 'Stochastic Euler Equations with Pseudo-differential Noise: Continuous and Discontinuous Pe'] Let $\tau^*$ be a stopping time satisfying $\mathbb P(\tau^*>0)=1$, and let $((\rho,u),\tau^{*})$ be a progressively measurable process on $\mathbb H_{\mathbf{r}}^s$ defined for $t\in[0,\tau^*)$. We call $((\rho,u),\tau^{*})$ a maximal $\mathbb H_{\mathbf{r}}^s$ classical solution to (equation Target problem (rho u) of the paper) if the following conditions hold ${\mathbb P}\text{-}{\rm a.s.}$: 





		\begin{enumerate}[label={\rm (\arabic*)},leftmargin=0.79cm]\setlength\itemsep{0.2em}

			\item  (equation Target problem (rho u) of the paper)  holds in $C(\mathbb K^d)$ for all $t\in[0,\tau^*)$;
			\item  $\sup_{[0,T]}\|(\mathbf{r}(\rho),u)(t)\|_{\mathbb H^s}<\infty$ for every $T<\tau^*$;
			\item $ 
			\limsup_{t\uparrow\tau^*}\|(\mathbf{r}(\rho), u)(t)\|_{\mathbb H^s}=\infty\  \text{on}\  \{\tau^*<\infty\}$.
		\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:52.745470+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5d5d8ee64cce3ab2097ebe2593edb758c4f0a84b91cd1e919876f69ce7d3c49f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 27 of arXiv:2506.10511, '2506.10511'] For any $\alpha, b>0$ and  any connected set $\Lambda=\{{\bm k}_1,\cdots,{\bm k}_n\}\subset G$, we say that $\Lambda$ is $(\alpha,b)$-good if at least $n/(2\cdot 3^d)$ of its indices $j\in[1,n]_\mathds{Z}$ satisfy the following conditions:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[(1)] For each $P\in\mathcal{C}_{\delta,L}(\Lambda)$, if $({\bm u}_{{\bm k}_j,1}, {\bm v}_{{\bm k}_j,1},{\bm u}_{{\bm k}_j,2},{\bm v}_{{\bm k}_j,2})$ is a $V_{3\delta L}({\bm k}_j)$-special pair of edges such that $P$ first uses $\langle {\bm u}_{{\bm k}_j,1}, {\bm v}_{{\bm k}_j,1}\rangle$ to enter $V_{\delta L}({\bm k}_j)$ and then does not leave $V_{3\delta L}({\bm k}_j)$ until it uses $\langle {\bm u}_{{\bm k}_j,2}, {\bm v}_{{\bm k}_j,2}\rangle$, then it satisfies
		\begin{equation*}
			|{\bm v}_{{\bm k}_j,1}- {\bm u}_{{\bm k}_j,2}|\geq \alpha\delta L \quad \text{and}\quad  D({\bm v}_{{\bm k}_j,1}, {\bm u}_{{\bm k}_j,2}; V_{3\delta L}({\bm k}_j)) \geq (b\alpha \delta L)^\theta.	
		\end{equation*}

		\item[(2)] For any two distinct indices ${j}_1$ and ${ j}_2$, we have ${\bm k}_{{ j}_1} - {\bm k}_{{ j}_2} \in (3\delta L)\mathds{Z}^d$.
	\end{enumerate}
	If $\Lambda$ is not $(\alpha,b)$-good, then we say that $\Lambda$ is $(\alpha,b)$-bad.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:09.912862+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5d6522932bf75225efc0256bc0073d3e3536c0cf005ee1eae00ac9aec2b2ce8e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2603.26936, 'Sharp Riemannian heat kernel estimates on the cut locus and the Parabolic Anderson model'] Suppose M has positive injectivity radius (meaning it is bounded from below by a positive constant) and sectional curvature bounded from above; note that this always holds if $M$ is compact. Then we can find constants $\zeta\in(0,1)$ and $D>0$ depending only on these two bounds (and with $D$ less than the injectivity radius of $M$) such that, for any $x,y\in M$ with $\bm{d}(x,y)< D$,
