{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:49.906717+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5de57ab784801a831e551c89a531480f39937896560815087758b714b7f0668f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:log-normality-SHF of arXiv:2511.08479, '\Large From disordered systems to the Critical 2D Stochastic Heat Flow'] For a Euclidean ball $B(x,\delta) := \big\{y \in \mathbb{R}^2 \colon \ |y-x| < \delta \big\}$, denote the uniform distribution over it by
${\ensuremath{\mathcal U}} _{B(x,\delta)}(\cdot) := \frac{1}{\pi \delta^2} \, \mathrm{1}_{B(x,\delta)}(\cdot)$.
Then for any $t > 0$ and $x\in\mathbb{R}^2$, the following convergence in distribution holds:
\begin{equation}
	\forall \varrho \in (0,\infty) \colon \qquad
	\mathscr{Z}_{t}^{\vartheta} \big({\ensuremath{\mathcal U}} _{B(x,\sqrt{e^{\varrho \vartheta}})}\big)
	\ \xrightarrow[\ \vartheta \to -\infty \ ]{d} \
