{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:31.000898+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5e785fc606ce3da93081a72c83c649455d8a69fc7e0b8518fe3c5e261e829d5d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 49 (Factor capture and positive exhaustion) of arXiv:2608.12492, 'Weak Limits of Wiener Chaos: Primitive--Fock Classification and Hilbert--Stein Extraction'] One has
\begin{equation}
 0\le\widehat N_q\le QR^2\Id.

\end{equation}
Applying the top-eigenspace rule to
$\widehat E_q=R_q^*\widehat N_qR_q$ gives
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{a,q}\norm{P_{\widehat V_*}r_{a,q}}^2
 \ge
 \widehat\kappa_{Q,d}
 \frac{\widehat{\mathfrak H}_Q(A,r)}{R^2},
 \qquad
 \boxed{\widehat\kappa_{Q,d}=\frac1{dQ^2}}.

\end{equation}
At extraction level $J$, stop when
\begin{equation}
 \widehat{\mathfrak H}_Q(A,r)
 \le
 \widehat\delta_J
 :=
 \frac{dQ^2R^4}{J}.

\end{equation}
Fewer than $J$ promotions occur, and along the nested trajectory
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{j\ge0}
 \widehat{\mathfrak H}_Q(A^{[j]},r^{[j]})
 \le dQ^2R^4,
 \qquad
 \widehat{\mathfrak H}_Q(A^{[J]},r^{[J]})
 \longrightarrow0.

\end{equation}
Consequently every proper residual--residual raw contraction and every
active--residual raw contraction tends to zero, while residual covariance is retained.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.016603+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5e78e0dbc83cb77fa0ee888426675bd1fb4b72ee17f3d1b240f68d897058d890","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:positive_parity_implies_signed_monotonicity of arXiv:2605.15401, 'Noise Tradeoffs, Stationary Information Flow, and Structural Balance in Unit-Birth Network'] Assume that for each ordered pair $j\neq i$ such that $f_i$ depends nontrivially on $x_j$, the map $x_j\mapsto f_i(x_{-i})$ is globally either nondecreasing or nonincreasing. Then the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item every cycle in the underlying signed graph, obtained by ignoring edge orientation, has positive parity, that is, contains an even number of negative edges;

    \item the signed interaction graph is structurally balanced;
    \item there exists $\sigma\in\{-1,1\}^N$ such that
    Assumption~(ass:signed_monotonicity of the paper) holds.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, every structurally balanced interaction graph yields a
signature $\sigma$ for which (equation eq:signed_monotonicity of the paper) holds. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.822636+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5e7966d455432b896971a0e8e1cc6d02937bfaf3a18289853ecac8bf3e16c1e2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Def: elementary differential trees of arXiv:2607.18635, 'Remainders of generalised Taylor expansions and a priori bounds for rough differential equ'] For $\sigma\in C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n,\mathbb{R}^n\otimes (\mathbb{R}^d)^\ast)$ we inductively define the \emph{elementary differential} $\Upsilon:{\mathcal T}\to C( \mathbb{R}^n;\mathbb{R}^n)$ by imposing that, for all $z\in\mathbb{R}^n$, 
		$[\Upsilon_{\bullet_j}(z)]^i:=\sigma^i_k(z)$, $i=1,\ldots,n$, $j=1,\ldots,d$, and, for all $\tau_1,\ldots, \tau_k\in{\mathcal T}$,
		\begin{align}
			&[\Upsilon_{[\tau_1\cdots \tau_k]_j}(z)]^i:=\sum_{i_1,\ldots,i_k=1}^n \frac{\partial^k\sigma^i_j(z)}{\partial z_{i_1}
				\cdots \partial z_{i_k}} \, [\Upsilon_{\tau_1}(z)]^{i_1}\cdots [\Upsilon_{\tau_k}(z)]^{i_k}\,.
		\end{align}
		We extend it to a map $\Upsilon:{\mathcal F}\to C( \mathbb{R}^n;\mathbb{R}^n)$ as a morphism with respect to the forest product such that $\Upsilon_\one=1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:52.740546+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5e7ce19686e9d0ee7c3e6084237a289d1ac0f2ee552b8d979633e648c4eab895","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 26 of arXiv:2506.10494, 'Geometric Jensen-Shannon Divergence Between Gaussian Measures On Hilbert Space'] Let $S,T \in \SymHS(\Hcal)_{< I}$. Let
	$\mu = \Ncal(0,Q)$, $Q = C_0^{1/2}(I-S)C_0^{1/2}$, $\nu = \Ncal(0,R)$, $R = C_0^{1/2}(I-T)C_0^{1/2}$.
	Then 
	\begin{align}

		&\left\la \log\left\{{\frac{d\mu}{d\mu_0}(x)}\right\}, \log\left\{{\frac{d\nu}{d\mu_0}(x)}\right\}\right\ra_{\Lcal^2(\Hcal,\mu_0)} =  \frac{1}{2}\la S(I-S)^{-1}, T(I-T)^{-1}\ra_{\HS}
		\nonumber
