{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:04:06.982402+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f5777039350a5513b20c6058a441bedf0d68d9fb788e6afce8961fc6929705d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:thm1 of arXiv:2607.26551, 'An RDT based confirmation of Lehner's formula for Kronecker-Gaussian matrices'] For large $n\in{\mathbb N}$ and fixed $k,l\in{\mathbb N}$, let $G_{i}\in{\mathbb R}^{n\times n}$ and $G_{i}^{(1)}\in{\mathbb R}^{k\times n},i=1,\dots,l$ be independent  standard normal matrices. For deterministic $A_i\in{\mathbb R}^{k\times k}$ such that $A_i=A_i^T\succeq 0,i=1,\dots,l$ and fixed $R$ such that $\mbox{Tr}(R^TR)=1$, let $F_i$ be as in 
((eq:inteq1ab0b0 of the paper)). Set
\begin{eqnarray}

 L & = &  
 \max_{X\in{\mathbb R}^{n\times k},X^TX=I} \sum_{i=1}^{l}  \sqrt{2}\mbox{Tr}(G_i^{(1)} X F_i)   .
\end{eqnarray}
Let $\xi$ be as in ((eq:kronrep6 of the paper)) and ((eq:inteq1ab0 of the paper)).  One then has  
\begin{eqnarray}

\lim_{n\rightarrow \infty}  \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}  {\mathbb E} \xi   \leq  \lim_{n\rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}} {\mathbb E} L,
\end{eqnarray}
with the righthand side being the so-called random dual.","status":"unverified"}

