{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.330367+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5fd42855f11d5596d0b9821aac74d8ed747ea0bee3d4c10cae9adb70c12ec376","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition def:couplingmatrices of arXiv:2606.01937, 'Stationary fluctuations for an exclusion process with mass and energy conservation'] Define the transformation matrix 
    \begin{equation*}
        R = \begin{pmatrix}
             \mathfrak{c}_1 & 1 \\
             \mathfrak{c}_2 & 1
        \end{pmatrix}\qquad \Longleftrightarrow\qquad R^{-1} = \frac{1}{\mathfrak{c}_1 - \mathfrak{c}_2}\begin{pmatrix}
            1 & -1 \\
            -\mathfrak{c}_2 & \mathfrak{c}_1
        \end{pmatrix}.
    \end{equation*}
    so that $R\mathbf{J}R^{-1}=\mathrm{diag}(\lambda_1,\lambda_2)$. Define also the coupling matrices 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbf{G}^i = \frac12 (R^{-1})^\top (\mathfrak{c}_i\mathbf{H}^1+\mathbf{H}^2)R^{-1} 
    \end{equation}
    for $i\in\{1,2\}$. Then, for any $\varphi\in\mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R} )$, any $t\in [0,T]$ and any $\varepsilon >0$, the term $\mathscr{B}_t^{N,i,\varepsilon}(\varphi )$ rewrites as 
    \begin{align}
        \mathscr{B}_t^{N,i,\varepsilon}(\varphi ) & = N^{a-\gamma -\frac32} \mathbf{G}_{11}^i\int_0^t\frac1N \sum_{x\in\mathbb{Z}} \mathcal{Z}_s^{N,1}\left( \iota_\varepsilon \big( \cdot\, ;\tfrac{x-(v_1-v_i)N^as}{N} \big)\right)^2 \nabla_N\varphi\Big( \frac xN\Big)\,\mathrm{d} s \\
        & \quad +N^{a-\gamma-\frac32}(\mathbf{G}_{12}^i +\mathbf{G}_{21}^i)\int_0^t\frac1N\sum_{x\in\mathbb{Z}} \mathcal{Z}_s^{N,1}\left(\iota_\varepsilon \big( \cdot\, ; \tfrac{x-(v_1-v_i)N^as}{N}\big)\right) \notag \\
        & \hspace{6cm}\times \mathcal{Z}_s^{N,2}\left(\iota_\varepsilon \big(\cdot\, ; \tfrac{x-(v_2-v_i)N^as}{N}\big)\right) \nabla_N\varphi\Big( \frac xN\Big)\,\mathrm{d} s \\ 
        & \quad + N^{a-\gamma -\frac32} \mathbf{G}_{22}^i\int_0^t \frac1N\sum_{x\in\mathbb{Z}} \mathcal{Z}_s^{N,2}\left(\iota_\varepsilon \big(\cdot\, ; \tfrac{x-(v_2-v_i)N^as}{N} \big)\right)^2 \nabla_N\varphi\Big( \frac xN\Big)\,\mathrm{d} s 
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.731208+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5fd7cc3b991aaaa658a491b1f3a815f1266bbc716fb1959c23c0e3814ba9fb4e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 14 of arXiv:2609.02732, 'Conservation of mass for solutions of Leibenson's equation on Riemannian Manifolds'] Suppose that the manifold $M$ admits the Sobolev inequality \begin{equation}\left(\int_{M}{|f|^{p\kappa} d\mu}\right)^{1/\kappa}\leq C\int_{M}{|\nabla f|^{p}d\mu}\quad \forall~f\in W^{1, p}(M),\end{equation}  with some Sobolev exponent $\kappa>1$. Let $\rho$ be a positive function on $M$ and let $u$ be a non-negative bounded weak solution of $\rho\partial _{t}u=\Delta _{p}u^{q}$ in $M\times [0, \infty)$, with initial function $u(\cdot, 0)=u_{0}\in L^{\zeta}(M, \rho d\mu)\cap L^{\infty}(M)$ for some $\zeta\geq1$. If $\rho$ is bounded and satisfies \begin{equation}\left|\left|\rho\right|\right|_{L^{\theta}(M, \mu)}<\infty,\end{equation} where \begin{equation}\theta=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
			\frac{\kappa}{\kappa-1-\frac{D}{\zeta-D}}, & \text{if }\kappa\geq \frac{\zeta}{\zeta-D}~\textnormal{and}~\zeta> 1, \\ 
			\infty, & \text{if }\kappa< \frac{\zeta}{\zeta-D}.
		\end{array}
		\right.\end{equation} Then $u$ has a finite extinction time in $M$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.710720+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5fd7f8a20b3b61c7284d7b1aa056f644865f9f966a149284141cfc5dea77776c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theoremHP of arXiv:2604.11212, 'Sofic measures'] Let~$Y$ be a shift space over the alphabet~$A$.
An invariant probability measure on~$Y$ has a non-negative linear representation if and only if it is sofic.
In this case, the linear representation can be chosen such that
\begin{enumerate}[label=\rm(\roman*)]
\item the matrix~$M=\sum_{a\in A}\varphi(a)$ is stochastic;
\item the vector~$\lambda$ is stochastic and~$\gamma$ has all its components equal to~$1$;
