{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:24:34.399602+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63051dc18583007f4ace30bf216a8d57cd0e29c42881fee54cd29cb944ce1d74","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Dfntn 8 of arXiv:2608.31054, 'Revisiting Picard's proof of the Picard--Weber--Weck selection theorem'] A set $\Omega \subseteq \R^d$ is a \textbf{global (strong) Lipschitz domain} if there exist an open cube $Q = (0,1)^d$ and a Lipschitz function $\Phi: \R^d \rightarrow \R^d $ that maps $Q$ bijectively to $\Omega$, and its restriction to $Q$, still denoted by
