{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.685911+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63c03c7a91792fd85a34482f99daa28cf00a6ea168cd2c7d8ae5df2b5c80d551","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Mainthm t:1.4i of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] Assume $(r_N)_{N \geq 1}$ is as in~(equation e:1.11i of the paper). Then,
with the underlying space is as in~(equation e:1.10i of the paper),
\begin{equation}

\widehat{\eta}_{N,r_N}
\underset{N \to \infty} \Longrightarrow \ \widehat{\eta}_{\rm D} \,,
\quad ; \qquad
	\widehat{\eta}_{\rm D} \,\big|\, \widehat{\mathcal Z}_{\rm D} \sim\  \text{\rm PPP}\bigl(\widehat{\mathcal Z}_{\rm D}(\text{\rm d}\mkern0.5mu x)\otimes\text{\rm e}\mkern0.7mu^{-\alpha s}\text{\rm d}\mkern0.5mu s\otimes\nu({\rm d} \omega)\bigr) \,,
\end{equation}
where $\alpha$ and $\nu$ as in~(equation e:1.10i of the paper), and $\widehat{\mathcal Z}_{\rm D}$ is a random Radon measure on ${\rm D}$.
The law of $\widehat{\mathcal Z}_{\rm D}$ is uniquely defined via the relation:
\begin{equation}

	\widehat{\mathcal Z}_{\rm D} {\rm e}^{\alpha \psi_{\rm D} \overline{h}_{\rm D}} \overset{{\rm d}} = \mathcal Z_{\rm D} \,,
\end{equation}
where $\overline{h}_{\rm D}$ is as in~(equation e:1.7p of the paper) and independent of $\widehat{\mathcal Z}_{\rm D}$, $\psi_{\rm D}: {\rm D} \to \mathbb{R}$ is the deterministic field:
\begin{equation}

	\psi_{\rm D}(\cdot) : =  {\rm Leb}({\rm D}) \frac{\int_{\rm D} G_{\rm D}(\cdot,z)\, {\rm d} x}{\int_{{\rm D} \times {\rm D}} G_{\rm D}(x,y)\, {\rm d} x {\rm d} y} \,,
\end{equation}
and $\mathcal Z_{\rm D}$ is as in~(equation eq:deflqgm of the paper). The joint law of $\overline{h}_{\rm D}$ and $\mathcal Z_{\rm D}$ as determined by~(equation e:1104.14n of the paper) is the same as that in the coupling of Theorem~(t:1.2i of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.600529+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63c35e7e03538fef7c7482357f5c5a084a5db6ce0d140e458944f876f6a1c6af","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:scalingreduction of arXiv:2606.11248, 'Persistent Homology of the Planar Wiener Sausage: Brownian Scaling and a Logarithmic Expec'] Suppose that there exist a normalization $a(T)$ and a nonnegative function $f$ on $(0,\infty)$ such that, for every fixed $r>0$,
\begin{equation}
