{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.163084+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_645ebc234a041417e7ddb7759f3599bfacb68e894da3163556a86e79e4053172","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:concentration of arXiv:2603.00773, 'Long-time $L^p$ Wasserstein contraction for diffusion processes without global dissipativi'] Under Assumptions~(Assum:basic of the paper), assume moreover that the contraction~(equation eq:contraction_def of the paper) holds with $p=1$ for some $C,\lambda>0$. Denote by $\mu_\infty$ the invariant measure of the diffusion process~(equation eq:EDS_X of the paper) (whose existence and uniqueness follow from~(equation eq:contraction_def of the paper)). Let $X$ solve~(equation eq:EDS_X of the paper) with $X_0\sim \nu_0$. Assume that $\nu_0$ satisfies $T_1(C_0)$ for some $C_0>0$. Then, for all Lipschitz $\varphi:\mathbb R^d\rightarrow \mathbb R$ with Lipschitz constant $L_\varphi>0$, and all $t>0$,
 \begin{equation}

\mathbb E_{\nu_0} \left( \exp \left(  \int_0^t \left[ \varphi(X_s) - \mathbb E\left( \varphi(X_s)\right) \right]  \text{d} s  \right)\right) \leqslant   \exp\left(  \left( C_0+  2 t |\sigma|^2 \right)   \frac{ C^2 L_\varphi^2 }{2 \lambda^2}\right)\,,
 \end{equation}
 and
  \begin{equation}

\mathbb E_{\nu_0} \left( \exp \left(  \int_0^t   \varphi(X_s)   \text{d} s  \right)\right) \leqslant  A e^{r t}  \,,
 \end{equation}
 with
 \[A= \exp\left(   \frac{C_0 C^2  L_\varphi^2 }{2 \lambda^2}  +   \frac{ L_\varphi C}{\lambda} \mathcal W_1(\nu,\mu_\infty) \right)\,, \qquad		r =   \mu_\infty(\varphi) +   \frac{  |\sigma|^2 C^2  L_\varphi^2 }{\lambda^2} \,.\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:44:39.752028+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_645f262324604036e06e1ed74adc2afd34f478ab379d48ed2bf4d0743e838559","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 33 of arXiv:2608.29872, 'Local Laws and Outlier Eigenvalues for Spiked Separable Covariance Matrices'] If $x\ne 0$ is not an eigenvalue of $\mathcal Q_1$, then it is an eigenvalue of $\widetilde{\mathcal Q}_1$ if and only if
