{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.813134+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_66eeac43ab731bb9d0f98e1957ebbe4546f0a5e408777a1667cf61f214ead9db","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:geodesics of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] For $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$, a \emph{geodesic}  from $(s,x)$ to $(t,y)$ in the directed landscape
    is a continuous function $\Pi:[s,t]\to \mathbb{R}$ such that $\Pi(s)=x,\Pi(t)=y$, and
    \begin{align}
        \mathcal{L}(s,x;t,y) = \int_s^t d\mathcal{L}\circ \Pi.
    \end{align}
    Equivalently, $\Pi$ is a geodesic if for all $k\in\mathbb{N}$ and all $s=s_0<s_1<\cdots<s_k=t$,
    \begin{align}
        \int_s^t d\mathcal{L}\circ \Pi
        =\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \mathcal{L}(s_i,\Pi(s_i); s_{i+1}, \Pi(s_{i+1})).
    \end{align}
    By [dov], it holds almost surely that for every $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$, there exists a \emph{rightmost} geodesic $\Pi_{(s,x),(t,y)}$ from $(s,x)$ to $(t,y)$, 
    in the sense that $\Pi_{(s,x),(t,y)} \geqslant \Pi'$ pointwise for any other geodesic $\Pi'$ from $(s,x)$ to $(t,y)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:04:59.425633+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_66f5c656c9327fa00dc80fdcef04219022efb20f28448472a0bb4cdf28051d59","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma CountingSimpleSets of arXiv:2605.18744, 'Lattice random-field Widom--Rowlinson models'] We have the following contour properties for $d\ge 2$ and finite $\Lambda\in  \mathfrak{A}_2$. 
