{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.158430+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_68a334a6b43b7a6e2ae5aa8b1dc8b7a4989ead88810af3de29b4acffa5e338fc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Any automorphism of ${\textup{O}}(2)$ is an inner automorphism.
    \begin{proof}
        As ${\textup{SO}}(2)$ is the identity component of ${\textup{O}}(2)$, $f$ restricts to an automorphism of ${\textup{SO}}(2)$. By [broecker-tom-dieck]*{Proposition~II.8.1}, we therefore need to have $f(A)=A$ or $f(A)=A^{-1}$ for all $A\in{\textup{SO}}(2)$. As $A$ and $A^{-1}$ become conjugate in ${\textup{O}}(2)$ via the reflection $R\coloneqq\begin{psmallmatrix}-1&0\\0&1\end{psmallmatrix}$, we see that $f\colon{\textup{O}}(2)\to{\textup{O}}(2)$ preserves conjugacy classes of elements in ${\textup{SO}}(2)$. 

        On the other hand, any matrix in ${\textup{O}}(2)\smallsetminus{\textup{SO}}(2)$ has $-1$ and $1$ as eigenvalues and hence is conjugate to $R$. As $f$ also has to preserve ${\textup{O}}(2)\smallsetminus{\textup{SO}}(2)$, we conclude that $f$ preserves also the conjugacy class of elements in ${\textup{O}}(2)\smallsetminus{\textup{SO}}(2)$, so that it is conjugate to the identity by Lemma~(lemma:conjugate-to-On of the paper).
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.065031+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_68a63d5bfeaf6d744a675e96a1c1b6f11661750019c37737f02187cd246a77f4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 22 of arXiv:2606.27078, 'Semimartingale Optimal Transport with Jumps: A General Framework and Equivalent Formulatio'] Let $\mu_0,\mu_1\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ have finite second moments. Then 
