{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.219676+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_68f4e0b07f269caa378ed72f9b8f177c05ee5915aae896452a0d85094996f3fd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 6 of arXiv:2607.17576, 'How much randomness is needed to symmetrize a random variable?'] Let $0<p<1$ and write $q:=1-p$. Let $X\sim\text{Geom}(p)$ be a geometric random variable with the probability mass function $f_X(k)=pq^k$ for $k\in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$. Let $Y$ be an integer-valued independent symmetrizer of $X$. Then we have for all $\alpha>0$ that
