{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.416766+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_691127494b28bc78d8fdda8f2cabc6602ed72ac8d9047a025cec3f12d409dc25","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 98 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Consider a variational datum satisfying Assumptions~\ref{Assumption_A}, \ref{Assumption_B} and \ref{Assumption_C}, with filling fractions $\hat{\boldsymbol{n}}^{(0)}$. Additionally, assume that the following properties hold for any $h \in [H]$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The equilibrium measure has only one band $[\alpha_h,\beta_h]$ in $[\hat{a}'_{h},\hat{b}'_{h}]$.
\item The function $s_h$ of Definition~\ref{GQdef2} satisfies $|s_h(z)| \geq \frac{1}{C}$ for any $z \in \amsmathbb{M}_{h}$.
\item If $\hat{a}'_{h} \in \amsmathbb{S}_{h}$, then we require a double zero: $\phi_h^{-}(\hat{a}'_{h})=[\phi_h^{-}]'(\hat{a}'_{h}) = 0$. Likewise, if $\hat{b}'_{h} \in \amsmathbb{S}_{h}$, then we require a double zero: $\phi_h^{+}(\hat{b}'_{h})=[\phi_h^{+}]'(\hat{b}'_{h}) = 0$.
\end{enumerate}
Then, there exists $\delta >0$ depending only on the constants in the assumptions such, for any variational datum differing from the original one only by the vector of filling fraction $\hat{\boldsymbol{n}}$ which satisfies $|\!|\hat{\boldsymbol{n}} - \hat{\boldsymbol{n}}^{(0)}|\!|_{\infty} \leq \delta$, the properties in \ref{Assumption_A}, \ref{Assumption_B} and \ref{Assumption_C} are satisfied. Moreover, if we let $\mu^{\hat{\boldsymbol{n}}}$ be the corresponding equilibrium measure, then the $\amsmathbb{C}^H$-valued function
 \[
 \hat{\boldsymbol{n}} \mapsto \big(\Gm_{\mu_h^{\hat{\boldsymbol{n}}}}(z)\big)_{h = 1}^{H}
\]
 is smooth, with limits in the definition of derivatives that are uniform over $z$ in compact subsets of
 $\amsmathbb C\setminus \amsmathbb{A}$. We have the formulae,
 \begin{equation}

\forall g,h \in [H]\quad \forall z \in \amsmathbb{C} \setminus [\alpha_h,\beta_h] \qquad \partial_{\hat{n}_{g}} \Gm_{\mu^{\hat{\boldsymbol{n}}}_h}(z) = \Op_h\big[0\,;\,\boldsymbol{e}^{(g)}\big] = \mathfrak{c}^{\textnormal{1st}}_{h;g}(z)
\end{equation}
in terms of the operator $\boldsymbol{\Upsilon}$ constructed in Section~\ref{sec:Masterpb} ($\boldsymbol{e}^{(1)},\ldots,\boldsymbol{e}^{(H)}$ is the canonical basis of $\amsmathbb{C}^H$), or equivalently the functions $\mathfrak{c}^{\textnormal{1st}}$ introduced in Definition~\ref{def:1stkind}. Simultaneously, the endpoints of the bands are also smooth functions of $\hat{\boldsymbol{n}}$. All the partial derivatives with respect to $\hat{\boldsymbol{n}}$ at any order depend on the intensity of interactions $\boldsymbol{\Theta}$ and the $H$-tuple of potentials $\boldsymbol{V}$ in a continuous way --- for the latter the topology induced by the norm appearing in the right-hand side of \eqref{eq_potential_inequality} is used.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.730206+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_69127caa6d2c491bc2ef45b701b9b3da995ab1a5c00b93dfd3bdda8268bf7378","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thmintro ET of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a $C^0$-foliation on $M$ transverse to $\partial M$.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item (Coarse version) Assume that $(M,\mathcal{F})$ is not homeomorphic to either of the following:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item $S^1 \times D^2$ with the standard foliation by disks,
            \item $\mathbb{T}^2\times I$ with a foliation semi-conjugate to an irrational foliation.
        \end{itemize}
        Then for every $\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a positive (resp.~negative) contact structure $\xi$  which is $\varepsilon$-close to $T\mathcal{F}$ in the $C^0$ topology, and whose boundary foliation dominates a foliation $\mathcal{G} = \mathcal{G}_\varepsilon$ on $\partial M$ which is monotone equivalent to $\partial \mathcal{F}$.
        \item (Fine version) If moreover $\mathcal{F}$ has neither compact planar leaves nor $\mathbb{T}^2$-type planar minimal sets (\zcref{def:pems}), then $\mathcal{G}$ can be chosen isotopic to $\partial \mathcal{F}$ along $\partial M$.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.250705+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6912ce12f6413e5980cac1af54569c41fde7f7c9b239ad23d15304119250a444","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 54 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] Fix constants $M,K$ satisfying \eqref{eq:costanti}. Define the sequence of real numbers $q_0, q_1, \ldots $ by, for each $i \ge 0$, setting $q_0 = \varepsilon_0$ and
\begin{flalign}

	q_{i+1}= q_i - \Delta_i, \qquad \text{where} \qquad \Delta_i = q_i^{1/3} N^{-2/3-M\delta}.	
\end{flalign}

\noindent We will always work with values of $i$ for which $q_i>0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:10.192792+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_69157b69ea6164c74396daabedd83004c44417a1764fcfb21b3c64129db3bd9f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 of arXiv:2605.03851, 'The Relay Random Tree:\\A stochastic geometry approach of multihop relay in an urban visib'] \textbf{Shade.}

Let $\landsc$ be a landscape, $(x_1, y_1)$ a point of $\Half$, and $(a, b)\in \landsc$ a building. We call the \textit{shade} of $(a, b)$ from $(x_1, y_1)$ the set of points right to $a$ that form a lower angle with $(x_1, y_1)$ than $(a, b)$ does:
\[S^{(x_1, y_1)}_{(a, b)} = \left\{ (x, y)\in \Half:x \geq a,\  \frac{y-y_1}{x-x_1}\leq \frac{b-y_1}{a-x_1}\right\}.\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.346127+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_691dda80cbb32897a7f4d0243d15fa2d6e71468da299cd67bd0fbf2bd0be4b25","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] We call an $ H $-valued, $ \mathbf{F} $-adapted, continuous stochastic process $ X(\cdot) $ a mild solution to (eqSEE of the paper) if   $F(\cdot, X(\cdot)) \in L^1(0, T ; H)$ a.s., $\widetilde{F}(\cdot, X(\cdot)) \in L_{\mathbb{F}}^{2}(0, T; H)$, and for any $t \in[0, T]$,
    \begin{align*}
    X(t)\!=\!S(t) X_0\!+\!\int_0^t\! S(t\!-\!s) F(s, X(s)) d s\!+\!\int_0^t\! S(t\!-\!s) \widetilde{F}(s, X(s)) d W(s),  \;\,  \text { a.s. }
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.832835+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6922506291b4fda8773503bbd3e09fc2dd33e980dd5e410fc8ee5e59ff454522","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm LSimplex of arXiv:2609.01751, 'Orthogonal polynomials which are eigenfunctions of a partial differential operator'] For ${\boldsymbol{\gamma}} \in {\mathbb R}^{d_1}$ with ${\gamma}_i  > -1$, $1\le i \le d_1$ and ${\boldsymbol{\kappa}} \in {\mathbb R}^{d_2+1}$ with ${\kappa}_i > -1$, $1 \le i \le d_2+1$, 
define
\begin{align} 
{\mathfrak D}_{{\boldsymbol{\gamma}},{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{L \rtimes \triangle}  : =\,& -(1-|{\bm y}|) \left({\mathfrak L}_{\boldsymbol{\gamma}}^{d_1, ({\bm x})}\right)^2 + {\mathfrak L}_{\boldsymbol{\gamma}}^{d_1, ({\bm x})} {\langle} {\bm x}, \nabla_{\bm x}{\rangle}+{\langle} {\bm x}, \nabla_{\bm x}{\rangle} {\mathfrak L}_{\boldsymbol{\gamma}}^{d_1, ({\bm x})} \\
 & +({\kappa}_{b_2+1} + |{\boldsymbol{\gamma}}| +d_1) {\mathfrak L}_{\boldsymbol{\gamma}}^{d_1, ({\bm x})} +  {\mathfrak L}_{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}^{d_2, ({\bm y})} - ({\langle} {\bm x}, \nabla_{{\bm x}} +{\langle} \bm y, \nabla_{\bm y}{\rangle})^2 
 \notag \\
 &     - (|{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}|+{\beta}+ d_2) ( {\langle} \bm x, \nabla_{\bm x} {\rangle} +{\langle} \bm y, \nabla_{\bm y} {\rangle})  \notag 
 \end{align}
Then ${\mathcal V}_n(\triangle^{d_1}  \rtimes {\mathbb R}_\Sigma^{d_2}, \bm W_{{\boldsymbol{\gamma}}, {\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{\triangle \rtimes \Sigma})$ is an eigenspace 
of $ {\mathfrak D}_{{\boldsymbol{\gamma}},{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{\triangle \rtimes \Sigma}$ for each $n \in {\mathbb N}_0$. More precisely, 
\begin{align} 
  {\mathfrak D}_{\mu,{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{\triangle \rtimes \Sigma} u =  - n (n + |{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}|+ |{\boldsymbol{\gamma}}|+d_1+d_2) u, \qquad \forall u \in {\mathcal V}_n\big(\triangle^{d_1}  \rtimes {\mathbb R}_\Sigma^{d_2}, \bm W_{{\boldsymbol{\gamma}}, {\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{\triangle \rtimes \Sigma}\big).
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.813825+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_69260b8a79a60e645aa57cdcea7397f95e6d107f23f8a9ed03465f7e7974b5d4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:sample_Gn of arXiv:2607.16911, 'Multipartite random graphs with given degrees: local limit, revisiting the giant, distance'] Consider graphs $G_{i,j}$ defined as follows. If $(i,j)$ is $\rm BAD$, we simply let $G_{i,j}$ be a uniform random simple graph with degree sequence $\mathbf d_i(n)$ if $i=j$ and with degree sequence $\mathbf d_{(i,j)}(n)$ if $i \neq j$. If $(i,j)$ is $\rm GOOD$, we let $G_{i,j}$ be $CM_n^{i,j}$. Recall $G_n$ is a uniform multipartite random graph with given degree sequences, satisfying Assumptions (assump:part_i_size of the paper), (assump:degree_regularity of the paper), (assump:one_good_deg_greater_2 of the paper) and (assump:offspring_degree_reg of the paper). The following statements are true:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The random graph $\mathbb G_n:=\mathcal G(G_{i,j}:i,j\in [k])$ conditionally on the event $\{\mathbb G_n \textrm{ is simple}\}$ has the same law as $G_n$.
        \item Moreover,
        \begin{align*}
            \liminf_{n \to \infty}\Prob{\mathbb G_n \textrm{ is simple}}=\left(\prod_{\substack{i\in [k]:\\(i,i)\textrm{ is GOOD}}}e^{-\phi_i/2-(\phi_i)^2/4}\right)\left(\prod_{\substack{i\neq j \in [k]:\\(i,j)\textrm{ is GOOD }}}e^{-\phi_{i,j}} \right)>0.
        \end{align*}
        Thus, for any subset $A_n$ of simple graphs, $\mathbb{P}\left(G_n\in A_n\right)=o(1)$ if and only if $\mathbb{P}\left(\mathbb G_n \in A_n\right)=o(1)$.  
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:47.663054+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6927fff262140bc1a64dd95140458f921cfe32364f558c6fde90ba0c189c6a6d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2605.09372, 'Sharp weighted norm estimates for martingale square functions'] Let $1<r<p<\infty$. Given a scalar weight $w\in A_p$, we have
