{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:41:33.824728+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_69e3cc0d0c35f94af61b707af0ccbced145d787f0d91a85543755da6e3684aa3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 of arXiv:2604.10266, 'A singular SDE driven by additive fractional Brownian motion with Hurst parameter $H<\frac'] Let $b(t)$ be a continuous function on $[0,\infty)$. Assume that continuous functions $x^i=\{x^i_t,\, t\geq 0\}$, $i=1, 2$, satisfy the following equations
\begin{equation*}
x^i_t=x_0+\int_0^tg_i(s)f_i(x^i_s)ds+\int_0^t h_i(x^i_s)ds+b(t), \ t\geq 0,
\end{equation*}
where $x_0\in\mathbb{R}$ is a constant, and $g_i, f_i, h_i$, $i=1, 2$, are continuous functions such that 
\begin{itemize}
