{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:17.747308+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6aaa84c66318bd98a0c4aafbed0883cff2785328d1120038056af7fef5814792","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2511.01424, '\bf Derivative formula for capacities'] \rm{
	Let $N\in \N$ and $\gamma:\{0,\ldots, N\}\to \Z^d$ be a finite path in $\Z^d$. We denote by $\cF_\gamma$ the range of a branching random walk indexed by a tree defined by taking a line of length $N+1$ and attaching trees to the left and the right of this line as follows: for $i\in \{0,\ldots, N\}$ the number of offspring of $i$ to the left (resp.\ to the right) of the line, denoted~$\ell_i$ (resp.\ $r_i$) have distribution
	\[
	\pr{\ell_i=\ell, r_i=r} = \mu(r+\ell+1)\quad  \text{ for } \ r,\ell\geq 0.
 	\]
 	The number of offspring of every other vertex which is not on the line is independently distributed according to $\mu$. 
	The root of the $i$-th tree is at location $\gamma(i)$ for $i=0,\ldots, N$ and we assign i.i.d.\ simple random walk increments to the edges of the attached trees. We write $\cF_{\gamma,-}$ for the range of the branching random walk indexed by the set of trees to the left of the line (including their roots) and excluding the first tree to the left. 
}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:25:35.797171+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ab050698ba651779dc4f877be35df6023a39e37168f32c2015b3b51a73c3ed7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.connected_decomposition of arXiv:2603.11043, 'An asymptotically optimal bound for the concentration function of a sum of independent int'] Let $\mu$ be a probability measure with support $V = \{x_1, \dots, x_k\} \subset \mathbb{R}$ and probabilities of the form $\mu(\{x_i\}) = \frac {a_i} N$, where $N, a_1, \dots, a_k \in \{1,2,\dots\}$. Suppose $N$ is even and $\mathcal{Q}(\mu) \le \frac 1 2$. Then $\mu$ can be represented as
    \[
        \mu = \sum_{i=1}^m w_i \mu_i; \quad w_i = \frac {a_i'} N > 0; \quad a_i' \in \{1,2,\dots\}
    \]
    where each $\mu_i$ is a uniform distribution on two distinct points in $V$
    and furthermore the graph $G = (V, E)$, where $E=\{\mathrm{supp}(\mu_i), i \in \{1, \dots, m\}\}$, is connected.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.295239+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ab38cf904d136a3b4364941c92d188c694942939a25fc36dd5047943a1aa89c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Tm Ito of arXiv:2411.05441, 'Sticky diffusions on star graphs : characterization and It\^o formula'] Let $X(t) = (i(t), x(t))$ be the process generated by (equation generator2 of the paper) and (equation eq:domain of the paper) with $\eta > 0$, started at $X(0) = \mathsf{x} \in \Gamma$, and let $f \in C_{b}^{1,2}(\mathbb{R}_+ \times \Gamma)$. Then,
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{split}
	& f(t,X(t)) = f(0,\mathsf{x}) + \int_0^t \left ( \partial_s f(s,X(s)) +  Lf(s,X(s)) \right ) \mathbf 1_{\{ X(s) \neq \mathsf{v} \}}  ds \\
	& \, + \int_0^t \sigma(X(s)) \partial_x f(s,X(s)) \mathbf{1}_{\{X(s) \neq \mathsf{v} \}} d \widetilde W(s)
	 + \int_0^t \left ( \eta\, \partial_s f(s,\mathsf{v}) +  \sum_{i=1}^N \rho_i \partial_x f_i(s,0) \right ) d \ell_X(s),
	\end{split}
	\end{equation}
	where $\widetilde W$ is the Brownian motion obtained in Theorem (tm:SDE of the paper). In particular, if $Lf(t,\cdot) \in C(\Gamma)$ for all $t \geq 0$, we have
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{split}
	& f(t,X(t)) = f(0,\mathsf{x}) + \int_0^t \left ( \partial_s f(s,X(s)) +  Lf(s,X(s)) \right )  ds \\
	& \, + \int_0^t \sigma(X(s)) \partial_x f(s,X(s)) \mathbf{1}_{\{X(s) \neq \mathsf{v} \}} d \widetilde W(s) + \int_0^t \left (\sum_{i=1}^N \rho_i \partial_x f_i(s,0) - \eta\, L f(s,\mathsf{v}) \right ) d \ell_X(s).
	\end{split}
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.123904+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ac343b8acd0ad59cb23bf8929b95c679921bacf545bef5a58f9bb7acef5ff62","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm of arXiv:2506.18207, 'Cartan's Path Development, the Logarithmic Signature and a Conjecture of Lyons-Sidorova'] Under the same assumption of Theorem (windingangletheorem of the paper). If $\theta$ is piecewise $C^1$ on $[0,T]$ and $\theta_T-\theta_0\in(4k\pi+\pi,4k\pi+3\pi)$ for some integer $k$, then $\gamma$ has finite radius of convergence for its logarithmic signature.
\begin{proof}
It is implied by Corollary (ODEequivalence of the paper), Remark (piecewiseC2path of the paper) and the calculation in (equation signcalculation of the paper). Actually, we have 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
&\int_l^{l+\pi}\rho_T^\lambda\cos(\varphi_T^\lambda-\zeta)d\zeta\\
\to&~e^{\lambda T}\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}\cos\left(\frac{\theta_{T_k+}-\theta_{T_k-}}{2}\right)\int_l^{l+\pi}\cos\left(\zeta-\frac{\theta_0}{2}\right)\cos\left(\zeta-\frac{\theta_T}{2}\right)d\zeta\\
=&\frac{e^{\lambda T}\pi}{2}\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}\cos\left(\frac{\theta_{T_k+}-\theta_{T_k-}}{2}\right)\cos\left(\frac{\theta_T-\theta_0}{2}\right).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
W.L.O.G. we can assume the all the intersection angles $|\theta_{T_k+}-\theta_{T_k-}|<\pi$ for any $k$. Actually, we have to assume this to make the ODE system consistent on each piece $\gamma|_{[T_k,T_{k+1}]}$, otherwise the condition has to change. Because $\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}\cos\left(\frac{\theta_{T_k+}-\theta_{T_k-}}{2}\right)>0$, it follows that
$$
\int_l^{l+\pi}\rho_T^\lambda\cos(\varphi_T^\lambda-\zeta)d\zeta\to-\infty~~~\Longleftrightarrow~~~\cos\left(\frac{\theta_T-\theta_0}{2}\right)<0,
$$
which finishes the proof.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:45.002330+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ac5ab1cd5836359c277434de59422a87c2d8597538db18b868a858a4b6502c4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 32 of arXiv:2605.11801, 'A non-local singular non-linear Fokker-Planck PDE'] Let  $g \in C_T  \shc^{(-\beta)+}  $, $f_T \in  \shc^{(1+\beta)+}$.
