{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.813437+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6b1da06176a7ba4b1fa814d1313a55413180e0fa8d3a9be9275d1d466873997c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:gamarnik_giant of arXiv:2607.16911, 'Multipartite random graphs with given degrees: local limit, revisiting the giant, distance'] Under the assumptions of Theorem (thm:giants_MCMs of the paper), assume further:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item If a pair $(i,j)$ is BAD, then $\mathbf d_{(i,j)}(n)$ and $\mathbf d_{(j,i)}(n)$ are null sequences for all $n \geq 1$, i.e., they only have zero entries.
        \item  Considering the natural offspring matrix $M$ on $S^2 \times S^2$ where the $((i_1,j_1),(i_2,j_2))$-th entry is the number $\Exp{D_{(i_1,j_1,j_2)}}\1_{\{j_1=i_2\}}$, there exists a $p\geq 1$ such that all entries of $M^p$ are strictly positive.
