{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.599834+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6b2e15dc16f5055a6bc8e2325dab625d8b7b72514cf4410c8dbe84385957b619","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:competition of arXiv:2509.16541, 'Two-stage Bootstrap Percolation'] Assume $(p,q)\to (0,0)$. Then 
\begin{itemize}
\item if $q\ll p^{2}/(\log(1/p)^2$, then $\mathbb{P}(\text{the origin is eventually in state $1$})\to 1$;
\item if $p^2\ll q\ll p^{1/2}$, then $\mathbb{P}(\text{the origin  remains in state $0$ forever})\to 1$; and 
\item if $p^{1/2}\log(1/p)\ll q$, then $\mathbb{P}(\text{the origin is eventually in state $2$})\to 1$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:49.834044+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6b2e3db6268d751c4d7f5e103e99a92ce86c02a21a665509551db18f88b0d9bd","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 17 of arXiv:2506.12632, 'Point process convergence of extremes in $K$-symmetric exclusion'] For any $A \subset \Z$ and $\bx \in \Omega_n^K$, 
