{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.956554+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ba344c12fc5d1a017cb926d28138162f947c6bdc659ebd5f151a4e94abc97e2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.30346, '\textbf{Classification complexity of homeomorphism group actions}'] Let \(G\) be a Polish group acting continuously on a Polish space \(X\). The action of \(G\) on \(X\) is \textit{turbulent} if
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(T1)] every orbit is meager,
        \item[(T2)] every orbit is dense, and
        \item[(T3)] every local orbit is somewhere dense, that is for any open \(U \subset X\), \( x \in U\) and open \(1_G \in V \subset G\), \(\mathcal{O}(x,U,V)\) is somewhere dense.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.979339+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ba37630d36ab8f3aeaa2c0e990354cf501d50d48e17e7d536bf3f0d52840e1b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 35 (Quadratic layer second derivative) of arXiv:2605.11451, '2605.11451'] Let $L,D,W$ be $C^1$ functions on an interval $I\subset(0,\infty)$, with $L>0$, $D\ge0$, $W\ge0$, and assume all level roots below are simple. If $I$ is noncompact, assume the integrals below are finite and the truncation limits over compact exhaustions of $I$ have vanishing tails. Define, for $x>0$ and $q=x^2$,
\[
 F(x)=\int_I W(R)\frac12\Big((L(R)^2-(x+D(R))^2)_+ +(L(R)^2-(x-D(R))^2)_+\Big)\,\dd R,
\]
and $G(q)=F(\sqrt q)$. Then, in the distributional sense,
\begin{align}
4x^3G''(q)=&\ x\sum_{|L-D|=x}\frac{W(R)L(R)}{|(L-D)'(R)|}
+x\sum_{L+D=x}\frac{W(R)L(R)}{|(L+D)'(R)|} \notag\\
&\quad -\int_{\{|L-D|<x<L+D\}}W(R)D(R)\,\dd R.
\end{align}
Endpoint and repeated-root cases follow by the approximation procedure described in the proof.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:28.094234+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ba3a6b2c9c49273b1ac25cd83c03b42afdc2600833db0a891d1e96ebbdf642b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 32 of arXiv:2607.13849, 'Rearranged Stochastic Heat Equation with an Entropy Gradient Structure'] Let $\mathbf{y},u \in E^K$ and $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$. Then the couple process $(\tilde{X}_t^{\mathbf{y},u,\vartheta},\tilde{\eta}_t^{\mathbf{y},u,\vartheta})_{t\in [0,T]}$ given by 
    \begin{align*}
        &\tilde{X}_t^{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta),\tau}:= \begin{cases}
            X_t^{\mathbf{y}_t^{*}}, &t \in [0,\tau_{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta)}]\\
             X_t^{\mathbf{y}_{\tau_{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta)}}^{*}}, &t\in [\tau_{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta)},T]
        \end{cases}
        \\
        &\tilde{\eta}_t^{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta),\tau}:= \begin{cases}
            \tilde{\eta}_t^{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta)}, &t \in [0,\tau_{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta)}]\\
             \eta_t^{\mathbf{y}_{\tau_{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta)}}^{*}}- \eta_{\tau_{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta)}}^{\mathbf{y}_{\tau_{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta)}}^{*}}+\tilde{\eta}_{\tau_{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta)}}^{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta),\tau}, &t\in [\tau_{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta)},T]
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    satisfies Definition \ref{Defi 317} under probability $\tilde{\mathbb{P}}^{\mathbf{y},(u,\vartheta),\tau}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:46:25.365600+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ba651fcf7b61f3f658b9d7a2f33bdcb3d5a0f1f41f6919e00f356da25314c2d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop series of arXiv:2604.14122, 'The scaling limit of random walk and\\ the intrinsic metric on planar critical percolation'] Let $V\in\mathfrak{C}$ and let $x,y,z\in \mathcal{X}\cap\overline{V}$ such that $z$ separates $x$ from $y$ in $\mathcal{X}\cap\overline{V}$. Then
\begin{equation}
    \mathfrak{d}_V(x,y)=\mathfrak{d}_V(x,z)+\mathfrak{d}_V(z,y).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.650773+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ba90e5376ff970d5a4743bf616908eb7e1b3392083fb3cffe1ed59ee280a8b7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop funct-ineq of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] Let $\alpha\in(0,\sqrt{2})$.  
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item For any $n\in\mathbb N$ and any $z_1\ldots z_n\in D$, $$\left\langle \prod_{k=1}^n:\! \sin(\alpha\phi(z_k)) \!: \right\rangle\leq\left\langle \prod_{k=1}^n:\! \cos(\alpha\phi(z_k)) \!: \right\rangle.$$
        \item For any $z,w\in D$,
        \begin{align*}
            \langle\field{\overline{\mathrm{cos}}(\alpha\phi(z))}\field{\overline{\mathrm{cos}}(\alpha\phi(w))}\rangle\leq\langle:\! \sin(\alpha\phi(z)) \!: :\! \sin(\alpha\phi(w)) \!: \rangle.
        \end{align*}
        \item For any $u\in[-\pi/2,\pi/2]$ and any $z,w\in D$, 
        \begin{align*}
            \langle\field{\overline{\mathrm{cos}}(\alpha\phi(z)+u)}\field{\overline{\mathrm{cos}}(\alpha\phi(w)+u)}\rangle \leq\langle:\! \sin(\alpha\phi(z)) \!: :\! \sin(\alpha\phi(w)) \!: \rangle.
        \end{align*}
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.228864+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ba94cffcbd72662b709386cadf645602f0786da980864f93b55c4ca126fa8ba","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition Prop_intensity implies top particle of arXiv:2608.23202, 'Locally finite Fixed points of Branching Brownian Motion'] Let $\Pi$ be a fixed point of BBM with drift $\lambda>\sqrt 2$ such that  

\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{E}[\Pi(K)] < \infty \textrm{ for all compact sets } K\subset \mathbb{R}. 
\end{equation}

Then,
\begin{equation}
     \mathbb{E}[\Pi([0, \infty))] < \infty.
\end{equation}

In particular, $\Pi([0,\infty)) < \infty$ almost surely.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.350718+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6baaf4ef0ee9f9ed9362fb2691aa85171089833741b5924feaa44d2f273c9e85","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2511.07350, 'Decoupling of clusters in independent sets in a percolated hypercube'] For all $p \in (0,1)$,
