{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:55:18.920044+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bae664eeb2f3b33e618da3c8d8f94237b0d6b8e37a0de0760efc4ca14f9f677","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:URDsoln of arXiv:2601.21762, 'Navier-Stokes with a fractional transport noise as a limit of multi-scale dynamics'] A process $u\in L^{2}_TH^1 \cap C_TH_w$ together with the $\alpha$-continuous unbounded rough driver $(A^1, A^2)$ on the scale $H^r$
 is said to be an {\it Unbounded Rough Driver  (URD) solution} to (equation eq:modeleqn of the paper), if 
  there exists a covering $\mathcal P=\{[s_k,t_k]\}$ of $[0,T]$ such that for any~$k$,
        the residual,
        \begin{equation}
            u^\natural_{s,t} := u_{s,t} - \int_{s}^{t} \nu \Delta u_r dr + \int_s^t \Pi(u_r\cdot \nabla u_r) dr - 
             A^1_{s,t}u_s - A^2_{s,t}u_s, \qquad s_k\le s\le t\le t_k,
        \end{equation}
        belongs to the finite variation space $ C_2^{\frac 1{3\alpha}\textrm{-var}}([s_k, t_k];H^{-3})$ of two parameter processes.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:38.631285+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bb07168ae3a27e4951e1fd8d0ffb52e11014518d82ead15eb06304a9534b90a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 16 of arXiv:2506.17045, '\LARGE{On bivariate Archimax copulas: Level sets, mass distributions and related results}'] Let $\psi \in \mathbf{\Psi}$, $A \in \mathcal{A}$, $C_{\psi,A} \in \mathcal{C}_{am}$, $\beta_\psi$ be defined as in eq. \eqref{eq:function_beta}, $\tau_A$ be Kendall's tau of $C_A \in \mathcal{C}_{ev}$ and $F_\psi^K$ be the Kendall distribution function of $C_\psi \in \mathcal{C}_{ar}$. Then the
    Kendall distribution function $F_{\psi,A}^K$ of $C_{\psi,A}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
F_{\psi,A}^K(t) = t + \beta_\psi(t)(\tau_A-1) = F_\psi^K(t) + \beta_\psi(t)\tau_A,
\end{equation}
for every $t \in (0,1]$. Furthermore, $F_{\psi,A}(0) = 0$ holds in the case that $\psi$ is strict and $F_{\psi,A}(0) = \beta_\psi(0)(\tau_A -1)$ holds in the case that $\psi$ is non-strict.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:34.580538+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bb287d80feb64d90991d048828b61e0eb680486b272f21bfdc672b00c78c5ea","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 33 of arXiv:2606.14876, 'High-Order Talagrand and Eldan--Gross Inequalities via Besov-Type Variance Functionals'] For a fixed function $f$, the map $r\mapsto \left\|f\right\|_{\Leb^{1/r}}$ is logarithmically convex. More specifically, for $\lambda\in [0,1]$, $p_1, p_2 \in [1,\infty]$ and $\frac{1}{p_\lambda} = \frac{1-\lambda}{p_1} + \frac{\lambda}{p_2}$, we have
\begin{equation*}
    \left\|f\right\|_{\Leb^{p_\lambda}} \leq \left\|f\right\|_{\Leb^{p_1}}^{1-\lambda}  \left\|f\right\|_{\Leb^{p_2}}^{\lambda}.
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:26.259026+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bb945ead2c4677df5b12467f33ad7dd57b86143739808d7fb2e0409a95bbf39","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 89 of arXiv:2605.23308, 'Convergence rate estimates for semigroups and heat kernels associated with resistance form'] $\rm{UP}$ is equivalent to the following.
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\exists a_{\rm{UP}}>1,\;\forall B(x,r)\neq F,\;B(x,a_{\rm{UP}}r)\setminus B(x,r)\neq \emptyset$
\end{itemize}
Furthermore, we may take the constants to satisfy $a_{\rm{UP}}=c_{\rm{UP}}^{-1}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.767043+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bbb3a2687d16d01665f2a4c01e5928971c3cc458881c45625cc94eee99f8c6c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Fix $q \in (0,1)$, $c \in (1, q^{-1})$, and define
\begin{equation}
\bar{\kappa} = \frac{(c-q)^2}{(1-qc)^2} - 1 > 0, \hspace{2mm} p^{\mathrm{top}} = \frac{q}{c-q}, \hspace{2mm} C^{\mathrm{top}} = \frac{q (c^2 - 2qc + 1)}{(c-q)(1-qc)}.
\end{equation}
Let $N \in \mathbb{N}$, and $\lpplinen_{1}$ be as in (\ref{Eq.Deflpplen}). Define the rescaled process $\mathcal{U}_1^{\mathrm{top},N}$ on $[0, \bar{\kappa})$ by
$$\mathcal{U}_1^{\mathrm{top},N}(t) = [p^{\mathrm{top}} (1 + p^{\mathrm{top}})]^{-1/2} N^{-1/2} \left( \lpplinen_{1}(tN) - C^{\mathrm{top}} N -  p^{\mathrm{top}} t N   \right) \mbox{ for } t \in [0, \bar{\kappa}).$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.258572+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bc3092f10e6dceaea4fc8c338f24692cbd4af90a7c712b5a6ee29983c6c271c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 127 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] For any $x \in [0, a]$ and $u \in \mathbb{C}$, let $z_x = z_x (u) = z(x, u) \in \mathbb{C}$ solve the equation (with $f_x(z)$ as in \eqref{fxz})
	\begin{flalign}

		\partial_x z_x = \displaystyle\frac{f_x(z_x)}{f_x (z_x) - 1}, \quad \text{where $z_0 = u$}.
	\end{flalign}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:01:39.505752+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bc5c65208baecb4f73a426487a511c81b2aae5085387506c03e49c12959af50","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:mixing of arXiv:2607.22991, 'Mixing times and spectra of non-equilibrium symmetric exclusion processes on general graph'] For any SEP with arbitrary heat baths on an $n$-vertex connected graph, the mixing time is $O(\tau_{\rm hit}\log n)$
and is also $\Omega(\tau_{\rm hit}/\log n)$.  More precisely:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $\tau_{\rm mix}(\varepsilon)\le 2\log_2(n\varepsilon^{-1})\tau_{\rm hit}$ for any $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$;
\item[(b)] $\tau_{\rm mix}(1/40) > \tau_{\rm hit}/a\ln n$ for some universal constant~$a>0$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:46:25.366967+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bc758bed7eaee2b1e64a8f167b58b87413959b59d3a9c547339e9b332d18fdb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 4arms of arXiv:2604.14122, 'The scaling limit of random walk and\\ the intrinsic metric on planar critical percolation'] For all $\zeta>0$, there exists $R=R(\zeta)$ large enough and a constant $C=C(R,\zeta)$ such that for all $n,k$, $x\in B^{(k)}$, and $a>0$ such that $R^{\kappa-k}\geq an\geq R$, we have
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{P}\!\left(\textrm{an arm from $B(x,a)$ to each of the $4$ sides of $B^{(k)}$}\right)\leq C\cdot\!\left(\frac{a}{R^{-k}}\right)^{\frac{5}{4}-\zeta}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.790662+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bd0601b6b41f4bb3462a237b500fe1180fa87053309692414e09b7f778dd0ea","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 64 (Vacuum Feynman diagram) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] A \emph{vacuum diagram} is a multigraph $\Gamma = (\cV,\cE)$, meaning there can 
be multiple edges between vertices. Its \emph{valuation} is defined by 
