where $e_\pm$ are the vertices connected by the edge $e$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:32:20.751659+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bd2bf6f48612581f5c91161cdef5a074b1344353e402ac490c51cecf844641a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2609.00448, 'Equivariant formality and representation theory'] (\cite[\S I.4]{Ho}, \cite[\S 3]{BZ}, \cite[Definitions 2.2 and 2.5]{F})
	Let $\delta: R(G)\to K^{-1}(G)$ be the $\mathbb{Z}$-linear map which sends a complex representation $\rho$ with underlying vector space $V$ to the $K$-theory class represented by the complex of vector bundles
	\begin{align*}
		0\longrightarrow G\times\mathbb{R}\times V&\longrightarrow G\times\mathbb{R}\times V\longrightarrow 0\\
		(g, t, v)&\mapsto\begin{cases}(g, t, -t\rho(g)v),&\ \text{if }t>0\\ (g, t, tv),&\ \text{if }t\leq 0.\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	More generally, for an element $\rho\in\text{ker}(i^*: R(G)\to R(K))$ which is the formal difference $\rho_1-\rho_2\in R(G)$ of two complex $G$-representations restricting to the same $K$-representations, we define the $\mathbb{Z}$-linear map $\delta^{G/K}:\text{ker}(i^*)\to K^{-1}(G/K)$ which sends $\rho$ to the complex of vector bundles
	\begin{align*}
		0\longrightarrow G/K\times\mathbb{R}\times V&\longrightarrow G/K\times\mathbb{R}\times V\longrightarrow 0\\
		(gK, t, v)&\mapsto\begin{cases}(gK, t, t\rho_1(g)\rho_2(g)^{-1}v),&\ \text{if }t>0\\ (gK, t, tv),&\ \text{if }t\leq 0.\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	The map $\delta_K^{G/K}: \text{ker}(i^*)\to K_K^{-1}(G/K)$ is defined using the same complex of vector bundles and equipping it with the $K$-action on $G/K\times \mathbb{R}\times V$ given by $k\cdot(gK, t, v)=(kgK, t, \rho_1(k)v)$. The map $\delta_K^G: R(G)\to K_K^{-1}(G)$ can be defined similarly (here $K$ acts on $G$ by conjugation). We will also use the same notation to denote the rationalized maps from the rational representation ring to the rational $K$-theory $K^{-1}(G; \mathbb{Q})$, $K^{-1}(G/K; \mathbb{Q})$ etc.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:25:49.893801+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bdeeaff261497007fe9a9b67ca05290cc4849b272751dc69ff12923713095fc","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 43 of arXiv:2609.02065, '\textbf{From Bowditch's question to Goldman's conjecture for type-preserving representatio'] Let $P: \univcover\times \univcover\to \univcover$ be a product map, and let $m, n, l\in \mathbb{Z}$.
Consider the following restrictions of $P$:
\begin{align*}
P_1:\ & (\Hyp_m\times \Hyp_n) \cap P^{-1}(\Ell_l) \to \Ell_l,
\\
P_2:\ & (\Hyp_m\times \Ell_n) \cap P^{-1}(\Ell_l) \to \Ell_l, \text{ and}
\\
P_3:\ & (\Ell_m \times \Ell_n) \cap P^{-1}(\Ell_l) \to \Ell_l,
\end{align*}
For each $i\in \{1,2,3\}$, every non-empty fiber of $P_i$ is path-connected.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:25:55.482282+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bdfc7728a1abc9e4d27ef4c2cede783ceedc8e0427f0803c2338cb48531cd29","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 24 (Uniform bounds for the defect measures of $u^h_{n}$) of arXiv:2607.28238, 'Well-posedness and large deviations for the obstacle problem of first-order stochastic con'] For every $p\geq0$, there exists $C>0$ independent of $n\in\mathbb N$, such that
\begin{align}
 \int_{\mathbb{T}^N\times[0,T]\times\mathbb{R}}(1+|\xi|^p)\dd q^h_{n}\leq C(p,M,D_0,T).
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:00:30.488739+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6be158b4e8a2182220ea46f5993c03a756e27b8630338fe912be736153269c7a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 8 of arXiv:2607.03924, 'Tree Coordinates and Range Martingales for Positive Operator-Valued Measures'] For a finite word $w$ and any $n\geq\left|w\right|$,
let $P^{\left(n\right)}_{w}$ be the orthogonal projection of $K_{n}$
onto 
\[
\bigoplus_{\left|u\right|=n,\,u\succeq w}H_{u}
\]
where $u\succeq w$ means $u$ extends $w$. Then 
\begin{equation}
P^{\left(n+1\right)}_{w}J_{n}=J_{n}P^{\left(n\right)}_{w},\qquad n\geq\left|w\right|.
\end{equation}
Hence there are projections $P_{w}\in B\left(K_{E}\right)$ such that
\begin{equation}
P_{w}U_{n}=U_{n}P^{\left(n\right)}_{w},\qquad n\geq\left|w\right|.
\end{equation}
Moreover, there is a unique PVM 
\[
P:\mathcal{B}\left(\Omega_{m}\right)\to B\left(K_{E}\right)
\]
such that 
\begin{equation}
P\left(\left[w\right]\right)=P_{w}
\end{equation}
for every finite word $w$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.634061+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6be4a3041a92c8ad7d9bcbdcee23af41d779117118546fe1624b51f904bd7332","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l:log.likelihood.local.region.bounds of arXiv:2607.02013, 'Resolution of the Detection Threshold Conjecture for Random Geometric Graphs in the $d>n$ '] Fix $r\ge2$ and $N\ge1$. For $\Lambda_\theta=\log L_\theta$ as defined by ~((eq:logL-explicit of the paper)), we have the bounds
\begin{align}

\sup\bigg\{|\Lambda_\theta(t)|
:0\le\theta\le1
\bigg\}
&\le
C_{r}\delta U(t)\,,\\

\sup\bigg\{ \bigg|
\frac{\partial^j}{\partial\theta^j}\Lambda_\theta(t)
\bigg| : 0 \le \theta \le 1\bigg\}
&\le
C_{r,j}[\delta U(t)]^j \,,
\end{align}
for all $U(t)\le c_0/\delta$, where the second line holds for all $1\le j\le N$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.700346+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6be625c1c1c07313be7ad77b61d84e09c747ce1a2958d506fb8db2608f053eb6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop coupling2 of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] If $\gamma > 0$ is taken small enough then, for any $\eta \in (0,\tfrac{1}{2})$ sufficiently small (depending only on $\gamma$) we have that, for all $k$ sufficiently large and $T \in [k,{\rm e}^k]$,
	\begin{equation}
		\mathbb{P} \bigg( \bigg\{ \sqrt{\widehat{L}_{\widetilde{v}_k}(0)} \in {\rm d} u\bigg\} \setminus \mathcal{L}^{\eta,[1,T-1]}_{\widetilde{v}_k} \bigg)=\frac{4}{\sqrt{\pi}} \cdot \frac{1}{Tk^{1/2+\gamma}}{\rm e}^{-2Tk^\gamma}\left(\widehat{u}{\rm e}^{2\sqrt{2}\widehat{u}}\right)\left(\widehat{v}{\rm e}^{-2\sqrt{2}\widehat{v}-\frac{\widehat{v}^2}{Tk^\gamma}}\right)(1+o_k(1))\,
	\end{equation} holds uniformly over $u \in \mathcal{Q}_k^\eta(0)$ and $v \in \mathcal{Q}_k^\eta(T)$, where $\widehat{u}:=u-\sqrt{2}k$ and $\widehat{v}:=v -\sqrt{2}(k+Tk^\gamma)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:09:23.966645+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6be8bf92760d58c36bbfa091a610120e9b226b07de2ceb8d5784bfee108a6da5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2607.22416, 'A multi-player optimal stopping game based on a spider Brownian bridge'] We say that $(\tau^{*},\sigma^{*}) \in \sT \times \sT$ is a Nash equilibrium (for the game with expected payoffs $(\GG_i, \LL_i)_{i = 1,2}$) if 
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{split}
            R_{1}(\tau^{*},\sigma^{*})=\sup_{\tau \in \sT} R_{1}(\tau,\sigma^{*}),  \\
            R_{2}(\tau^{*},\sigma^{*})=\sup_{\sigma \in \sT} R_{2}(\tau^{*},\sigma).
        \end{split}
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:07:48.555138+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6be9781bf98089ae6d73c9284942334f695f544b7e7118027cd9fa81f7eea974","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 (Cai--Lu quadratic lower bound) of arXiv:2609.01436, '2609.01436'] Assume \cref{ass:CL}.  Let
$\varphi\in C_b^{1,1}([0,\infty))$ be nonnegative and convex.  Then
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal J[\varphi](y,z)
 \geq-\frac12(\varphi(y)\sqrt z+\varphi(z)\sqrt y),
 \qquad y,z\geq0.
\end{equation}
Consequently, if $F\in\mathcal B_1^+([0,\infty))$ has mass $N$ and energy
$E$, then
\begin{equation}
 \iint\mathcal J[\varphi]\,dF^{\otimes2}
 \geq-\sqrt{NE}\int\varphi\,dF.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

