{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.822563+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6cb87619b47dfe0310b0a65eb2f475a6e359d9bcd6e78f90e8eb03b818073d78","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor: duality of arXiv:2504.04792, 'Uniqueness and Longtime Behavior of the Completely Positively Correlated Symbiotic Branchi'] Let $\dot{W}$ and $\dot{W_0}$ be two independent white noises on $\mathbb{R}_+ \times \mathbb{R}$. Let $\psi \in C_{\text{tem}}^+$ and $\phi \in C_{\text{rap}}^+$. Define $(V_t)_{t \geq 0}$ and $(\Tilde{V}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ as the unique solutions with values in $C_{\text{tem}}^+$ and $C_{\text{rap}}^+$, respectively, of equation $((eq: dual of the paper))$, driven by $\dot{W}$ and $\dot{W_0}$, respectively, with initial conditions $V_0 = \psi$  and $\Tilde{V}_0 = \phi$. Then, for each $T > 0$, the following self-duality formula holds:
\begin{equation}

    \mathbb{E}\left[e^{-\langle \Tilde{V}_T, \psi \rangle}\right] = \mathbb{E}\left[e^{-\langle \phi, V_T \rangle }\right].
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:07:17.255625+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6cbc87cecccbf1699da4218eeeb2ab983a9b6a93e9a89d82bb9469a02cad3356","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition BB_HH of arXiv:2603.12774, 'Synchronization by noise for stochastic differential equations driven by fractional Browni'] For \( 0 < H < 1 \), let \( C_0^\infty(\mathbb{R}^-, \mathbb{R}^d) \) denote the space of smooth, compactly supported functions \( \omega: \mathbb{R}^- \to \mathbb{R}^d \) satisfying \( \omega(0) = 0 \). For \( \omega \in C_0^\infty(\mathbb{R}^-, \mathbb{R}^d) \), define
	\begin{align}
		\Vert \omega \Vert_{\mathcal{B}_H}
		:= \sup_{s,t \in \mathbb{R}^-} 
		\frac{|\omega(t) - \omega(s)|}
		{\sqrt{1 + |t| + |s|}\, |t-s|^{\frac{1-H}{2}}}.
	\end{align}
	The space \( \mathcal{B}_H \) is defined as the closure of \( C_0^\infty(\mathbb{R}^-, \mathbb{R}^d) \) with respect to this norm and is a separable Banach space.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.068725+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6cc4796a536f29b1cedb29197fe9ee2e86676ef810165480a976f52bd419fdee","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition $\ba$-admissible orientations of arXiv:2509.06273, 'Normalized Matching Property for Competing Urn Models'] For any  \. $\ba=(\mathrm{a}_1,\ldots, \mathrm{a}_d) \in \mathbb N^d$\., an 
 orientation $O$ of $G$ is 
