{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:06:21.667958+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6f54834d52c1b1c8707613fb515afe0f8e60c21527e2129164eed3085ff00e9e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2607.20838, 'Long-memory Markov chains with power-law intensities'] The latent processes $\mathbf{X}_n = \{(X_n, c_n)\}$ evolve according to 
	\begin{equation}
		\mathbf{X}_n = \Psi(\mathbf{X}_{n-1}, \tau_n) = (\Psi_x(x, c, t), \Psi_c(x, c, t)).

	\end{equation}
	where
	\begin{align}
		\Psi_x(x, c, t) &= x \left(1 + \frac{t}{c}\right)^{-p} + \xi,  \\
		\Psi_c(x, c, t) &= \frac{\Psi_x(x, c, t)}{\,\frac{x}{c} \left(1 + \tfrac{t}{c}\right)^{-p-1} + \tfrac{\xi}{\gamma}}, 
	\end{align}
	with parameters $\mu> 0, \gamma> 0, \xi > 0$ and $p>1$.
	Equivalently, the update equations can be written as
	\begin{align*}
		&X_n = X_{n-1} \left(1  + \frac{\tau_n}{c_{n-1}} \right)^{-p} + \xi \\
		&\frac{X_n}{c_n} =  \frac{X_{n-1}}{c_{n-1}} \left(1  + \frac{\tau_n}{c_{n-1}} \right)^{-p-1} + \frac{\xi}{\gamma}.
	\end{align*}
	Define $\Phi(t, x, c)$ as
	\begin{align}
		\Phi(t, x, c) & = \int_0^t  \lambda_{n-1}(s; x, c) \D s= \int_0^t \left( \mu + \frac{x c^p}{(c+s)^p} \right)\, \D s \\
		& = \mu t + \dfrac{x c}{p-1}\left( 1 - \bigg(1+\dfrac{t}{c}\bigg)^{-p+1}  \right). 
	\end{align}
	The inter-arrival time $\tau_n$ is generated via the inverse transform
	\[
	\tau_n = \Phi^{-1}(\varepsilon_n,  X_{n-1}, c_{n-1}),
	\]
	where the inverse is taken with respect to $t$ for fixed $x$ and $c$,
	and $\{\varepsilon_n\}$ is an i.i.d. sequence drawn from $F_\varepsilon$
	satisfying $F_\varepsilon(0) = 0$ (so that $\varepsilon_n \ge 0$ a.s.), $\mu_{\varepsilon} = \mathbb{E}[\varepsilon] < \infty$,
	and $\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon^2] < \infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.776848+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6f549eb8f81829a27918dfe5c3bff4160b5cb38425a8b45e119eec4939ade5e7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 4 of arXiv:2603.25459, 'Cram\'{e}r-type moderate deviation for double index permutation statistics'] Let $\text{Des}(\pi)$ and $\text{Inv}(\pi)$ be the number of descents and inversions of a random permutation $\pi\in S_{n}$, $W$ and $T$ are normalized statistics defined as (\ref{normalized-descent-inversion}). Then we have 
