{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.442649+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_70be948bac045b482e0789a203fe9b2cda2f9152ad1668e66999e653d08a78fd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 17 of arXiv:2604.07478, 'Mixing Times and Cutoff for the Rook's Walk'] Let $P$ be the transition matrix of the RWBD chain. Then, $\Phi$ is an eigenfunction of $P$ with eigenvalue $\alpha$ if
         \[\alpha= 1 - \frac{n}{d(n-1)}\quad \text{and}\quad \Phi(x) = 1 \frac{nx}{d(n-1)}\]
         \begin{proof}
           Clearly $\alpha = \lambda_1$. Hence, consider $K_1(x)$
            \begin{align*}
            K_1(x) &= \sum_{j=0}^1 (-1)^j{x \choose j}{d-x \choose 1-j}(n-1)^{1-j}\\
            &= (-1)^0{x\choose 0}{d-x \choose 1}(n-1)^{1} + (-1)^1{x \choose 1}{d-x\choose 0}(n-1)^{0}\\
            &= (d-x)(n-1) -x\\
            &= d(n-1) -nx\\
            &\propto 1 -\frac{nx}{d(n-1)} = \Phi(x)
            \end{align*}
            Thus, $\Phi(x)$ is an eigenfunction with eigenvalue $\alpha$.
         \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.314315+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_70c35b99c5bd181e73a64e78d22af5a7788f56c3d0df57d58edd5cc9a8ab33b4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lemlwp1 of arXiv:2608.30777, 'Inhomogeneous nonlinear Schr\"odinger equation \\ in Fourier-Lebesgue and modulation space'] Let $\alpha \in (1,5-2b),\, 0<b\leq 1/4$ and $p_{0}$  be as follows 
