 		\mathbf{P}\Big[ \mathbf{T}_{\Lambda_n}\leq \frac{c}{\log^2(n)} \Big] \leq O(\frac{1}{n^4}). 
 	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.157850+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_71aa900d9df597f04047133d873d5117ff4a3663a26f57df525a2136b29a298b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop gl-Thom-space-la of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] The global functor ${\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak t\kern-.5pt\mathfrak h}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{gl}$ is a left adjoint.
    \begin{proof}
        Proposition~(prop:thom-space-marvelous of the paper) immediately implies the existence of a pointwise right adjoint. To see that these pointwise right adjoints satisfy the Beck--Chevalley condition, we may then equivalently show that ${\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak t\kern-.5pt\mathfrak h}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{gl}$ satisfies the Beck--Chevalley condition with respect to the left adjoints along restriction. This follows as usual by observing that the Beck--Chevalley condition holds on the pointset level and that all functors in sight are left Quillen.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.366054+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_71ae308398fa2ca00b730c245c97db39d0b581222641142e2f2f12f2dcd1a68b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition claim:moment expansion of arXiv:2606.02472, 'Correlated uniform attachment trees'] Let $X_1,\ldots,X_n$ be Bernoulli random variables and let $X=\sum_{i=1}^{n} X_i$. Suppose that for any subset of indices $S\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n\}$ with $\abs{S}\leq 4$, there is a constant $\widetilde C$ such that

    \ben{
        \mathbb{P} \left( X_i = 1 \text{ for all } i \in S \right) \leq \widetilde C \prod_{i \in S} \mathbb{P} (X_i = 1).
    }
 Then there is a constant $\widehat C$ depending only on $\widetilde C$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E} \left[ X^4 \right] \leq \widehat C \bclr{\mathbb{E}[X]+\mathbb{E}[X]^4}.

    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:58:40.873197+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_71b711eeb3b77120963f63496d4d95ac4ed6b2b75962c6546727b2f2fe4595b7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop Zph2 of arXiv:2605.10617, 'Logarithmic scaling of selective sweep curves:\\ from tents to houses'] Assume that $Z_1^N$ starts in $\big\lfloor\tfrac {\log N}{\varphi_N}\big\rfloor$.
    Then for any $T$, $\delta>0$
    \begin{equation}
    \lim_{N\to\infty} \mathbb{P} \Big(  \sup_{t \leq T} \Big| \log_N^+(Z^N_{1} (t \varphi_N^{-1} \log N)) - (b+at) \Big| >\delta \Big)
        = 0,    
    \end{equation}
    i.e.\ $\big(\log_N^+(Z^N_{1} (t \varphi_N^{-1} \log N)),t\in[0,T]\big)$ converges uniformly in probability to $(b+at)_{t\in[0,T]}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:13:18.538477+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_71b74051784e01176be74d62f2e753793053bc45fb96c7486d427221fe24fd69","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 30 (Monotone Density Theorem, \protect{\autocite[Theorem 1.7.2) of arXiv:2608.30668, 'Recurrence and capacity of stable branching random walks'] {BinghamGoldieTeugels1987RegularVariation}}]

Let \(f\) be a positive measurable function, locally bounded on
\([A,+\infty)\). Assume that \(f\) is ultimately monotone.

\begin{itemize}
\item
  If there is \(C>0\), \(\beta>0\) and a slowly varying function
  \(\ell\) such that

  \begin{equation*}{
  \int_{A}^{x}f(t)\dd t \sim_{x \to +\infty} C x^{\beta} \ell(x),
   }\end{equation*}

  then \(f(x) \sim_{x \to +\infty} \beta C x^{\beta-1} \ell(x)\).
\item
  If \(\int_{A}^{+\infty}f(t)\dd t < +\infty\) and in addition there is
  \(C>0\), \(\beta < 0\) and a slowly varying function \(\ell\) such
  that

  \begin{equation*}{
