{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:57:16.077355+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_723ecd6baa3beb45091cacbe32e9810fc6f6aee938b3689d1ad7a1623ea4bc8d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Pro hash of arXiv:2507.00737, 'Dispersion models on a circle: universal properties and asymptotic results'] We have the following convergences in distribution, [Flajolet \& Poblete \& Viola [flajolet1997Analysis], Janson [MR1871562],
	Chassaing \& Marckert [MR1814521]], 
	\[{\sf Cost}(n) / n^{3/2}\xrightarrow[n]{(d)} \int_0^1 e(t)\mathrm{d} t\]
	and [Chassaing \& Louchard\footnote{In [chassaing2002phase], \eref{eq:cl} is not stated, but it is a consequence of their Lemma 3.7, in which they prove the convergence in distribution of the {\L}ukasiewicz  path encoding the parking at time $n-\lambda\sqrt{n}$, and normalized by $n^{1/2}$ and suitably change of origin, to  $(e_\lambda(s)-\underline{e_\lambda}(s), s\in [0,1]$). The cost being an integral of the {\L}ukasiewicz  path, the convergence stated in our Proposition (pro:hash of the paper) follows (this tool is used in a lot of places in the literature, including  [MR1814521], [MR3547745], [MW2019]).}[chassaing2002phase]]
	\begin{equation}
	{\sf Cost}(t_n(\lambda)) / n^{3/2}\xrightarrow[n]{(d)} \int_{0}^1 {\sf e}_\lambda(s)-\underline{{\sf e}_\lambda}(s) \mathrm{d} s.\end{equation}
	where ${\sf e}$ is again, the Brownian excursion, and
	${\sf e}_\lambda(s)= {\sf e}(s)-\lambda s,~\textrm{and }~ \underline{{\sf e}_\lambda}(s)= \min_{t \leq s} ~{\sf e}_\lambda(t).$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.778150+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_723f221d15d9035ffb8cc10d671af00953c660340f0c8d001093b265d45e37f9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem conv of arXiv:2411.09257, 'Iterated Generalized Counting Process and its Extensions'] Let $0=t_0\le t_1\le\dots\le t_n=t$ be a partition of $[0,t]$ and $h_X^{*(m)}(\cdot)$ denotes the $m$-fold convolution of the density function $h_{X_1}(\cdot)$ of $X_1$. Then, the finite dimensional distribution of compound IGCP has the following form:
{\small\begin{align}
\hat{H}_{Z(t_1), Z(t_2),\dots, Z(t_n)}(x_1,x_2,\dots,x_n)&=\sum_{\substack{m_l\ge0\\ l=1,2,\dots,n}}\Big(\prod_{l=1}^{n}\hat{p}(m_l,\Delta t_l)\Big)\nonumber\\
&\ \  \cdot\int_{-\infty}^{u_1}\int_{-\infty}^{u_2}\dots \int_{-\infty}^{u_n}\Big(\prod_{r=1}^{n}h_{X_{1}}^{*(m_r)}(y_r)\Big)\mathrm{d}y_n\mathrm{d}y_{n-1}\dots\mathrm{d}y_1,
\end{align}}
where $\Delta t_l=t_l-t_{l-1}$ and $u_r=x_r-(y_1+y_2+\dots+y_{r-1})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:37.357345+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7240de24aefa6919d32b6808c0c6526841e1ed10664c1e2ff2b9e665d74303bf","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2606.17859, 'Full $\Gamma-$expansion for the level-two large deviation rate functionals of non-reversib'] Consider a non empty Polish space $\mc X$ and a sequence of
functionals $U_n: \mc X \to [0,+\infty]$. A full $\Gamma-$expansion of
$U_n$ is given by the speeds $(\alpha^{(p)}_n, n\geq 1)$,
$-\mf r \leq p \leq \mf q$, and the functionals
$U^{(p)}: \mc X \to [0,+\infty]$, $-\mf r \leq p \leq \mf q$, if:

\begin{enumerate}

\item The speeds $\alpha^{(-\mf r)}_n, \dots, \alpha^{(\mf q)}_n$ are
positive sequences such that $\alpha^{(p)}_n\prec \alpha^{(p+1)}_n $,
for every $-\mf r \leq p < \mf q$;

\item $\alpha_{\epsilon}^{(p)} U_n$ $\Gamma-$converges to $U^{(p)}$,
$-\mf r \leq p \leq \mf q$;

\item $U^{(p+1)}(x)$ is finite if, and only if, $x$ belongs to the
$0-$level set of $U^{(p)}$, for every $-\mf r \leq p < \mf q$;

\item $\beta_n U_n$ $\Gamma-$converges to $0$, for every sequence
$\beta_n \prec \alpha_n^{(-\mf r)}$; and

\item the $0-$level set of $U^{(\mf q)}$ is a singleton.
\end{enumerate}

In this case, we express the expansion as 
\begin{equation*}
U_n = \sum_{p\in -\mf r}^{\mf q}\frac{1}{\alpha_{n}^{(p)}} \, U^{(p)}.
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:37:25.945492+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7241e6f9bcc1f4eb119e519e85ad3413f59c6854961c1c516cd6637d900ce595","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 56 (Habiro diagonal lift) of arXiv:2608.29585, '2608.29585'] Let $K$ be a zero-framed knot, let $n\ge2$, and let
