instead of the function $\Phi_{m,n}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:14:46.379047+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72ce0212db90b545c4d8e7063c70dadf13798546b4b02bb80c7f039aa05932ee","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] The integrals over the symmetric group $S_N$ are given by
$$\int_{S_N}u_{i_1j_1}\ldots u_{i_kj_k}=\begin{cases}
\frac{(N-|\ker i|)!}{N!}&{\rm if}\ \ker i=\ker j\\
0&{\rm otherwise}
\end{cases}$$
where $\ker i$ denotes as usual the partition of $\{1,\ldots,k\}$ whose blocks collect the equal indices of $i$, and where $|.|$ denotes the number of blocks.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.743446+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72ce592e9ca93f6348f1fb6c72e1c2251e71a4ddcc78b9b3ace3389512d82e26","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem surftype of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] Let $\lambda$ be a connected surface (i.e., $2$-manifold), possibly non-compact and possibly with boundary. Then at least one of the following holds:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\pi_1(\lambda)$ remains nontrivial after capping off all the closed boundary components of $\lambda$.
        \item $\lambda$ is a compact genus 0 surface.
        \item $\lambda$ becomes homeomorphic to $\mathbb{R}^2$ after filling the closed boundary components with disks and deleting the open boundary components. In that case, $\pi_1(\lambda)$ is freely generated by its closed boundary components.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.588041+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72d9e648386aaf2af43273034d8107490b8856d912a96f641e1bb14c64fef306","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition solution definition (u P) of arXiv:2605.16963, 'Stochastic Euler Equations with Pseudo-differential Noise: Continuous and Discontinuous Pe'] Let $\tau^*$ be a stopping time with $\mathbb P(\tau^*>0)=1$, and let $(u,\tau^*) \triangleq (u(t))_{t\in [0,\tau^*)}$ be a progressively measurable process on $H_{\rm div}^s$.
		The pair $(u,\tau^*)$ is called a maximal $H_{\rm div}^s$ classical solution to (equation Target problem (u P) damp of the paper) if ${\mathbb P}\text{-}{\rm a.s.}$ the following conditions are satisfied:
		\begin{itemize}
			\item (equation Target problem (u P) damp of the paper) holds as an equality in $C(\mathbb K^d)$ for all $t\in[0,\tau^*)$;
			\item  $\sup_{[0,T]}\|u(t)\|_{H^s}<\infty$ for all $T<\tau^*$;
			\item $\limsup_{t\uparrow\tau^*}\|u(t)\|_{\mathbb H^s}=\infty$ a.s. on $\{\tau^*<\infty\}.$
		\end{itemize}
		In particular, if $\mathbb P(\tau^*=\infty)=1$, the solution is called global.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:43:45.398498+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72da5752a9cca9945e048b12b0e42c62668c70ef77df98d551defeb0677c0886","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2609.04143, 'The Geometry of Real Anisotropic Bohnenblust--Hille Constants'] Let $\mathbf q=(q_1,\ldots,q_m)$ be a Bohnenblust--Hille exponent, fix $i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}$, and put $\mathbf q^+=(q_1,\ldots,q_m,2)$. Then
\begin{equation}
C_{\mathbf q^+}^{(m+1)} \ge2^{1/q_i-1/2}C_{\mathbf q}^{(m)}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:47:32.875079+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72db16a332ba16586e897eb7575cd4faece405be5c34e5870b44305a65e90f78","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 10 (Finite directional coefficient module) of arXiv:2609.02449, 'Finite Directional Coefficient Modules over Euler Fibers of $A$-Hypergeometric Systems'] The \emph{directional relation submodule} is
\[
U_{\Lambda,\bm{w}}^{\mathrm{dir}}
:=
U_{\Lambda}^{\mathrm{Eul}}
+
U_{\Lambda}^{\mathrm{tr}}
+
U_{\Lambda,\bm{w}}^{\mathrm{van}}
\subset
F_{\Lambda},
\]
and the \emph{finite directional coefficient module} is
