{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:41:33.832543+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72dc5f9c5aac6b5650e3644ac29117a0a9630930f84a1b177011c089d3dd9c76","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 25 (Smooth conditional fiber weights) of arXiv:2604.10792, 'Variable-Length Markov Chains on Finite Quivers: Boundary-Window Identifiability, Exact De'] Assume exact depth $r$ at $\theta_0$ together with \cref{ass:regularity}, and fix $m<r$. Let $y\in\cS_m$ and $z\in F_y$. Then, for $\theta$ near $\theta_0$, the stationary conditional weight
\begin{equation}
\alpha_z(\theta):=P_{\theta,\mathrm{stat}}(Z_t^{(r)}=z\mid Z_t^{(m)}=y)
\end{equation}
is well defined and $C^1$ in $\theta$.
More explicitly,
\begin{equation}
\alpha_z(\theta)=\frac{\pi_\theta(z)}{\sum_{w\in F_y}\pi_\theta(w)},
\end{equation}
where $\pi_\theta$ is the stationary law of the depth-$r$ chain.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:05:52.432847+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72e2190de48bbfbc18d4c852a2dd5b1a7b60bdc27f6fdbc9457205575b1c8e04","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9--lemma6.61 of arXiv:2504.15992, 'Repeated singular values of a random symmetric matrix and decoupled singular value estimat'] Assume that $A_n$ satisfies the assumptions in Theorem (Theorem1.2 of the paper) and $X\sim\operatorname{Col}_n(\zeta)$. Fix any $\kappa>0$, $\Delta>0$ and some $\sigma\in(0,1)$. Then there exists an event $\mathcal{E}_\sigma(A_n)$ with $\mathbb{P}(A_n\in\mathcal{E}_\sigma(A_n))\geq 1-\exp(-\Omega(n^{\sigma/2}))$ such that for any $A_n\in\mathcal{E}_\sigma(A_n)$, the following holds:

   \begin{enumerate}
       \item Fix any given $(\theta_1,\theta_2)\in\mathbb{R}^2$ satisfying $|\theta_1\theta_2|=1$.   Then there exists some $J\in\{1,2\}$ depending on $A_n$ and $|\theta_1/\theta_2|$ such that, for any $1\leq k\leq {c_0}_{(9--lemma6.61 of the paper)}n^\sigma$ and for any \begin{equation}s\in\left(e^{-cn}, \frac{C_{(9--lemma6.61 of the paper)}\cdot \mu_k(\lambda_J)}{{\left(\prod_{i=1}^2\mu_1(\lambda_i)\right)^{1/2}}}\right),\end{equation} we have the estimate
    \begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}_{\widetilde{X}}\left(\left\|\sum_{i=1}^2 \frac{\theta_i}{\mu_1(\lambda_i)}(A_n-\lambda_i I_n)^{-1}\widetilde{X}\right\|_2\leq s
\right) \lesssim s e^{-ck},
    \end{equation} where ${c_0}_{(9--lemma6.61 of the paper)}>0$ and $C_{(9--lemma6.61 of the paper)}>0$ are two constants that depend only on $\kappa,\Delta$ and $B$. The constant $c>0$ depends only on $B$.
    \item  Fix any given $(\theta_1,\theta_2)\in\mathbb{R}^2$ satisfying $\max(|\theta_1|,|\theta_2|)=1$. Then there exist $I,J\in\{1,2\}$ depending on $A_n$ and $|\theta_1/\theta_2|$ such that, for any $1\leq k\leq {c_0}_{(9--lemma6.61 of the paper)}n^\sigma$ and any 
    \begin{equation}
s\in(e^{-cn},\frac{\mu_k(\lambda_J)}{\mu_1(\lambda_I)})
    \end{equation} we have the same estimate (equation 2122122121122 of the paper).
     \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:57:20.690526+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72e4e3f52ba610c721d919413cea8239c36d1602a00b173e2a5ce633d20efcd3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:concentration3 of arXiv:2411.10235, '\textbf{Smooth transport map via diffusion process}'] There exists  $0<C_\star<C_2$ such that if $t\in [0,1)$  and $x\in \mathbb{R}^d$ satisfy \begin{equation}
1-t^2\leq C_\star^{-1}\log(\epsilon^{-1})^{-C_\star} \text{ and } \|tx\|^2\leq 1-C_2^{-1}\log(\epsilon^{-1}+1)^{-K},
\end{equation}
then for all $l\in \{1,...,\lfloor \beta \rfloor\}$ we have
    $$\int \|f^l(y)-f^l(tx)\|^2dp^{t,x}(y)\leq C\log(\epsilon^{-1})^{C_3}(1-t^2)^{\beta-\lfloor \beta \rfloor }\log((1-t^2)^{-1})^{-2\theta}.$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.315578+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72e515c361011ac3306d7f1cf4fbe176788500557735fff66860bd7115861e41","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.30973, 'Pseudo-Anosov flow and dynamics on guts'] A \emph{sutured manifold} \((N,R_{+},R_{-},\gamma)\) is a compact oriented 3-manifold \(N\) with boundary together with a set \(\gamma\subset \partial N\) satisfying the following conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \(\gamma\) consists of pairwise disjoint annuli \(A(\gamma)\) and tori \(T(\gamma)\). A component of \(A(\gamma)\) is called a \emph{sutured annulus} and a component of \(T(\gamma)\) is called a \emph{sutured torus}.
\item Each sutured annulus contains a homologically nontrivial oriented simple closed curve, called a \emph{suture}.
\item \(R_{+}\cup R_{-}= \partial N\setminus \operatorname{int}(\gamma)\) is oriented such that the orientation of \(R_{\pm}\) is coherent with the orientation of sutures in \(\partial R_{\pm}\) by boundary orientation principle.
Denote by \(R_{+}(\gamma)\) (resp. \(R_{-}(\gamma)\)) the components of \(\partial N\setminus \operatorname{int}(\gamma)\) whose normal vectors point out of (resp. into) \(N\).
\end{enumerate}
In practice, we usually abbreviate the notation to \((N,\gamma)\), or more simply to \(N\), when there is no ambiguity.","status":"unverified"}

