{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.805786+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_73335bb05bf709b767ec0ac14d5a2ef7ed0f33f4e0984fb04083cd0905c83998","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop p:CDRP-scaling of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] There exists an event $\Xi_0\subset \Xi$ with $\mathbb{P}(\Xi_0)=1$ such that
    \begin{enumerate}[label={\rm(\roman*)}]
        \item\textup{(Translation).} For each $(u,z) \in \mathbb{R}^2$, on the event $\Xi_0\cap\mathsf{T}(u,z)$, it holds for all $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$, all $\beta\geqslant 0$, and all Borel sets $S\in\mathcal{B}(C([s,t]))$ that
        \begin{align*}
            \mathbb{P}_{\beta,(s+u, x+z),(t+u, y+z)}\left(
                X(\cdot + u) - z
                \in S\right)
            \circ \mathcal{T}_{-u, -z}
            =
            \mathbb{P}_{\beta,(s,x),(t,y)}(X\in S).
        \end{align*}
        \item\textup{(Shearing).} For each $(r,\nu)\in\mathbb{R}^2$, on the event $\Xi_0\cap\mathsf{S}(r,\nu)$, it holds for all $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$, all $\beta\geqslant 0$, and all Borel sets $S\in\mathcal{B}(C([s,t]))$ that
        \begin{align*}
            \mathbb{P}_{\beta,(s, x+\nu(s-r)),(t, y+\nu(t-r))}\left(
                X(\cdot) - \nu(\cdot - r)
                \in S
            \right)
            \circ \mathcal{S}_{r, -\nu}
            =
            \mathbb{P}_{\beta,(s,x),(t,y)}(X\in S).
        \end{align*}
        \item\textup{(Diffusive scaling).}
        For each $\lambda>0$, on the event $\Xi_0\cap \mathsf{D}(\lambda)$, it holds for all $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$, all $\beta\geqslant 0$, and all Borel sets $S\in\mathcal{B}(C([s,t]))$ that
        \begin{align*}
            \mathbb{P}_{\beta\lambda^{-1/2}, (\lambda^2 s, \lambda x),(\lambda^2 t,\lambda y)}\left(
                \lambda^{-1}X(\lambda^2\cdot)
                \in S
            \right)
            \circ \mathcal{D}_{\lambda^{-2},\lambda^{-1}}
            =
            \mathbb{P}_{\beta,(s,x),(t,y)}\left(X\in S\right).
        \end{align*}
    \end{enumerate}
    The events $\mathsf{T}(u,z), \mathsf{S}(r,\nu), \mathsf{D}(\lambda)$ are defined in (t:AJRS-Z-scaling of the paper).
    Like in (t:AJRS-Z-scaling of the paper), the composition notation $\circ$ reflects that the above polymer probabilities are regarded as real-valued functions of the white noise.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:48:27.282176+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_733540549a5bb64b3cd71e5f721951833b09d5cbac94add88d9d4df3f88f9ce2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 4 of arXiv:2604.25336, 'Central limit theorem for slow-fast system under mixed fractional Brownian motion'] Under assumptions \textbf{H1}-\textbf{H3} and \(\bar h (x)= \int_{\mathbb{R}^{m}} h(x,y)\mu(\dd y)=0\). Define that 
\begin{equation}
u(x,y) =\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathcal{P}_t^x h(x, Y_t^x) \dd t.
\end{equation} 
Then \(u(x,y)\) is the solution to the Poisson equation \eqref{Poisson_eq}.
Moreover, \(u(x,y)\in C^2\). For all \((x,y)\in \mathbb{R}^n\times \mathbb{R}^m\), \(D_xu(x,y)\) and \(D^2_{xx}u(x,y)\) is linear growth, that is for all \((x,y)\in\mathbb{R}^m\times \mathbb{R}^n\), there exists a constant $L_u>0$ such that 
$$|D_xu(x,y)|\vee|D^2_{xx}u(x,y)|\le L_u(1+|x|+|y|).$$
And \(D_yu(x,y), D^2_{xy}u(x,y), D^2_{yy}u(x,y)\) is globally bounded, that is there exists a constant $L_u>0$ such that 
$$|D_yu(x,y)|\vee|D^2_{xy}u(x,y)|\vee|D^2_{yy}u(x,y)|\le L_u.$$
Moreover, when \(Y\) is independent of \(x\), \(\sup_{x\in \mathbb{R}^m}|u(x,y)|\le C(1+|y|)\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.825297+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7336970761cf8a7bb364f09225fe4a08313267250fa58c23a21ad4be09fb129f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:optimal-four-mark of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] For every $h\geq2$, put
