{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.370096+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7340b2dd62dfd5d72ed52bf42126fa6a749ce91cf7be28f26ffb7099be747182","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition max2 of arXiv:2507.10294, 'Guessing Strategies for Shelf-Shuffling Machines'] Let $n\geq 5$. Then, $I_2^*=I_{n-1}^*=\{2,3\}$, i.e. the player should guess card $2$ or card $3$ in positions $2$ and $n-1$.
    \begin{proof}
        Note that the $n=2$ case was covered in the proof of Proposition $(first card of the paper)$. One verifies that when $n=3$, $p_{2,2}=p_{3,2}=1/2$. When $n=4$, we have $p_{3,2}=p_{4,2}=p_{3,3}=p_{4,3}=3/8$, and Proposition $(doubly stochastic of the paper)$ implies that the player should guess card $3$ or $4$ in position $2$ and card $3$ or $4$ in position $3$.
        We will show that $p_{2,2}$ and $p_{3,2}$ are the unique maxima of column $2$. The column $n-1$ case will follow by Proposition $(guessing strategy symmetry of the paper)$. We have, for any $i\leq n$, $p_{i,2}=\frac{i-1+\binom{i-1}{n-2}}{2^i}$. The binomial coefficient vanishes when $i-1<n-2$, i.e. when $i\leq n-2$. For the other two cases, we have $p_{n-1,2}=\frac{n-1}{2^{n-1}}=p_{n,2}$ using Lemma $(last two rows are equal of the paper)$. So the problem has been reduced to finding
        \begin{equation}
        \max_{1\leq i\leq n-2}\frac{i-1}{2^i}
        \end{equation}
        and comparing this value with $p_{n,2}=\frac{n}{2^{n}}$. Some calculus shows that the maximum in equation $(max2 of the paper)$ is $1/4$ and is achieved only at $i=2$ and $i=3$. To compare this with $p_{n,2}$, we note that by the same calculus used above, for $n\geq 2$, the sequence $p_{n,2}=\frac{n}{2^n}$ is strictly decreasing with $p_{4,2}=1/4$. Hence, $p_{n,2}<1/4$ for all $n\geq 5$ and thus the maxima are unique.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:38:04.517286+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7341292fe0c1ca43047eab231ae6a171ff5427ddc8d684b108801958e155b5aa","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:biskup_xi of arXiv:2607.18011, 'From local giants to locality in long-range percolation'] \thlabel{def:renorm_scheme_2}
