{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:47:04.934027+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_75f4066411262c476a161231a1b95f91f82bd1e8ff48af6989b80b1473e95f75","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2412.02777, 'How to quantify the coherence of a set of beliefs'] A \textit{dissimilarity function} $\ell(p, q)$ is a function $\ell:[0, 1]\times [0, 1]\rightarrow [0, \infty]$ satisfying 
    \begin{enro}
        \item $\ell$ is lower semi-continuous with respect to $p$
        \item $\ell$ is strictly convex when finite with respect to $p$
        \item $\ell(p, q)=0$ if and only if $p=q$.
    \end{enro}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:52:23.590813+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_75fc00604b94c85124c8a2280e6c93766ea447879430dc19c664351d60664ae0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Maruyama \cite{Maruyama1949} of arXiv:2602.19773, 'Regularization of a stationary point process by a stationary increments perturbation'] Let $X$ be a real-valued stationary Gaussian process, then
			\begin{itemize}
				\item $X$ is ergodic (and weakly-mixing) if and only if $\lambda$ is non-atomic.
				\item $X$ is mixing if and only if 
				\begin{equation*}
					r(t)\underset{\|t\|\to +\infty}{\longrightarrow}0.
				\end{equation*}
			\end{itemize}
			In particular, if $\lambda$ is absolutely continuous, then $X$ is mixing. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:31:20.600263+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_75fe81ba1f49b08e6706e5b6c7e10bc41666ac7040855aedd59bb6a9c87cbce4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 25 (Exact posterior covariance geometry) of arXiv:2608.25706, 'Fast rates in Bayesian online learning with approximate posteriors'] \label[lemma]{prop:gp-covariance}
