{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.919711+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7683c069f078e7b65c2c6eae93d19e99e9a7166ca9da98d90f784a4e904fd0cf","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem mom-est of arXiv:2411.06974, '{\bf Distribution dependent SDEs with multiplicative fractional noise}'] Let $\frac 1 {2}<\beta<H$. Assume that $\widetilde{b}$ is of linear growth:
$$|\widetilde{b}(r,x)|\leq K_{\widetilde{b}}+L_{\widetilde{b}}(1+|x|),\ \ r\in[S,T],\ \ x\in\mathbb R^d,$$
and $\widetilde{\sigma}$ is bounded  by a positive constant $M_{\widetilde{\sigma}}$ and
\begin{align*}
\|\widetilde{\sigma}(r,x)-\widetilde{\sigma}(s,y)\|\leq K_{\widetilde{\sigma}}|r-s|^{\gamma_0}+L_{\widetilde{\sigma}}|x-y|,\ \ r,s\in[S,T], \ \ x,y\in\mathbb R^d
\end{align*}
for some $\gamma_0 >1-\beta$, where $K_{\widetilde{b}},L_{\widetilde b},K_{\widetilde{\sigma}},L_{\widetilde{\sigma}}$ are all positive constants.
Then for the solution $\widetilde{X}$ of equation (equation Lemma 3.2-0 of the paper) and any $p\geq 1$, there holds
\begin{align*}
&\left[\mathbb E\left(\|\widetilde{X}\|_{S,T,\infty}+\|\widetilde{X}\|_{S,T,\beta}\right)^p\right]^{\frac 1 p}\cr
\leq& C_1(T-S,H,\beta,\gamma_0,p)\left(1+K_{\widetilde{b}}+K_{\widetilde{\sigma}} \right)+ C_2(T-S,H,\beta,\gamma_0,p)\left(\mathbb E|\widetilde{X}(S)|^p\right)^{\frac 1 p},
\end{align*}
where $C_i(T-S,H,\beta,\gamma_0,p),i=1,2$ are two positive constants which are independent of $S,T$, $K_{\widetilde b},K_{\widetilde \sigma}$, $\widetilde X(S)$ and decreasing as $T-S$ decreases, and satisfy
\begin{align*}
\lim_{T-S\rightarrow 0^+}C_1(T-S,H,\beta,\gamma_0,p)=0\ \ \ \mathrm{and}\ \ \ \lim_{T-S\rightarrow 0^+}C_2(T-S,H,\beta,\gamma_0,p)=1.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:32:34.745241+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_768a30769bc3e188ca117bd492b0b34fbf9c83204868c52801e31a4e3b75ad32","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:mart_bound of arXiv:2411.07878, 'Bernstein-type and Bennett-type inequalities for unbounded matrix martingales'] Let $(\mathbf{Y}_i)_{i=1}^n \subset \mathbb{H}(d)$ be a matrix-valued stochastic process adapted to filtration $(\mathscr{F}_i)_{i=1}^n$. 
    Define
    \begin{gather*}
        \mathbf{V}_i \coloneqq \ln \E\left[e^{\mathbf{Y}_i} \middle| \mathscr{F}_{i-1}\right] \in \mathbb{H}(d), \quad i = 1, \dots, n,\\
        \mathbf{Z}_k \coloneqq \sum_{i=1}^k (\mathbf{Y}_i - \mathbf{V}_i), \quad k = 0, \dots, n.
    \end{gather*}

    Then for any $\mathbf{A} \in\mathbb{H}(d)$ it holds that
    \begin{equation}

        \E\left[\tr \exp\left\{\mathbf{Z}_k - \mathbf{A} \right\} \middle| \mathscr{F}_{k-1}\right] 
        \le \tr \elr{\mathbf{Z}_{k-1} - \mathbf{A}} \text{~~a.s.\ for all~~} k \in [n],
    \end{equation}
    and
    \[
    \mathbb{P}\lrc{\max_{k \in [n]} \lambda_{\max}\left(\mathbf{Z}_k - \mathbf{A}\right) \ge 0}
    \le \tr e^{-\mathbf{A}} .
    \]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:57:16.076707+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_768cc96bc1868709e2cfa88e69907f55d99e3b3e4038f0113abbac4bb64483c2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem eq:PilingD of arXiv:2507.00737, 'Dispersion models on a circle: universal properties and asymptotic results'] Let $A$ be a valid DDM\xspace process on ${\cal C}_n$. If the masses $(m_0,\cdots,m_{k-1})$ are non-negative multiple of $1/n$ and satisfy $W(m[k])<1$, then, the discrete piling propensity is
	\begin{equation}
	Q^{(n)}(m[k])=\mathbb{P}({\bf N}_k^{(n)}=1) =
	\left(W(m[k])+\frac1n\right)^{k-1}.
	\end{equation} Moreover, for any $\ell\in{\cal C}_n$, by letting $A_j$ be the starting point of the block $j$, we have,
	\begin{equation}\mathbb{P}\left({\bf N}_k^{(n)}=1,A_0=\ell\right)= Q^{(n)}(m[k])/ n.  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:31:14.326846+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_769618146375c651b213db912c6846802a59241623da4b3cbe054e4dd04c55b8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 3.6 of arXiv:2606.16237, 'The Backward Stochastic Partial Differential Integral Equations: Solvability and Compariso'] Assume that Assumptions (ass:b1 of the paper)--(ass:b3 of the paper) hold. If for a nonnegative integer $n$ and any multi-index $\alpha$ such that $|\alpha| \leq n$, we have
  \begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
    \mathop{\mathrm{ess}\sup}\limits_{\Omega\times[0,T]\times \mathbb{R}^d} &\left( |D^{\alpha}a| + |D^{\alpha}b| + |D^{\alpha}c| + |D^{\alpha}\sigma| + |D^{\alpha}\nu| \right)+\mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}\limits_{\Omega\times[0,T]\times Z \times \mathbb{R}^d} |D^\alpha k(x,e)| \leq L,\\
    &\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ f_0\in  \mathbb{H}^n\ \ \text{and}\ \ \varphi \in L^2(\Omega, \mathscr{F}_T; H^{n+1}),
\end{aligned}
  \end{equation*}
then BSPDIE (equation eq:3.2 of the paper) admits a unique strong solution $(u, q, r)\in \mathbb{S}^2(H^{n+1}) \cap \mathbb{H}^{n+2}\times \mathbb{H}^{n+1}\times\mathbb{H}^{\nu,n+1}$ satisfying
  \begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
    \mathbb{E}\left[\sup_{0 \leq t \leq T} \| u(t) \|_{n+1}^2 \right] + ||| u |||_{n+2}^2 + ||| q |||_{n+1}^2 + ||| r |||_{\nu, n+1}^2\leq C \left( ||| f_0 |||_{n}^2 + \mathbb{E} \| \varphi \|_{n+1}^2 \right),
    \end{split}
  \end{equation}
  where \( {{C = C(L,\kappa,\alpha,\delta,T)}} \) is a positive constant.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:48:00.293274+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_769ab81b0ec4c606be9dc3b2dd2136fef9e0912aacd667e9402fa98dc17cf663","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:kappaFormulas of arXiv:2609.03094, 'Discrete Gromov--Wasserstein Duality: Algorithms and Isomorphism Testing'] Fix a graph $G\in\mathcal G_N$ with $m>0$ edges. Then, $\kappa(G,G)\geq m+4$ with equality if and only if $G=G_{ij}$ for some $i,j\in[N]$ with $i\neq j$. Furthermore, for each $i,j\in[N]$ with $i\neq j$,
 \begin{equation}

            \kappa(G,G_{ij}) = \begin{cases} 2(\mathrm{deg}(v_i(G))+\mathrm{deg}(v_j(G)))+1, & \text{if } (\mathrm{A}_G)_{ij}=1 ,
            \\
            2(\mathrm{deg}(v_i(G))+\mathrm{deg}(v_j(G))),&\text{otherwise},
            \end{cases}
      \end{equation}
        where $v_i(G)$ denotes the $i$-th vertex of $G$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:31.971970+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_76a0a78a9e01bcebaf35169a5bb0d05aa6698ffb85e9f29809edecbb4bd2f075","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2606.10313, '\textbf{Invariance principles for additive functionals of voter models}'] For any integer $m\geq 1$, $H_1, H_2: \{0, 1\}^m\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$,
\[
x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_m, y_1, y_2, \ldots, y_m\in G
\]
and $t, r\geq 0$, we have
\begin{align*}
&\left|{\rm Cov}_{\mu_p}\left(H_1(\eta_{t+r}, x_1, \ldots, x_m), H_2(\eta_t, y_1, \ldots, y_m)\right)\right|\\
&\leq \frac{2\|H_1\|_\infty\|H_2\|_\infty}{C_2}\sum_{i=1}^m\sum_{j=1}^m\int_0^{+\infty}p_{s+r}(x_i, y_j)ds,
\end{align*}
where $C_2$ is defined as in Assumption \eqref{Assumption 1.5} and $\|H_1\|_\infty=\max_{\iota\in \{0, 1\}^m}|H_1(\iota)|$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.934035+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_76a1a1469c6c52a624192450c5927ad116b98cb71b079f58b7b1a24412d000f7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:gauge_change_loop of arXiv:2506.12171, 'The multinomial dimer model'] Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite bipartite graph. The weight function $\mathbf w : E\to \mathbb{R}$ is gauge equivalent to the uniform weight function if and only if for every loop $\gamma$ of $G$, we have 
    \begin{align}
        w_{e_1}w_{e_3}\dots w_{e_{2k-1}} = w_{e_2} w_{e_4}\dots w_{e_{2k}}
    \end{align}
    where $e_1,\dots, e_{2k}$ are the edges around $\gamma$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:53:54.888227+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_76a43d86d10a360f69c4c09de35dd2841688a8dd3e2563721847604bd6558f4f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2607.17087, 'Transportation-cost inequalities for invariant measures of stochastic reaction-diffusion e'] Let $u^i(t,\cdot)$ denote the solution to equation \eqref{260702.2110}, $i=1,2$.
\begin{itemize}
  \item [(i)] Assume that (H2) holds. If $\alpha>-\frac{1}{2}\pi^2$,
then for any $ T\geq 0$,
\begin{align}
    \sup_{t\in [0,T]}\mathbb{E} \Vert u^2(t) - u^1(t)\Vert_{L^1} \leq 2\sqrt{C_1(\alpha)} \left( \mathbb{E} \int_{0}^{T}\int_{0}^{1} |h^2(s,y) - h^1(s,y)|^2 dsdy \right)^{\frac{1}{2}},
\end{align}
where the constant $C_1(\alpha)$ is independent of $T$ and given by
\begin{align}
   C_1(\alpha) :=8\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{(2k+1)^2 \pi^2 [(2k+1)^2\pi^2 + 2\alpha]} .
\end{align}
  \item [(ii)] Assume that (H1) and (H2) hold. Suppose that
  \begin{align}
    \sup_{t\in [0,T]} \mathbb{E}\int_{0}^{1}|u^2(t,x) - u^1(t,x)|^2  dx <\infty  \quad\text{for any }\  T\geq 0.
  \end{align}
  Let
  \[
  F_2(\alpha):= 2\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{(2k+1)^2\pi^2 + 2\alpha}.
  \]
Let $\alpha_2^*$ be the unique solution to $F_2(\alpha)L_{\sigma}^2 =1$ with the convention that $\alpha_2^* =-\frac{1}{2}\pi^2$ when $L_{\sigma}=0$. If $\alpha> \alpha_2^*$, then for any $T\geq 0$,
\begin{align}
\sup_{t\in [0,T]}\left(\mathbb{E} \int_{0}^{1} |u^2(t,x) - u^1(t,x)|^2  dx\right)^{\frac{1}{2}} \leq 2 C_2(\alpha) \left(\mathbb{E}\int_{0}^{T} \int_{0}^{1}| h^2(s,y) - h^1(s,y) |^2 dsdy\right)^{\frac{1}{2}} ,
\end{align}
where the constant $C_2(\alpha)$ is independent of $T$ and given by
\begin{align*}
  C_2(\alpha) :=  \left(1-  \sqrt{F_2(\alpha) L_{\sigma}^2} \right)^{-1} \sqrt{F_1(\alpha)} \quad \text{and} \quad
   F_1(\alpha):= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^2\pi^2 + 2\alpha}, \quad \text{for}\  \alpha>\alpha_2^*.
\end{align*}

    \item [(iii)] Assume that (H1) and (H2) hold. Suppose that
    \begin{align}
      \sup_{t\in [0,T]}\mathbb{E}\Big[\sup_{x\in [0,1]}|u^2(t,x) - u^1(t,x)|^2\Big] <\infty \quad\text{for any }\  T\geq 0.
    \end{align}
  If
  \begin{align}
    \alpha> \alpha_{\infty}^{*}:= \inf_{p>2} \left(K_p L_{\sigma}^2\right)^{\frac{2p}{p-2}} ,
\end{align}
where the constant $K_p$ is defined by \eqref{260630.1705},
then for any $T\geq 0$,
\begin{align}
\sup_{t\in [0,T]}\left(\mathbb{E} \Big[\sup_{x\in [0,1]} |u^2(t,x) - u^1(t,x)|^2 \Big]  \right)^{\frac{1}{2}} \leq 2 C_{\infty}(\alpha) \left(\mathbb{E}\int_{0}^{T} \int_{0}^{1} | h^2(s,y) - h^1(s,y) |^2 dsdy\right)^{\frac{1}{2}},
\end{align}
where the constant $C_{\infty}(\alpha)$ is independent of $T$ and given by
\[
C_{\infty}(\alpha) := \left(1 - \sqrt{F_3(\alpha) L_{\sigma}^2} \right)^{-1} \sqrt{F_2(\alpha)} \quad\text{ and } \quad F_3(\alpha):= \inf_{p>2} \frac{K_p }{\alpha^{\frac{p-2}{2p}}}  ,  \quad \text{ for } \alpha>\alpha_{\infty}^* .
\]
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.009027+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_76a7b91860a324a9c2b4496c78ff873e3b6ab412f66014bee21c42dda4537b3e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition ei4LUE of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] [log-gas]
   Suppose $a\in\mathbb{N}$. Let $X$ be an $(N+a)\times N$ matrix with i.i.d.\ complex standard Gaussian entries, then the joint distribution of the eigenvalues of $L_{a,N}:=X^*X$ has the following probability density function:
    \begin{equation}
        p_{L_{a,N},2}(x_1,x_2,...,x_N)=\frac{1}{C_{L_{a,N},2}}\prod_{1\le j< k\le N}\chi_{\{x_j\ge0\}}|x_j-x_k|^2\sum_{j=1}^N x_j^a\exp(-x_j)
    \end{equation}
    for $C_{L_{a,N},2}$ appropriate normalization constant.

    In addition, an $N$-dimensional nonnegative random vector $(x_1,x_2,..,x_N)$ with the joint probability density function as (equation J4LUE of the paper) is called a \textbf{Laguerre Unitary Ensemble (LUE)} with parameter $a$.
   For convention, we say the Wishart matrix $L_{a,N}$ belongs to the LUE. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.238785+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_76a7ded8561e662bc62e34c483f811f5200e0d82f9221ba42be0cff668ce6069","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: prob: late points of arXiv:2411.16398, 'Large Deviations of Cover Time of Tori in Dimensions $d\geq 3$'] {prevost2023phase}}]

    For all $\alpha_1>\alpha_0>0$ and $\beta_0>0$, there exists a constant $C=C(\alpha_0,\alpha_1,\beta_0)>0$ such that for all $N \in \mathbb{N}$ and $G\subset \mathbb{T}_N$ such that $\capacity(G) \leq \beta_0$,
    \begin{equation}

        \left|\frac{\mathsf{P}(G \subset \mathcal{L}^\alpha)}{N^{-d\alpha g(0)\capacity(G)}} - 1\right| \leq C\frac{(\log N)^{3/2}}{N^{(d-2)/2}} \quad 
