\item $\widetilde{B}^{2s}_{p,q}(\mathbb{R}^2) \hookrightarrow T^{s,\alpha}_{p,q}B(\mathbb{R}^2) \hookrightarrow \widetilde{B}^{(1+\alpha)s}_{p,q}(\mathbb{R}^2)$,

\end{itemize}
and these embeddings are optimal.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:14:46.370862+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_76b0024f46ed3c0e8d5ee6f48738cebb191ccc69c2708a55e90db6e23ac2cabf","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] Given variables $f,g:X\to\mathbb R$, we can talk about their covariance
\begin{eqnarray*}
cov(f,g)
&=&E[(f-E(f))(g-E(g))]\\
&=&E(fg)-E(f)E(g)
\end{eqnarray*}
with this quantity having the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $cov(f,f)=V(f)$.

\item If $f,g$ are independent, $cov(f,g)=0$.

\item The converse of this latter fact is, unfortunately, not true.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:37.357719+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_76b0da983e8d806b00714d57433e0a31b6fa758bbff4a71390cd6c7a7dec3fcd","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2606.17859, 'Full $\Gamma-$expansion for the level-two large deviation rate functionals of non-reversib'] Let $m\in C^2(\bb T)$ be the density of a probability measure
($m\ge 0$, $\int_{\bb T} m(x)\, dx =1$). Suppose that there exists
$c>0$ such that $m(x) \ge c$ for all $x\in\bb T$.  Let
$H\colon \bb T \to \bb R$ be given by
\begin{equation}

H(x) \;=\; \frac{1}{2} \, \log \big( \mss a(x)\, m (x) \big)
\;+\; \frac{1}{2\epsilon} 
\frac{\<\mss b/\mss a\>}{\<1/(\mss a\,m)\> }  \,
\int_0^x \frac{1}{\mss a(y)\, m (y)} \, dy  \,-\,
\frac{1}{2\epsilon} 
\int_0^x \frac{\mss b(y)}{\mss a(y)} \, dy \;,
\end{equation}
where, recall, $\<h\> = \int_{\bb T}h(y)\,dy$. Then, $H$ belongs to
$C^2(\bb T)$ and $m$ is the unique stationary state for the diffusion
induced by the generator $\ms L_{H,\epsilon}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:15.496064+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_76b1b879706c67d3fe5cadd8b6f886fa99dba5b75dad82cc92b89ed94190e9db","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Problem 39 (\cite{BW}) of arXiv:2512.02538, 'On the spectral geometry of Liouville quantum gravity'] Fix $\gamma \in (0,2)$, and suppose that the LQG eigenfunctions $\ef_n$ are normalised to have unit $L^2 ( \mu_\ph)$-norm. Show that, as $n \to \infty$,
