{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:57:51.748138+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_77dab096144150d739f7c8e0a65361418f54f04bd9eb428798fd113bfce751df","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 14 of arXiv:2608.30138, 'High-frequency wave propagation for the viscoelastic wave equation with singular memory'] Let $k\ge 1$. Define
\[
\|u\|_{Y_k(T)} := \sum_{j=0}^{k+1}
\|\partial_t^j u\|_{C([0,T];\,H^{k+1-j}(\Omega))}.
\]
Then, under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm_well_posed}, for $0\le \varepsilon\le\varepsilon_0$, the solution satisfies
\[
\begin{aligned}
\|u\|_{Y_k(T)}
&\le
C_{k,T}
\bigg(
\|u_0\|_{H^{k+1}(\Omega)}
+
\|v_0\|_{H^k(\Omega)}
\\
&\qquad
+
\sum_{\ell=0}^{k}
\|\partial_t^\ell u_-\|_{C_{\mathrm b}((-\infty,0];\,H^{k+1-\ell}(\Omega))}
+
\sum_{\ell=0}^{k}
\|\partial_t^\ell f\|_{C([0,T];\,H^{k-\ell}(\Omega))}
\bigg).
\end{aligned}
\]
Here $C_{k,T}$ depends on $k,T,\varepsilon_0$, the elliptic constants, and the kernel bounds, but not on the particular data or on
$\varepsilon\in[0,\varepsilon_0]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:29:35.085967+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_77e0a85fe574eabc137c6cc62cf0cf5cebf047bb9814763bf6b1fca0c69780b8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2609.03819, 'Stochastic damped wave and Euler-Bernoulli equations with singular locally H\"older contin'] Assume \ref{H0}, \ref{H1} and \ref{H3} hold. Then, there exist positive constants $C_1,C_2$, independent of $t\in(0,\infty)$ and $n\in\N$, such that
\begin{align}

\left\|e^{tA_n}\mathcal V_n\right\|_{\mathscr L(U,H_n)}\leq C_1t^{-\gamma}e^{-C_2\mu_1^{\alpha\wedge (1-\alpha)}t}, \qquad t\in(0,\infty), \qquad n\in\N.
\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}
\gamma:=\begin{cases}
\dfrac{\beta}{\alpha}, & \alpha\in\left(0,\frac12\right],\\[2mm]
\max\left\{\dfrac{\beta}{\alpha};\ \dfrac{\beta+\frac12-\alpha}{1-\alpha}\right\}, 
& \alpha\in\left[\frac12,1\right),
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
We notice that, for $\alpha\in[\frac12,1)$, $\gamma=(\beta+\frac12-\alpha)(1-\alpha)^{-1}$ if and only if $\beta\geq\frac14$ and $\alpha\leq 2\beta$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:05:57.793228+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_77e0d27142b14f38dd344c70b4622ce8d20db186380352e4b68857c5057d69e5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 56 of arXiv:2608.30549, '\vspace{-1cm}Maximally Spread Out Measures and Implications\\{} for Phase Transitions in A'] Let $\eps_f>0$, let $f\colon (0,\eps_f)\to \NN_{\ge2}$, and let $s>0$.
