{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.611325+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78d63a87d2f680081ab99a405e093faae014de5c26eb85609391b6f51420014b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:construction of arXiv:2412.09087, 'General Markovian randomized equilibrium existence and construction in zero-sum Dynkin gam'] \enskip

\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item The equilibrium value of our game (Definition (def:equilibrium-defs of the paper)) exists and coincides with the equilibrium value $V$ of the associated game (Proposition (thm:assoc-game of the paper)). In particular, Proposition (asso-game:characterization of the paper) provides a characterization of the equilibrium value and Figure (fig-assoc of the paper) provides an illustrative example. 

\item  A global Markovian randomized $\epsilon$-Nash equilibrium exists for each $\epsilon>0$, and the equilibrium value $V$ can be attained using Markovian randomized stopping times. 
In particular, for each fixed $\epsilon>0$, the pair $(\tau_{\Psi^{(1)}},\tau_{\Psi^{(2)}})$ is a global Markovian randomized $\epsilon$-Nash equilibrium when
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
\Psi^{(1)}_t &:= A_t^{(1)}+\infty \mathbb{I}\{\tau^{D_1^{*} \backslash ({B^{h<g<f}} \cup {B^{h<f\leq g}})} \leq t \},\\
\Psi^{(2)}_t &:= A_t^{(2)}+\infty \mathbb{I}\{\tau^{D_2^{*} \backslash ({B^{g<f<h}} \cup {B^{f\leq g<h}})} \leq t \}, 
\end{split}
\end{align}
with 
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
A_t^{(1)}:&= \int_0^t \gamma^{(1)}_\epsilon(X_s)  \mathbb{I}{\{X_s \in  D_1^{*} \cap (B^{h<g<f} \cup {B^{h<f\leq g}})  \}}ds + \sum_{y \in \mbox{iso}(D_1^{*} \cap (B^{h<g<f} \cup {B^{h<f\leq g}}) )} \Gamma_\epsilon^{1}(y) l_t^y,\\
A_t^{(2)}:&= \int_0^t \gamma^{(2)}_\epsilon(X_s)  \mathbb{I}{\{X_s \in  D_2^{*} \cap 
({B^{g<f<h}} \cup {B^{f\leq g<h}}) \}}ds + \sum_{y \in \mbox{iso}(D_2^{*} \cap ({B^{g<f<h}} \cup {B^{f\leq g<h}}))} \Gamma_\epsilon^{2}(y) l_t^y,
\end{split}
\end{align}
where $\gamma^{(i)}_\epsilon(\cdot)$ and $\Gamma^{(i)}_\epsilon(\cdot)$ are real-valued functions $i=1,2$ (specified in Constructions (construction1 of the paper)--(construction2 of the paper), Appendix (appendix-construction of the paper)).
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.529487+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78dc016f8a25e8f8570958c171222e54eeebee8dd5987b2a1241315843d4b11a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition eq:sharp-tail-cost of arXiv:2607.27613, 'Regularized Bulk Universality versus Bounded-Disorder Nonuniversality\\for Annealed Comple'] \label[proposition]{prop:sharp-one-spike}
If \(\xi\) is sharp sub-Gaussian, then for every \(a>0\) and \(\eta>0\),
