		\]
		Here, $\mathsf{d}(x,y)$ denotes the chemical distance on $\mathscr{C}_\infty$, i.e., minimum length of the open paths connecting $x$ and $y$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:19:06.722509+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_790498f4f1ca84b4653b3cfed2b30efd4eb791351c0c0e028c6e2ea4e9809a02","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm5.1' of arXiv:2606.01944, 'Propagation of Chaos for Mean-field Mean Reflected Backward Stochastic Differential Equati'] The reflected BSDE (equation eq7' of the paper) has a unique solution $(\{Y^i,Z^i\}_{1\leq i\leq N},K^{(N)})$ with $K^{(N)}\in \mathcal{A}^2(\mathbb{F}^{(N)})$, $Y^i\in \mathcal{S}^2(\mathbb{F}^{(N)})$ and $Z^i\in \mathcal{H}^2(\mathbb{F}^{(N)};\mathbb{R}^N)$ for $i=1,\cdots,N$. Moreover, there exists a constant $C$ independent of $N$, such that
