{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.528956+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_797c9e91b016512fcbcdf93eba75da405b0409f56c56c03a9a67f275a6ce7f9c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2604.01852, 'The on/off Brownian snake'] Let $f:[0,\sigma] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^d$ be a continuous function and let $L:[0,\sigma] \rightarrow [0,\infty)$ be a continuous increasing function with $L(0)=0$. Consider the Lebesgue-Stieltjes measure $dL$ defined from $L$. Let $Y$ be the measure defined via Borel-measurable functions $\Phi:\mathbb{R}^d \rightarrow [0,\infty)$ as 
    \begin{equation*}
        \langle Y, \Phi \rangle \coloneqq \int_{b \in \mathbb{R}^d} \Phi(b)Y(db) := \int_{r=0}^\sigma \Phi(f(r)) dL(r).
