{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.024672+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a3c96a61b56e9d0eaf0c8f8e8da65e7496f1ca7f981a87fd02a854a3a476c0c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 (\citeauthor{F10}, \citeyear{F10}, Lemma 5.9) of arXiv:2606.06739, 'The relationship between the transition functions of the labeled and unlabeled versions of'] For $\bm m=(m_1,\ldots,m_k)$ with $m_1\ge\cdots\ge m_k\ge1$, $\bm n=(n_1,\ldots,n_l)$ with $n_1\ge\cdots\ge n_l\ge1$, $k,l\in{\bf N}$, $m=|\bm m|$, and $n=|\bm n|$,
\begin{align}
&\textbf{E}[P_{\bm m}(\rho(\varepsilon V_1^{\bm n},\ldots,\varepsilon V_l^{\bm n},(1-\varepsilon)\rho_1,(1-\varepsilon)\rho_2,\ldots))]\nonumber\\
&\quad{}=C(\bm m)\sum_{r=0}^{k\wedge l}\bigg[\sum_{\substack{I\subset\{1,\ldots,k\}:\\ |I|=r}}\;\sum_{\substack{\gamma:I\mapsto\{1,\ldots,l\}\\ \text{\rm one-to-one}}}\nonumber\\
&\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad\dfrac{\prod_{i\in I}(m_i+n_{\gamma(i)}-1)!\,\prod_{i\in I^c}(m_i-1)!}{\prod_{i\in I}(n_{\gamma(i)}-1)!}\bigg]\,\frac{\theta^{k-r}}{(n+\theta)_{(m)}}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:04:06.982573+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a48138356a3b8076d944c99cee8bf8bf543241ecacd5a8a7eb27e18c208112a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:lbstrthm1 of arXiv:2607.26551, 'An RDT based confirmation of Lehner's formula for Kronecker-Gaussian matrices'] For large $n\in{\mathbb N}$ and fixed $k,l\in{\mathbb N}$, let $G_{i}\in{\mathbb R}^{n\times n}$, $G_{i}^{(1,1)},G_{i}^{(1,2)},G_{i}^{(1)}\in{\mathbb R}^{k\times n},i=1,\dots,l$ be independent  standard normal matrices. Let $R$, $A_i$,  and $F_i$ be as in  Theorem (thm:thm1 of the paper). Also, let $U_iD_iU_i^T=F_i$ be the eigen-decomposition of $F_i$ ($U_i$ is unitary and $D$ is diagonal). For  $t\in(0,1)$ and $Q\in{\mathcal Q}$, let $D^{(2)}(t)$   be as in ((eq:lbstr3a1 of the paper)) and  ((eq:lbstr3 of the paper)). Set $G_i^{(1,3)} = Q G_i^{(1,1)} + \sqrt{I-Q^TQ}G_i^{(1,2)}$, 
 \begin{eqnarray}

 \bar{\zeta}_i^{(1,1)}(X^{(1)}) & = & \mbox{Tr}(G_i^{(1,1)}  X^{(1)} F_i) 
 \nonumber \\
 \bar{\zeta}_i^{(1,3)}(X^{(2)}) & = & \mbox{Tr}(G_i^{(1,3)}  X^{(2)} F_i) , 
 \end{eqnarray} 
and
\begin{equation}

{\mathcal G}_{D_u} (X^{(1)},X^{(2)}) =    
\sqrt{2} \left (
\sqrt{t} \sum_{i=1}^{l}    \bar{\zeta}_i^{(1,1)}(X^{(1)})
+\sqrt{t} \sum_{i=1}^{l}    \bar{\zeta}_i^{(1,3)}(X^{(2)})
+\sqrt{1-t} \sum_{j=1}^{2}  \sum_{i=1}^{l}   \zeta_i^{(2)}(X^{(j)})
\right ) .
\end{equation} 
Let
\begin{equation}

L^{(2)}(t)=  \max_{(X^{(1)},X^{(2)})\in\bar{{\mathcal X}}}  
 {\mathcal G}_{D_u} (X^{(1)},X^{(2)}) .
\end{equation} 
 One then has  
\begin{eqnarray}

\lim_{n\rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}} {\mathbb E} D^{(2)}(t) 
\leq 
\lim_{n\rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}} {\mathbb E} L^{(2)}(t) .
\end{eqnarray}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:34:20.778699+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a4c07be5c42dd61342adff35d543752ff50cfce730e81b17504947c92bd327e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 4 (Uniform one-sided endpoint and maximum estimates) of arXiv:2609.02257, 'Aperiodic random walks: uniform estimates, local limits and invariance principles'] There are constants \(C,c>0\) such that, for all sufficiently large
\(N\) and all \(L,H,R\in\Z_{\ge0}\),
\begin{align}
 q_{N,L}(0,H)
 &\le C\frac{H+1}{(L+1)^{3/2}}
 \exp\left\{-c\frac{H^2}{L+H+1}\right\},         \\
 q_{N,L}\bigl(0,H;\max_{j\le L}S_N(j)-H\ge R\bigr)
 &\le C\frac{H+1}{(L+1)^{3/2}}
 \exp\left\{
   -c\frac{H^2}{L+H+1}
   -c\frac{R^2}{L+R+1}
 \right\}.                   
\end{align}
Consequently,
\begin{align}
 \sum_{H\ge0}
 q_{N,L}\bigl(0,H;\max_{j\le L}S_N(j)\ge R\bigr)
 &\le \frac{C}{\sqrt{L+1}}
 \exp\left\{-c\frac{R^2}{L+R+1}\right\},
 \\
 q_{N,L}\bigl(0,0;\max_{j\le L}S_N(j)\ge R\bigr)
 &\le \frac{C}{(L+1)^{3/2}}
 \exp\left\{-c\frac{R^2}{L+R+1}\right\}.

\end{align}
The same statements hold for the dual array. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:32:19.900130+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a4c42db052c867fdf21052c74d503c55b0195be797983a45a34225ca64be35c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2608.02319, 'Averaging Principle for Ordinary Differential Equations in a Multiscale Random Environment'] Let $z\in\mathbb R^d$, then for all $i\in I$, $x_i\in E_i$ and $T>0$, if assumptions \eqref{hyp:Lip} and \eqref{hyp:ErgoF} are satisfied and $F_i(z,\,\cdot\,)\in\mathcal A_i$, we have
\begin{align*}
\mathbf E_{x_i}\big[\varphi(\mathbf Z_T^{n,z})\,\mathbf 1_{\{T\leq\tau_1^n\}}\big]\underset{n\to\infty}{\longrightarrow}\mathbf E_{x_i}\big[\varphi(\bar{\mathbf Z}_T^z)\,\mathbf 1_{\{T\leq\tau_1\}}\big],\quad\forall\varphi\in C_b^1(Z).
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.294457+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a51bd5eea9de76e1fceb9360bd347666061dd42cbf2959e4b8f71d7990810ae","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Tm item:characterization of arXiv:2411.05441, 'Sticky diffusions on star graphs : characterization and It\^o formula'] Let $X(t) = (i(t), x(t))$ be a sticky diffusion on a star graph $\Gamma$ with stickiness parameter $\eta > 0$. Then,
	\begin{enumerate}[label={\rm(\roman*)}]
	 \item  there exists a nonsticky diffusion $Y$ on $\Gamma$ and a strictly increasing continuous function $V^{-1}$ such that $X$ and $Y(V^{-1}(\cdot))$ are indistinguishable. Moreover, there exists an increasing process $\ell_X$, adapted to the completion of the natural filtration of $X$ with respect to the collection of probability measures $\left ( \mathbf P_\mathsf{x} \right)_{\mathsf{x} \in \Gamma}$, such that $\ell_X$ almost surely increases only when $X = \mathsf{v}$ and $V^{-1}(t) = t - \eta \ell_X(t)$;

	 \item  up to an extension of the filtered probability space, there exists a one-dimensional Brownian motion $W$ such that, almost surely, we have
	 \begin{equation} 
		\begin{cases}
		dx(t) = \sigma_{i(t)}(x(t)) \mathbf{1}_{\{x(t) \ne 0\}}\, d W(t) + b_{i(t)}(x(t)) \mathbf{1}_{\{x(t) \ne 0\}}\, dt + d\ell_X(t), \\
		\eta \ell_X(t) = \int_0^t \mathbf 1_{\{X(s) = \mathsf{v} \}} ds;
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation}

	\item  for every $f \in C_b^{1,2}(\mathbb{R}_+ \times \Gamma)$, almost surely, we have the It\^o formula
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{split}
	& f(t,X(t)) = f(0,X(0)) + \int_0^t \left ( \partial_s f(s,X(s)) +  Lf(s,X(s)) \right ) \mathbf 1_{\{ X(s) \neq \mathsf{v} \}}  ds \\
	& \quad + \int_0^t \sigma(X(s)) \partial_x f(s,X(s)) \mathbf{1}_{\{X(s) \neq \mathsf{v} \}} d  W(s)
	 + \int_0^t \left ( \eta\, \partial_s f(s,\mathsf{v}) +  \sum_{i=1}^N \rho_i \partial_x f_i(s,0) \right ) d \ell_X(s).
	\end{split}
	\end{equation}
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.645710+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a52fd427da5d4ef8d649326be357951e6e3c21484bd7b1fea84de1b5f6ed520","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:ballvol of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] Assume that ~ $0 \leq \mu \ll 1$ and $T_0$ a finite tree of height $r$ with $K$ vertices at level $r$. For any $M \in \mathbb{N}$, it holds that
\begin{equation} 
\nu_n ^\mu \big(\mathcal{B}_{1/r} (T_0) \big) \geq\big(1 + o(1)\big)K \frac{e^{-\mu r}}{4^{|T_0| -K}} \left( \sum\limits_{S=1} ^M  C_{S-1} 4^{-S} \right)^{K-1} \, ,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{B}_{1/r}(T_0)$ is the open ball of radius $1/r$ around the tree $T_0$ in the local topology.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:33:12.352993+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a57afcea96c197da349cd90d9f01a0de6da1f3e4efa3f4d8cd575599f32e59b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Dfn 199 (truncation) of arXiv:2608.29416, 'Algorithmic threshold for high-dimensional projection pursuit I: general theory'] For the spherical perceptron, let $\vX\equiv\vX^{\RomI:\RomIII}$ be the three-dimensional process defined by \eqref{e:X.decomp} and \eqref{e:vX} above. Given a (large constant) parameter $\trK$, we define a \textbf{truncated} process $\vX^\trstar$ by initializing
$\vX^\trstar(q_0,a)=\vX(q_0,a) = (0,0,0)\in\R^3$, and setting
    \[
    \vX^\trstar(q_{d+1},a)
    =\begin{cases}
    \vX(q_{d+1},a) &\textup{if $\vX^\trstar(q_d,a)\in[-\trK,\trK)^3$,}\\
    \vX^\trstar(q_d,a) &\textup{otherwise,}
    \end{cases}
    \]
so that the process $\vX^\trstar$ stays frozen upon exiting $[-\trK,\trK)^3$. For the Ising perceptron, we apply the above procedure to obtain $\vX^{\trstar,\RomI:\RomIII}$. We separately define $X^{\trstar,\Ising}$ to be the process $X^\Ising$ from \eqref{e:Delta.X.Ising}, frozen upon exiting $[-\trK,\trK)$. We then denote $\vX^\trstar\equiv(\vX^{\trstar,\RomI:\RomIII},X^{\trstar,\Ising})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.449866+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a58924c8e2153ff91a9a6b7e20dca626e6a2ccf5c9877b7a2bf1011077e5ecc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] Let $n,l\in\N, m\in\N_0$, $p_1,...,p_d:\R^l\to\R$ be real-valued (multivariate) trigonometric polynomials, and $x_1,...,x_n\in\R^d$. We say that $x_1,...,x_n$ form an \textit{averaging set} of $(p_1,...,p_d)$ of order $m$ and size $n$ if they form an averaging set of
    \begin{equation}
        (p_1,...,p_d)(\lambda_l|_{[0,2\pi)^l}) = \int_{[0,2\pi)^l} \I\{(p_1(x),...,p_d(x))\in\cdot\}\, dx
    \end{equation}
    of order $m$ and size $n$, i.e.,
    \begin{equation}
        \frac{1}{(2\pi)^l}\int_{[0,2\pi)^l} (p_1(x),...,p_d(x))^{\otimes q}\, dx = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{k=1}^n x_k^{\otimes q},\quad q\in\{0,...,m\}.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:45.374295+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a5c42cea1bdf89a2cea26b16071e035daaf3f462856ea2d415b69abef1b19e7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition thm:schervish are str proper for estimates of arXiv:2412.06420, 'Accuracy, Estimates, and Representation Results'] {schervish2014dominating}}]
	Suppose $\mathsf{acc}$ has the form:
	\begin{equation}
		\mathsf{acc}_k(x)=\mathsf{acc}_k(k)-\int_x^k(k-t)\;\;\lambda(\dd{t})
	\end{equation}
	where $\lambda$ is finite on every bounded interval and mutually absolutely continuous with Lebesgue integral; then $\mathsf{acc}$ is proper. 

(We will here prove this for finitely supported probabilities, i.e., for any finitely supported probability $p$, $\mathrm{Exp}_p[\mathsf{acc}_V(x)]$ is maximised at $x=\mathrm{Exp}_p[V]$.)

	If $\lambda$ gives positive measure to every non-generate interval, then $\mathsf{acc}$ is \emph{strictly} proper, i.e., this maximum is unique.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:15:08.951726+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a5f9760a1b5dbb910d1a2fea574d3e3f3b202de776cf5986ee0c4e759554185","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Definition_block_height of arXiv:2411.10829, 'Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble and its Laplace transform'] For a block process $\vec \mathbf p$ and compatible virtual block process $\vec\boldsymbol{\upsilon}$, a compatible \emph{block height} is a function $\mathbf H:\mathbf \Delta\cup\{\mathbf Q_\ell\mid \ell\in  \llbracket 0, m\rrbracket\}\cup\{\mathbf Q_{\ell-1}+\boldsymbol{\upsilon}_\ell \mid \ell\in  \llbracket 1, m\rrbracket,\, \boldsymbol{\upsilon}_\ell\ne \varnothing\} \to \mathbb R_{\ge 0}$, such that
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathbf H(\mathbf Q_\ell)=\mathbf p^0(\mathbf Q_\ell-)$ for each $\ell \in \llbracket 1, m\rrbracket$ and $\mathbf H(0)=0$;
    \item $\mathbf H(\mathbf Q_{\ell-1}+\boldsymbol{\upsilon}_\ell)=\mathbf p^0(\mathbf Q_{\ell-1}-)$ for each $\ell$ with $\boldsymbol{\upsilon}_\ell\neq\varnothing$;
    \item for all $x\in \mathbf \Delta$, we have $\mathbf H(x)\ge  \max\bigl(\mathbf p^0(x),\mathbf p^0(x-)\bigr)$;
    \item if $j\in \llbracket 1, m+\mathbf u\rrbracket$ and $x\in \mathbf \Delta$ is a discontinuity point for $\mathbf p_j$, then the jump $\mathbf p_j(x)-\mathbf p_j(x-)$ and the difference $\mathbf H(x)-\mathbf p^0(x-)-\mathbf p_j(x)$ have the same sign;
    \item if $\boldsymbol{\upsilon}_\ell=\varnothing$ for $\ell\in \llbracket 1, m\rrbracket$, then  $\mathbf H\bigl(\min\bigl(\bigcup_{j=1}^{m+\mathbf u}\mathbf \Delta_{j,\ell}\cup \{\mathbf Q_\ell\}\bigr)\bigr)\ge \mathbf H(\mathbf Q_{\ell-1})$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:50:30.569652+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a6088c76c8b0d00d2108ebb917c24d0d593d0b3e3112bfda03a54e5cf264f49","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 19 (Spherical second and fourth moments in a subspace) of arXiv:2605.30779, '2605.30779'] Let \(\mathbb F\in\{\mathbb R,\mathbb C\}\), and put
  \[
    \beta_{\mathbb F}:=\dim_{\mathbb R}\mathbb F
    =
    \begin{cases}
      1, & \mathbb F=\mathbb R,\\
      2, & \mathbb F=\mathbb C.
    \end{cases}
  \]
  Equip \(\mathbb F^N\) with the standard Euclidean/Hermitian inner product. Let \(V\subset \mathbb F^N\) be an \(m\)-dimensional \(\mathbb F\)-linear subspace, and let \(P\) be the matrix representing the orthogonal projection onto \(V\). If \(v\) is a random unit vector in \(V\) uniformly distributed on the unit sphere of \(V\), then, with $v^\ast$ understood as $v^{\top}$ if $\mathbb F = \realR$,
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb E[vv^\ast]=\frac1m P.
  \end{equation}
  Moreover, for any \(N\times N\) matrices \(A\) and \(B\) that are real symmetric if \(\mathbb F = \realR\) and are Hermitian if \(\mathbb F = \compC\),
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb E\bigl[(v^\ast A v)(v^\ast B v)\bigr]
    =
    \frac{
      \tr(PAP)\tr(PBP)+\frac{2}{\beta_{\mathbb F}}\tr(PAPBP)
    }{
      m\bigl(m+\frac{2}{\beta_{\mathbb F}}\bigr)
    }.
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.804791+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a62a54ab46c0836584209ae73a933c48123bf1751b0853e3b7c20ff4614ae13","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemm l:crossterms of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] Fix a Borel set $B\in\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}^2)$ and $n\in{\mathbb{Z}_{\geqslant0}}$.
    For all $f,g\in L^{2}(\mathbb{R}^{2n})$ with $f,g\geqslant 0$ almost everywhere,
