\mu_{\mathcal{T},\nu}(A) = \sum_{t \in \mathcal{T}} \nu_t(A_{t}), \mbox{ where } A_{t} = \{ y \in \mathbb{R}: (t,y) \in A\}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.122042+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7aacf2dc62a94b28398cfb0c6e565f95d15bfdcdad754072642286b398a7dbe9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:radial-qf of arXiv:2608.18293, 'Critical tensor covariance at the Marchenko--Pastur threshold'] Under (equation eq:base-assumptions of the paper) and (equation eq:critical-scaling of the paper), there exists a deterministic sequence $\varepsilon_n\to0$ such that for every deterministic complex $p\times p$ matrix $A$,
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}\left|
  \frac{x^{\mathsf T}Ax}{p}
  -R_n\frac{\operatorname{Tr} A}{p}
  \right|^2
  \le \varepsilon_n\|A\|_{\mathrm{op}}^2.

\end{equation}
For all sufficiently large $n$, one may take
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{\quad
  \varepsilon_n=\bigl(\sqrt{\delta_n}+\sqrt{\kappa_n}\bigr)^2,
  \quad}

\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
  \delta_n=\frac{vd}{n-d}e^{vd^2/(n-d)},
  \qquad
  \kappa_n=
  \frac{8d(n-d)}{n(n-1)}
  \exp\!\left(\frac{2vd^2}{n-d}\right).

\end{equation}
Both terms tend to zero.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:04:39.788465+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7ab31dea35268499d44ff42fcb05af18f94b9d01325c06f3a7dfb61c06848d97","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:tailrl-binary-recovery of arXiv:2609.02987, 'Tail Likelihood Reinforcement Learning'] Suppose $r_i\in\{0,1\}$ and let
$M:=\sum_{i=1}^N\mathbbm{1}\{r_i=1\}$. If $M>0$, then every successful
rollout receives weight $1/M$ and every unsuccessful rollout receives weight
zero. Hence the uncentered TailRL{} estimator reduces to
\begin{equation}
g_{\mathrm{TailRL}}^{(N)}(x)
=
\frac{1}{M}
\sum_{i:r_i=1}S_i,

\end{equation}
which is the MaxRL estimator. After mean-centering, successful rollouts have
weight $1/M-1/N$ and unsuccessful rollouts have weight $-1/N$. If $M=0$,
all uncentered and centered weights are zero.","status":"unverified"}

