{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.960168+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7b144391a4e38920fac61e48ac85e0c21f25943f9afdd04bbc5bf19b6038cca1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 15 (Stable Central Limit Theorem) of arXiv:2608.12070, 'Central limit theorems for a heavy-tailed subcritical Galton-Watson process with state-dep'] (\cite[XVII.5, Theorem 2]{feller1971})
If $\{Z_n\}_{n\geq 1}$ is a sequence of independent random variables with zero mean and regularly varying tails of the form \eqref{add} with index $\alpha$ ($1<\alpha<2$), and if there exist $ p,q\in [0,1]$ such that
\begin{align} 
\frac{\P(Z>x)}{\P(|Z|>x)}\to  p,\quad \frac{\P(Z<-x)}{\P(|Z|>x)}\to  q, \quad\quad x\rightarrow \infty,
\end{align}
then
\begin{align*}
\frac{S_n}{a_n} \stackrel{d}{\rightarrow} S_\alpha(\rho,0),
\end{align*}
where $\{a_n\}$ is a sequence satisfying $n a_n^{-\alpha}L(a_n)\to 1$, $\rho=p-q$, and $S_\alpha(\rho,0)$ is a random variable following an $\alpha$-stable distribution with the following characteristic function:
$$\exp\left(-|t|^\alpha \Gamma(1-\alpha)\cos\frac{\pi\alpha}{2} (1+i\rho\mathrm{sgn}(t)\tan\frac{\pi\alpha}{2})\right).  $$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:52.743970+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7b16dc3f1de20277fa06ec6d33a797a8846a1a5212ee178a97fa0fc55a0e4a01","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 of arXiv:2506.10511, '2506.10511'] For each $i\in [1,K]_{\mathds{Z}}$,
