{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.286768+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7b373f6fa971aabe24bb1321efdb008f619628ae018bff88549fe0a295377241","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 15 of arXiv:2606.22964, 'Signature McKean--Vlasov stochastic differential equations'] Let $\left(\mathcal{X},\rho\right)$ be a metric space. A map $f:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{X}$ is said to be a contraction mapping if there exists a real constant $k\in[0,1)$ such that
