{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.170017+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7c1c509811d62dd2f97ced110abf49365bb2cb2af96ad0e104a1e5d4ff899015","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def_permuton_from_gt of arXiv:2412.07258, 'Permuton limit of a generalization of the Mallows and $k$-card-minimum models'] Given $g$ as in Definition (def_gen_perm_model of the paper), let us  define the function $V_{g}: [0,1)\times [0,1) \to \mathbb{R}$ as follows. For each $y \in [0,1)$, let $V_{g}(\cdot,y)$ denote the unique solution of the initial value problem
\begin{align}
V_{g}(0,y) = y, \qquad \partial_x V_{g}(x,y) = \frac{g(V_{g}(x,y))}{g(x)}.
\end{align}
 Noting that (equation Vdef of the paper) is a separable ODE, for any $(x,y)\in[0,1)^2$ we obtain
    \begin{align}
        V_{g}(x,y) = u^{-1}(u(x) + u(y)), \quad
\text{where} \quad u(x) = \int_0^x \frac{1}{g(z)}\, \mathrm{d}z.
\end{align}
Our assumptions on $g$ (cf.\ Definition (def_gen_perm_model of the paper)) imply $\lim_{x \to 1_-} u(x)=+\infty$, thus $V_{g}$ continuously extends to $[0,1]^2$ if we define $V_{g}(1,y)=V_{g}(x,1)=1$. Defining
\begin{equation}
F_{g}(x,y):=x+y-V_{g}(x,y)\end{equation}
we obtain
$F_{g}(x,0) = 0$, $ F_{g}(0,y) = 0 $, $F_{g}(x,1) = x$,  $ F_{g}(1,y) = y $ and
\begin{equation}
f_{g}(x,y):=  \partial_x \partial_y F_{g}(x,y)= \frac{-g'\left(V_{g}(x,y) \right) g\left( V_{g}(x,y) \right) }{g(x)g(y)} \geq 0, \qquad  x,y \in [0,1),
\end{equation}
thus one sees that $F_{g}$ is the c.d.f.\ (and therefore $V_{g}$ is the $\cup$-c.d.f.) of a permuton $\mu_{g}$.","status":"unverified"}

