{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:22:08.475716+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7c2865054bee8cbb4bdbb9ec22c736ad5591dd05a7e0f6fdaa2cf20a9b2fd55c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2607.29020, 'Entropic Symmetrization Resistance'] \label[lemma]{lem:f_- and u are symmetrizers}
Let $G$ be a compact group with Haar measure $\mu$. 
Writing $f_-(\cdot) = f(\cdot^{-1}) \in \mathcal{P}(G)$, and $u = \frac{1}{\mu(G)} \in \mathcal{P}(G)$, we have $\{f_-,u\} \subseteq \Sym(f)$. 

\begin{proof}
We observe that 
\begin{align*}
\Sym = \Sym_L 
&= \{ g \in \mathcal{P}(G) \mid \text{for all $z \in G$, } (g * f)(z) = (g * f)(z^{-1}) \} \\
&= \{ g \in \mathcal{P}(G) \mid \text{for all $z \in G$, } \int_{G} g(w) (f(w^{-1}z) - f(w^{-1}z^{-1}) ) \,d\mu(w) = 0 \} ,
\end{align*}
thus clearly 
$u \in \Sym$. For $f_-$, compute
\begin{align*}
(f_- * f)(z^{-1}) 
&= \int_G f_-(w) f(w^{-1}z^{-1}) \,d\mu(w) \\
&= \int_G f_-(zw)f(w^{-1}) \,d\mu(w) \\
&= \int_G f_-(u)f(u^{-1}z) \,d\mu(u) 
= (f_- * f)(z). \qedhere
\end{align*}
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.671907+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7c2d10debd90adddb1812600e05ef562e21f8e72e17ecbe479b5940dee2765d5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:symmetric:positive of arXiv:2605.28106, 'Gaussian Processes with Sample Paths in Reproducing Kernel Banach Spaces'] Let \( R : X^* \to X \) be an operator.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item \( R \) is \emph{symmetric} if \( \langle Rf, g \rangle = \langle Rg, f \rangle \) for all \( f,g \in X^* \) .
        \item \( R \) is \emph{positive} if \( \langle Rf, f \rangle \geq 0 \) for all \( f \in X^* \).
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.894646+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7c2fc40954291b90c674887d194f886c30fb00e05fdb08dd259e76d1fba67601","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn of arXiv:2411.11408, 'Multidimensional specific relative entropy between continuous martingales'] We write $$F_l(X):=\frac{1}{2}(tr(X)-l-\log(\det X)),$$ for every symmetric positive definite matrix $X\in \mathbb{R}^{l \times l}$. Thus 
\begin{align*}
  H(\mathcal{N}_l(\mu, \Sigma_1)|\mathcal{N}_l(\mu, \Sigma_2))
  & = \textstyle  \frac{1}{2}\Big(tr(\Sigma_2^{-1}\Sigma_1)-l-\log( \frac{\det \Sigma_1}{\det \Sigma_2})\Big) \\
  &=\textstyle  \frac{1}{2}\Big(tr(\Sigma_1^{1/2}\Sigma_2^{-1}\Sigma_1^{1/2})-l-\log( \det (\Sigma_1^{1/2}\Sigma_2^{-1}\Sigma_1^{1/2}))\Big) \\
  &=F_l(\Sigma_1^{1/2}\Sigma_2^{-1}\Sigma_1^{1/2})\\
  &=F_l(\Sigma_2^{-1/2}\Sigma_1\Sigma_2^{-1/2}) .
    \end{align*}
    With some abuse of notation we still write $F(\Sigma_2^{-1}\Sigma_1)$ for the above number, even if $\Sigma_2^{-1}\Sigma_1$ need not be symmetric.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.334139+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7c3f6d14478c136c13f9c80af0508e6615119371b9474aaa6f15953a930ea0c0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2511.06196, 'Central limit theorem for high temperature spin models via martingale embedding'] Let \(A \subset \{1,\ldots,n\}\) and choose arbitrary \(\epsilon > 0\).
