{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.607854+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7e3e735741fcc37591354c426b710d1f1987efe88004c7caaf44f2dc7a82e820","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 11 of arXiv:2607.08242, 'Convergence of random sums in non-commutative probability'] Let $\mathcal{S}_{N_n}$ be as in Theorem \ref{thm31}, except that its summands $\{a_i\}$ are independent and identically distributed variables on an NCP $(\mathcal{A},\varphi)$. Let $z$ be a standard Gaussian variable in $\tilde{\mathcal{A}}$, which is independent of $\tilde{N}_n$ (as defined above).
