{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.272223+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7f892d6d8a13ce5479ab97fac2d57595338d32315017243817dcfdc5afecc17b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:OCD of arXiv:2601.09976, 'Stochastic Calculus as Operator Factorization:\\ An Operator--Covariant Derivative and Uni'] Let $X$ be a square-integrable process with energy space~$\mathcal{H}_X$ and stochastic integral $\delta_X \colon \mathcal{H}_X \to L^2(\Omega)$. For $F \in L^2(\Omega)$, the \emph{operator--covariant derivative} $D_X F \in \mathcal{H}_X$ is the unique element satisfying
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[F \cdot \delta_X(u)] \;=\; \langle D_X F,\, u \rangle_{\mathcal{H}_X}
\quad \text{for all } u \in \mathcal{H}_X.
\end{equation}
The operator $D_X \colon L^2(\Omega) \to \mathcal{H}_X$ is called the \emph{operator--covariant derivative}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.561155+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7f8a7032ae202c09f0524037d80d5ca6dbbbfd4f1cdc5f71f9c3ac7fafed6c89","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem analyticity_general of arXiv:2606.29622, 'Affine Structure of the Brownian Signature'] Let $\bm p \in \mathcal B\,$, and $\bm \xi$ be as in (equation eq:def_xi of the paper). Then, defining $\bm u_t := \mathbb{E}[\bm \xi_t]$ for all $t \in [0\,,T]\,$, we have:
