{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:23:58.621957+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8238aa97a699e65fa2d49ae9b2bd62c6838dde1ec4f830aef694154df72e1b24","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 184 (The Cartesian gradient of $\upxi$ and $|Z \SigmatTan|_g$) of arXiv:2609.03101, 'The emergence of the Cauchy horizon from the crease for $3D$ compressible Euler flow'] \item 
	Given a type $\binom{m}{n}$ spacetime tensorfield $\upxi$, 
	we define its \emph{Cartesian gradient} 
	$\pmb{\partial} \upxi$ to be the type $\binom{m}{n+1}$ spacetime tensorfield with Cartesian components:
	\begin{align} 
		(\pmb{\partial} \upxi)_{\beta_1 \beta_2 \cdots \beta_{n+1}}^{\alpha_1 \cdots \alpha_m}
		& \eqdef
		\p_{\beta_1} \upxi_{\beta_2 \cdots \beta_{n+1}}^{\alpha_1 \cdots \alpha_m}.
	\end{align}
	\item Let $\SigmatTan$ be a $\Sigma_t$-tangent vectorfield and let $Z$ be a spacetime vectorfield.
	Relative to the Cartesian coordinates, 
	we define (see Remark~\ref{R:OMITTINGZEROCOMPONENTINCARTESIANCOORDINATES}) $|Z \SigmatTan|_g \geq 0$
	as follows:
	\begin{align} 
		|Z \SigmatTan|_g^2 
		& \eqdef g_{ab} (Z \SigmatTan^a) Z \SigmatTan^b,
	\end{align}
	where as usual $Z \SigmatTan^a = Z^{\alpha} \partial_{\alpha} \SigmatTan^a$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:23:05.284066+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_823dc14d573a00f1c6bb408a3292d94d7305bc2c595b38edf8347740f274dac1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 (Exact shape-constrained frontiers) of arXiv:2608.21496, '2608.21496'] For integers $N>m\ge1$:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item For monotone reliability,
\begin{equation}
\Delta^{\uparrow}_{m,N}
=2\inf_{p\in\cP_m}\|u^N-p(u)\|_\infty.

\end{equation}
Thus the worst monotone worlds may be chosen as step functions, and the exact validation problem is uniform monomial approximation rather than the unrestricted $L^1$ problem.
\item For every $0<L<\infty$, monotone $L$-Lipschitz reliability has the exact finite dual
\begin{equation}
\Delta^{\uparrow,L}_{m,N}
=\inf_{a\in\mathbb R^m}\{H_L(z_a)+H_L(-z_a)\}.

\end{equation}
Evaluation therefore reduces to the $m$ audit coefficients and the scalar threshold in Eq.~\ref{eq:cappedthreshold}.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:48:27.282255+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8244c256f14346bb175a17585f0be9e933b1d8608a8d2cd64ef67fc03091e63f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2604.25336, 'Central limit theorem for slow-fast system under mixed fractional Brownian motion'] Under assumptions \textbf{A1}-\textbf{A3} and \(\bar g (x)= \int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}} g(x,y)\mu(dy)=0\). Define that 
\begin{equation}
\Psi(x,y) =\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathcal{P}_t g(x, Y_t) \dd t.
\end{equation} 
The \(\Psi(x,y)\) is the solution to the Poisson equation \eqref{Poisson_eq} where \(h\) is replaced by \(g\).
Moreover, \(\Psi(x,y)\in C^2\). For all \((x,y)\in \mathbb{R}^n\times \mathbb{R}^m\), \(D_x\Psi(x,y)\) and \(D^2_{xx}\Psi(x,y)\) is linear growth. \(D_y\Psi(x,y), D^2_{xy}\Psi(x,y), D^2_{yy}\Psi(x,y)\) is globally bounded. 
Furthermore, since \(Y\) is independent of \(x\), \(\Psi\in C^2\) is Lipshitz continuous with respect to \(y\) uniformly over all \(x\) . (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:49:15.474754+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82476d8bde15eeacd0830a751b0cab028c10666ea9bf7e54969c94c02fa0deae","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma orth-spc-ev-lrQ of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] For any $V\in\textbf{\textup{L}}$, the functor $\ev_V\colon\cat{$\bm G$-\textbf{\textup{L}}-Top}\to\cat{$\bm{({\textup{O}}(V)\times G)}$-Top}$ is both left and right Quillen for the level model structure on the source and the $\mathcal G_{{\textup{O}}(V),G}$-model structure on the target.
    \begin{proof}
        The functor $\ev_V$ has both adjoints (given by enriched Kan extension), and it is clear from the definitions that it preserves weak equivalences and fibrations. It only remains to show that it also preserves cofibrations, for which it again suffices to show that $\textbf{\textup{L}}(U,V)\times_\varphi G=\varphi_!(\textbf{\textup{L}}(U,V))$ is a cofibrant in the $\mathcal G_{{\textup{O}}(V),G}$-model structure for every homomorphism $\varphi\colon H\to G$ and any finite faithful $H$-representation $U$. 

        By Example~(ex:graph-restr-rQ of the paper), it will suffice to treat the case $\varphi=\textup{id}$. If $\textbf{\textup{L}}(U,V)=\emptyset$, there is nothing to prove, otherwise we may identify $U$ with a subspace of $V$. Then the homeomorphism ${\textup{O}}(V)/{\textup{O}}(V-U)\to\textbf{\textup{L}}(U,V)$ from Remark~(rk:L-top-explicit of the paper) is just the restriction map, which is easily seen to be $(H\times G)$-equivariant with respect to the action on the source via pre- and postcomposition. By Illman's theorem, ${\textup{O}}(V)$ admits the structure of an $({\textup{O}}(V-U)\times {\textup{O}}(V)\times H)$-CW-complex; moreover, the isotropy of any cell of this decomposition has to be contained in $\mathcal G_{{\textup{O}}(V-U)\times {\textup{O}}(V),H}$ as $H$ acts freely on ${\textup{O}}(V)$ by faithfulness of $U\subset V$. Thus, ${\textup{O}}(V)$ is $\mathcal G_{{\textup{O}}(V-U)\times {\textup{O}}(V),H}$-cofibrant, and hence $\textbf{\textup{L}}(U,V)\cong{\textup{O}}(V)/{\textup{O}}(V-U)$ is $\mathcal G_{{\textup{O}}(V),H}$-cofibrant by another application of Example~(ex:graph-restr-rQ of the paper)
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.269591+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8247e47daae202f2f884e49860540df5c381ecd3b10e3b7f7159824732fe5d42","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lu of arXiv:2412.09535, 'Local limit theorem for joint subgraph counts'] Let $p$, $\lambda$, $I$, $x_i$ be as in (cor:linear of the paper). For $i \in I$ let $\chi_i = (x_i-p)/\sqrt{p(1-p)}$ and for $S \subseteq I$ let $\chi_S = \prod_{i \in S} \chi_i$. For all $i \in I$, let $\delta_i$ be a real number. Let $\mathcal{S}$ be a collection of subsets of $I$ of size at least $2$ and for all $S \in \mathcal{S}$ let $\delta_S$ be a real number. Suppose $n$ is a positive integer and $\eps > 0$ such that
\begin{cond}
\item \label[scond]{scond:lu1}$\abs{I} \leq n^{1/\eps}$;
\item \label[scond]{scond:lu2}$\abs{S} \leq 1/\eps$ for all $S \in \mathcal{S}$;
\item \label[scond]{scond:lu3}$\abs{\delta_i} \leq 1.2\pi \sqrt{p(1-p)}$ for all $i \in I$;
\item \label[scond]{scond:lu4}$\sum_{i \in I} \delta_i^2 \geq n^\eps$;
\item \label[scond]{scond:lu5}$\sum_{S \in \mathcal{S}} \delta_S^2 \leq n^{-\eps}$.
\end{cond}
Then
\[\abs*{\sete\brac*{e^{i\paren*{\sum_{i \in I} \delta_i \chi_i + \sum_{S \in \mathcal{S}} \delta_S \chi_S}}}} = n^{-\omega_{\lambda,\eps}(1)}.\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.077540+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82491e3b709e62fd6fd37d73701e8f08a045b977ea7a7d3003ac9d90ad21a5af","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:int_case_rho of arXiv:2509.15446, 'On the pair correlation function of the $\Sineb$ process'] With the notations introduced above, the density  of the $\operatorname{HP}_{\beta, n}$ process is given by
\begin{align}
