{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.088187+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_827eef1327babed6145cb3adfb932cc42b33ca8fdd94e66607d3845a2f57caa1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm-representation of arXiv:2411.12623, 'Random signed measures'] A random signed measure $\xi$ on $S$ has independent increments iff a.s.
	\begin{equation}
		\xi (B)=\sum_{j=1}^p X_j\delta_{s_j}(B)+\alpha(B)+\int_{\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}}x\Psi(B\times dx),\quad B\in\hat{\mathcal{S}}
	\end{equation}
	where $p\in\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$, $X_1,...,X_p$ are independent real-valued random variables, $s_1,...s_p$ are fixed points in $S$, $\alpha$ is given by the difference of two deterministic atomless measures in $\mathcal{M}_S$ and $\Psi$ is a Poisson process on $S\times (\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\})$ independent of $X_1,...,X_p$. The intensity measure of $\Psi$, which we denote by $F$, satisfies 
	\begin{equation}
		\int_{\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}}1\wedge|x| F(B\times dx)<\infty,\quad\textnormal{for every $B\in\hat{\mathcal{S}}$.}
	\end{equation}
	and $F(\{s\}\times (\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}))=0$ for every $s\in S$. Moreover, the representation ((eq-representation of the paper)) is almost surely unique. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:43:04.690273+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82876e163fc799b68b0331f6cee0bcf6c0ccb884cec583e690ac8fe800e6f3f0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2605.17328, 'Transportation cost inequalities for mean reflection SPDEs with white noise'] {\rm(\cite[Lemma 3.1]{KS2019})}
